export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"partcraft","name":"PartCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"partcraft","title":"What's new in 0.6","summary":"PartCraft 0.6 adds sizes typed as you draw, drag handles, one click from a face to a feature, values in the history, base planes that step back, nesting flat parts on sheets, tolerance stack-ups, configurations that turn features off, and permanent magnets in 2-D magnetics.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["whats new","what's new","release notes","changelog","changes","0.6","version","new features","dimension as you draw","typed size","drag handle","extrude from face","cut into","hole here","base planes","history values","relations","nest","nesting","sheet","plate","dxf","laser","waterjet","tolerance","stack-up","stackup","rss","worst case","iso 2768","gd&t","frame analysis","frame3d","configuration","suppress","magnet","permanent magnet","magnetics","interface size","toolbar","pen","touch","indonesian"],"headings":[{"id":"new","text":"New","level":2},{"id":"sketching-and-changing-parts","text":"Sketching and changing parts","level":3},{"id":"making-and-checking","text":"Making and checking","level":3},{"id":"scripts-and-analysis","text":"Scripts and analysis","level":3},{"id":"changed","text":"Changed","level":2},{"id":"fixed","text":"Fixed","level":2},{"id":"in-every-app","text":"In every app","level":2},{"id":"not-in-this-version-yet","text":"Not in this version yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThis is what changed in PartCraft 0.6, compared with 0.5. Sketching and changing a part take fewer clicks, and flat parts and assemblies get new checks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new\"\u003ENew\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"sketching-and-changing-parts\"\u003ESketching and changing parts\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDimension as you draw.\u003C/strong\u003E While drawing a line, rectangle, circle or arc, type its size (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the next one: angle, height, arc angle) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The shape is drawn at that size and each typed size becomes a dimension, so sketches come out sized. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPartCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension as you draw\u003C/span\u003E turns it off. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sketch-basics#type-the-size-as-you-draw\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sketch-basics\" data-hash=\"type-the-size-as-you-draw\"\u003EType the size as you draw\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag handles.\u003C/strong\u003E Select an Extrude and drag the arrow on its end face to change the depth, or a Revolve&#039;s curved arrow to change its angle. The part follows as you drag, letting go is one undo step, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E goes back. Type in the box beside the arrow to set the value exactly. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/change-sizes#drag-it-in-the-view\" data-topic=\"partcraft/change-sizes\" data-hash=\"drag-it-in-the-view\"\u003EDrag it in the view\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne click from a face to a feature.\u003C/strong\u003E Pick a flat face and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude from it\u003C/span\u003E (pulls the whole face out), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut into it\u003C/span\u003E (pushes it in) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHole here\u003C/span\u003E (a hole through where you clicked). Each is one undo step; type a number straight after for the depth or the diameter. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/extrude-and-cut#pull-or-push-a-whole-face\" data-topic=\"partcraft/extrude-and-cut\" data-hash=\"pull-or-push-a-whole-face\"\u003EPull or push a whole face\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/holes\" data-topic=\"partcraft/holes\"\u003EDrill holes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EValues in the history.\u003C/strong\u003E Each feature&#039;s row shows its main number, such as &quot;Extrude1 25 mm&quot; or &quot;Hole1 Ø6.6 thru&quot;. Double-click it, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F2\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, to change it right there, with equations and undo included. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/change-sizes#change-the-number-in-the-history\" data-topic=\"partcraft/change-sizes\" data-hash=\"change-the-number-in-the-history\"\u003EChange the number in the history\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe base planes step back.\u003C/strong\u003E Once a part has a solid, the Front, Top and Right planes are drawn faint and clicks go through them to the part. They come back in full while you choose where to sketch or when you select one in the history. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBase planes\u003C/span\u003E hides them altogether. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/workspace#the-3d-view-middle\" data-topic=\"partcraft/workspace\" data-hash=\"the-3d-view-middle\"\u003EThe 3D view\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERepeated relations in one row.\u003C/strong\u003E The sketch panel lists &quot;Coincident × 5&quot; instead of five rows. Open the row to see each one, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E all of them at once. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/relations-dimensions#the-relations-list\" data-topic=\"partcraft/relations-dimensions\" data-hash=\"the-relations-list\"\u003EThe relations list\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConfigurations can turn features off.\u003C/strong\u003E Save a configuration with a feature suppressed, such as no holes or no chamfer. Applying it turns that feature off and the others back on, with its sizes, in one step: one part, with and without. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/design-tables#turn-features-on-and-off-in-a-configuration\" data-topic=\"partcraft/design-tables\" data-hash=\"turn-features-on-and-off-in-a-configuration\"\u003ETurn features on and off in a configuration\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"making-and-checking\"\u003EMaking and checking\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENest flat parts on sheets.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENest flat parts on sheets…\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENest on sheets…\u003C/span\u003E under a sheet metal flat pattern, lays flat parts on plates or a roll with edge margins, a space between parts, turns and grain. It shows how many plates and how much material it takes, and saves the layout as a DXF for the laser or waterjet. Parts come from the flat pattern, from every sheet metal part of an assembly, or from any DXF file; small parts are placed inside big parts&#039; holes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/nest-flat-parts\" data-topic=\"partcraft/nest-flat-parts\"\u003ENest flat parts on sheets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETolerance stack-ups.\u003C/strong\u003E In an assembly, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStack-up\u003C/span\u003E lets you click a chain of faces through the parts and see how much the gap at its end can vary: worst case and RSS (root sum square), how many assemblies in a million fall outside your limits, and the dimensions that matter most, ranked first. Tolerances come from each part&#039;s general tolerance (±, +/−, ISO 2768), from the GD&amp;T frames on its drawing, or typed. It is saved with the assembly, and exports as CSV. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/tolerance-stackup\" data-topic=\"partcraft/tolerance-stackup\"\u003ETolerance stack-ups\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"scripts-and-analysis\"\u003EScripts and analysis\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrame analysis in the analysis library.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Eframe3d\u003C/code\u003E is frame analysis written out as a script you can read, run and change. DetailCraft and BimCraft export a whole model for it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-library#groups\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-library\" data-hash=\"groups\"\u003EThe analysis library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPermanent magnets in 2-D magnetics.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Emagnetostatic2d\u003C/code\u003E takes permanent magnets, with a remanence and direction for each region. A new example puts a magnet in an iron core with an air gap and works out the magnet&#039;s working point, how much flux leaks, the field in the gap and the pull. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/magnetic-fields-2d#permanent-magnets\" data-topic=\"partcraft/magnetic-fields-2d\" data-hash=\"permanent-magnets\"\u003EPermanent magnets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"changed\"\u003EChanged\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENewer file versions, only when needed.\u003C/strong\u003E An assembly with a stack-up is saved as assembly version 5, and a part with a configuration that turns features off is saved as part version 4. PartCraft 0.5 refuses both with its &quot;newer version&quot; message instead of opening them wrong. Assemblies without a stack-up, and parts without such a configuration, are saved as before. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/save-reopen\" data-topic=\"partcraft/save-reopen\"\u003ESave and reopen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe toolbar stays one row on a 1440 px window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fixed\"\u003EFixed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETyping a single digit after selecting a feature, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, did nothing. Two or more digits worked; one digit works now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-every-app\"\u003EIn every app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe toolbar can&#039;t get lost.\u003C/strong\u003E While full screen hides the bars, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top of the window. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E puts the toolbar, panels, screen mode and menu style back to how the app starts (your interface size and panel text size stay). In CadCraft Studio, its own \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/lost-toolbar\" data-topic=\"basics/lost-toolbar\"\u003EWhere did my toolbar go?\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA menu on the toolbar.\u003C/strong\u003E Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it, to arrange it, lock it so nothing moves by accident, or reset it. Ribbon menu styles collapse to their tab names: a tab opens its tools for one command; double-click a tab or use the pin to keep the ribbon open. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/lost-toolbar#the-toolbars-own-menu\" data-topic=\"basics/lost-toolbar\" data-hash=\"the-toolbars-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInterface size.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs 80 % to 140 % of normal, for this app only, so two apps side by side on one monitor both fit. The drawing keeps its own zoom. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/lost-toolbar#everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\" data-topic=\"basics/lost-toolbar\" data-hash=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPen and touch.\u003C/strong\u003E On a tablet or a touch-screen laptop with a pen, your palm is ignored while you draw, fingers can be left to moving the view, a two-finger tap undoes and a three-finger tap redoes, and the pen&#039;s side button and eraser end can undo or redo. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/pen-and-touch\" data-topic=\"basics/pen-and-touch\"\u003EDraw with a pen on a tablet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELive sessions can grow past 20 people.\u003C/strong\u003E In CadCraft Studio, when more people knock than the usual 20, Studio tells you what to expect and you choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E for this session or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecent edits\u003C/span\u003E in the Live panel shows who changed what. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/work-together-live#when-many-people-join\" data-topic=\"basics/work-together-live\" data-hash=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWork together live\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBahasa Indonesia\u003C/strong\u003E is new in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELanguage\u003C/span\u003E: a machine-made draft of every word, not yet reviewed by a native speaker. \u003Cstrong\u003ESpanish (Mexico)\u003C/strong\u003E is still a draft too. Help topics are in English.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInstallers are still not signed\u003C/strong\u003E (signing comes next, in 0.7), and CadCraft Studio is not in an app store yet. Check the SHA-256 beside each file on getcadcraft.com/download. On Windows, if SmartScreen stops the installer, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore info\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun anyway\u003C/span\u003E. On a Mac, the first time, right-click CadCraft Studio and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E. There is no iPad or iPhone build yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFixed:\u003C/strong\u003E with the bars hidden, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar…\u003C/span\u003E in Settings was grayed out, and from the keyboard it lost its place after the first move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-in-this-version-yet\"\u003ENot in this version yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStack-ups run in one direction only. GD&amp;T frames give ± half the zone, a material-condition bonus isn&#039;t counted, and RSS assumes ±3σ normal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFaint base planes can&#039;t be clicked. A typed line angle other than 0°, 90°, 180° or 270° isn&#039;t dimensioned. There&#039;s no drag handle on Through all or up-to ends, or on values set by an equation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA big nest runs for a few seconds and the page waits.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMagnetics: permanent magnets are in 2-D only. A mesher for curved parts, saturating steel and motor parts aren&#039;t built yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EConfigurations: variant packs, library variants, a configurator page and picking variants in BimCraft aren&#039;t built yet. More machining strategies (3-D finishing, rest machining, posts, turning) aren&#039;t built yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything else is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"This is what changed in PartCraft 0.6, compared with 0.5. Sketching and changing a part take fewer clicks, and flat parts and assemblies get new checks. New Sketching and changing parts Dimension as you draw. While drawing a line, rectangle, circle or arc, type its size (Tab for the next one: angle, height, arc angle) and press Enter. The shape is drawn at that size and each typed size becomes a dimension, so sketches come out sized. Settings › PartCraft › Dimension as you draw turns it off. See Type the size as you draw. Drag handles. Select an Extrude and drag the arrow on its end face to change the depth, or a Revolve's curved arrow to change its angle. The part follows as you drag, letting go is one undo step, and Esc goes back. Type in the box beside the arrow to set the value exactly. See Drag it in the view. One click from a face to a feature. Pick a flat face and choose Extrude from it (pulls the whole face out), Cut into it (pushes it in) or Hole here (a hole through where you clicked). Each is one undo step; type a number straight after for the depth or the diameter. See Pull or push a whole face and Drill holes. Values in the history. Each feature's row shows its main number, such as \"Extrude1 25 mm\" or \"Hole1 Ø6.6 thru\". Double-click it, or press F2, to change it right there, with equations and undo included. See Change the number in the history. The base planes step back. Once a part has a solid, the Front, Top and Right planes are drawn faint and clicks go through them to the part. They come back in full while you choose where to sketch or when you select one in the history. Views › Base planes hides them altogether. See The 3D view. Repeated relations in one row. The sketch panel lists \"Coincident × 5\" instead of five rows. Open the row to see each one, or Select or Delete all of them at once. See The relations list. Configurations can turn features off. Save a configuration with a feature suppressed, such as no holes or no chamfer. Applying it turns that feature off and the others back on, with its sizes, in one step: one part, with and without. See Turn features on and off in a configuration. Making and checking Nest flat parts on sheets. Export › Nest flat parts on sheets…, or Nest on sheets… under a sheet metal flat pattern, lays flat parts on plates or a roll with edge margins, a space between parts, turns and grain. It shows how many plates and how much material it takes, and saves the layout as a DXF for the laser or waterjet. Parts come from the flat pattern, from every sheet metal part of an assembly, or from any DXF file; small parts are placed inside big parts' holes. See Nest flat parts on sheets. Tolerance stack-ups. In an assembly, Stack-up lets you click a chain of faces through the parts and see how much the gap at its end can vary: worst case and RSS (root sum square), how many assemblies in a million fall outside your limits, and the dimensions that matter most, ranked first. Tolerances come from each part's general tolerance (±, +/−, ISO 2768), from the GD&T frames on its drawing, or typed. It is saved with the assembly, and exports as CSV. See Tolerance stack-ups. Scripts and analysis Frame analysis in the analysis library. frame3d is frame analysis written out as a script you can read, run and change. DetailCraft and BimCraft export a whole model for it. See The analysis library. Permanent magnets in 2-D magnetics. magnetostatic2d takes permanent magnets, with a remanence and direction for each region. A new example puts a magnet in an iron core with an air gap and works out the magnet's working point, how much flux leaks, the field in the gap and the pull. See Permanent magnets. Changed Newer file versions, only when needed. An assembly with a stack-up is saved as assembly version 5, and a part with a configuration that turns features off is saved as part version 4. PartCraft 0.5 refuses both with its \"newer version\" message instead of opening them wrong. Assemblies without a stack-up, and parts without such a configuration, are saved as before. See Save and reopen. The toolbar stays one row on a 1440 px window. Fixed Typing a single digit after selecting a feature, then Enter, did nothing. Two or more digits worked; one digit works now. In every app The toolbar can't get lost. While full screen hides the bars, a small Show bars tab stays at the top of the window. Settings › General › Reset the layout puts the toolbar, panels, screen mode and menu style back to how the app starts (your interface size and panel text size stay). In CadCraft Studio, its own Settings has Reset the layout in every app. See Where did my toolbar go?. A menu on the toolbar. Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it, to arrange it, lock it so nothing moves by accident, or reset it. Ribbon menu styles collapse to their tab names: a tab opens its tools for one command; double-click a tab or use the pin to keep the ribbon open. See The toolbar's own menu. Interface size. Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs 80 % to 140 % of normal, for this app only, so two apps side by side on one monitor both fit. The drawing keeps its own zoom. See Everything is too big or too small. Pen and touch. On a tablet or a touch-screen laptop with a pen, your palm is ignored while you draw, fingers can be left to moving the view, a two-finger tap undoes and a three-finger tap redoes, and the pen's side button and eraser end can undo or redo. Settings › General › Pen and touch. See Draw with a pen on a tablet. Live sessions can grow past 20 people. In CadCraft Studio, when more people knock than the usual 20, Studio tells you what to expect and you choose Allow anyway for this session or Keep it at 20. Recent edits in the Live panel shows who changed what. See Work together live. Bahasa Indonesia is new in Settings › General › Language: a machine-made draft of every word, not yet reviewed by a native speaker. Spanish (Mexico) is still a draft too. Help topics are in English. Installers are still not signed (signing comes next, in 0.7), and CadCraft Studio is not in an app store yet. Check the SHA-256 beside each file on getcadcraft.com/download. On Windows, if SmartScreen stops the installer, choose More info, then Run anyway. On a Mac, the first time, right-click CadCraft Studio and choose Open. There is no iPad or iPhone build yet. Fixed: with the bars hidden, Arrange the toolbar… in Settings was grayed out, and from the keyboard it lost its place after the first move. Not in this version yet Stack-ups run in one direction only. GD&T frames give ± half the zone, a material-condition bonus isn't counted, and RSS assumes ±3σ normal. Faint base planes can't be clicked. A typed line angle other than 0°, 90°, 180° or 270° isn't dimensioned. There's no drag handle on Through all or up-to ends, or on values set by an equation. A big nest runs for a few seconds and the page waits. Magnetics: permanent magnets are in 2-D only. A mesher for curved parts, saturating steel and motor parts aren't built yet. Configurations: variant packs, library variants, a configurator page and picking variants in BimCraft aren't built yet. More machining strategies (3-D finishing, rest machining, posts, turning) aren't built yet. Everything else is in What is not built yet."},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"partcraft","title":"Get started with PartCraft","summary":"What PartCraft does today, what your browser needs, and a first part in ten minutes, from sketch to saved file.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["start","first run","quick start","introduction","tutorial","first part","plate","parametric","part modeler"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-need","text":"What you need","level":2},{"id":"open-partcraft","text":"Open PartCraft","level":2},{"id":"your-first-part-a-plate-with-a-hole","text":"Your first part: a plate with a hole","level":2},{"id":"sketch-the-outline","text":"Sketch the outline","level":3},{"id":"give-it-sizes","text":"Give it sizes","level":3},{"id":"extrude-it","text":"Extrude it","level":3},{"id":"add-the-hole","text":"Add the hole","level":3},{"id":"save-it","text":"Save it","level":3},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft is the parametric part and assembly app of the CadCraft family. You draw a 2D sketch, tie it down with relations and dimensions, turn it into a solid with features such as extrude, revolve and hole, and change any number later: the part rebuilds from its history. Parts then go into assemblies, where joints place them and let you check the motion.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat this build does:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EJob\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketches\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines, rectangles, circles, arcs, points and centerlines, with relations and dimensions, and a live state: under, fully or over defined\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESolid features\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude, cut, revolve, revolved cut, simple and counterbored holes, linear and circular patterns, mirror, fillets and chamfers on straight edges between flat faces, and shells and drafts on flat faces. Sweeps, lofts and the other features that need an exact kernel are not built yet in this version: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHistory\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit any feature&#039;s numbers, suppress, roll back, reorder and delete, with undo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssemblies\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParts joined by rigid, revolute and slider joints, a clash check and a motion check\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn A3 sheet with three views, an isometric, a section, a detail view, overall sizes, datums, feature control frames and surface finish symbols, exported as PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFiles\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts own part and assembly files; STL, 3MF, faceted STEP, DXF and PDF out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything runs in your browser, on your computer. \u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/files\" data-topic=\"partcraft/files\"\u003EFiles: open, save and export\u003C/a\u003E for where files go.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-need\"\u003EWhat you need\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA current Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari with \u003Cstrong\u003EWebGL\u003C/strong\u003E (3D graphics) and \u003Cstrong\u003EWebAssembly\u003C/strong\u003E turned on. Both are on by default. The solid geometry is built by a WebAssembly mesh kernel that loads with the app: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA mouse is easiest. A trackpad works, and touch screens work for viewing and simple sketching: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/trackpad-and-touch\" data-topic=\"partcraft/trackpad-and-touch\"\u003ETrackpad, touch and small screens\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA screen at least 1280 pixels wide shows the whole toolbar in one row. Narrower windows scroll the toolbar sideways; on a phone the panels open as sheets from a bar at the bottom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMore about browsers: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft runs in the browser, and on the desktop inside CadCraft Studio. Cloud saving is not available yet: you open parts from your computer and save them back to it as files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-partcraft\"\u003EOpen PartCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen PartCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the screen says &quot;Loading the solid kernel…&quot;. This takes a moment the first time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYou see an empty 3D view with three base planes, the history on the left (&quot;Part1&quot;, then Front plane, Top plane, Right plane) and a box in the view headed &quot;Start a part&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe chip near the right end of the status bar says &quot;Mesh kernel&quot;: PartCraft builds every part with the mesh kernel in this version, so round faces are flat facets. You can start modeling straight away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the view says the browser cannot show 3D, or the kernel did not load, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/troubleshooting#the-app-does-not-start\" data-topic=\"partcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"the-app-does-not-start\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-part-a-plate-with-a-hole\"\u003EYour first part: a plate with a hole\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou will make a 60 × 40 mm plate, 10 mm thick, with a 6 mm hole through it. Units are millimeters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"sketch-the-outline\"\u003ESketch the outline\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003EFront plane\u003C/strong\u003E in the history on the left. The plane highlights in the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The view turns to look straight at the plane, and the Line tool is active.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the Rectangle tool.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer onto the origin (the small red circle with a cross). A small square shows that it snaps. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove up and to the right and click again. You have a rectangle. Its lines are blue: blue means they can still move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"give-it-sizes\"\u003EGive it sizes\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the Dimension tool.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the top line, then click a little above it. A number box opens with the current length.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E60\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The rectangle changes to 60 mm wide.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the left line, then click a little to the left of it. Type \u003Ccode\u003E40\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe chip at the top left of the view now says \u003Cstrong\u003EFully defined\u003C/strong\u003E and the lines turn to ink (black on the Paper theme, light on the Model theme). Every point and size is fixed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you started the rectangle away from the origin, the chip still says &quot;Under defined · 2 degrees of freedom&quot;. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for Select, click one corner, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click the origin, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E• Coincident\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"extrude-it\"\u003EExtrude it\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E until the sketch closes (usually twice).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E. A 10 mm solid appears. &quot;Extrude1&quot; is added to the history, with &quot;Sketch1&quot; indented under it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe right panel shows the extrude&#039;s settings. To change the thickness, type a new number in \u003Cstrong\u003EDepth (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The part rebuilds at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"add-the-hole\"\u003EAdd the hole\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the large flat face at the front of the plate (the end of the extrusion). The whole face highlights and the right panel says \u003Cstrong\u003EFace\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch on this face\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the Point tool and click near the middle of the face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHole\u003C/span\u003E. A 6 mm hole goes through the plate. Change \u003Cstrong\u003EDiameter (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel if you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EPart\u003C/strong\u003E box at the bottom left now reads a volume, a surface area, the size (60 × 40 × 10 mm) and &quot;Through holes: 1&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"save-it\"\u003ESave it\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYour browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003EPart1.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E to its downloads folder. The file holds the whole history: open it again with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E to keep editing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo rename the part first, click empty space in the view so nothing is selected and type a new \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-to-go-next\"\u003EWhere to go next\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sketch-basics\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sketch-basics\"\u003EDraw your first sketch\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/relations-dimensions\" data-topic=\"partcraft/relations-dimensions\"\u003EFully define a sketch\u003C/a\u003E explain the sketcher properly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/edit-history\" data-topic=\"partcraft/edit-history\"\u003EChange a part through its history\u003C/a\u003E shows how to edit what you built.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\" data-topic=\"partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\"\u003ERound, bevel and hollow\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/drawing-pdf\" data-topic=\"partcraft/drawing-pdf\"\u003EMake a drawing\u003C/a\u003E finish the part. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E lists what this version does not do, such as sweeps and lofts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"partcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from another parametric modeler\u003C/a\u003E lists the habits that work differently here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"partcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E is the full list of shortcuts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/help\" data-topic=\"basics/help\"\u003EUsing this help\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"PartCraft is the parametric part and assembly app of the CadCraft family. You draw a 2D sketch, tie it down with relations and dimensions, turn it into a solid with features such as extrude, revolve and hole, and change any number later: the part rebuilds from its history. Parts then go into assemblies, where joints place them and let you check the motion. What this build does: Job What you get Sketches Lines, rectangles, circles, arcs, points and centerlines, with relations and dimensions, and a live state: under, fully or over defined Solid features Extrude, cut, revolve, revolved cut, simple and counterbored holes, linear and circular patterns, mirror, fillets and chamfers on straight edges between flat faces, and shells and drafts on flat faces. Sweeps, lofts and the other features that need an exact kernel are not built yet in this version: see What is not built yet History Edit any feature's numbers, suppress, roll back, reorder and delete, with undo Assemblies Parts joined by rigid, revolute and slider joints, a clash check and a motion check Drawings An A3 sheet with three views, an isometric, a section, a detail view, overall sizes, datums, feature control frames and surface finish symbols, exported as PDF Files Its own part and assembly files; STL, 3MF, faceted STEP, DXF and PDF out Everything runs in your browser, on your computer. Nothing is uploaded. See Files: open, save and export for where files go. What you need A current Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari with WebGL (3D graphics) and WebAssembly turned on. Both are on by default. The solid geometry is built by a WebAssembly mesh kernel that loads with the app: see The mesh kernel. A mouse is easiest. A trackpad works, and touch screens work for viewing and simple sketching: see Trackpad, touch and small screens. A screen at least 1280 pixels wide shows the whole toolbar in one row. Narrower windows scroll the toolbar sideways; on a phone the panels open as sheets from a bar at the bottom. More about browsers: Browsers and computers. PartCraft runs in the browser, and on the desktop inside CadCraft Studio. Cloud saving is not available yet: you open parts from your computer and save them back to it as files. Open PartCraft Open PartCraft. Wait while the screen says \"Loading the solid kernel…\". This takes a moment the first time. You see an empty 3D view with three base planes, the history on the left (\"Part1\", then Front plane, Top plane, Right plane) and a box in the view headed \"Start a part\". The chip near the right end of the status bar says \"Mesh kernel\": PartCraft builds every part with the mesh kernel in this version, so round faces are flat facets. You can start modeling straight away. If the view says the browser cannot show 3D, or the kernel did not load, see Troubleshooting. Your first part: a plate with a hole You will make a 60 × 40 mm plate, 10 mm thick, with a 6 mm hole through it. Units are millimeters. Sketch the outline Click Front plane in the history on the left. The plane highlights in the view. Choose Sketch in the toolbar, or press S. The view turns to look straight at the plane, and the Line tool is active. Press R for the Rectangle tool. Move the pointer onto the origin (the small red circle with a cross). A small square shows that it snaps. Click. Move up and to the right and click again. You have a rectangle. Its lines are blue: blue means they can still move. Give it sizes Press D for the Dimension tool. Click the top line, then click a little above it. A number box opens with the current length. Type 60 and press Enter. The rectangle changes to 60 mm wide. Click the left line, then click a little to the left of it. Type 40 and press Enter. The chip at the top left of the view now says Fully defined and the lines turn to ink (black on the Paper theme, light on the Model theme). Every point and size is fixed. If you started the rectangle away from the origin, the chip still says \"Under defined · 2 degrees of freedom\". Press S for Select, click one corner, Shift+click the origin, then choose • Coincident in the right panel. Extrude it Choose Exit in the toolbar, or press Esc until the sketch closes (usually twice). Choose Extrude. A 10 mm solid appears. \"Extrude1\" is added to the history, with \"Sketch1\" indented under it. The right panel shows the extrude's settings. To change the thickness, type a new number in Depth (mm) and press Enter or Tab. The part rebuilds at once. Add the hole Click the large flat face at the front of the plate (the end of the extrusion). The whole face highlights and the right panel says Face. Choose Sketch on this face in the right panel. Press P for the Point tool and click near the middle of the face. Choose Exit. Choose Hole. A 6 mm hole goes through the plate. Change Diameter (mm) in the right panel if you like. The Part box at the bottom left now reads a volume, a surface area, the size (60 × 40 × 10 mm) and \"Through holes: 1\". Save it Press Ctrl+S or choose Save. Your browser downloads Part1.partcraft.json to its downloads folder. The file holds the whole history: open it again with Open to keep editing. To rename the part first, click empty space in the view so nothing is selected and type a new Name in the right panel. Where to go next Draw your first sketch and Fully define a sketch explain the sketcher properly. Change a part through its history shows how to edit what you built. Round, bevel and hollow and Make a drawing finish the part. What is not built yet lists what this version does not do, such as sweeps and lofts. Coming from another parametric modeler lists the habits that work differently here. Keys and mouse is the full list of shortcuts. Using this help."},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"partcraft","title":"The PartCraft screen","summary":"A tour of every bar and panel: the toolbar and how it changes, the history, the part facts, the 3D view, the properties panel and the status bar.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["screen","layout","toolbar","history","feature tree","properties","panel","status bar","interface","ui","tour","full screen"],"headings":[{"id":"the-app-bar","text":"The app bar","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar","text":"The toolbar","level":2},{"id":"the-history-left","text":"The history (left)","level":2},{"id":"part-facts","text":"Part facts","level":3},{"id":"the-3d-view-middle","text":"The 3D view (middle)","level":2},{"id":"while-you-pick-edges-or-faces","text":"While you pick edges or faces","level":3},{"id":"on-the-drawing-sheet","text":"On the drawing sheet","level":3},{"id":"while-a-sketch-is-open","text":"While a sketch is open","level":3},{"id":"the-properties-panel-right","text":"The properties panel (right)","level":2},{"id":"the-status-bar-bottom","text":"The status bar (bottom)","level":2},{"id":"on-a-narrow-screen","text":"On a narrow screen","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe screen has five parts: the toolbar across the top, the history on the left, the 3D view in the middle, the properties panel on the right and the status bar along the bottom. Above them all sits the CadCraft app bar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-app-bar\"\u003EThe app bar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe thin bar at the very top belongs to the CadCraft site, not to PartCraft itself. It shows the app name and where it runs, a version menu, \u003Cstrong\u003EAbout PartCraft\u003C/strong\u003E, the screen-mode button and the Paper / Model theme switch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPaper\u003C/strong\u003E is dark lines on a light sheet. \u003Cstrong\u003EModel\u003C/strong\u003E is light lines on a dark background. Sketch colors follow the theme.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) has a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPartCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab: which mouse button turns the view, mouse wheel or trackpad, and relations while sketching. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"partcraft/settings\"\u003EPartCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe screen-mode button, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, cycles through normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. With the bars hidden, move the pointer to the top edge (or tap there) to bring them back. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E returns to normal. This key is \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a Mac too, not ⌘.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar\"\u003EThe toolbar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar is grouped. It changes with what you are doing, so it fits one row:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButtons\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShown\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E (a menu)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlways\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhen no sketch is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECenterline\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstruction\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly while a sketch is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeatures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolve\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHole\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircular\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMirror\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShell\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E (a menu: Revolved cut, Plane, Axis, Point, Sweep, Loft, Draft)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn the part, not while a sketch or the drawing is open. Fillet and Chamfer work on straight edges between two flat faces; Shell and Draft on flat faces. Sweep and Loft are not built yet in this version (they need an exact kernel); clicking one says so\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeatures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly while you pick edges or faces for a fillet, chamfer, shell or draft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn the part (usable once the part has a solid)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDatum\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrame\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinish\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetail\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly on the drawing sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn the part (usable once the part has a solid)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd copy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoint\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClash\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit part\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly in an assembly\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhile you edit a part from an assembly\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E (a menu: Cut, Copy, Paste, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Select all, Select none)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlways; each item works on the sketch, the history or the assembly you are in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EView\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENormal to\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E (a menu: Measure, Mass properties, Section, Draft, Zebra, Curvature, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBase planes\u003C/span\u003E, then Isometric, Front, Back, Top, Bottom, Right, Left)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlways\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHover over a button to see what it does and its key. A grayed-out button cannot be used right now: for example the feature buttons while a sketch is open, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoint\u003C/span\u003E before the assembly has two parts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu in the Edit group lists each editing action with its key. What copy and paste act on follows where you are; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"partcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E. A grayed-out item&#039;s tooltip says why it cannot act now.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E menu holds \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTL, for 3D printing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3MF, for 3D printing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP242 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP214 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP214 (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF of the sketch or flat face\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing (PDF): views, section, detail, GD&amp;T\u003C/span\u003E. The two exact STEP items are grayed: they are not built yet in this version, so use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-history-left\"\u003EThe history (left)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel on the left is the part&#039;s feature history, the list PartCraft rebuilds from top to bottom.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe heading is the part&#039;s name (&quot;Part1&quot; until you rename it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFront plane\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETop plane\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ERight plane\u003C/strong\u003E come first. Click one to select it; it highlights in the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBelow them are your features in the order they were made. A sketch that a feature uses is indented under that feature.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA flag at the right end of a row tells you its state:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFlag\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(none)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilt, and a sketch is fully defined\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E−\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA sketch that is under defined (it can still move)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E+\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA sketch that is over defined\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E⚠\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilt, with a warning (hover to read it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E⚠ error\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe feature failed. Hover to read why, or click it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E⚠ not built\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe feature needs another one that failed or is off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eoff\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESuppressed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach feature shows its main number after its name, such as &quot;25 mm&quot; for an Extrude or &quot;Ø6.6 thru&quot; for a hole. Double-click the number, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F2\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on the row, to change it right there. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/change-sizes\" data-topic=\"partcraft/change-sizes\"\u003EChange sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a feature to edit it on the right. Double-click a sketch to open it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you roll back, a \u003Cstrong\u003ERollback bar · roll forward\u003C/strong\u003E row sits in the list. Features below it are grayed and not built.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a row focused, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E move between rows, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↑\"\u003E⌥↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↓\"\u003E⌥↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E move the feature itself, where the history allows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn an assembly the same panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003EParts\u003C/strong\u003E (the first is marked &quot;grounded&quot;) and \u003Cstrong\u003EJoints\u003C/strong\u003E instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"part-facts\"\u003EPart facts\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the history, the \u003Cstrong\u003EPart\u003C/strong\u003E box lists the solid&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EVolume\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface area\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESize (X × Y × Z)\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EThrough holes\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EBodies\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETriangles\u003C/strong\u003E, the number of \u003Cstrong\u003EFeatures\u003C/strong\u003E and how many are \u003Cstrong\u003EFailing\u003C/strong\u003E. Volumes and areas are of the faceted solid built by the mesh kernel, a little under the true value for round shapes (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/features#curves-resolution\" data-topic=\"partcraft/features\" data-hash=\"curves-resolution\"\u003EFeatures and their options\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn an assembly the box is headed \u003Cstrong\u003EAssembly\u003C/strong\u003E: Parts, Instances, Joints, Not joined and Interference.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-3d-view-middle\"\u003EThe 3D view (middle)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view shows the solid, the base planes and any sketch not yet used by a feature.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe base planes are drawn in full while the part is empty. Once it has a solid they step back: drawn faint, so they don&#039;t cover the part, and clicks go through them to the part. They come back in full while you choose where to sketch (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch\u003C/span\u003E with nothing picked) and when you select one in the history. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBase planes\u003C/span\u003E hides them altogether; they still show while you choose where to sketch. PartCraft remembers that choice.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag to turn the part, right-drag or middle-drag to pan, roll the wheel to zoom at the pointer. Details in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"partcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHover a face, edge or plane to see it light up. Click to pick it. Faces highlight whole and edges are drawn and picked whole. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click collects several edges or faces. They stay marked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe view cube at the top right turns with the part: click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to turn, the house button goes back to the start. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/view-cube\" data-topic=\"partcraft/view-cube\"\u003EThe view cube\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA selected Extrude shows an orange arrow on its end face, a Revolve a curved arrow at the end of its turn: drag it to change the depth or the angle, or type in the box beside it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/change-sizes#drag-it-in-the-view\" data-topic=\"partcraft/change-sizes\" data-hash=\"drag-it-in-the-view\"\u003EChange sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen a part is empty, a box headed \u003Cstrong\u003EStart a part\u003C/strong\u003E lists the first steps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop a \u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft-asm.json\u003C/code\u003E file anywhere on the view to open it. Opening \u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.stp\u003C/code\u003E files is not built yet in this version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"while-you-pick-edges-or-faces\"\u003EWhile you pick edges or faces\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShell\u003C/span\u003E with nothing picked (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick edges\u003C/span\u003E on such a feature), the part shows as it was before that feature. Click edges or faces to add or drop them; their marks show what is in. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E finishes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\" data-topic=\"partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\"\u003ERound, bevel and hollow\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"on-the-drawing-sheet\"\u003EOn the drawing sheet\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E replaces the 3D view with the A3 drawing sheet. Wheel zooms, right or middle drag pans, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fits. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/drawing-pdf\" data-topic=\"partcraft/drawing-pdf\"\u003EMake a drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"while-a-sketch-is-open\"\u003EWhile a sketch is open\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view turns to look straight at the sketch plane and a 2D sketch layer appears on top. The part shows only the features made before this sketch. A strip at the top left shows the sketch&#039;s state chip (&quot;Fully defined&quot;, &quot;Under defined · 3 degrees of freedom&quot; and so on) and its name, such as &quot;Sketch2 on a face of Extrude1&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-properties-panel-right\"\u003EThe properties panel (right)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel on the right shows whatever is selected, and its heading names it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPart\u003C/strong\u003E (nothing selected): the part&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E and the \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E setting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA plane: a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch on it\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003EFace\u003C/strong\u003E: what kind it is (Flat face, Cylinder with its radius and axis, Cone, Sphere or Torus), its area, which feature made it, and for a flat face \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch on this face\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENormal to\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport face as DXF\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude from it\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut into it\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHole here\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/extrude-and-cut#pull-or-push-a-whole-face\" data-topic=\"partcraft/extrude-and-cut\" data-hash=\"pull-or-push-a-whole-face\"\u003EPull or push a whole face\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003EEdge\u003C/strong\u003E: its kind (straight, a circle with its centre and radius, or a curve), its length and the two faces it lies between (such as &quot;Extrude1 end face and Extrude1 side&quot;), with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/span\u003E buttons.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESeveral edges or faces picked with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: how many, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/span\u003E for edges, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA feature: its numbers and options, the dimensions of its sketch, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESuppress\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoll back to here\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove up\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove down\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E and its \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn open sketch: its state, the relations you can add to the selection, the list of relations and dimensions, \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd by coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit sketch\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport as DXF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the drawing sheet: the section and detail settings, or the selected GD&amp;T symbol.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn an assembly: the assembly, the selected part, or the selected joint.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery number box takes effect when you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click away. The part rebuilds at once; there is no OK button. Use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-status-bar-bottom\"\u003EThe status bar (bottom)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the left, the pointer&#039;s coordinates in the sketch (only while a sketch is open).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the middle, messages: what to do next, and why something was refused. Read it when nothing seems to happen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENear the right, the kernel chip: always &quot;Mesh kernel&quot; in this version. Hover it for what that means. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the right, a chip: &quot;Rebuilding…&quot; while the part rebuilds, then &quot;Rebuilt in 120 ms&quot; when all is well, or &quot;1 feature failing&quot;. In an assembly it says &quot;Placed in … ms&quot; or counts joint problems.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-narrow-screen\"\u003EOn a narrow screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUp to 760 pixels wide, such as on a phone, the view keeps the screen: the toolbar is one row that scrolls sideways, and the panels open as sheets from a bar at the bottom. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E hides the toolbar and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E puts it at the bottom. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/trackpad-and-touch\" data-topic=\"partcraft/trackpad-and-touch\"\u003ETrackpad, touch and small screens\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The screen has five parts: the toolbar across the top, the history on the left, the 3D view in the middle, the properties panel on the right and the status bar along the bottom. Above them all sits the CadCraft app bar. The app bar The thin bar at the very top belongs to the CadCraft site, not to PartCraft itself. It shows the app name and where it runs, a version menu, About PartCraft, the screen-mode button and the Paper / Model theme switch. Paper is dark lines on a light sheet. Model is light lines on a dark background. Sketch colors follow the theme. Settings (the gear, or Ctrl+,) has a PartCraft tab: which mouse button turns the view, mouse wheel or trackpad, and relations while sketching. See PartCraft settings. The screen-mode button, or Ctrl+Shift+F, cycles through normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. With the bars hidden, move the pointer to the top edge (or tap there) to bring them back. Esc returns to normal. This key is Ctrl+Shift+F on a Mac too, not ⌘. The toolbar The toolbar is grouped. It changes with what you are doing, so it fits one row: Group Buttons Shown File New, Open, Save, Export (a menu) Always Sketch Sketch When no sketch is open Sketch Exit, Line, Rectangle, Circle, Arc, Point, Centerline, Fillet, Dimension, Construction Only while a sketch is open Features Extrude, Cut, Revolve, Hole, Linear, Circular, Mirror, Fillet, Chamfer, Shell, More (a menu: Revolved cut, Plane, Axis, Point, Sweep, Loft, Draft) On the part, not while a sketch or the drawing is open. Fillet and Chamfer work on straight edges between two flat faces; Shell and Draft on flat faces. Sweep and Loft are not built yet in this version (they need an exact kernel); clicking one says so Features Done Only while you pick edges or faces for a fillet, chamfer, shell or draft Drawing Drawing On the part (usable once the part has a solid) Drawing Datum, Frame, Finish, Detail, PDF, Back Only on the drawing sheet Assembly Assembly On the part (usable once the part has a solid) Assembly Add copy, Insert, Joint, Clash, Edit part, Close Only in an assembly Assembly Back While you edit a part from an assembly Edit Undo, Redo, Copy, Paste, More (a menu: Cut, Copy, Paste, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Select all, Select none) Always; each item works on the sketch, the history or the assembly you are in View Fit, Normal to, Views (a menu: Measure, Mass properties, Section, Draft, Zebra, Curvature, Base planes, then Isometric, Front, Back, Top, Bottom, Right, Left) Always Hover over a button to see what it does and its key. A grayed-out button cannot be used right now: for example the feature buttons while a sketch is open, or Joint before the assembly has two parts. The More menu in the Edit group lists each editing action with its key. What copy and paste act on follows where you are; see Copy, paste and duplicate. A grayed-out item's tooltip says why it cannot act now. The Export menu holds STL, for 3D printing, 3MF, for 3D printing, STEP AP242 (exact solid), STEP AP214 (exact solid), STEP (faceted, mesh kernel), STEP AP214 (faceted, mesh kernel), DXF of the sketch or flat face and Drawing (PDF): views, section, detail, GD&T. The two exact STEP items are grayed: they are not built yet in this version, so use STEP (faceted, mesh kernel). The history (left) The panel on the left is the part's feature history, the list PartCraft rebuilds from top to bottom. The heading is the part's name (\"Part1\" until you rename it). Front plane, Top plane and Right plane come first. Click one to select it; it highlights in the view. Below them are your features in the order they were made. A sketch that a feature uses is indented under that feature. A flag at the right end of a row tells you its state: Flag Meaning (none) Built, and a sketch is fully defined − A sketch that is under defined (it can still move) + A sketch that is over defined ⚠ Built, with a warning (hover to read it) ⚠ error The feature failed. Hover to read why, or click it ⚠ not built The feature needs another one that failed or is off off Suppressed Each feature shows its main number after its name, such as \"25 mm\" for an Extrude or \"Ø6.6 thru\" for a hole. Double-click the number, or press F2 on the row, to change it right there. See Change sizes. Click a feature to edit it on the right. Double-click a sketch to open it. When you roll back, a Rollback bar · roll forward row sits in the list. Features below it are grayed and not built. With a row focused, ↑ and ↓ move between rows, and Alt+↑ / Alt+↓ move the feature itself, where the history allows. In an assembly the same panel lists Parts (the first is marked \"grounded\") and Joints instead. Part facts Under the history, the Part box lists the solid's Volume, Surface area, Size (X × Y × Z), Through holes, Bodies, Triangles, the number of Features and how many are Failing. Volumes and areas are of the faceted solid built by the mesh kernel, a little under the true value for round shapes (see Features and their options). In an assembly the box is headed Assembly: Parts, Instances, Joints, Not joined and Interference. The 3D view (middle) The view shows the solid, the base planes and any sketch not yet used by a feature. The base planes are drawn in full while the part is empty. Once it has a solid they step back: drawn faint, so they don't cover the part, and clicks go through them to the part. They come back in full while you choose where to sketch (Sketch with nothing picked) and when you select one in the history. Views › Base planes hides them altogether; they still show while you choose where to sketch. PartCraft remembers that choice. Drag to turn the part, right-drag or middle-drag to pan, roll the wheel to zoom at the pointer. Details in Keys and mouse. Hover a face, edge or plane to see it light up. Click to pick it. Faces highlight whole and edges are drawn and picked whole. Shift+click collects several edges or faces. They stay marked. The view cube at the top right turns with the part: click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to turn, the house button goes back to the start. See The view cube. A selected Extrude shows an orange arrow on its end face, a Revolve a curved arrow at the end of its turn: drag it to change the depth or the angle, or type in the box beside it. See Change sizes. When a part is empty, a box headed Start a part lists the first steps. Drop a .partcraft.json or .partcraft-asm.json file anywhere on the view to open it. Opening .step and .stp files is not built yet in this version. While you pick edges or faces After Fillet, Chamfer or Shell with nothing picked (or Pick edges on such a feature), the part shows as it was before that feature. Click edges or faces to add or drop them; their marks show what is in. Done or Esc finishes. See Round, bevel and hollow. On the drawing sheet Drawing replaces the 3D view with the A3 drawing sheet. Wheel zooms, right or middle drag pans, F fits. See Make a drawing. While a sketch is open The view turns to look straight at the sketch plane and a 2D sketch layer appears on top. The part shows only the features made before this sketch. A strip at the top left shows the sketch's state chip (\"Fully defined\", \"Under defined · 3 degrees of freedom\" and so on) and its name, such as \"Sketch2 on a face of Extrude1\". The properties panel (right) The panel on the right shows whatever is selected, and its heading names it: Part (nothing selected): the part's Name and the Curves setting. A plane: a Sketch on it button. A Face: what kind it is (Flat face, Cylinder with its radius and axis, Cone, Sphere or Torus), its area, which feature made it, and for a flat face Sketch on this face, Normal to, Export face as DXF, Extrude from it, Cut into it and Hole here (see Pull or push a whole face). An Edge: its kind (straight, a circle with its centre and radius, or a curve), its length and the two faces it lies between (such as \"Extrude1 end face and Extrude1 side\"), with Fillet and Chamfer buttons. Several edges or faces picked with Shift: how many, with Fillet and Chamfer for edges, and Clear. A feature: its numbers and options, the dimensions of its sketch, then Suppress, Roll back to here, Move up, Move down, Delete and its Name. An open sketch: its state, the relations you can add to the selection, the list of relations and dimensions, Add by coordinates, and Exit sketch, Fit and Export as DXF. On the drawing sheet: the section and detail settings, or the selected GD&T symbol. In an assembly: the assembly, the selected part, or the selected joint. Every number box takes effect when you press Enter or Tab, or click away. The part rebuilds at once; there is no OK button. Use Ctrl+Z to go back. The status bar (bottom) On the left, the pointer's coordinates in the sketch (only while a sketch is open). In the middle, messages: what to do next, and why something was refused. Read it when nothing seems to happen. Near the right, the kernel chip: always \"Mesh kernel\" in this version. Hover it for what that means. See The mesh kernel. On the right, a chip: \"Rebuilding…\" while the part rebuilds, then \"Rebuilt in 120 ms\" when all is well, or \"1 feature failing\". In an assembly it says \"Placed in … ms\" or counts joint problems. On a narrow screen Up to 760 pixels wide, such as on a phone, the view keeps the screen: the toolbar is one row that scrolls sideways, and the panels open as sheets from a bar at the bottom. Tools hides the toolbar and Move puts it at the bottom. See Trackpad, touch and small screens."},{"slug":"files","scope":"partcraft","title":"Files: open, save and export","summary":"The part and assembly files PartCraft opens and saves, every export format and what it keeps, where files go, and what changes offline or between browsers.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["open","save","export","download","file","partcraft.json","assembly file","stl","3mf","step","dxf","pdf","where are my files","offline","save as"],"headings":[{"id":"what-partcraft-opens","text":"What PartCraft opens","level":2},{"id":"saving","text":"Saving","level":2},{"id":"exporting","text":"Exporting","level":2},{"id":"unsaved-work","text":"Unsaved work","level":2},{"id":"online-and-offline","text":"Online and offline","level":2},{"id":"differences-between-browsers","text":"Differences between browsers","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft works on files on your computer. It never uploads a part, and there is no account or cloud storage involved. Cloud saving is not available yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-partcraft-opens\"\u003EWhat PartCraft opens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExtension\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA PartCraft part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or drop it on the 3D view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA PartCraft assembly\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft-asm.json\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: PartCraft sees which kind it is\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA STEP solid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.stp\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet in this version: it needs an exact kernel, and the status bar says &quot;Opening STEP files: not built yet…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESTL, DXF, IGES and other programs&#039; own part files cannot be opened (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E). How STEP import worked in builds with an exact kernel: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/import-step\" data-topic=\"partcraft/import-step\"\u003EOpen a STEP file and build on it\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERules when opening:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA part or assembly file name must end in \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E, and the file must be under 50 MB.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFiles are checked before anything is shown. A damaged or edited file is refused with a plain reason, for example &quot;This is not a PartCraft file.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA file saved by a newer PartCraft is refused: &quot;This file was saved by a newer PartCraft (file version 3); this one reads version 2.&quot; Older files open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the part on screen has unsaved changes, PartCraft asks first: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave first\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving\"\u003ESaving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E saves what is on screen:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a part: \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;part name&gt;.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E, the complete history (sketches, relations, dimensions, features, the rollback position), the drawing sheet&#039;s section, detail and GD&amp;T notes, and the full text of any STEP solid imported in an earlier build. Features this version cannot build are saved back unchanged. The solid itself is not stored; PartCraft rebuilds it from the history when the file opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn an assembly: \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;assembly name&gt;.partcraft-asm.json\u003C/code\u003E, with \u003Cstrong\u003Eevery part inside it\u003C/strong\u003E. One file carries the whole assembly, so you can send it on its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving is a \u003Cstrong\u003Edownload\u003C/strong\u003E. Your browser puts the file in its downloads folder, or asks where to put it if you set it to ask. Saving again makes a new download; the browser may name it \u003Ccode\u003EPart1 (1).partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E rather than replace the old one. Rename or move files in \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E as you like.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECharacters other than letters, digits, spaces, \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E in the part name become \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E in the file name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost browsers have a setting to ask where to save each download. Turn it on if you want a &quot;Save as&quot; choice every time: in Chrome and Edge it is under Downloads in the settings; in Firefox under General, Files and Applications; in Safari under General, File download location.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMore about where downloads go and how to keep files safe: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"exporting\"\u003EExporting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E menu writes other formats. Each is a download, named after the part.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMenu item\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat is in it\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTL, for 3D printing\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.stl\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBinary STL of the solid&#039;s triangles, millimeters, \u003Cstrong\u003EZ up\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3MF, for 3D printing\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.3mf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3MF with the solid as one object, millimeters, \u003Cstrong\u003EZ up\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP242 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet in this version (grayed): it needs an exact kernel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP214 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet in this version (grayed)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA solid made of flat faces from the mesh: \u003Cstrong\u003Eround surfaces are many small flat facets\u003C/strong\u003E. Millimeters, in PartCraft&#039;s own axes (Y up). The STEP to use for other CAD programs in this version\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF of the sketch or flat face\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; - &lt;sketch&gt;.dxf\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; - face.dxf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDXF R12. A sketch: lines, arcs, circles and points in the sketch&#039;s own coordinates, on layers PROFILE, CONSTRUCTION and POINTS. A picked flat face: its outlines as closed polylines (round edges as their facets)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing (PDF): views, section, detail, GD&amp;T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; - drawing.pdf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe A3 drawing sheet: three views, isometric, section and detail if set, overall sizes, GD&amp;T symbols, title block\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP242 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP214 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E are not built yet in this version: use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E. It has the right shape and volume, but every round face is many flat facets, so other programs cannot select a hole as a cylinder. Set \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E finer first if that matters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExports are of the part on screen. Export is turned off in an assembly; there is no assembly export yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESTL, 3MF, the faceted STEP and the face DXF are always made of facets. The \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E setting (in the right panel with nothing selected) sets how fine they are, from 32 to 256 facets per turn. Choose Fine or Very fine before exporting STL or 3MF for a smooth print.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe PDF drawing uses the reader&#039;s built-in Helvetica font. Characters it cannot show print as &quot;?&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStep by step: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/export-files\" data-topic=\"partcraft/export-files\"\u003EExport for printing, STEP and DXF\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/drawing-pdf\" data-topic=\"partcraft/drawing-pdf\"\u003EMake a drawing sheet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"unsaved-work\"\u003EUnsaved work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPartCraft keeps a recovery copy of the open part or assembly in this browser a moment after each change, so a crash or a closed tab does not lose it: the next time you open PartCraft it asks &quot;Recover your work?&quot;. It is not a save: save to a file to keep your work. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover unsaved work\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you try to close the tab or reload with unsaved changes, the browser asks &quot;Leave site?&quot;. That is the browser&#039;s own box; PartCraft cannot change its wording.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E and closing an assembly ask before discarding unsaved changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-and-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything happens on your computer: the mesh kernel (a WebAssembly program) is downloaded with the app and builds geometry locally, and files are read from and written to your disk. No part data goes over the network. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo work with no connection at all, save PartCraft for offline use while you are online: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar and wait while it counts (&quot;Saving 40 of 212&quot;) until it says \u003Cstrong\u003EOffline ready\u003C/strong\u003E. The saved copy includes the 3D library and the mesh kernel, so afterwards modeling, saving, every export and drawings all work offline. If it says \u003Cstrong\u003ECould not save\u003C/strong\u003E, click it to try again while online; \u003Cstrong\u003EOffline not available\u003C/strong\u003E means this browser window cannot keep a copy (private windows and some managed computers). With the copy saved, clicking \u003Cstrong\u003EOffline ready\u003C/strong\u003E offers \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the offline copy\u003C/span\u003E. Details: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:online\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOnline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are online. Even without saving for offline use, once PartCraft has loaded and the status bar chip says &quot;Mesh kernel&quot;, you can keep working if the connection drops. Just do not reload the page until you are back online.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline. If PartCraft is already open, keep working; saving and exporting still work. If the app bar says \u003Cstrong\u003EOffline ready\u003C/strong\u003E, PartCraft also opens from scratch with no connection; otherwise do not reload this tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-between-browsers\"\u003EDifferences between browsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDownloads.\u003C/strong\u003E Each browser has its own downloads folder and its own way of asking. If a browser asks whether this site may download files, allow it, or saving and exporting cannot work.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag and drop\u003C/strong\u003E of a file onto the view works in Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFiles from a phone or tablet.\u003C/strong\u003E On iPhone and iPad, Open shows the Files app; saved files go to Files, Downloads. On Android they go to the Downloads folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no difference in what the files contain between browsers or operating systems.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"PartCraft works on files on your computer. It never uploads a part, and there is no account or cloud storage involved. Cloud saving is not available yet. What PartCraft opens File Extension How A PartCraft part .partcraft.json Open, Ctrl+O, or drop it on the 3D view A PartCraft assembly .partcraft-asm.json The same: PartCraft sees which kind it is A STEP solid .step, .stp Not built yet in this version: it needs an exact kernel, and the status bar says \"Opening STEP files: not built yet…\" STL, DXF, IGES and other programs' own part files cannot be opened (see What is not built yet). How STEP import worked in builds with an exact kernel: Open a STEP file and build on it. Rules when opening: A part or assembly file name must end in .json, and the file must be under 50 MB. Files are checked before anything is shown. A damaged or edited file is refused with a plain reason, for example \"This is not a PartCraft file.\" A file saved by a newer PartCraft is refused: \"This file was saved by a newer PartCraft (file version 3); this one reads version 2.\" Older files open. If the part on screen has unsaved changes, PartCraft asks first: Cancel, Discard changes or Save first. Saving Save or Ctrl+S saves what is on screen: On a part: \u003Cpart name\u003E.partcraft.json, the complete history (sketches, relations, dimensions, features, the rollback position), the drawing sheet's section, detail and GD&T notes, and the full text of any STEP solid imported in an earlier build. Features this version cannot build are saved back unchanged. The solid itself is not stored; PartCraft rebuilds it from the history when the file opens. In an assembly: \u003Cassembly name\u003E.partcraft-asm.json, with every part inside it. One file carries the whole assembly, so you can send it on its own. Saving is a download. Your browser puts the file in its downloads folder, or asks where to put it if you set it to ask. Saving again makes a new download; the browser may name it Part1 (1).partcraft.json rather than replace the old one. Rename or move files in Finder / File Explorer / your file manager as you like. Characters other than letters, digits, spaces, _, . and - in the part name become _ in the file name. Most browsers have a setting to ask where to save each download. Turn it on if you want a \"Save as\" choice every time: in Chrome and Edge it is under Downloads in the settings; in Firefox under General, Files and Applications; in Safari under General, File download location. More about where downloads go and how to keep files safe: Where your files go. Exporting The Export menu writes other formats. Each is a download, named after the part. Menu item File What is in it STL, for 3D printing \u003Cname\u003E.stl Binary STL of the solid's triangles, millimeters, Z up 3MF, for 3D printing \u003Cname\u003E.3mf 3MF with the solid as one object, millimeters, Z up STEP AP242 (exact solid) \u003Cname\u003E.step Not built yet in this version (grayed): it needs an exact kernel STEP AP214 (exact solid) \u003Cname\u003E.step Not built yet in this version (grayed) STEP (faceted, mesh kernel) \u003Cname\u003E.step A solid made of flat faces from the mesh: round surfaces are many small flat facets. Millimeters, in PartCraft's own axes (Y up). The STEP to use for other CAD programs in this version DXF of the sketch or flat face \u003Cname\u003E - \u003Csketch\u003E.dxf or \u003Cname\u003E - face.dxf DXF R12. A sketch: lines, arcs, circles and points in the sketch's own coordinates, on layers PROFILE, CONSTRUCTION and POINTS. A picked flat face: its outlines as closed polylines (round edges as their facets) Drawing (PDF): views, section, detail, GD&T \u003Cname\u003E - drawing.pdf The A3 drawing sheet: three views, isometric, section and detail if set, overall sizes, GD&T symbols, title block STEP AP242 (exact solid) and STEP AP214 (exact solid) are not built yet in this version: use STEP (faceted, mesh kernel). It has the right shape and volume, but every round face is many flat facets, so other programs cannot select a hole as a cylinder. Set Curves finer first if that matters. Exports are of the part on screen. Export is turned off in an assembly; there is no assembly export yet. STL, 3MF, the faceted STEP and the face DXF are always made of facets. The Curves setting (in the right panel with nothing selected) sets how fine they are, from 32 to 256 facets per turn. Choose Fine or Very fine before exporting STL or 3MF for a smooth print. The PDF drawing uses the reader's built-in Helvetica font. Characters it cannot show print as \"?\". Step by step: Export for printing, STEP and DXF and Make a drawing sheet. Unsaved work PartCraft keeps a recovery copy of the open part or assembly in this browser a moment after each change, so a crash or a closed tab does not lose it: the next time you open PartCraft it asks \"Recover your work?\". It is not a save: save to a file to keep your work. See Recover unsaved work. If you try to close the tab or reload with unsaved changes, the browser asks \"Leave site?\". That is the browser's own box; PartCraft cannot change its wording. New, Open and closing an assembly ask before discarding unsaved changes. Online and offline Everything happens on your computer: the mesh kernel (a WebAssembly program) is downloaded with the app and builds geometry locally, and files are read from and written to your disk. No part data goes over the network. See The mesh kernel. To work with no connection at all, save PartCraft for offline use while you are online: choose Save for offline in the app bar and wait while it counts (\"Saving 40 of 212\") until it says Offline ready. The saved copy includes the 3D library and the mesh kernel, so afterwards modeling, saving, every export and drawings all work offline. If it says Could not save, click it to try again while online; Offline not available means this browser window cannot keep a copy (private windows and some managed computers). With the copy saved, clicking Offline ready offers Save again (get the newest version) and Remove the offline copy. Details: Working offline. You are online. Even without saving for offline use, once PartCraft has loaded and the status bar chip says \"Mesh kernel\", you can keep working if the connection drops. Just do not reload the page until you are back online. You are offline. If PartCraft is already open, keep working; saving and exporting still work. If the app bar says Offline ready, PartCraft also opens from scratch with no connection; otherwise do not reload this tab. Differences between browsers Downloads. Each browser has its own downloads folder and its own way of asking. If a browser asks whether this site may download files, allow it, or saving and exporting cannot work. Drag and drop of a file onto the view works in Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari. Files from a phone or tablet. On iPhone and iPad, Open shows the Files app; saved files go to Files, Downloads. On Android they go to the Downloads folder. There is no difference in what the files contain between browsers or operating systems."},{"slug":"solid-kernels","scope":"partcraft","title":"The mesh kernel","summary":"This version of PartCraft builds every part with a mesh kernel that runs in your browser. What that means for round faces, volumes and files, how to tell it is working, and which features wait for an exact kernel.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["kernel","mesh","manifold","exact","b-rep","faceted","curves","opencascade","occt","fillet needs exact","not built","offline","webassembly"],"headings":[{"id":"what-waits-for-an-exact-kernel","text":"What waits for an exact kernel","level":2},{"id":"round-faces-and-curves","text":"Round faces and Curves","level":2},{"id":"online-and-offline","text":"Online and offline","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA geometry kernel is the engine that turns your history into a solid. This version of PartCraft has one: the \u003Cstrong\u003Emesh kernel\u003C/strong\u003E (manifold-3d). It runs on your computer as a small WebAssembly program and is ready a moment after the app opens. Nothing about your part is sent anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMesh kernel\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound shapes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlat facets. \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E in the part panel sets how fine\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVolumes and areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOf the faceted solid, a little low on round parts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketches, extrude, cut, revolve, holes, patterns, mirror, reference planes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet and Chamfer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn straight edges between flat faces; rounds that meet are mitred\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShell and Draft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShell opens flat faces (exact wall thickness); Draft tilts flat faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExports\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTL, 3MF, STEP (faceted), drawing PDF and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing views\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawn from the facets, section views included\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFaces and edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENamed and known by kind (flat, cylinder, cone, sphere, torus; straight, circle), taken from the sketches: the Simulation, Generative and Manufacture workspaces, Measure, picking and the hole table use them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe chip near the right end of the status bar says \u003Cstrong\u003EMesh kernel\u003C/strong\u003E while it builds your part.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-waits-for-an-exact-kernel\"\u003EWhat waits for an exact kernel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn exact (B-rep) kernel keeps true circles, cylinders and cones, and smooth fillets on any edge, sweeps, lofts, surfaces, sheet metal, ribs, several bodies, STEP import and exact STEP export are built on one. Earlier lab builds of PartCraft used OpenCascade for that. Its license (the LGPL) does not fit this release, where every app is Apache-2.0 and uses only permissively licensed libraries, so it is not included. Nothing here has a date.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUntil then those features say \u003Cstrong\u003ENot built yet\u003C/strong\u003E when you choose them: the status bar gives the reason, and their buttons&#039; tooltips end with &quot;not built yet (needs an exact kernel)&quot;. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E lists each one with what to do instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA part made with them in an earlier build still opens. Those features stay in the history marked as not built (hover one for the reason), the rest of the part builds, and saving keeps them unchanged.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"round-faces-and-curves\"\u003ERound faces and Curves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe mesh kernel draws a circle as a polygon. \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E in the part panel sets how many sides a full circle gets; more sides look smoother and measure closer to the true value, and take longer to build. Volumes, areas and distances from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E are of this faceted solid: for a hole or a boss they are a little smaller than the round shape&#039;s.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-and-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe mesh kernel is part of the app&#039;s own download. To work with no connection, save PartCraft for offline use while you are online: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar and wait until it says \u003Cstrong\u003EOffline ready\u003C/strong\u003E. Details: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A geometry kernel is the engine that turns your history into a solid. This version of PartCraft has one: the mesh kernel (manifold-3d). It runs on your computer as a small WebAssembly program and is ready a moment after the app opens. Nothing about your part is sent anywhere. Mesh kernel Round shapes Flat facets. Curves in the part panel sets how fine Volumes and areas Of the faceted solid, a little low on round parts Sketches, extrude, cut, revolve, holes, patterns, mirror, reference planes Yes Fillet and Chamfer On straight edges between flat faces; rounds that meet are mitred Shell and Draft Shell opens flat faces (exact wall thickness); Draft tilts flat faces Exports STL, 3MF, STEP (faceted), drawing PDF and DXF Drawing views Drawn from the facets, section views included Faces and edges Named and known by kind (flat, cylinder, cone, sphere, torus; straight, circle), taken from the sketches: the Simulation, Generative and Manufacture workspaces, Measure, picking and the hole table use them The chip near the right end of the status bar says Mesh kernel while it builds your part. What waits for an exact kernel An exact (B-rep) kernel keeps true circles, cylinders and cones, and smooth fillets on any edge, sweeps, lofts, surfaces, sheet metal, ribs, several bodies, STEP import and exact STEP export are built on one. Earlier lab builds of PartCraft used OpenCascade for that. Its license (the LGPL) does not fit this release, where every app is Apache-2.0 and uses only permissively licensed libraries, so it is not included. Nothing here has a date. Until then those features say Not built yet when you choose them: the status bar gives the reason, and their buttons' tooltips end with \"not built yet (needs an exact kernel)\". What is not built yet lists each one with what to do instead. A part made with them in an earlier build still opens. Those features stay in the history marked as not built (hover one for the reason), the rest of the part builds, and saving keeps them unchanged. Round faces and Curves The mesh kernel draws a circle as a polygon. Curves in the part panel sets how many sides a full circle gets; more sides look smoother and measure closer to the true value, and take longer to build. Volumes, areas and distances from Measure are of this faceted solid: for a hole or a boss they are a little smaller than the round shape's. Online and offline The mesh kernel is part of the app's own download. To work with no connection, save PartCraft for offline use while you are online: choose Save for offline in the app bar and wait until it says Offline ready. Details: Working offline."},{"slug":"sketch-basics","scope":"partcraft","title":"Draw your first sketch","summary":"Open a sketch on a plane or a flat face, draw lines, rectangles, circles, arcs, points and centerlines, round a corner, select, drag and delete.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["sketch","draw","line","rectangle","circle","arc","point","centerline","construction","fillet","sketch fillet","round corner","snap","select","window","drag","profile"],"headings":[{"id":"start-a-sketch","text":"Start a sketch","level":2},{"id":"the-drawing-tools","text":"The drawing tools","level":2},{"id":"type-the-size-as-you-draw","text":"Type the size as you draw","level":3},{"id":"snapping","text":"Snapping","level":3},{"id":"round-a-corner","text":"Round a corner","level":2},{"id":"construction-geometry","text":"Construction geometry","level":2},{"id":"select-drag-and-delete","text":"Select, drag and delete","level":2},{"id":"zoom-and-pan-in-the-sketch","text":"Zoom and pan in the sketch","level":2},{"id":"close-the-sketch","text":"Close the sketch","level":2},{"id":"what-makes-a-good-profile","text":"What makes a good profile","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery solid starts as a sketch: a flat drawing on a plane. This walkthrough covers the drawing tools. Tying the sketch down with relations and dimensions is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/relations-dimensions\" data-topic=\"partcraft/relations-dimensions\"\u003EFully define a sketch\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-a-sketch\"\u003EStart a sketch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can sketch on a base plane or on any flat face of the part.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick where to sketch:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea base plane: click \u003Cstrong\u003EFront plane\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETop plane\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ERight plane\u003C/strong\u003E in the history, or click the plane in the view;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea flat face: click the face in the view. The whole face highlights.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. (With a face picked you can also choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch on this face\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel; with a plane, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch on it\u003C/span\u003E.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view turns square to the plane, the sketch tools appear in the toolbar, and the Line tool is ready. If you press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch\u003C/span\u003E with nothing picked, the base planes show and the status bar says &quot;Click a plane or a flat face to sketch on. Esc stops.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA curved face cannot hold a sketch: &quot;That face is curved. Pick a flat face or a plane to sketch on.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA sketch on a face follows that face.\u003C/strong\u003E If the face was made by a sketch edge or an extrude&#039;s end, the new sketch moves when the feature changes (a deeper pocket takes the sketch on its floor with it). If the panel says the face is &quot;Fixed where picked&quot;, the sketch stays where the face is now.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-drawing-tools\"\u003EThe drawing tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach tool has a toolbar button and a key. Choose the same button again, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go back to Select.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow to draw\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the start, then click each next point. Lines chain end to end. End the chain with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, a right-click or a double-click. Clicking an existing point also ends it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick one corner, then the opposite corner. You get four lines joined at the corners, two horizontal and two vertical\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the center, then a point on the circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the center, then the start, then the end. The arc runs the way you move the pointer round the center\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick. A point on its own marks a hole center for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHole\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECenterline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELike a line, one segment at a time. Drawn dashed; it is construction geometry, used as a revolve axis or a reference\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a corner where two lines meet, type the radius, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the points the curve passes through; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ends, a click on the first point closes it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sketch-tools\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sketch-tools\"\u003ESplines, trim, offset and other sketch tools\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the piece of a curve to remove, up to the nearest crossings\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/relations-dimensions\" data-topic=\"partcraft/relations-dimensions\"\u003EFully define a sketch\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you draw, a small box next to the pointer shows the length, the size, the diameter or the radius.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"type-the-size-as-you-draw\"\u003EType the size as you draw\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter the first click of a line, rectangle, circle or arc, you can type the size instead of clicking it. Typing a number opens the box beside the pointer with that number in it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBoxes (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E moves to the next)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat happens on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECenterline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength, then Angle (°) from the sketch&#039;s X axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe line is drawn that long, toward the pointer or at the typed angle; the chain goes on from its end\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWidth, then Height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe rectangle is drawn from the first corner toward the pointer; a size you did not type comes from the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDiameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe circle is drawn at that diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadius after the center, then Arc angle (°) after the start\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe start goes that far from the center; then the arc goes that far round, the way you moved the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach size you typed becomes a \u003Cstrong\u003Edriving dimension\u003C/strong\u003E in the same step, so the sketch comes out sized: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes away the shape and its dimensions together. Clicking instead of pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps what you typed and takes the rest from the click. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the box and goes back to the pointer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn this off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPartCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EDimension as you draw\u003C/strong\u003E if you only want to draw by clicks. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"partcraft/settings\"\u003EPartCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, at the end of the sketch tools, holds the control-point spline, Extend, Offset, Mirror, Polygon, Slot, the sketch patterns and Project edge: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sketch-tools\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sketch-tools\"\u003ESplines, trim, offset and other sketch tools\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"snapping\"\u003ESnapping\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESnapping adds relations for you, so the sketch stays joined when it moves:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn an \u003Cstrong\u003Eend point or the origin\u003C/strong\u003E, a small square shows. The new point is made coincident with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a \u003Cstrong\u003Eline, circle or arc\u003C/strong\u003E, a small cross shows. The new point is put on that curve.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA line within 3 degrees of horizontal or vertical snaps to it and gets a horizontal or vertical relation. The label shows &quot;H&quot; or &quot;V&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo avoid a snap, zoom in so you click farther from the point or curve.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"round-a-corner\"\u003ERound a corner\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corner point where two lines meet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA box opens with a suggested radius. Type the radius and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth lines are trimmed and joined by a tangent arc with a radius dimension. Messages you may see: &quot;A fillet needs a corner where exactly two lines meet.&quot; and &quot;That radius is too big for these lines.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sketch fillet rounds a corner of the profile, so the extrude gets rounded vertical edges. To round edges of the solid itself (for example the top edges of a plate), use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E feature on the part instead: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\" data-topic=\"partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\"\u003ERound, bevel and hollow\u003C/a\u003E. In a sketch, the toolbar&#039;s Fillet is the sketch tool; on the part, it is the feature.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"construction-geometry\"\u003EConstruction geometry\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConstruction lines, circles and arcs are dashed and gray. They guide the sketch but are not part of the profile that gets extruded or revolved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the lines, circles or arcs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstruction\u003C/span\u003E. Press it again to switch them back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-drag-and-delete\"\u003ESelect, drag and delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E until no tool is active) for Select.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point, line, circle, arc, relation symbol or dimension to select it. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click adds to the selection or takes away. On Windows and Linux \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click does the same; on a Mac, ⌘+click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag on empty space to draw a window: everything fully inside it is selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a point or a line to move it. The solver keeps every relation and dimension as it moves, so only free geometry moves. Drag a circle&#039;s edge to change its radius.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a dimension&#039;s number to move the label.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel, to delete the selection. Deleting geometry deletes the relations on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"zoom-and-pan-in-the-sketch\"\u003EZoom and pan in the sketch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERoll the wheel to zoom at the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag with the middle or right button to pan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar or the right panel fits the sketch in the view. In a sketch the \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E key is the Fillet tool, not Fit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"close-the-sketch\"\u003EClose the sketch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit sketch\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E until the sketch closes. Each \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first cancels what is in progress, then returns to Select, then clears the selection, and the next one closes the sketch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sketch stays selected, so the next \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolve\u003C/span\u003E uses it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-makes-a-good-profile\"\u003EWhat makes a good profile\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolve\u003C/span\u003E use \u003Cstrong\u003Eevery closed loop\u003C/strong\u003E of the sketch&#039;s solid (non-construction) lines, circles, arcs and splines. Loops inside loops become holes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery end must meet exactly one other end. &quot;The profile is open: an end at (12.5, 40) is not joined to anything.&quot; names the place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo three ends may meet at one point: &quot;The profile branches at (0, 20): more than two ends meet there.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoints and construction geometry are ignored by the profile.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENext: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/relations-dimensions\" data-topic=\"partcraft/relations-dimensions\"\u003EFully define a sketch with relations and dimensions\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every solid starts as a sketch: a flat drawing on a plane. This walkthrough covers the drawing tools. Tying the sketch down with relations and dimensions is in Fully define a sketch. Start a sketch You can sketch on a base plane or on any flat face of the part. Pick where to sketch: a base plane: click Front plane, Top plane or Right plane in the history, or click the plane in the view; a flat face: click the face in the view. The whole face highlights. Choose Sketch in the toolbar or press S. (With a face picked you can also choose Sketch on this face in the right panel; with a plane, Sketch on it.) The view turns square to the plane, the sketch tools appear in the toolbar, and the Line tool is ready. If you press Sketch with nothing picked, the base planes show and the status bar says \"Click a plane or a flat face to sketch on. Esc stops.\" A curved face cannot hold a sketch: \"That face is curved. Pick a flat face or a plane to sketch on.\" A sketch on a face follows that face. If the face was made by a sketch edge or an extrude's end, the new sketch moves when the feature changes (a deeper pocket takes the sketch on its floor with it). If the panel says the face is \"Fixed where picked\", the sketch stays where the face is now. The drawing tools Each tool has a toolbar button and a key. Choose the same button again, press S, or press Esc to go back to Select. Tool Key How to draw Line L Click the start, then click each next point. Lines chain end to end. End the chain with Esc, Enter, a right-click or a double-click. Clicking an existing point also ends it Rectangle R Click one corner, then the opposite corner. You get four lines joined at the corners, two horizontal and two vertical Circle C Click the center, then a point on the circle Arc A Click the center, then the start, then the end. The arc runs the way you move the pointer round the center Point P Click. A point on its own marks a hole center for Hole Centerline N Like a line, one segment at a time. Drawn dashed; it is construction geometry, used as a revolve axis or a reference Fillet F Click a corner where two lines meet, type the radius, press Enter Spline B Click the points the curve passes through; Enter ends, a click on the first point closes it. See Splines, trim, offset and other sketch tools Trim T Click the piece of a curve to remove, up to the nearest crossings Dimension D See Fully define a sketch While you draw, a small box next to the pointer shows the length, the size, the diameter or the radius. Type the size as you draw After the first click of a line, rectangle, circle or arc, you can type the size instead of clicking it. Typing a number opens the box beside the pointer with that number in it: Tool Boxes (Tab moves to the next) What happens on Enter Line, Centerline Length, then Angle (°) from the sketch's X axis The line is drawn that long, toward the pointer or at the typed angle; the chain goes on from its end Rectangle Width, then Height The rectangle is drawn from the first corner toward the pointer; a size you did not type comes from the pointer Circle Diameter The circle is drawn at that diameter Arc Radius after the center, then Arc angle (°) after the start The start goes that far from the center; then the arc goes that far round, the way you moved the pointer Each size you typed becomes a driving dimension in the same step, so the sketch comes out sized: Ctrl+Z takes away the shape and its dimensions together. Clicking instead of pressing Enter keeps what you typed and takes the rest from the click. Esc closes the box and goes back to the pointer. Turn this off in Settings › PartCraft › Dimension as you draw if you only want to draw by clicks. See PartCraft settings. More, at the end of the sketch tools, holds the control-point spline, Extend, Offset, Mirror, Polygon, Slot, the sketch patterns and Project edge: see Splines, trim, offset and other sketch tools. Snapping Snapping adds relations for you, so the sketch stays joined when it moves: On an end point or the origin, a small square shows. The new point is made coincident with it. On a line, circle or arc, a small cross shows. The new point is put on that curve. A line within 3 degrees of horizontal or vertical snaps to it and gets a horizontal or vertical relation. The label shows \"H\" or \"V\". To avoid a snap, zoom in so you click farther from the point or curve. Round a corner Press F or choose Fillet. Click the corner point where two lines meet. A box opens with a suggested radius. Type the radius and press Enter. Both lines are trimmed and joined by a tangent arc with a radius dimension. Messages you may see: \"A fillet needs a corner where exactly two lines meet.\" and \"That radius is too big for these lines.\" A sketch fillet rounds a corner of the profile, so the extrude gets rounded vertical edges. To round edges of the solid itself (for example the top edges of a plate), use the Fillet feature on the part instead: see Round, bevel and hollow. In a sketch, the toolbar's Fillet is the sketch tool; on the part, it is the feature. Construction geometry Construction lines, circles and arcs are dashed and gray. They guide the sketch but are not part of the profile that gets extruded or revolved. Select the lines, circles or arcs. Press Q or choose Construction. Press it again to switch them back. Select, drag and delete Press S (or Esc until no tool is active) for Select. Click a point, line, circle, arc, relation symbol or dimension to select it. Shift+click adds to the selection or takes away. On Windows and Linux Ctrl+click does the same; on a Mac, ⌘+click. Drag on empty space to draw a window: everything fully inside it is selected. Drag a point or a line to move it. The solver keeps every relation and dimension as it moves, so only free geometry moves. Drag a circle's edge to change its radius. Drag a dimension's number to move the label. Press Delete or Backspace, or choose Delete in the right panel, to delete the selection. Deleting geometry deletes the relations on it. Zoom and pan in the sketch Roll the wheel to zoom at the pointer. Drag with the middle or right button to pan. Fit in the toolbar or the right panel fits the sketch in the view. In a sketch the F key is the Fillet tool, not Fit. Close the sketch Choose Exit in the toolbar or Exit sketch in the right panel, or press Esc until the sketch closes. Each Esc first cancels what is in progress, then returns to Select, then clears the selection, and the next one closes the sketch. The sketch stays selected, so the next Extrude or Revolve uses it. What makes a good profile Extrude and Revolve use every closed loop of the sketch's solid (non-construction) lines, circles, arcs and splines. Loops inside loops become holes. Every end must meet exactly one other end. \"The profile is open: an end at (12.5, 40) is not joined to anything.\" names the place. No three ends may meet at one point: \"The profile branches at (0, 20): more than two ends meet there.\" Points and construction geometry are ignored by the profile. Next: Fully define a sketch with relations and dimensions."},{"slug":"view-cube","scope":"partcraft","title":"The view cube","summary":"Click a face, edge or corner of the cube in the corner of the view to look from there, drag it to orbit, turn the compass ring, step around a face-on view, and go home.","section":"tasks","order":11,"keywords":["view cube","viewcube","cube","compass","north","home view","standard views","top","front","right","iso","corner","edge","orbit","rotate view","axis icon","corner indicator","navigation"],"headings":[{"id":"look-from-a-side-an-edge-or-a-corner","text":"Look from a side, an edge or a corner","level":2},{"id":"orbit-by-dragging-the-cube","text":"Orbit by dragging the cube","level":2},{"id":"the-compass-ring","text":"The compass ring","level":2},{"id":"face-on-views-arrows","text":"Face-on views: arrows","level":2},{"id":"home","text":"Home","level":2},{"id":"with-the-keyboard","text":"With the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"axis-icon-or-nothing-instead","text":"Axis icon or nothing instead","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe view cube sits at the top right of the 3D view (not while sketching or on the drawing sheet). It turns with the view, so it always shows which way you are looking: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E is the part&#039;s +Y side (the Top plane), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E the +Z side (the Front plane faces you when you look at it), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E the +X side. It stays see-through until you point at it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-from-a-side-an-edge-or-a-corner\"\u003ELook from a side, an edge or a corner\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint at the cube. The part under the pointer lights up: the middle of a face, an edge (it lights on both faces that share it) or a corner (on all three faces).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick it. The view turns to look from there, keeping the same middle and zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are 26 places to click: 6 faces (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E), 12 edges and 8 corners. A corner gives the classic three-quarter view; an edge looks halfway between two faces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicking \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E gives the same views as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, without the zoom to fit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"orbit-by-dragging-the-cube\"\u003EOrbit by dragging the cube\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the cube to turn the view, the same as orbiting in the view itself. A drag is never taken as a click, so you can grab the cube anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-compass-ring\"\u003EThe compass ring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe ring under the cube carries N, E, S and W.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag the ring\u003C/strong\u003E to turn the model about the vertical axis, as if it stood on a turntable. The ring follows the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClick a letter\u003C/strong\u003E to look from that side, keeping how steeply you look down. In a view from straight above, the letter you click goes to the bottom of the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"face-on-views-arrows\"\u003EFace-on views: arrows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you look straight at a face, small arrows appear round the cube:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe four \u003Cstrong\u003Etriangles\u003C/strong\u003E bring the face on that side to the front: the triangle above the cube shows the face that was at the top, and so on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe two \u003Cstrong\u003Ecurved arrows\u003C/strong\u003E at the top right turn the picture a quarter turn clockwise or counter-clockwise, without changing which face you look at. Use them to turn a plan so that a long wall runs across the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"home\"\u003EHome\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe house button at the top left of the cube goes back to the three-quarter view and fits the part in. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does the same while the cube has the keyboard focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-the-keyboard\"\u003EWith the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to the cube, then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Echoose a side, edge or corner round the cube, in eighth-turn steps (screen readers say which)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Echoose a higher or lower one, up to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E or down to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Elook from the one chosen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift ←\"\u003E⇧←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift →\"\u003E⇧→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eturn the view a quarter turn about the vertical axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe home view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eleave the cube\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeys pressed on the cube stay with the cube: they do not start PartCraft tools.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"axis-icon-or-nothing-instead\"\u003EAxis icon or nothing instead\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPartCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab, and set \u003Cstrong\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView cube\u003C/span\u003E (the start), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis icon\u003C/span\u003E (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left: X red, Y green, Z blue) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENone\u003C/span\u003E. This browser remembers the choice. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/settings#corner-indicator\" data-topic=\"partcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"corner-indicator\"\u003EPartCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe cube shows the part as PartCraft builds it: Y up, so the cube&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E is the Top plane. The compass letters are only for turning; a part has no north.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The view cube sits at the top right of the 3D view (not while sketching or on the drawing sheet). It turns with the view, so it always shows which way you are looking: Top is the part's +Y side (the Top plane), Front the +Z side (the Front plane faces you when you look at it), Right the +X side. It stays see-through until you point at it. Look from a side, an edge or a corner Point at the cube. The part under the pointer lights up: the middle of a face, an edge (it lights on both faces that share it) or a corner (on all three faces). Click it. The view turns to look from there, keeping the same middle and zoom. There are 26 places to click: 6 faces (Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Left, Right), 12 edges and 8 corners. A corner gives the classic three-quarter view; an edge looks halfway between two faces. Clicking Front, Top or Right gives the same views as Views in the toolbar, without the zoom to fit. Orbit by dragging the cube Drag the cube to turn the view, the same as orbiting in the view itself. A drag is never taken as a click, so you can grab the cube anywhere. The compass ring The ring under the cube carries N, E, S and W. Drag the ring to turn the model about the vertical axis, as if it stood on a turntable. The ring follows the pointer. Click a letter to look from that side, keeping how steeply you look down. In a view from straight above, the letter you click goes to the bottom of the screen. Face-on views: arrows When you look straight at a face, small arrows appear round the cube: The four triangles bring the face on that side to the front: the triangle above the cube shows the face that was at the top, and so on. The two curved arrows at the top right turn the picture a quarter turn clockwise or counter-clockwise, without changing which face you look at. Use them to turn a plan so that a long wall runs across the screen. Home The house button at the top left of the cube goes back to the three-quarter view and fits the part in. Home does the same while the cube has the keyboard focus. With the keyboard Tab to the cube, then: Key Does ← / → choose a side, edge or corner round the cube, in eighth-turn steps (screen readers say which) ↑ / ↓ choose a higher or lower one, up to Top or down to Bottom Enter or Space look from the one chosen Shift+← / Shift+→ turn the view a quarter turn about the vertical axis Home the home view Esc leave the cube Keys pressed on the cube stay with the cube: they do not start PartCraft tools. Axis icon or nothing instead Open Settings (Ctrl+,), the PartCraft tab, and set Corner indicator to View cube (the start), Axis icon (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left: X red, Y green, Z blue) or None. This browser remembers the choice. See PartCraft settings. The cube shows the part as PartCraft builds it: Y up, so the cube's Top is the Top plane. The compass letters are only for turning; a part has no north."},{"slug":"sketch-tools","scope":"partcraft","title":"Splines, trim, offset and other sketch tools","summary":"Draw fit-point and control-point splines, trim and extend curves, offset a chain, mirror about a centerline, add polygons and slots, pattern sketch geometry and project model edges.","section":"tasks","order":15,"keywords":["spline","fit point","control point","b-spline","nurbs","curve","trim","extend","offset","mirror","symmetric","polygon","hexagon","slot","pattern","linear pattern","circular pattern","project","project edge","include","convert entities"],"headings":[{"id":"splines","text":"Splines","level":2},{"id":"trim-and-extend","text":"Trim and extend","level":2},{"id":"offset","text":"Offset","level":2},{"id":"mirror","text":"Mirror","level":2},{"id":"polygons-and-slots","text":"Polygons and slots","level":2},{"id":"sketch-patterns","text":"Sketch patterns","level":2},{"id":"project-edges-of-the-part","text":"Project edges of the part","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThese tools work in an open sketch. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpline\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim\u003C/span\u003E are in the toolbar. The others are under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E at the end of the sketch tools. Each tool is one undo step, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stops it. For the basic drawing tools see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sketch-basics\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sketch-basics\"\u003EDraw your first sketch\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"splines\"\u003ESplines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA spline is a smooth curve. There are two kinds.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow to draw\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the points the curve passes through. End with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, a right-click or a double-click. Click the first point again to close the spline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EControl-point spline\u003C/span\u003E (under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the control points. The curve is pulled toward them but only touches the first and last. The dashed control polygon shows while you draw and afterwards\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe points are sketch points.\u003C/strong\u003E Drag them, or give them relations and dimensions like any other point. A spline&#039;s first point can snap onto a line&#039;s end, which joins them with a coincident relation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETangent at an end.\u003C/strong\u003E Select a line, arc or spline that meets one end of a spline, and the spline, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ET Tangent\u003C/span\u003E. The spline&#039;s end direction then follows the line. A tangent away from the spline&#039;s ends is refused: &quot;A tangent to a spline needs the line, arc or spline to meet one end of it.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnds.\u003C/strong\u003E Without a tangent relation, a fit-point spline&#039;s ends are natural (no curvature at the very end). The curve is cubic and passes through every point, with its spacing set by the distances between the points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClosed splines\u003C/strong\u003E make a profile on their own. Open splines join lines and arcs into a closed loop like any other curve.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDeleting a point inside a spline removes that point and keeps the spline, while it still has at least two points (three when closed).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExtrude and revolve build the spline with the mesh kernel from short straight pieces (true curves need an exact kernel: not built yet in this version). DXF export writes a spline as a polyline that stays within 0.01 mm of the curve, because DXF R12 has no spline entity.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and extend\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint at a curve: the piece that would go shows red, up to the nearest crossings. Click to remove it. A curve that crosses nothing is removed whole\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtend\u003C/span\u003E (under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a line or arc near the end to extend. It grows to the next curve it meets. &quot;There is nothing for that end to reach.&quot; when nothing is in the way\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETrim and Extend work on lines, arcs, circles and splines (Extend: lines and arcs).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA trimmed \u003Cstrong\u003Ecircle\u003C/strong\u003E becomes an arc. Its radius or diameter dimension moves to the arc.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrimming the middle of a line leaves two lines that stay on the same straight line. Trimming the middle of an arc leaves two arcs with the same center and an equal relation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA trimmed \u003Cstrong\u003Espline\u003C/strong\u003E becomes a control-point spline that follows the old curve exactly. A tangent relation at an end that is kept stays.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe new end is put on the curve that cut it (on the line, arc or circle, or coincident with its end). Splines cannot hold a point, so a spline that cuts adds no relation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERelations that no longer fit are removed\u003C/strong\u003E, and the status bar says how many. For example, a length dimension on a trimmed line is removed, but horizontal or parallel relations stay.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offset\"\u003EOffset\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a line, arc, circle or spline. Its whole chain highlights: all the curves joined end to end, up to a point where three or more meet. To offset only part of a chain, select those curves first, then choose Offset and click one of them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer to the side where the copy goes. A dashed preview follows. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for both sides.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick, type the distance, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines and arcs are offset exactly. Corners are extended or trimmed until they meet. The copy has one \u003Cstrong\u003EOffset\u003C/strong\u003E dimension, and the other pieces follow it: double-click the dimension to change the whole copy. A distance too big for the chain is refused, for example &quot;That distance is too big for this chain: a side would turn inside out.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Espline\u003C/strong\u003E is offset by a new fit-point spline within 0.001 mm of the true offset. This copy is fixed: it does not follow later changes, and it has no Offset dimension. A spline in a chain needs smooth (tangent) joins at its ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mirror\"\u003EMirror\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith Select, pick what to mirror.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMirror\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the line or centerline to mirror about.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach mirrored point gets a \u003Cstrong\u003ESymmetric\u003C/strong\u003E relation with its original, so the copy follows when the original changes. Points that lie on the mirror line are shared, and mirrored circles get an Equal relation. If nothing was selected, click the items to mirror, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then click the mirror line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"polygons-and-slots\"\u003EPolygons and slots\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the center, then a corner, then type the number of sides (3 to 64). The sides are equal and the corners sit on a construction circle. Dimension the circle to size the polygon\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlot\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick one end&#039;s center, then the other end&#039;s center, then click to set the width. Two lines, tangent to two equal half circles\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sketch-patterns\"\u003ESketch patterns\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the geometry first, then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear pattern\u003C/span\u003E: click a start point, then where the first copy goes, then type the number of copies (including the original).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircular pattern\u003C/span\u003E: click the center, then type the number of copies, spread evenly round a full turn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copies bring the relations among the selected geometry with them (a circular pattern leaves out horizontal and vertical ones). The copies are not linked to the original: change one, and the others stay as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"project-edges-of-the-part\"\u003EProject edges of the part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProject edge\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a straight or circular edge of the part behind the sketch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe edge is copied onto the sketch plane exactly and fixed (Fix relation). It does not follow later changes to the part. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to make it construction geometry. A circle tilted to the sketch plane would project to an ellipse, so it is refused. So are free-form edges and edges that point straight out of the sketch. Projecting needs an exact kernel: it is not built yet in this version.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"These tools work in an open sketch. Spline and Trim are in the toolbar. The others are under More at the end of the sketch tools. Each tool is one undo step, and Esc stops it. For the basic drawing tools see Draw your first sketch. Splines A spline is a smooth curve. There are two kinds. Tool Key How to draw Spline B Click the points the curve passes through. End with Enter, Esc, a right-click or a double-click. Click the first point again to close the spline Control-point spline (under More) Click the control points. The curve is pulled toward them but only touches the first and last. The dashed control polygon shows while you draw and afterwards The points are sketch points. Drag them, or give them relations and dimensions like any other point. A spline's first point can snap onto a line's end, which joins them with a coincident relation. Tangent at an end. Select a line, arc or spline that meets one end of a spline, and the spline, then choose T Tangent. The spline's end direction then follows the line. A tangent away from the spline's ends is refused: \"A tangent to a spline needs the line, arc or spline to meet one end of it.\" Ends. Without a tangent relation, a fit-point spline's ends are natural (no curvature at the very end). The curve is cubic and passes through every point, with its spacing set by the distances between the points. Closed splines make a profile on their own. Open splines join lines and arcs into a closed loop like any other curve. Deleting a point inside a spline removes that point and keeps the spline, while it still has at least two points (three when closed). Extrude and revolve build the spline with the mesh kernel from short straight pieces (true curves need an exact kernel: not built yet in this version). DXF export writes a spline as a polyline that stays within 0.01 mm of the curve, because DXF R12 has no spline entity. Trim and extend Tool Key How Trim T Point at a curve: the piece that would go shows red, up to the nearest crossings. Click to remove it. A curve that crosses nothing is removed whole Extend (under More) E Click a line or arc near the end to extend. It grows to the next curve it meets. \"There is nothing for that end to reach.\" when nothing is in the way Trim and Extend work on lines, arcs, circles and splines (Extend: lines and arcs). A trimmed circle becomes an arc. Its radius or diameter dimension moves to the arc. Trimming the middle of a line leaves two lines that stay on the same straight line. Trimming the middle of an arc leaves two arcs with the same center and an equal relation. A trimmed spline becomes a control-point spline that follows the old curve exactly. A tangent relation at an end that is kept stays. The new end is put on the curve that cut it (on the line, arc or circle, or coincident with its end). Splines cannot hold a point, so a spline that cuts adds no relation. Relations that no longer fit are removed, and the status bar says how many. For example, a length dimension on a trimmed line is removed, but horizontal or parallel relations stay. Offset Choose Offset under More or press O. Click a line, arc, circle or spline. Its whole chain highlights: all the curves joined end to end, up to a point where three or more meet. To offset only part of a chain, select those curves first, then choose Offset and click one of them. Move the pointer to the side where the copy goes. A dashed preview follows. Press B for both sides. Click, type the distance, and press Enter. Lines and arcs are offset exactly. Corners are extended or trimmed until they meet. The copy has one Offset dimension, and the other pieces follow it: double-click the dimension to change the whole copy. A distance too big for the chain is refused, for example \"That distance is too big for this chain: a side would turn inside out.\" A spline is offset by a new fit-point spline within 0.001 mm of the true offset. This copy is fixed: it does not follow later changes, and it has no Offset dimension. A spline in a chain needs smooth (tangent) joins at its ends. Mirror With Select, pick what to mirror. Choose Mirror under More or press M. Click the line or centerline to mirror about. Each mirrored point gets a Symmetric relation with its original, so the copy follows when the original changes. Points that lie on the mirror line are shared, and mirrored circles get an Equal relation. If nothing was selected, click the items to mirror, press Enter, then click the mirror line. Polygons and slots Tool How Polygon Click the center, then a corner, then type the number of sides (3 to 64). The sides are equal and the corners sit on a construction circle. Dimension the circle to size the polygon Slot Click one end's center, then the other end's center, then click to set the width. Two lines, tangent to two equal half circles Sketch patterns Select the geometry first, then: Linear pattern: click a start point, then where the first copy goes, then type the number of copies (including the original). Circular pattern: click the center, then type the number of copies, spread evenly round a full turn. The copies bring the relations among the selected geometry with them (a circular pattern leaves out horizontal and vertical ones). The copies are not linked to the original: change one, and the others stay as they are. Project edges of the part Choose Project edge under More. Click a straight or circular edge of the part behind the sketch. The edge is copied onto the sketch plane exactly and fixed (Fix relation). It does not follow later changes to the part. Press Q to make it construction geometry. A circle tilted to the sketch plane would project to an ellipse, so it is refused. So are free-form edges and edges that point straight out of the sketch. Projecting needs an exact kernel: it is not built yet in this version."},{"slug":"relations-dimensions","scope":"partcraft","title":"Fully define a sketch with relations and dimensions","summary":"Add relations from the right panel and dimensions with the Dimension tool, read the under, fully and over defined colors, and fix a sketch that is over defined.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["relations","constraints","dimensions","smart dimension","fully defined","under defined","over defined","degrees of freedom","coincident","horizontal","vertical","parallel","perpendicular","tangent","equal","concentric","midpoint","fix","conflict","redundant"],"headings":[{"id":"read-the-sketch-state","text":"Read the sketch state","level":2},{"id":"add-a-dimension","text":"Add a dimension","level":2},{"id":"add-a-relation","text":"Add a relation","level":2},{"id":"a-worked-example-an-l-shaped-bracket-profile","text":"A worked example: an L-shaped bracket profile","level":2},{"id":"fix-an-over-defined-sketch","text":"Fix an over defined sketch","level":2},{"id":"drag-to-find-what-is-free","text":"Drag to find what is free","level":2},{"id":"the-relations-list","text":"The relations list","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA sketch is \u003Cstrong\u003Efully defined\u003C/strong\u003E when every point and every size is fixed by relations and dimensions. A fully defined sketch changes only when you change a number, so the part behaves predictably when you edit it later. PartCraft does not force you to fully define a sketch, but it always shows how close you are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-sketch-state\"\u003ERead the sketch state\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe state shows in three places: the chip at the top left of the view, the message at the top of the right panel, and the flag in the history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EState\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChip\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGeometry color\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnder defined\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Under defined · 3 degrees of freedom&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome geometry can still move. Drag blue geometry to see which way\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFully defined\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Fully defined&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInk (black on Paper, light on Model)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery point and size is fixed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOver defined\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Over defined&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed on the relations involved\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome relations contradict each other, or one is already implied by others\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConstruction geometry is gray and dashed whatever its state. Selected items are orange. A &quot;degree of freedom&quot; is one way something can still move: a free point has two (left-right and up-down), a free circle has three (its center and its radius).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe legend under the message in the right panel repeats the colors.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-dimension\"\u003EAdd a dimension\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick what to measure:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eone line\u003C/strong\u003E: its length;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eone circle\u003C/strong\u003E: its diameter; \u003Cstrong\u003Eone arc\u003C/strong\u003E: its radius;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Etwo points\u003C/strong\u003E (an end point, a center, a loose point or the origin): the distance between them;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Ea point and a line\u003C/strong\u003E: the distance from the point to the line;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Etwo parallel lines\u003C/strong\u003E: the distance between them; \u003Cstrong\u003Etwo lines at an angle\u003C/strong\u003E: the angle between them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter one item, the status bar says &quot;Click a second item, or click where the dimension goes.&quot; Click a second item, or click in empty space where you want the number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA number box opens with the current value. Type the size and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps the measured value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhere you place the number decides the kind\u003C/strong\u003E for a slanted line or two points: place it above or below to get a horizontal distance, to the left or right to get a vertical distance, and elsewhere for the true (aligned) distance.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe box accepts plain numbers in millimeters or degrees, such as \u003Ccode\u003E25\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12.5\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1e3\u003C/code\u003E. A leading \u003Ccode\u003E⌀\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EØ\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E, or a trailing \u003Ccode\u003Emm\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E°\u003C/code\u003E, is ignored. Angles are 0 to 180 degrees. Arithmetic such as \u003Ccode\u003E20+5\u003C/code\u003E is not accepted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change a dimension later, double-click its number in the sketch, or choose \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E beside it in the list in the right panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also select geometry first (Select tool) and choose a dimension button in the right panel: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHorizontal distance\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVertical distance\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiameter\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E. Only the ones that fit the selection are shown. This is the way to give a circle a radius instead of a diameter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-relation\"\u003EAdd a relation\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelations are added from the right panel, not with a tool.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for Select.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one item, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click a second one if the relation needs two. The origin can be selected too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe right panel says &quot;Selected: line l3, point p1&quot; and shows a button for each relation that fits, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E∥ Parallel\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E• Coincident\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the button. The sketch solves at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf no relation fits, the panel says &quot;No relation fits this selection. Select one or two items (Shift+click adds).&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe full list of relations, what each one needs and what it does is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sketch-relations\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sketch-relations\"\u003ESketch relations and dimensions\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelations show in the sketch as small symbols next to the geometry (H, V, ∥, ⊥, T, =, ◎, M, ⚓). Coincident dots are hidden unless the geometry is selected or the relation is in trouble. Click a symbol to select the relation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-worked-example-an-l-shaped-bracket-profile\"\u003EA worked example: an L-shaped bracket profile\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESketch on \u003Cstrong\u003EFront plane\u003C/strong\u003E. With the Line tool, start on the origin and click six corners of an L, finishing on the origin again. The chain ends by itself when you click the first point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines drawn close to horizontal or vertical already have H and V relations, except the last one: a line that ends on an existing point gets no automatic relation. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click that last line and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EV Vertical\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel. The chip says &quot;Under defined · 4 degrees of freedom&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Click the bottom line, click below it, type \u003Ccode\u003E80\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the left line, click left of it, type \u003Ccode\u003E60\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDimension the top short line \u003Ccode\u003E20\u003C/code\u003E and the inner vertical line \u003Ccode\u003E45\u003C/code\u003E the same way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe chip now says \u003Cstrong\u003EFully defined\u003C/strong\u003E and the whole L turns to ink.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fix-an-over-defined-sketch\"\u003EFix an over defined sketch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a new relation or dimension contradicts the others, PartCraft does not move anything. The geometry stays exactly as it was, the relations involved turn red, and the status bar names them:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Over defined: Horizontal, Distance 40 cannot all be met. Undo, or delete one of them.&quot; The relations conflict.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Over defined: Parallel is already implied. Delete it.&quot; The relation adds nothing: the others already force it. (A rectangle&#039;s sides are already parallel, for example.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo fix it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to undo the last change, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the red item in the \u003Cstrong\u003ERelations and dimensions\u003C/strong\u003E list in the right panel (a group that holds it is open and red) and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EDelete\u003C/strong\u003E beside it. You can also click its symbol or number in the sketch and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are no &quot;driven&quot; (reference) dimensions in this build. A dimension that would only report a size the other relations already fix is treated as over defined. Read sizes in the panel instead: select a line and look at the Dimension buttons, or check the part facts after you extrude.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn over defined sketch with a real conflict makes the feature that uses it fail (&quot;Over defined: 1 relation cannot all be met.&quot;), and the history marks it \u003Cstrong\u003E⚠ error\u003C/strong\u003E. A sketch that is only redundant still builds, with a ⚠ warning.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drag-to-find-what-is-free\"\u003EDrag to find what is free\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Select, drag any blue point or line. The solver moves it as little as possible while keeping every relation. If a drag would break a relation, the geometry snaps back. This is the quickest way to see which dimension is still missing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-relations-list\"\u003EThe relations list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith nothing selected, the right panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003ERelations and dimensions\u003C/strong\u003E. Each row has:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E (dimensions only): opens the number box;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESelect\u003C/strong\u003E: selects it in the sketch;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete\u003C/strong\u003E: removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelations of one kind that appear more than once share one row, such as &quot;Coincident × 5&quot; for a rectangle tied to the origin. Click the row to open it: each relation is listed by what it ties (&quot;point p2, point p4&quot;) with its own \u003Cstrong\u003ESelect\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EDelete\u003C/strong\u003E. The row itself has:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESelect\u003C/strong\u003E: selects all of them in the sketch;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete all\u003C/strong\u003E: removes all of them in one step (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings them back).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA group that holds an over defined relation opens by itself, with its name in red. Dimensions are always listed one per row, with their values.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith geometry selected, the list shows only the \u003Cstrong\u003ERelations on the selection\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A sketch is fully defined when every point and every size is fixed by relations and dimensions. A fully defined sketch changes only when you change a number, so the part behaves predictably when you edit it later. PartCraft does not force you to fully define a sketch, but it always shows how close you are. Read the sketch state The state shows in three places: the chip at the top left of the view, the message at the top of the right panel, and the flag in the history. State Chip Geometry color What it means Under defined \"Under defined · 3 degrees of freedom\" Blue Some geometry can still move. Drag blue geometry to see which way Fully defined \"Fully defined\" Ink (black on Paper, light on Model) Every point and size is fixed Over defined \"Over defined\" Red on the relations involved Some relations contradict each other, or one is already implied by others Construction geometry is gray and dashed whatever its state. Selected items are orange. A \"degree of freedom\" is one way something can still move: a free point has two (left-right and up-down), a free circle has three (its center and its radius). The legend under the message in the right panel repeats the colors. Add a dimension Press D or choose Dimension. Click what to measure: one line: its length; one circle: its diameter; one arc: its radius; two points (an end point, a center, a loose point or the origin): the distance between them; a point and a line: the distance from the point to the line; two parallel lines: the distance between them; two lines at an angle: the angle between them. After one item, the status bar says \"Click a second item, or click where the dimension goes.\" Click a second item, or click in empty space where you want the number. A number box opens with the current value. Type the size and press Enter. Esc keeps the measured value. Where you place the number decides the kind for a slanted line or two points: place it above or below to get a horizontal distance, to the left or right to get a vertical distance, and elsewhere for the true (aligned) distance. The box accepts plain numbers in millimeters or degrees, such as 25, 12.5 or 1e3. A leading ⌀, Ø or R, or a trailing mm or °, is ignored. Angles are 0 to 180 degrees. Arithmetic such as 20+5 is not accepted. To change a dimension later, double-click its number in the sketch, or choose Edit beside it in the list in the right panel. You can also select geometry first (Select tool) and choose a dimension button in the right panel: Distance, Horizontal distance, Vertical distance, Radius, Diameter or Angle. Only the ones that fit the selection are shown. This is the way to give a circle a radius instead of a diameter. Add a relation Relations are added from the right panel, not with a tool. Press S for Select. Click one item, then Shift+click a second one if the relation needs two. The origin can be selected too. The right panel says \"Selected: line l3, point p1\" and shows a button for each relation that fits, such as ∥ Parallel or • Coincident. Choose the button. The sketch solves at once. If no relation fits, the panel says \"No relation fits this selection. Select one or two items (Shift+click adds).\" The full list of relations, what each one needs and what it does is in Sketch relations and dimensions. Relations show in the sketch as small symbols next to the geometry (H, V, ∥, ⊥, T, =, ◎, M, ⚓). Coincident dots are hidden unless the geometry is selected or the relation is in trouble. Click a symbol to select the relation. A worked example: an L-shaped bracket profile Sketch on Front plane. With the Line tool, start on the origin and click six corners of an L, finishing on the origin again. The chain ends by itself when you click the first point. Lines drawn close to horizontal or vertical already have H and V relations, except the last one: a line that ends on an existing point gets no automatic relation. Press S, click that last line and choose V Vertical in the right panel. The chip says \"Under defined · 4 degrees of freedom\". Press D. Click the bottom line, click below it, type 80, Enter. Click the left line, click left of it, type 60, Enter. Dimension the top short line 20 and the inner vertical line 45 the same way. The chip now says Fully defined and the whole L turns to ink. Fix an over defined sketch When a new relation or dimension contradicts the others, PartCraft does not move anything. The geometry stays exactly as it was, the relations involved turn red, and the status bar names them: \"Over defined: Horizontal, Distance 40 cannot all be met. Undo, or delete one of them.\" The relations conflict. \"Over defined: Parallel is already implied. Delete it.\" The relation adds nothing: the others already force it. (A rectangle's sides are already parallel, for example.) To fix it: Press Ctrl+Z to undo the last change, or Find the red item in the Relations and dimensions list in the right panel (a group that holds it is open and red) and choose Delete beside it. You can also click its symbol or number in the sketch and press Delete. There are no \"driven\" (reference) dimensions in this build. A dimension that would only report a size the other relations already fix is treated as over defined. Read sizes in the panel instead: select a line and look at the Dimension buttons, or check the part facts after you extrude. An over defined sketch with a real conflict makes the feature that uses it fail (\"Over defined: 1 relation cannot all be met.\"), and the history marks it ⚠ error. A sketch that is only redundant still builds, with a ⚠ warning. Drag to find what is free In Select, drag any blue point or line. The solver moves it as little as possible while keeping every relation. If a drag would break a relation, the geometry snaps back. This is the quickest way to see which dimension is still missing. The relations list With nothing selected, the right panel lists Relations and dimensions. Each row has: Edit (dimensions only): opens the number box; Select: selects it in the sketch; Delete: removes it. Relations of one kind that appear more than once share one row, such as \"Coincident × 5\" for a rectangle tied to the origin. Click the row to open it: each relation is listed by what it ties (\"point p2, point p4\") with its own Select and Delete. The row itself has: Select: selects all of them in the sketch; Delete all: removes all of them in one step (Ctrl+Z brings them back). A group that holds an over defined relation opens by itself, with its name in red. Dimensions are always listed one per row, with their values. With geometry selected, the list shows only the Relations on the selection."},{"slug":"add-by-coordinates","scope":"partcraft","title":"Add sketch geometry by typing coordinates","summary":"Use Add by coordinates in the sketch panel to place lines, centerlines, rectangles, circles and points at exact positions, with the keyboard only.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["coordinates","typed input","keyboard entry","numeric entry","exact position","x y","absolute coordinates","accessibility","no mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"add-a-shape","text":"Add a shape","level":2},{"id":"example-a-slotted-plate-outline-typed","text":"Example: a slotted plate outline, typed","level":2},{"id":"typed-geometry-is-placed-not-tied-down","text":"Typed geometry is placed, not tied down","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhen you know the numbers, typing them is faster than clicking and dimensioning. \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd by coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E sits in the right panel whenever a sketch is open. It also lets you build a sketch without a pointer at all.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECoordinates are in millimeters, in the sketch&#039;s own axes: x to the right and y up as you look at the sketch, with (0, 0) at the origin (the red circle with a cross).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-shape\"\u003EAdd a shape\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a sketch (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sketch-basics\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sketch-basics\"\u003EDraw your first sketch\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, scroll to \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd by coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the shape in \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd\u003C/strong\u003E: Line, Centerline, Rectangle, Circle or Point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in the boxes:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShape\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBoxes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrom x, y\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ETo x, y\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenterline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrom x, y\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ETo x, y\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERectangle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECorner x, y\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EOpposite corner x, y\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECircle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECentre x, y\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ERadius\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAt x, y\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in any box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe shape appears and the status bar says, for example, &quot;Added a rectangle.&quot; The boxes empty, ready for the next one. Each addition is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType a pair as two numbers separated by a comma or a space: \u003Ccode\u003E0, 0\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E25 -10\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E12.5,40\u003C/code\u003E. Negative numbers and decimals are fine. A radius must be more than zero. If something does not read as a number, the status bar says: &#039;Type coordinates as &quot;x, y&quot; and a radius above zero.&#039;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"example-a-slotted-plate-outline-typed\"\u003EExample: a slotted plate outline, typed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a sketch on \u003Cstrong\u003EFront plane\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdd\u003C/strong\u003E: Rectangle. \u003Cstrong\u003ECorner x, y\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E-50, -30\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EOpposite corner x, y\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E50, 30\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdd\u003C/strong\u003E: Circle. \u003Cstrong\u003ECentre x, y\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E-30, 0\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003ERadius\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E5\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECentre x, y\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E30, 0\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003ERadius\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E5\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit sketch\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E: a 100 × 60 plate with two 10 mm holes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typed-geometry-is-placed-not-tied-down\"\u003ETyped geometry is placed, not tied down\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETyped coordinates put geometry in the right place, but they are \u003Cstrong\u003Enot relations\u003C/strong\u003E. The shapes stay blue (under defined) and could move if you drag them or change another dimension:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea typed rectangle comes with its own corner, horizontal and vertical relations, like a drawn one, but nothing ties it to the origin;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea typed line has no horizontal or vertical relation, even if you typed a level line;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea typed circle&#039;s center is free.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make the sketch fully defined, add dimensions and relations afterwards (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/relations-dimensions\" data-topic=\"partcraft/relations-dimensions\"\u003EFully define a sketch\u003C/a\u003E). For example, select a rectangle corner and the origin and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E• Coincident\u003C/span\u003E, or dimension the circle centers from the origin.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout a pointer you can get this far: move to a plane row in the history with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the arrow keys, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to select it, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to sketch, add shapes here, and reach \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E beside each dimension and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit sketch\u003C/span\u003E with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Selecting geometry, and so adding relations or new dimensions, still needs a click or a tap in this build.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArcs cannot be typed yet. Draw them with the Arc tool, or type a circle and use relations.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThere are no relative coordinates (from the last point) or polar coordinates (length and angle) in this box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETyped geometry does not snap to existing points. Type the same coordinates as an existing point, then add a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E• Coincident\u003C/span\u003E relation between them if they must stay joined.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"When you know the numbers, typing them is faster than clicking and dimensioning. Add by coordinates sits in the right panel whenever a sketch is open. It also lets you build a sketch without a pointer at all. Coordinates are in millimeters, in the sketch's own axes: x to the right and y up as you look at the sketch, with (0, 0) at the origin (the red circle with a cross). Add a shape Open a sketch (see Draw your first sketch). In the right panel, scroll to Add by coordinates. Choose the shape in Add: Line, Centerline, Rectangle, Circle or Point. Fill in the boxes: Shape Boxes Line From x, y and To x, y Centerline From x, y and To x, y Rectangle Corner x, y and Opposite corner x, y Circle Centre x, y and Radius Point At x, y Choose Add, or press Enter in any box. The shape appears and the status bar says, for example, \"Added a rectangle.\" The boxes empty, ready for the next one. Each addition is one undo step. Type a pair as two numbers separated by a comma or a space: 0, 0, 25 -10 or 12.5,40. Negative numbers and decimals are fine. A radius must be more than zero. If something does not read as a number, the status bar says: 'Type coordinates as \"x, y\" and a radius above zero.' Example: a slotted plate outline, typed Open a sketch on Front plane. Add: Rectangle. Corner x, y: -50, -30. Opposite corner x, y: 50, 30. Enter. Add: Circle. Centre x, y: -30, 0. Radius: 5. Enter. Centre x, y: 30, 0. Radius: 5. Enter. Choose Exit sketch, then Extrude: a 100 × 60 plate with two 10 mm holes. Typed geometry is placed, not tied down Typed coordinates put geometry in the right place, but they are not relations. The shapes stay blue (under defined) and could move if you drag them or change another dimension: a typed rectangle comes with its own corner, horizontal and vertical relations, like a drawn one, but nothing ties it to the origin; a typed line has no horizontal or vertical relation, even if you typed a level line; a typed circle's center is free. To make the sketch fully defined, add dimensions and relations afterwards (see Fully define a sketch). For example, select a rectangle corner and the origin and choose • Coincident, or dimension the circle centers from the origin. Without a pointer you can get this far: move to a plane row in the history with Tab and the arrow keys, press Enter to select it, press S to sketch, add shapes here, and reach Edit beside each dimension and Exit sketch with Tab. Selecting geometry, and so adding relations or new dimensions, still needs a click or a tap in this build. Limits Arcs cannot be typed yet. Draw them with the Arc tool, or type a circle and use relations. There are no relative coordinates (from the last point) or polar coordinates (length and angle) in this box. Typed geometry does not snap to existing points. Type the same coordinates as an existing point, then add a • Coincident relation between them if they must stay joined."},{"slug":"extrude-and-cut","scope":"partcraft","title":"Extrude a boss and cut through it","summary":"Turn a sketch into a solid with Extrude, set blind, mid-plane, up-to or two-direction depths, a taper, or one contour of the sketch, then sketch on a face and Cut a pocket or a through slot.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["extrude","boss","extruded boss","cut","extruded cut","pocket","through all","blind","mid-plane","reverse","depth","cut-extrude","boss-extrude","pad","up to face","up to next","up to vertex","offset from surface","direction 2","two directions","draft while extruding","taper","contour","region","selected contours"],"headings":[{"id":"extrude-a-boss","text":"Extrude a boss","level":2},{"id":"set-the-depth-and-direction","text":"Set the depth and direction","level":3},{"id":"up-to-a-face-the-next-face-or-a-vertex","text":"Up to a face, the next face or a vertex","level":3},{"id":"two-directions-taper-one-contour","text":"Two directions, taper, one contour","level":3},{"id":"cut-a-pocket","text":"Cut a pocket","level":2},{"id":"pull-or-push-a-whole-face","text":"Pull or push a whole face","level":2},{"id":"profiles-with-islands","text":"Profiles with islands","level":2},{"id":"edit-it-later","text":"Edit it later","level":2},{"id":"common-messages","text":"Common messages","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E pushes a sketch&#039;s closed profile straight out of its plane to make material. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E does the same but removes material. Both are the same feature underneath; you can switch one to the other later.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"extrude-a-boss\"\u003EExtrude a boss\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a closed profile and close the sketch (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sketch-basics\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sketch-basics\"\u003EDraw your first sketch\u003C/a\u003E). It stays selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA 10 mm solid appears, &quot;Extrude1&quot; joins the history with its sketch indented under it, and the status bar says &quot;Extrude1 added. Change its numbers on the right.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhich sketch is used:\u003C/strong\u003E the sketch you just closed, or the sketch selected in the history. With nothing selected, PartCraft takes the most recent sketch that no feature uses yet. If there is none: &quot;Make a sketch first: pick a plane, Sketch, draw a closed profile, Exit.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"set-the-depth-and-direction\"\u003ESet the depth and direction\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the right panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOperation\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoss (add material), Cut (remove material), Intersect (keep the overlap), New body (not built yet in this version: it needs an exact kernel)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoss\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnd\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlind (a depth), Mid-plane (both ways), Through all, Up to face or plane, Up to next face, Up to vertex, Offset from face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlind\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDepth (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAny size above zero (shown for Blind and Mid-plane)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReverse direction\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrows to the other side of the sketch plane (not shown for Mid-plane)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType a number and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: the part rebuilds immediately. \u003Cstrong\u003EMid-plane\u003C/strong\u003E puts half the depth on each side of the sketch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"up-to-a-face-the-next-face-or-a-vertex\"\u003EUp to a face, the next face or a vertex\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot built yet in this version: every &quot;up to&quot; end needs an exact kernel, and the feature says &quot;Extruding up to a face, the next face or a vertex: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel).&quot; Use a Blind depth you measure. With an exact kernel the ends work like this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUp to face or plane\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EUp to\u003C/strong\u003E takes a flat face (Pick, then click it) or a reference plane. A face parallel to the sketch gives an exact depth; a slanted one cuts the end on the slope. If the face is behind the sketch: &quot;… Try Reverse direction.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUp to next face\u003C/strong\u003E: stops at the first flat face of the body the extrusion meets (curved faces are not supported here: it says so).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUp to vertex\u003C/strong\u003E: stops level with a vertex (click near the end of an edge) or a point feature.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOffset from face\u003C/strong\u003E: like Up to face, stopping \u003Cstrong\u003EOffset (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E short of it (measured square to the face).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"two-directions-taper-one-contour\"\u003ETwo directions, taper, one contour\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESecond direction\u003C/strong\u003E (Blind): also extrudes \u003Cstrong\u003ESecond depth (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E to the other side of the sketch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETaper (°)\u003C/strong\u003E: the sides lean in as they go (negative: out), like a draft; one direction only. Not built yet in this version (it needs an exact kernel): leave it at 0.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERegions\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EPick regions\u003C/strong\u003E, then click inside the loops to extrude; only those regions are used (a circle inside a square: click inside the circle for a cylinder, between them for a plate with a hole). \u003Cstrong\u003EAll\u003C/strong\u003E goes back to every loop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelow these, \u003Cstrong\u003ESketch dimensions\u003C/strong\u003E lists the sketch&#039;s driving dimensions, so you can change the profile&#039;s sizes without opening the sketch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThrough all is only for cuts.\u003C/strong\u003E A boss set to Through all fails with &quot;Through all is for cuts. Use a depth for a boss.&quot; Switching a feature from Cut back to Boss changes Through all to Blind for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-a-pocket\"\u003ECut a pocket\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the flat face you want to cut into. It highlights, and the right panel says \u003Cstrong\u003EFace\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch on this face\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the pocket&#039;s outline, for example a rectangle, and dimension it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E. It starts as \u003Cstrong\u003EThrough all\u003C/strong\u003E: it removes material right through the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a pocket, set \u003Cstrong\u003EEnd\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003EBlind (a depth)\u003C/strong\u003E and type the \u003Cstrong\u003EDepth (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA cut goes into the material, away from the face you sketched on. If the status bar says &quot;There is nothing to cut on that side of the sketch. Try the other direction.&quot;, tick \u003Cstrong\u003EReverse direction\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sketch on the pocket&#039;s floor follows the floor: change the pocket depth and the sketch moves with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pull-or-push-a-whole-face\"\u003EPull or push a whole face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make a face stand out further or sink in, you don&#039;t need to draw its outline. Click the flat face, then in the right panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude from it\u003C/span\u003E pulls the face out: PartCraft adds a sketch on the face holding its outline (holes in the face stay holes) and an Extrude of 10 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut into it\u003C/span\u003E pushes the face in: the same sketch and a Cut halfway into the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth are one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes away the sketch and the feature together. The new feature is selected, so type a number straight away and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for its depth. The outline is copied as fixed lines and arcs: it does not follow later changes to the face. If part of the outline can&#039;t be copied (a free-form curve), the sketch opens instead with what could be copied; finish it, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"profiles-with-islands\"\u003EProfiles with islands\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sketch can hold several closed loops. Loops side by side become separate bosses (or cuts); a loop inside another becomes a hole through the boss. For example, a rectangle with two circles inside extrudes as a plate with two holes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo use only some loops, pick them with \u003Cstrong\u003ERegions\u003C/strong\u003E (above); another feature can use the other loops of the same sketch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-it-later\"\u003EEdit it later\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick &quot;Extrude1&quot; in the history to bring back its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click its sketch (indented under it) to open the sketch; close it to rebuild.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/edit-history\" data-topic=\"partcraft/edit-history\"\u003EChange a part through its history\u003C/a\u003E for suppressing, rolling back, reordering and deleting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-messages\"\u003ECommon messages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The sketch has no closed profile to extrude.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sketch has no closed loop of solid lines. Close the gaps, or switch construction lines back with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The profile is open: an end at (x, y) is not joined to anything.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the sketch and join that end to the next line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;There is nothing to cut yet.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA cut needs a solid to cut. Make a boss first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Nothing is left of the part after this.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe cut removed everything. Check its size and direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMore in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/troubleshooting\" data-topic=\"partcraft/troubleshooting\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Extrude pushes a sketch's closed profile straight out of its plane to make material. Cut does the same but removes material. Both are the same feature underneath; you can switch one to the other later. Extrude a boss Draw a closed profile and close the sketch (see Draw your first sketch). It stays selected. Choose Extrude. A 10 mm solid appears, \"Extrude1\" joins the history with its sketch indented under it, and the status bar says \"Extrude1 added. Change its numbers on the right.\" Which sketch is used: the sketch you just closed, or the sketch selected in the history. With nothing selected, PartCraft takes the most recent sketch that no feature uses yet. If there is none: \"Make a sketch first: pick a plane, Sketch, draw a closed profile, Exit.\" Set the depth and direction In the right panel: Setting Choices Default Operation Boss (add material), Cut (remove material), Intersect (keep the overlap), New body (not built yet in this version: it needs an exact kernel) Boss End Blind (a depth), Mid-plane (both ways), Through all, Up to face or plane, Up to next face, Up to vertex, Offset from face Blind Depth (mm) Any size above zero (shown for Blind and Mid-plane) 10 Reverse direction Grows to the other side of the sketch plane (not shown for Mid-plane) Off Type a number and press Enter or Tab: the part rebuilds immediately. Mid-plane puts half the depth on each side of the sketch. Up to a face, the next face or a vertex Not built yet in this version: every \"up to\" end needs an exact kernel, and the feature says \"Extruding up to a face, the next face or a vertex: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel).\" Use a Blind depth you measure. With an exact kernel the ends work like this: Up to face or plane: Up to takes a flat face (Pick, then click it) or a reference plane. A face parallel to the sketch gives an exact depth; a slanted one cuts the end on the slope. If the face is behind the sketch: \"… Try Reverse direction.\" Up to next face: stops at the first flat face of the body the extrusion meets (curved faces are not supported here: it says so). Up to vertex: stops level with a vertex (click near the end of an edge) or a point feature. Offset from face: like Up to face, stopping Offset (mm) short of it (measured square to the face). Two directions, taper, one contour Second direction (Blind): also extrudes Second depth (mm) to the other side of the sketch. Taper (°): the sides lean in as they go (negative: out), like a draft; one direction only. Not built yet in this version (it needs an exact kernel): leave it at 0. Regions: Pick regions, then click inside the loops to extrude; only those regions are used (a circle inside a square: click inside the circle for a cylinder, between them for a plate with a hole). All goes back to every loop. Below these, Sketch dimensions lists the sketch's driving dimensions, so you can change the profile's sizes without opening the sketch. Through all is only for cuts. A boss set to Through all fails with \"Through all is for cuts. Use a depth for a boss.\" Switching a feature from Cut back to Boss changes Through all to Blind for you. Cut a pocket Click the flat face you want to cut into. It highlights, and the right panel says Face. Choose Sketch on this face (or Sketch in the toolbar). Draw the pocket's outline, for example a rectangle, and dimension it. Choose Exit. Choose Cut. It starts as Through all: it removes material right through the part. For a pocket, set End to Blind (a depth) and type the Depth (mm). A cut goes into the material, away from the face you sketched on. If the status bar says \"There is nothing to cut on that side of the sketch. Try the other direction.\", tick Reverse direction. A sketch on the pocket's floor follows the floor: change the pocket depth and the sketch moves with it. Pull or push a whole face To make a face stand out further or sink in, you don't need to draw its outline. Click the flat face, then in the right panel: Extrude from it pulls the face out: PartCraft adds a sketch on the face holding its outline (holes in the face stay holes) and an Extrude of 10 mm. Cut into it pushes the face in: the same sketch and a Cut halfway into the part. Both are one step: Ctrl+Z takes away the sketch and the feature together. The new feature is selected, so type a number straight away and press Enter for its depth. The outline is copied as fixed lines and arcs: it does not follow later changes to the face. If part of the outline can't be copied (a free-form curve), the sketch opens instead with what could be copied; finish it, then choose Exit and Extrude or Cut. Profiles with islands A sketch can hold several closed loops. Loops side by side become separate bosses (or cuts); a loop inside another becomes a hole through the boss. For example, a rectangle with two circles inside extrudes as a plate with two holes. To use only some loops, pick them with Regions (above); another feature can use the other loops of the same sketch. Edit it later Click \"Extrude1\" in the history to bring back its settings. Double-click its sketch (indented under it) to open the sketch; close it to rebuild. See Change a part through its history for suppressing, rolling back, reordering and deleting. Common messages Message What to do \"The sketch has no closed profile to extrude.\" The sketch has no closed loop of solid lines. Close the gaps, or switch construction lines back with Q \"The profile is open: an end at (x, y) is not joined to anything.\" Open the sketch and join that end to the next line \"There is nothing to cut yet.\" A cut needs a solid to cut. Make a boss first \"Nothing is left of the part after this.\" The cut removed everything. Check its size and direction More in Troubleshooting."},{"slug":"revolve","scope":"partcraft","title":"Revolve a turned part","summary":"Sketch half a profile beside a centerline, revolve it into a round part, choose the axis and angle, and use a revolved cut for grooves.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["revolve","revolved boss","revolved cut","turned part","lathe","shaft","axis","centerline","groove","angle","rev cut"],"headings":[{"id":"revolve-a-stepped-shaft","text":"Revolve a stepped shaft","level":2},{"id":"settings","text":"Settings","level":2},{"id":"cut-a-groove","text":"Cut a groove","level":2},{"id":"revolved-faces-in-assemblies","text":"Revolved faces in assemblies","level":2},{"id":"true-curves-and-facets","text":"True curves and facets","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolve\u003C/span\u003E spins a profile around an axis, like a part made on a lathe: shafts, pulleys, flanges, knobs. Revolved cut (in the Features group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu) spins a profile to remove material, for grooves and recesses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"revolve-a-stepped-shaft\"\u003ERevolve a stepped shaft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESketch on \u003Cstrong\u003EFront plane\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECenterline\u003C/span\u003E. Start on the origin and click a point straight to the right, for example 100 mm along. This is the axis. It is dashed: construction geometry is not part of the profile.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for Line. Draw the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehalf\u003C/strong\u003E profile above the centerline as one closed loop: start on the centerline, go up, step along, and come back down to the centerline, then along it to the start. Its ends must meet: the loop must be closed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDimension the radii (heights above the centerline) and the lengths. A height of 10 above the centerline makes a diameter of 20.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolve\u003C/span\u003E. The shaft appears, a full turn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe profile must stay on one side of the axis. If it crosses: &quot;The profile crosses the axis. Keep it on one side of the centerline.&quot; A profile edge may lie on the axis.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOperation\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoss (add material), Cut (remove material), Intersect (keep the overlap) or New body\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoss for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolve\u003C/span\u003E, Cut for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolved cut\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow far to spin, from just above 0 up to 360\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E360\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAxis\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirst centerline, or any line or centerline in the sketch, listed as &quot;Centerline 1 (l1)&quot; or &quot;Line 3 (l3)&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirst centerline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA partial angle makes a wedge, spun from the sketch plane in one direction.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the sketch has no centerline and you leave \u003Cstrong\u003EAxis\u003C/strong\u003E on First centerline: &quot;Draw a centerline (a construction line) in the sketch to revolve about.&quot; You can also pick a solid line of the profile as the axis.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-a-groove\"\u003ECut a groove\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith the shaft built, open a new sketch on \u003Cstrong\u003EFront plane\u003C/strong\u003E (the same plane as the shaft&#039;s sketch).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a centerline along the same axis: start it on the origin and keep it level so it gets an H, or type it with \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd by coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E (for example from \u003Ccode\u003E0, 0\u003C/code\u003E to \u003Ccode\u003E100, 0\u003C/code\u003E). A new sketch cannot snap to another sketch&#039;s lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a small closed rectangle that overlaps the shaft&#039;s edge where the groove goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolved cut\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"revolved-faces-in-assemblies\"\u003ERevolved faces in assemblies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA revolve has an axis that other features and assemblies can use:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea \u003Cstrong\u003ECircular\u003C/strong\u003E pattern uses the last revolve&#039;s axis by default (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/patterns-mirror\" data-topic=\"partcraft/patterns-mirror\"\u003EPattern and mirror features\u003C/a\u003E);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ein an assembly, a joint between faces made by revolves starts as a \u003Cstrong\u003Erevolute\u003C/strong\u003E joint, and \u003Cstrong\u003ECentre on\u003C/strong\u003E offers &quot;Axis of Revolve1&quot; (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/build-assembly\" data-topic=\"partcraft/build-assembly\"\u003EBuild an assembly\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"true-curves-and-facets\"\u003ETrue curves and facets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft builds a revolve with the mesh kernel (the status bar chip says &quot;Mesh kernel&quot;): round shapes are flat facets, 64 per turn by default, and volumes are a little under the true value (about 0.16 % at 64). The view, STL, 3MF and STEP exports are made of those facets: set \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E (click empty space, then the right panel) to Fine (128) or Very fine (256) for smoother ones. True cylinders, cones and tori need an exact kernel: not built yet in this version. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Revolve spins a profile around an axis, like a part made on a lathe: shafts, pulleys, flanges, knobs. Revolved cut (in the Features group's More menu) spins a profile to remove material, for grooves and recesses. Revolve a stepped shaft Sketch on Front plane. Press N for Centerline. Start on the origin and click a point straight to the right, for example 100 mm along. This is the axis. It is dashed: construction geometry is not part of the profile. Press L for Line. Draw the half profile above the centerline as one closed loop: start on the centerline, go up, step along, and come back down to the centerline, then along it to the start. Its ends must meet: the loop must be closed. Dimension the radii (heights above the centerline) and the lengths. A height of 10 above the centerline makes a diameter of 20. Choose Exit. Choose Revolve. The shaft appears, a full turn. The profile must stay on one side of the axis. If it crosses: \"The profile crosses the axis. Keep it on one side of the centerline.\" A profile edge may lie on the axis. Settings Setting What it does Default Operation Boss (add material), Cut (remove material), Intersect (keep the overlap) or New body Boss for Revolve, Cut for More › Revolved cut Angle (°) How far to spin, from just above 0 up to 360 360 Axis First centerline, or any line or centerline in the sketch, listed as \"Centerline 1 (l1)\" or \"Line 3 (l3)\" First centerline A partial angle makes a wedge, spun from the sketch plane in one direction. If the sketch has no centerline and you leave Axis on First centerline: \"Draw a centerline (a construction line) in the sketch to revolve about.\" You can also pick a solid line of the profile as the axis. Cut a groove With the shaft built, open a new sketch on Front plane (the same plane as the shaft's sketch). Draw a centerline along the same axis: start it on the origin and keep it level so it gets an H, or type it with Add by coordinates (for example from 0, 0 to 100, 0). A new sketch cannot snap to another sketch's lines. Draw a small closed rectangle that overlaps the shaft's edge where the groove goes. Choose Exit, then More › Revolved cut. Revolved faces in assemblies A revolve has an axis that other features and assemblies can use: a Circular pattern uses the last revolve's axis by default (see Pattern and mirror features); in an assembly, a joint between faces made by revolves starts as a revolute joint, and Centre on offers \"Axis of Revolve1\" (see Build an assembly). True curves and facets PartCraft builds a revolve with the mesh kernel (the status bar chip says \"Mesh kernel\"): round shapes are flat facets, 64 per turn by default, and volumes are a little under the true value (about 0.16 % at 64). The view, STL, 3MF and STEP exports are made of those facets: set Curves (click empty space, then the right panel) to Fine (128) or Very fine (256) for smoother ones. True cylinders, cones and tori need an exact kernel: not built yet in this version. See The mesh kernel."},{"slug":"holes","scope":"partcraft","title":"Drill holes and counterbores at sketch points","summary":"Put points in a sketch on a face, make simple, counterbored, countersunk or tapped (ISO metric) holes at every point, set diameter and depth, and fix holes that drill the wrong way.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["hole","hole wizard","drill","modelled thread","3D printing","counterbore","cbore","countersink","csink","tapped hole","thread","tap drill","metric thread","M6","through all","blind hole","bolt hole","points","hole centers"],"headings":[{"id":"drill-holes-in-a-face","text":"Drill holes in a face","level":2},{"id":"settings","text":"Settings","level":2},{"id":"a-counterbored-bolt-hole","text":"A counterbored bolt hole","level":2},{"id":"a-bolt-circle","text":"A bolt circle","level":2},{"id":"when-holes-go-the-wrong-way","text":"When holes go the wrong way","level":2},{"id":"holes-in-assemblies","text":"Holes in assemblies","level":2},{"id":"countersunk-holes","text":"Countersunk holes","level":2},{"id":"tapped-holes","text":"Tapped holes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHole\u003C/span\u003E drills one hole at each loose point of a sketch. A loose point is one made with the Point tool, not the end of a line or the center of a circle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drill-holes-in-a-face\"\u003EDrill holes in a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the flat face to drill into, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch on this face\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click where each hole goes. Dimension the points from the edges or the origin if their position matters (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/relations-dimensions\" data-topic=\"partcraft/relations-dimensions\"\u003EFully define a sketch\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHole\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHoles go through the part, 6 mm across. The right panel shows \u003Cstrong\u003EHoles\u003C/strong\u003E: how many were made, &quot;one per sketch point&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne hole, fast:\u003C/strong\u003E click the flat face where the hole goes and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHole here\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel. PartCraft adds a sketch on the face with one point where you clicked and a Hole through the part, in one step. Type a number straight away and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for its diameter. To place it exactly, open the new sketch and dimension the point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhich sketch is used:\u003C/strong\u003E the sketch you just closed or the selected one; otherwise the most recent unused sketch that has loose points. If there is none: &quot;Make a sketch with points where the holes go first (Sketch on a face, then Point).&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESimple, Counterbore, Countersink, Tapped (ISO metric)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESimple\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDiameter (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe hole&#039;s diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThrough all\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETicked: right through the part. Clear it for a blind hole\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETicked\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDepth (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShown when Through all is clear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECounterbore ⌀\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShown for Counterbore: the wider recess&#039;s diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E11\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECounterbore depth\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShown for Counterbore\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReverse direction\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrills to the other side of the sketch plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHoles drill into the material, square to the sketch plane. A blind hole&#039;s depth is measured from the sketch plane; its bottom is flat.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-counterbored-bolt-hole\"\u003EA counterbored bolt hole\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake the holes as above.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003ECounterbore\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EDiameter (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Ccode\u003E6.6\u003C/code\u003E (clearance for an M6 bolt), \u003Cstrong\u003ECounterbore ⌀\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Ccode\u003E11\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ECounterbore depth\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Ccode\u003E6.5\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe counterbore must be wider than the hole (&quot;The counterbore must be wider than the hole.&quot;) and, for a blind hole, shallower than it (&quot;The counterbore must be shallower than the hole.&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-bolt-circle\"\u003EA bolt circle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor holes evenly spaced round a center, make \u003Cstrong\u003Eone\u003C/strong\u003E hole and pattern it: select the Hole feature, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircular\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/patterns-mirror\" data-topic=\"partcraft/patterns-mirror\"\u003EPattern and mirror features\u003C/a\u003E). The pattern follows when you change the hole&#039;s size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-holes-go-the-wrong-way\"\u003EWhen holes go the wrong way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;There is nothing to drill on that side of the sketch. Try reversing the direction.&quot; The sketch sits on the far side of the material. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003EReverse direction\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Put sketch points where the holes go (Point tool), then make the hole.&quot; The sketch has no loose points. Line ends and circle centers do not count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;There is nothing to drill yet.&quot; A hole needs a solid. Make a boss first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"holes-in-assemblies\"\u003EHoles in assemblies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach hole has an axis. In an assembly, \u003Cstrong\u003ECentre on\u003C/strong\u003E can put a joint on a hole&#039;s axis, listed as &quot;Hole1, hole 2&quot;, so a pin or bolt part lines up with it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/build-assembly\" data-topic=\"partcraft/build-assembly\"\u003EBuild an assembly\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"countersunk-holes\"\u003ECountersunk holes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003ECountersink\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ECountersink ⌀\u003C/strong\u003E (at the surface) and \u003Cstrong\u003ECountersink angle\u003C/strong\u003E: 90° (ISO metric flat-head screws) or 82° (inch screws). Take the countersink diameter from the screw maker&#039;s data (a little over the screw head&#039;s diameter). The cone&#039;s face is named &quot;countersink&quot; in the panels.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tapped-holes\"\u003ETapped holes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003ETapped (ISO metric)\u003C/strong\u003E and choose the \u003Cstrong\u003EThread\u003C/strong\u003E, M2 to M24 coarse. The hole is drilled at the tap drill size from the table below (nominal diameter minus pitch, to the drill series, as ISO 2306 lists for a 6H thread); the \u003Cstrong\u003EDiameter (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E box is not used. For a blind hole, \u003Cstrong\u003EThread depth (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E sets how deep the thread goes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThread\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM2\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM2.5\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM3\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM4\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM5\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM6\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM8\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM10\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM12\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM14\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM16\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM18\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM20\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM22\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EM24\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPitch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.4\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.45\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.7\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.8\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1.25\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1.75\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap drill ⌀\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1.6\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.05\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3.3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4.2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6.8\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10.2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E14\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E15.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E17.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E19.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E21\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe thread is \u003Cstrong\u003Ecosmetic\u003C/strong\u003E at first: the model has the drilled hole, the view draws the thread&#039;s major diameter as dashed circles, and the drawing sheet carries the callout as a note, such as &quot;4× M8×1.25 - 6H, thread through&quot;. STEP exports the drilled hole only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETick \u003Cstrong\u003EModelled\u003C/strong\u003E to cut the real helical thread into the hole, for 3D printing. \u003Cstrong\u003EClearance (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E makes it that much larger all round, so a printed bolt fits: 0.1 to 0.2 mm suits most printers. The callout then ends &quot;(modelled)&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/threads\" data-topic=\"partcraft/threads\"\u003EThreads on shafts and bores, drawn or modelled\u003C/a\u003E, which also puts threads on shafts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECounterdrilled holes, inch threads and pipe threads are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Hole drills one hole at each loose point of a sketch. A loose point is one made with the Point tool, not the end of a line or the center of a circle. Drill holes in a face Click the flat face to drill into, then choose Sketch on this face. Press P and click where each hole goes. Dimension the points from the edges or the origin if their position matters (see Fully define a sketch). Choose Exit. Choose Hole. Holes go through the part, 6 mm across. The right panel shows Holes: how many were made, \"one per sketch point\". One hole, fast: click the flat face where the hole goes and choose Hole here in the right panel. PartCraft adds a sketch on the face with one point where you clicked and a Hole through the part, in one step. Type a number straight away and press Enter for its diameter. To place it exactly, open the new sketch and dimension the point. Which sketch is used: the sketch you just closed or the selected one; otherwise the most recent unused sketch that has loose points. If there is none: \"Make a sketch with points where the holes go first (Sketch on a face, then Point).\" Settings Setting Choices Default Type Simple, Counterbore, Countersink, Tapped (ISO metric) Simple Diameter (mm) The hole's diameter 6 Through all Ticked: right through the part. Clear it for a blind hole Ticked Depth (mm) Shown when Through all is clear 10 Counterbore ⌀ Shown for Counterbore: the wider recess's diameter 11 Counterbore depth Shown for Counterbore 6 Reverse direction Drills to the other side of the sketch plane Off Holes drill into the material, square to the sketch plane. A blind hole's depth is measured from the sketch plane; its bottom is flat. A counterbored bolt hole Make the holes as above. Set Type to Counterbore. Set Diameter (mm) to 6.6 (clearance for an M6 bolt), Counterbore ⌀ to 11 and Counterbore depth to 6.5. The counterbore must be wider than the hole (\"The counterbore must be wider than the hole.\") and, for a blind hole, shallower than it (\"The counterbore must be shallower than the hole.\"). A bolt circle For holes evenly spaced round a center, make one hole and pattern it: select the Hole feature, then choose Circular (see Pattern and mirror features). The pattern follows when you change the hole's size. When holes go the wrong way \"There is nothing to drill on that side of the sketch. Try reversing the direction.\" The sketch sits on the far side of the material. Tick Reverse direction. \"Put sketch points where the holes go (Point tool), then make the hole.\" The sketch has no loose points. Line ends and circle centers do not count. \"There is nothing to drill yet.\" A hole needs a solid. Make a boss first. Holes in assemblies Each hole has an axis. In an assembly, Centre on can put a joint on a hole's axis, listed as \"Hole1, hole 2\", so a pin or bolt part lines up with it. See Build an assembly. Countersunk holes Set Type to Countersink, then Countersink ⌀ (at the surface) and Countersink angle: 90° (ISO metric flat-head screws) or 82° (inch screws). Take the countersink diameter from the screw maker's data (a little over the screw head's diameter). The cone's face is named \"countersink\" in the panels. Tapped holes Set Type to Tapped (ISO metric) and choose the Thread, M2 to M24 coarse. The hole is drilled at the tap drill size from the table below (nominal diameter minus pitch, to the drill series, as ISO 2306 lists for a 6H thread); the Diameter (mm) box is not used. For a blind hole, Thread depth (mm) sets how deep the thread goes. Thread M2 M2.5 M3 M4 M5 M6 M8 M10 M12 M14 M16 M18 M20 M22 M24 Pitch 0.4 0.45 0.5 0.7 0.8 1 1.25 1.5 1.75 2 2 2.5 2.5 2.5 3 Tap drill ⌀ 1.6 2.05 2.5 3.3 4.2 5 6.8 8.5 10.2 12 14 15.5 17.5 19.5 21 The thread is cosmetic at first: the model has the drilled hole, the view draws the thread's major diameter as dashed circles, and the drawing sheet carries the callout as a note, such as \"4× M8×1.25 - 6H, thread through\". STEP exports the drilled hole only. Tick Modelled to cut the real helical thread into the hole, for 3D printing. Clearance (mm) makes it that much larger all round, so a printed bolt fits: 0.1 to 0.2 mm suits most printers. The callout then ends \"(modelled)\". See Threads on shafts and bores, drawn or modelled, which also puts threads on shafts. Counterdrilled holes, inch threads and pipe threads are not built yet."},{"slug":"threads","scope":"partcraft","title":"Threads on shafts and bores, drawn or modelled","summary":"Put an ISO metric or trapezoidal thread on any round face, a shaft or a bore. Keep it cosmetic for drawings, or model the real helical thread for 3D printing, with a clearance so printed parts fit.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["thread","threads","modelled thread","modeled thread","helical","helix","screw","bolt","nut","shaft","bore","external thread","internal thread","cosmetic thread","metric thread","trapezoidal","acme","lead screw","Tr16x4","M10","3D printing","print","clearance","fit","left hand","tapped"],"headings":[{"id":"thread-a-shaft-or-a-bore","text":"Thread a shaft or a bore","level":2},{"id":"settings","text":"Settings","level":2},{"id":"modelled-threads-for-3d-printing","text":"Modelled threads for 3D printing","level":2},{"id":"tapped-holes","text":"Tapped holes","level":2},{"id":"when-a-thread-does-not-build","text":"When a thread does not build","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThread\u003C/span\u003E puts a thread on round faces: the outside of a shaft (an external thread) or the inside of a bore (an internal thread). PartCraft tells which from the face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA thread is \u003Cstrong\u003Ecosmetic\u003C/strong\u003E at first: the face stays plain, the view draws the thread as dashed circles and the drawing sheet notes it, such as &quot;M10×1.5 - 6g, thread 20 long&quot;. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003EModelled\u003C/strong\u003E to cut the real helical thread into the part, for 3D printing or a model that must look right.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"thread-a-shaft-or-a-bore\"\u003EThread a shaft or a bore\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBuild the shaft or the bore at the thread&#039;s size: a Ø10 shaft for an M10 bolt, or a Ø8.5 bore (the tap drill) for an M10 nut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the round face, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThread\u003C/span\u003E. With no round face selected, PartCraft asks you to click the faces, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, check the \u003Cstrong\u003ESize\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EFit to the face\u003C/strong\u003E picks the size by itself: for a shaft, the size whose major diameter is the shaft&#039;s; for a bore, the size whose minor diameter it fits.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe thread starts at the face&#039;s open end, where the part does not carry on along the axis: the free end of a shaft, the mouth of a bore. \u003Cstrong\u003EStart at the other end\u003C/strong\u003E turns that round.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EForm\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO metric (60°), M1.6 to M64 coarse; trapezoidal (Tr, 30°), Tr8×1.5 to Tr60×9\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO metric\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESize\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit to the face, or one size from the list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit to the face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe whole face\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClear it to give a \u003Cstrong\u003ELength (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E, measured from the start\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETicked\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStart at the other end\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe thread runs from the other end of the face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHand\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight hand or left hand\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight hand\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModelled\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut the real helical thread\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClearance (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShown when Modelled is ticked: how much smaller a shaft&#039;s thread is, and how much larger a bore&#039;s, all round\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA size that does not suit the face is built anyway, cut to the face, and the panel says so, such as &quot;A Ø12 mm shaft is not M10&#039;s Ø10 mm&quot;. Several faces can share one Thread feature; with \u003Cstrong\u003EFit to the face\u003C/strong\u003E, each gets its own size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modelled-threads-for-3d-printing\"\u003EModelled threads for 3D printing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA modelled thread follows the standard basic profile: ISO 68-1 for metric threads (flat crests and roots, 60° flanks), ISO 2901 for trapezoidal ones (30° flanks). The \u003Cstrong\u003EClearance\u003C/strong\u003E moves the whole profile in (a shaft) or out (a bore) so printed parts screw together:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E0.1 to 0.2 mm\u003C/strong\u003E suits most printers. Print a test bolt and nut first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E0\u003C/strong\u003E is the standard profile, for parts that are machined or for checking a fit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA bolt and a nut with the same clearance never touch along their flanks: the gap between them is twice the clearance, all round.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFine pitches print poorly: M3 and smaller, or a pitch under 0.5 mm, is close to what a 0.4 mm nozzle can draw. Model larger threads, and use a heat-set insert or a tapped hole for small ones.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA modelled thread is part of the solid: STL, 3MF and STEP exports carry it, and Measure measures it. It makes the part heavier to rebuild: an M10 thread 20 long is about 150,000 triangles.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tapped-holes\"\u003ETapped holes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003ETapped (ISO metric)\u003C/strong\u003E hole has a \u003Cstrong\u003EModelled\u003C/strong\u003E switch too, with the same \u003Cstrong\u003EClearance\u003C/strong\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/holes\" data-topic=\"partcraft/holes\"\u003EDrill holes and counterbores at sketch points\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-thread-does-not-build\"\u003EWhen a thread does not build\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;A thread goes on a round face (a cylinder): a shaft or a bore.&quot; The face picked is flat or curved some other way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;No standard size fits a round face of Ø0.6 mm: choose a size.&quot; Fit to the face found nothing within 12 % of the face. Choose a \u003Cstrong\u003ESize\u003C/strong\u003E, or change the face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;A face this thread was on is gone (…). Pick it again.&quot; An earlier change removed the face. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick faces\u003C/span\u003E and click the face again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInch threads (UNC, UNF), pipe threads, tapered threads, thread run-outs and chamfered thread starts are not built yet. The exact kernel shows a modelled thread as cosmetic.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Thread puts a thread on round faces: the outside of a shaft (an external thread) or the inside of a bore (an internal thread). PartCraft tells which from the face. A thread is cosmetic at first: the face stays plain, the view draws the thread as dashed circles and the drawing sheet notes it, such as \"M10×1.5 - 6g, thread 20 long\". Tick Modelled to cut the real helical thread into the part, for 3D printing or a model that must look right. Thread a shaft or a bore Build the shaft or the bore at the thread's size: a Ø10 shaft for an M10 bolt, or a Ø8.5 bore (the tap drill) for an M10 nut. Click the round face, then choose More › Thread. With no round face selected, PartCraft asks you to click the faces, then Done. In the right panel, check the Size. Fit to the face picks the size by itself: for a shaft, the size whose major diameter is the shaft's; for a bore, the size whose minor diameter it fits. The thread starts at the face's open end, where the part does not carry on along the axis: the free end of a shaft, the mouth of a bore. Start at the other end turns that round. Settings Setting Choices Default Form ISO metric (60°), M1.6 to M64 coarse; trapezoidal (Tr, 30°), Tr8×1.5 to Tr60×9 ISO metric Size Fit to the face, or one size from the list Fit to the face The whole face Clear it to give a Length (mm), measured from the start Ticked Start at the other end The thread runs from the other end of the face Off Hand Right hand or left hand Right hand Modelled Cut the real helical thread Off Clearance (mm) Shown when Modelled is ticked: how much smaller a shaft's thread is, and how much larger a bore's, all round 0.1 A size that does not suit the face is built anyway, cut to the face, and the panel says so, such as \"A Ø12 mm shaft is not M10's Ø10 mm\". Several faces can share one Thread feature; with Fit to the face, each gets its own size. Modelled threads for 3D printing A modelled thread follows the standard basic profile: ISO 68-1 for metric threads (flat crests and roots, 60° flanks), ISO 2901 for trapezoidal ones (30° flanks). The Clearance moves the whole profile in (a shaft) or out (a bore) so printed parts screw together: 0.1 to 0.2 mm suits most printers. Print a test bolt and nut first. 0 is the standard profile, for parts that are machined or for checking a fit. A bolt and a nut with the same clearance never touch along their flanks: the gap between them is twice the clearance, all round. Fine pitches print poorly: M3 and smaller, or a pitch under 0.5 mm, is close to what a 0.4 mm nozzle can draw. Model larger threads, and use a heat-set insert or a tapped hole for small ones. A modelled thread is part of the solid: STL, 3MF and STEP exports carry it, and Measure measures it. It makes the part heavier to rebuild: an M10 thread 20 long is about 150,000 triangles. Tapped holes A Tapped (ISO metric) hole has a Modelled switch too, with the same Clearance. See Drill holes and counterbores at sketch points. When a thread does not build \"A thread goes on a round face (a cylinder): a shaft or a bore.\" The face picked is flat or curved some other way. \"No standard size fits a round face of Ø0.6 mm: choose a size.\" Fit to the face found nothing within 12 % of the face. Choose a Size, or change the face. \"A face this thread was on is gone (…). Pick it again.\" An earlier change removed the face. Click Pick faces and click the face again. Inch threads (UNC, UNF), pipe threads, tapered threads, thread run-outs and chamfered thread starts are not built yet. The exact kernel shows a modelled thread as cosmetic."},{"slug":"reference-geometry","scope":"partcraft","title":"Reference planes, axes and points","summary":"Make planes offset from a face, at an angle, through three points, midway, tangent to a cylinder, square to an edge or through an edge and a point; axes; points at edge middles, crossings and plane corners; sketch on them and use them as revolve axes, pattern directions and mirror planes.","section":"tasks","order":65,"keywords":["datum","between objects","midpoint","intersection point","plane through edge and point","reference plane","work plane","construction plane","datum plane","offset plane","plane at angle","midplane","tangent plane","reference axis","construction axis","datum axis","reference point","work point","sketch on plane"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-plane","text":"Make a plane","level":2},{"id":"sketch-on-a-plane","text":"Sketch on a plane","level":2},{"id":"axes-and-points","text":"Axes and points","level":2},{"id":"when-a-reference-is-gone","text":"When a reference is gone","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EReference geometry is the scaffolding you build features on: a \u003Cstrong\u003Eplane\u003C/strong\u003E to sketch on where no face is, an \u003Cstrong\u003Eaxis\u003C/strong\u003E to revolve or pattern about, a \u003Cstrong\u003Epoint\u003C/strong\u003E to stop an extrude at. It has no material of its own. Planes show in the view as pale squares, axes as dashed lines, points as small crosses; all three join the history and follow what they were made from when you change a size upstream.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey are in the Features group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlane\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-plane\"\u003EMake a plane\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a flat face, or a base plane in the history, to start from it (optional).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlane\u003C/span\u003E. It starts as an offset plane 20 % of the part&#039;s size away from what you picked (or from the top plane).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel choose the \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E and its references:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EReferences\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENumbers\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset from a plane or face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrom plane\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDistance (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E, negative for the other side\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAt an angle, through an edge or axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrom plane\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETurn about\u003C/strong\u003E (a world axis, an axis feature, or a picked straight edge)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThrough three points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPoint 1\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint 2\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint 3\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMidplane between two\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFirst plane\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESecond plane\u003C/strong\u003E (parallel: half way; meeting: the plane that halves the angle)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETangent to a cylinder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECylinder\u003C/strong\u003E (a cylindrical face), \u003Cstrong\u003ENear point\u003C/strong\u003E (which side)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESquare to an edge, at a point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdge\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EAt point\u003C/strong\u003E (the plane goes through the edge&#039;s point nearest it, and through the point too)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThrough an edge and a point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStraight edge\u003C/strong\u003E (or an axis), \u003Cstrong\u003EThrough point\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESquare to an edge at its middle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdge\u003C/strong\u003E (half way along it, square to it there), or with no edge \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint 1\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint 2\u003C/strong\u003E (the plane half way between them)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery reference has a \u003Cstrong\u003EPick\u003C/strong\u003E button: choose it, then click in the view. A plane slot takes a base plane, a reference plane or a flat face; a point slot takes the end of an edge (click near that end), a point feature, or a spot on a face; an edge slot takes an edge. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels a pick. \u003Cstrong\u003EFlip the normal\u003C/strong\u003E turns the plane over (which way a sketch on it faces).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sketch-on-a-plane\"\u003ESketch on a plane\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the plane (in the history or by clicking its square in the view) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003ESketch on it\u003C/strong\u003E in its panel. The sketch follows the plane: change the plane&#039;s distance and the sketch, and every feature on it, moves too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"axes-and-points\"\u003EAxes and points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis\u003C/span\u003E: through two points, the axis of a cylinder or cone, where two planes meet, along a straight edge (or the axis of a circular edge), or square to a face through a point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint\u003C/span\u003E: at coordinates, at a vertex or sketch point, or at the centre of a circular edge, plus the points &quot;between objects&quot;:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPoint type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EReferences\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMiddle of an edge (or between two points)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdge\u003C/strong\u003E (half way along its length, also for arcs and curves), or with no edge \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint 1\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint 2\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere an edge or axis meets a face or plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdge\u003C/strong\u003E (straight or curved) or an axis, and \u003Cstrong\u003EMeets plane\u003C/strong\u003E (a plane or flat face) or \u003Cstrong\u003EOr a face\u003C/strong\u003E (a flat face or a cylinder). A circle crosses a plane twice and a line goes through a cylinder twice: \u003Cstrong\u003EThe other crossing\u003C/strong\u003E takes the second\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere three planes meet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFirst\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESecond\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EThird plane\u003C/strong\u003E (planes or flat faces)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach one fails with a plain message when its references do not fit: a line parallel to the plane, a line that misses the cylinder, an edge that does not reach the plane, three planes that do not meet in one point, a point on the line it should fix a plane with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse them in other features:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhere\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERevolve\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAxis\u003C/strong\u003E: pick the axis feature from the list (it must lie in the sketch&#039;s plane)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECircular pattern\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAxis\u003C/strong\u003E: the axis feature\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear pattern\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDirection\u003C/strong\u003E: an axis feature, or a plane (its normal)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMirror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMirror plane\u003C/strong\u003E: a reference plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnd\u003C/strong\u003E: Up to face or plane, Up to vertex (a point feature). Not built yet in this version (it needs an exact kernel)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENeutral plane\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-reference-is-gone\"\u003EWhen a reference is gone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plane made from a face that no longer exists fails with a message naming it, such as &quot;The face it sits on is gone from Extrude1.&quot; It is never moved to another face. The sketches and features on it are marked &quot;not built&quot; until you pick a new reference; the rest of the part stays as it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis version builds every part with the mesh kernel, which makes planes, axes and points from base planes, sketch points, coordinates and other references only. References on faces and edges of the solid need an exact kernel: not built yet in this version (&quot;It uses a face or edge of the solid, which only an exact kernel can find: not built yet in this version.&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Reference geometry is the scaffolding you build features on: a plane to sketch on where no face is, an axis to revolve or pattern about, a point to stop an extrude at. It has no material of its own. Planes show in the view as pale squares, axes as dashed lines, points as small crosses; all three join the history and follow what they were made from when you change a size upstream. They are in the Features group's More menu: Plane, Axis, Point. Make a plane Click a flat face, or a base plane in the history, to start from it (optional). Choose More, then Plane. It starts as an offset plane 20 % of the part's size away from what you picked (or from the top plane). In the right panel choose the Type and its references: Type References Numbers Offset from a plane or face From plane Distance (mm), negative for the other side At an angle, through an edge or axis From plane, Turn about (a world axis, an axis feature, or a picked straight edge) Angle (°) Through three points Point 1, Point 2, Point 3 — Midplane between two First plane, Second plane (parallel: half way; meeting: the plane that halves the angle) — Tangent to a cylinder Cylinder (a cylindrical face), Near point (which side) — Square to an edge, at a point Edge, At point (the plane goes through the edge's point nearest it, and through the point too) — Through an edge and a point Straight edge (or an axis), Through point — Square to an edge at its middle Edge (half way along it, square to it there), or with no edge Point 1 and Point 2 (the plane half way between them) — Every reference has a Pick button: choose it, then click in the view. A plane slot takes a base plane, a reference plane or a flat face; a point slot takes the end of an edge (click near that end), a point feature, or a spot on a face; an edge slot takes an edge. Esc cancels a pick. Flip the normal turns the plane over (which way a sketch on it faces). Sketch on a plane Select the plane (in the history or by clicking its square in the view) and choose Sketch, or Sketch on it in its panel. The sketch follows the plane: change the plane's distance and the sketch, and every feature on it, moves too. Axes and points Axis: through two points, the axis of a cylinder or cone, where two planes meet, along a straight edge (or the axis of a circular edge), or square to a face through a point. Point: at coordinates, at a vertex or sketch point, or at the centre of a circular edge, plus the points \"between objects\": Point type References Middle of an edge (or between two points) Edge (half way along its length, also for arcs and curves), or with no edge Point 1 and Point 2 Where an edge or axis meets a face or plane Edge (straight or curved) or an axis, and Meets plane (a plane or flat face) or Or a face (a flat face or a cylinder). A circle crosses a plane twice and a line goes through a cylinder twice: The other crossing takes the second Where three planes meet First, Second and Third plane (planes or flat faces) Each one fails with a plain message when its references do not fit: a line parallel to the plane, a line that misses the cylinder, an edge that does not reach the plane, three planes that do not meet in one point, a point on the line it should fix a plane with. Use them in other features: Where How Revolve Axis: pick the axis feature from the list (it must lie in the sketch's plane) Circular pattern Axis: the axis feature Linear pattern Direction: an axis feature, or a plane (its normal) Mirror Mirror plane: a reference plane Extrude End: Up to face or plane, Up to vertex (a point feature). Not built yet in this version (it needs an exact kernel) Draft Neutral plane When a reference is gone A plane made from a face that no longer exists fails with a message naming it, such as \"The face it sits on is gone from Extrude1.\" It is never moved to another face. The sketches and features on it are marked \"not built\" until you pick a new reference; the rest of the part stays as it was. This version builds every part with the mesh kernel, which makes planes, axes and points from base planes, sketch points, coordinates and other references only. References on faces and edges of the solid need an exact kernel: not built yet in this version (\"It uses a face or edge of the solid, which only an exact kernel can find: not built yet in this version.\")."},{"slug":"patterns-mirror","scope":"partcraft","title":"Pattern and mirror features","summary":"Repeat features in a row with Linear, round an axis with Circular, and copy features or the whole body across a plane with Mirror.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["pattern","linear pattern","circular pattern","polar array","array","mirror","symmetric","copy features","bolt circle","seed","mirror body"],"headings":[{"id":"linear-pattern","text":"Linear pattern","level":2},{"id":"use-an-edge-as-the-direction","text":"Use an edge as the direction","level":3},{"id":"circular-pattern","text":"Circular pattern","level":2},{"id":"example-the-eight-bolt-holes-of-a-flange","text":"Example: the eight bolt holes of a flange","level":3},{"id":"mirror","text":"Mirror","level":2},{"id":"messages","text":"Messages","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPatterns and mirrors copy features you have already built. The copies are part of the history: change the original and the copies follow.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat gets copied is what the feature does: a boss pattern adds material, a cut or hole pattern removes it. Patterns can be patterned and mirrored again. An imported STEP solid can be copied too; fillets, chamfers and shells cannot.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"linear-pattern\"\u003ELinear pattern\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the feature to copy in the history (for example a Hole or a Cut). With nothing selected, the last solid feature is used.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E. Three copies appear 20 mm apart along X.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, set:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFeatures to copy\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETick every feature to copy. Only features above the pattern are listed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selected feature\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDirection\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EX axis, Y axis, Z axis, a line or centerline from any earlier sketch, the axis of a revolve, or a picked edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EX axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpacing (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDistance between neighbors\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E20\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECount\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal number, \u003Cstrong\u003Eincluding the original\u003C/strong\u003E (1 to 1000)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReverse direction\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopies go the other way\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"use-an-edge-as-the-direction\"\u003EUse an edge as the direction\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a straight edge of the part in the view. The right panel says \u003Cstrong\u003EEdge\u003C/strong\u003E and shows its length.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E: the pattern runs along that edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr select the pattern, pick an edge, and choose &quot;Picked edge (use the one picked now)&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003EDirection\u003C/strong\u003E. A picked edge is fixed in space: it does not follow later changes. A sketch line follows its sketch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"circular-pattern\"\u003ECircular pattern\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the feature to copy (for example one hole near the rim of a disc).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircular\u003C/span\u003E. Six copies appear equally spaced all the way round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFeatures to copy\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs for Linear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selected feature\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAxis\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EX, Y or Z axis, a sketch line, the axis of a revolve, or a picked edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe last revolve&#039;s axis if there is one, else the Y axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E360 spaces the copies equally all round. Less spreads them over that angle, first and last at the ends\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E360\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECount\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal number, including the original\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReverse direction\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns the other way\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe world axes pass through the origin. For a disc not centered on the origin, use a sketch centerline or a revolve&#039;s axis.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"example-the-eight-bolt-holes-of-a-flange\"\u003EExample: the eight bolt holes of a flange\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERevolve the flange (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/revolve\" data-topic=\"partcraft/revolve\"\u003ERevolve a turned part\u003C/a\u003E) about the Y axis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESketch on the flange&#039;s flat top face, put one point on the bolt circle, and make a Hole.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith the Hole selected, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircular\u003C/span\u003E. The axis is already &quot;Axis of Revolve1&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003ECount\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Ccode\u003E8\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mirror\"\u003EMirror\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the feature to mirror in the history, or select nothing to mirror \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe whole body as built so far\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMirror\u003C/span\u003E. It mirrors across the \u003Cstrong\u003ERight plane\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo mirror the whole body across a face of the part instead, click that flat face first, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMirror\u003C/span\u003E. (Clicking a face clears the history selection, so this always mirrors the whole body.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESettings in the right panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMirror\u003C/strong\u003E: tick \u003Cstrong\u003EThe whole body (as built so far)\u003C/strong\u003E, or clear it and tick the features to copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMirror plane\u003C/strong\u003E: Front plane, Top plane, Right plane, the face it uses now (&quot;A face of Extrude1&quot; or &quot;A fixed plane&quot;), or &quot;The face picked now&quot;. To mirror chosen features across a face: select the Mirror in the history, click the face in the view, and choose &quot;The face picked now&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMirroring the whole body across a face doubles the part, for example a half bracket into a full one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"messages\"\u003EMessages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Build a feature to copy first.&quot; or &quot;Build a solid first.&quot; There is nothing solid to copy yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Choose the features to copy.&quot; Every \u003Cstrong\u003EFeatures to copy\u003C/strong\u003E box is cleared.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Extrude2 has no solid to copy.&quot; That feature failed or is suppressed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Fillet1 changes the body&#039;s edges or walls; it cannot be copied. Copy the features that make solids (extrudes, revolves, holes, imports).&quot; Fillets, chamfers and shells cannot be patterned or mirrored; pattern first, then fillet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;There is no body to mirror yet.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPattern copies cannot be skipped one by one, and a pattern cannot follow a sketch curve. Sketch patterns and mirrors are not built yet either: draw repeated sketch geometry by hand or with \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd by coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Patterns and mirrors copy features you have already built. The copies are part of the history: change the original and the copies follow. What gets copied is what the feature does: a boss pattern adds material, a cut or hole pattern removes it. Patterns can be patterned and mirrored again. An imported STEP solid can be copied too; fillets, chamfers and shells cannot. Linear pattern Select the feature to copy in the history (for example a Hole or a Cut). With nothing selected, the last solid feature is used. Choose Linear. Three copies appear 20 mm apart along X. In the right panel, set: Setting What it does Default Features to copy Tick every feature to copy. Only features above the pattern are listed The selected feature Direction X axis, Y axis, Z axis, a line or centerline from any earlier sketch, the axis of a revolve, or a picked edge X axis Spacing (mm) Distance between neighbors 20 Count Total number, including the original (1 to 1000) 3 Reverse direction Copies go the other way Off Use an edge as the direction Click a straight edge of the part in the view. The right panel says Edge and shows its length. Choose Linear: the pattern runs along that edge. Or select the pattern, pick an edge, and choose \"Picked edge (use the one picked now)\" in Direction. A picked edge is fixed in space: it does not follow later changes. A sketch line follows its sketch. Circular pattern Select the feature to copy (for example one hole near the rim of a disc). Choose Circular. Six copies appear equally spaced all the way round. Set: Setting What it does Default Features to copy As for Linear The selected feature Axis X, Y or Z axis, a sketch line, the axis of a revolve, or a picked edge The last revolve's axis if there is one, else the Y axis Angle (°) 360 spaces the copies equally all round. Less spreads them over that angle, first and last at the ends 360 Count Total number, including the original 6 Reverse direction Turns the other way Off The world axes pass through the origin. For a disc not centered on the origin, use a sketch centerline or a revolve's axis. Example: the eight bolt holes of a flange Revolve the flange (see Revolve a turned part) about the Y axis. Sketch on the flange's flat top face, put one point on the bolt circle, and make a Hole. With the Hole selected, choose Circular. The axis is already \"Axis of Revolve1\". Set Count to 8. Mirror Select the feature to mirror in the history, or select nothing to mirror the whole body as built so far. Choose Mirror. It mirrors across the Right plane. To mirror the whole body across a face of the part instead, click that flat face first, then choose Mirror. (Clicking a face clears the history selection, so this always mirrors the whole body.) Settings in the right panel: Mirror: tick The whole body (as built so far), or clear it and tick the features to copy. Mirror plane: Front plane, Top plane, Right plane, the face it uses now (\"A face of Extrude1\" or \"A fixed plane\"), or \"The face picked now\". To mirror chosen features across a face: select the Mirror in the history, click the face in the view, and choose \"The face picked now\". Mirroring the whole body across a face doubles the part, for example a half bracket into a full one. Messages \"Build a feature to copy first.\" or \"Build a solid first.\" There is nothing solid to copy yet. \"Choose the features to copy.\" Every Features to copy box is cleared. \"Extrude2 has no solid to copy.\" That feature failed or is suppressed. \"Fillet1 changes the body's edges or walls; it cannot be copied. Copy the features that make solids (extrudes, revolves, holes, imports).\" Fillets, chamfers and shells cannot be patterned or mirrored; pattern first, then fillet. \"There is no body to mirror yet.\" Pattern copies cannot be skipped one by one, and a pattern cannot follow a sketch curve. Sketch patterns and mirrors are not built yet either: draw repeated sketch geometry by hand or with Add by coordinates."},{"slug":"bodies","scope":"partcraft","title":"Combine, move and scale bodies; path patterns","summary":"Make separate bodies, then join, cut or intersect them after the fact, move, turn, align or copy them, scale them evenly or by X, Y and Z, and copy features or bodies along a curve.","section":"tasks","order":72,"keywords":["body","bodies","multi-body","new body","combine","boolean","join","cut","intersect","subtract","union","keep tools","move body","copy body","rotate body","align","translate","scale","non-uniform scale","stretch","affine","path pattern","pattern along curve","array along path","curve driven pattern"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-separate-body","text":"Make a separate body","level":2},{"id":"rename-or-hide-a-body","text":"Rename or hide a body","level":2},{"id":"combine-bodies","text":"Combine bodies","level":2},{"id":"move-or-copy-a-body","text":"Move or copy a body","level":2},{"id":"scale-a-body","text":"Scale a body","level":2},{"id":"copy-along-a-path","text":"Copy along a path","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot built yet in this version.\u003C/strong\u003E Working with several bodies needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes it as it works with an exact kernel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA part can hold more than one solid. Each separate solid is a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebody\u003C/strong\u003E. Most features add to or cut from every body they touch; the tools on this page work on whole bodies you choose. They are in the Features group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECombine\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove body\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale body\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPath pattern\u003C/span\u003E. All of them need the exact kernel (OpenCascade).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-separate-body\"\u003EMake a separate body\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet an extrude&#039;s or a revolve&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EOperation\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003ENew body\u003C/strong\u003E in its right panel (sweeps and lofts have it too). The new solid stays separate even where it overlaps the others. \u003Cstrong\u003EIntersect\u003C/strong\u003E keeps only the overlap of the new shape with what is there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBodies are named after the feature that started them: &quot;Extrude2&quot;. A copy is named after its original and the feature that copied it: &quot;Extrude2, copy 1 of Move1&quot;. The names stay the same when you change sizes upstream, so the features that use a body keep finding it. With two or more bodies, the part panel (click empty space) lists them with their volumes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rename-or-hide-a-body\"\u003ERename or hide a body\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick empty space in the view to see the part panel. Its body list (shown when the part has two or more bodies) has a name box and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide\u003C/span\u003E button on each row.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERename:\u003C/strong\u003E type a new name in the box and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click away. Every panel then uses it: Combine, Move body, the patterns. Clear the box to go back to the name from the feature that made the body.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHide:\u003C/strong\u003E click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide\u003C/span\u003E on its row. The body leaves the view and clicks go through it, so you can pick faces behind it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E brings it back; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E brings back every hidden body.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHiding only changes the view. A hidden body is still built, counted in the volume and the measurements, and saved in STL, 3MF and STEP exports. Names and hidden bodies are saved in the part file, and each change is one step of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"combine-bodies\"\u003ECombine bodies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShift+click a face of the \u003Cstrong\u003Etarget\u003C/strong\u003E body, then a face of each \u003Cstrong\u003Etool\u003C/strong\u003E body.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECombine\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel choose the \u003Cstrong\u003EOperation\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOperation\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EResult\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoin\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe target and the tools become one body\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe tools are taken away from the target\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIntersect\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly what the target and every tool share is kept\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETarget body\u003C/strong\u003E is the one that keeps its name. Tick or untick \u003Cstrong\u003ETool bodies\u003C/strong\u003E, or use \u003Cstrong\u003EPick in the view\u003C/strong\u003E and click their faces. \u003Cstrong\u003EKeep the tool bodies\u003C/strong\u003E leaves the tools in place as well (handy for a mold and its core). If a tool body was already used up by an earlier Combine, the panel says so instead of picking another body.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-or-copy-a-body\"\u003EMove or copy a body\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove body\u003C/span\u003E. The newest body is ticked; tick the ones to move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003EMove\u003C/strong\u003E type:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMove\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you set\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETranslate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EΔX\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EΔY\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EΔZ\u003C/strong\u003E in millimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint to point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrom\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ETo\u003C/strong\u003E: click near the end of an edge, a point feature, or a spot on a face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERotate about an axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAxis\u003C/strong\u003E (X, Y, Z, an axis feature, or \u003Cstrong\u003EPick\u003C/strong\u003E: a straight edge, a circle or a cylinder) and \u003Cstrong\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlign a face to a face or plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFace to move\u003C/strong\u003E (a flat face of the body), \u003Cstrong\u003EOnto\u003C/strong\u003E (a flat face or a plane)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlign turns the body so its face lies on the other face, face to face, then slides it along that face&#039;s normal only. \u003Cstrong\u003ESame way\u003C/strong\u003E turns it the other way round. \u003Cstrong\u003ECopy\u003C/strong\u003E keeps the original and adds the moved copy as a new body.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scale-a-body\"\u003EScale a body\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale body\u003C/span\u003E, and tick the bodies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a \u003Cstrong\u003EScale\u003C/strong\u003E, or untick \u003Cstrong\u003EUniform\u003C/strong\u003E and type \u003Cstrong\u003EScale X\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EScale Y\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EScale Z\u003C/strong\u003E (along the world axes).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAbout\u003C/strong\u003E is the point that stays put: the centre of the bodies&#039; box, or a point you pick.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA uniform scale is exact. A non-uniform scale turns circles into ellipses: a cylinder becomes an elliptic cylinder, which no analytic surface holds, so curved faces become B-spline surfaces that stay within 0.0000004 mm of the exact shape; flat faces and straight edges stay exact. The panel says which happened. Scale factors must be more than zero (mirror with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMirror\u003C/span\u003E instead).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-along-a-path\"\u003ECopy along a path\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the feature to copy in the history, or leave nothing selected to copy the newest body.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPath pattern\u003C/span\u003E. The newest unused sketch is taken as the path.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel set:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECopy\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBodies (each copy a new body) or Features (joined or cut like the original)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPath\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETick sketches, or \u003Cstrong\u003EPick model edges\u003C/strong\u003E; the pieces must join end to end\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECount\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal number, including the original\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpacing\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEqual (spread to the end of an open path, or all round a closed one) or a set distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOrientation\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFollow the path (each copy turns with it) or Fixed (copies stay parallel)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReverse direction\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGo the other way along the path\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copies start where the path passes nearest the original and are spaced by length along the curve.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELinear and circular patterns can copy bodies too: tick \u003Cstrong\u003ECopy bodies\u003C/strong\u003E in their panel and choose the bodies; each copy is a new body.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBodies need the exact kernel; the mesh kernel keeps one solid and says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA non-uniform scale makes curved faces into close B-spline approximations (see above), not exact cylinders or cones; offset surfaces cannot be scaled that way yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFollowing the path on a twisting 3D curve carries the copy without twisting it; on flat paths the turn is exact.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECombine works on solids only. Surface bodies are joined with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStitch\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/reference-geometry\" data-topic=\"partcraft/reference-geometry\"\u003EReference planes, axes and points\u003C/a\u003E for points and planes &quot;between objects&quot;, and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/patterns-mirror\" data-topic=\"partcraft/patterns-mirror\"\u003EPattern and mirror features\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Not built yet in this version. Working with several bodies needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. What is not built yet says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes it as it works with an exact kernel. A part can hold more than one solid. Each separate solid is a body. Most features add to or cut from every body they touch; the tools on this page work on whole bodies you choose. They are in the Features group's More menu: Combine, Move body, Scale body and Path pattern. All of them need the exact kernel (OpenCascade). Make a separate body Set an extrude's or a revolve's Operation to New body in its right panel (sweeps and lofts have it too). The new solid stays separate even where it overlaps the others. Intersect keeps only the overlap of the new shape with what is there. Bodies are named after the feature that started them: \"Extrude2\". A copy is named after its original and the feature that copied it: \"Extrude2, copy 1 of Move1\". The names stay the same when you change sizes upstream, so the features that use a body keep finding it. With two or more bodies, the part panel (click empty space) lists them with their volumes. Rename or hide a body Click empty space in the view to see the part panel. Its body list (shown when the part has two or more bodies) has a name box and a Hide button on each row. Rename: type a new name in the box and press Enter or click away. Every panel then uses it: Combine, Move body, the patterns. Clear the box to go back to the name from the feature that made the body. Hide: click Hide on its row. The body leaves the view and clicks go through it, so you can pick faces behind it. Show brings it back; Show all brings back every hidden body. Hiding only changes the view. A hidden body is still built, counted in the volume and the measurements, and saved in STL, 3MF and STEP exports. Names and hidden bodies are saved in the part file, and each change is one step of Undo. Combine bodies Shift+click a face of the target body, then a face of each tool body. Choose More, then Combine. In the right panel choose the Operation: Operation Result Join The target and the tools become one body Cut The tools are taken away from the target Intersect Only what the target and every tool share is kept Target body is the one that keeps its name. Tick or untick Tool bodies, or use Pick in the view and click their faces. Keep the tool bodies leaves the tools in place as well (handy for a mold and its core). If a tool body was already used up by an earlier Combine, the panel says so instead of picking another body. Move or copy a body Choose More, then Move body. The newest body is ticked; tick the ones to move. Choose the Move type: Move What you set Translate ΔX, ΔY, ΔZ in millimeters Point to point From and To: click near the end of an edge, a point feature, or a spot on a face Rotate about an axis Axis (X, Y, Z, an axis feature, or Pick: a straight edge, a circle or a cylinder) and Angle (°) Align a face to a face or plane Face to move (a flat face of the body), Onto (a flat face or a plane) Align turns the body so its face lies on the other face, face to face, then slides it along that face's normal only. Same way turns it the other way round. Copy keeps the original and adds the moved copy as a new body. Scale a body Choose More, then Scale body, and tick the bodies. Type a Scale, or untick Uniform and type Scale X, Scale Y and Scale Z (along the world axes). About is the point that stays put: the centre of the bodies' box, or a point you pick. A uniform scale is exact. A non-uniform scale turns circles into ellipses: a cylinder becomes an elliptic cylinder, which no analytic surface holds, so curved faces become B-spline surfaces that stay within 0.0000004 mm of the exact shape; flat faces and straight edges stay exact. The panel says which happened. Scale factors must be more than zero (mirror with Mirror instead). Copy along a path Select the feature to copy in the history, or leave nothing selected to copy the newest body. Choose More, then Path pattern. The newest unused sketch is taken as the path. In the right panel set: Setting What it does Copy Bodies (each copy a new body) or Features (joined or cut like the original) Path Tick sketches, or Pick model edges; the pieces must join end to end Count Total number, including the original Spacing Equal (spread to the end of an open path, or all round a closed one) or a set distance Orientation Follow the path (each copy turns with it) or Fixed (copies stay parallel) Reverse direction Go the other way along the path The copies start where the path passes nearest the original and are spaced by length along the curve. Linear and circular patterns can copy bodies too: tick Copy bodies in their panel and choose the bodies; each copy is a new body. Limits Bodies need the exact kernel; the mesh kernel keeps one solid and says so. A non-uniform scale makes curved faces into close B-spline approximations (see above), not exact cylinders or cones; offset surfaces cannot be scaled that way yet. Following the path on a twisting 3D curve carries the copy without twisting it; on flat paths the turn is exact. Combine works on solids only. Surface bodies are joined with Stitch. See also Reference planes, axes and points for points and planes \"between objects\", and Pattern and mirror features."},{"slug":"tolerance-stackup","scope":"partcraft","title":"Tolerance stack-ups","summary":"Pick a chain of faces through the parts of an assembly and see how much the gap at its end can vary, worst case and RSS, with the dimensions that matter most ranked first.","section":"tasks","order":72,"keywords":["tolerance stack-up","stackup","tolerance analysis","tolerance chain","dimension chain","loop","gap","clearance","worst case","rss","root sum square","statistical tolerancing","contributors","sensitivity","gd&t","general tolerance","iso 2768"],"headings":[{"id":"pick-the-chain","text":"Pick the chain","level":2},{"id":"give-each-link-its-tolerance","text":"Give each link its tolerance","level":2},{"id":"read-the-results","text":"Read the results","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Estack-up\u003C/strong\u003E adds up the dimensions along a chain through several parts to see how much a result — usually a gap — can vary when each part is made anywhere inside its tolerances.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-the-chain\"\u003EPick the chain\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an assembly and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStack-up\u003C/span\u003E in the Assembly group. A card opens over the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what the chain is measured \u003Cstrong\u003Ealong\u003C/strong\u003E: X, Y or Z of the assembly, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe first face&#039;s normal\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick the chain\u003C/span\u003E and click the faces the chain runs through, \u003Cstrong\u003Ein order\u003C/strong\u003E: start at one side of the gap, go through the parts, and end at the other side of the gap.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E or Enter. Backspace takes the last click back; Esc cancels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo clicks on the same part make that part&#039;s dimension (its distance along the chain). A click on another part makes an \u003Cstrong\u003Einterface\u003C/strong\u003E between them, where the parts meet; it has no tolerance unless you type one. The links add or take away by the way the chain runs, and they close the loop: the nominal gap is what the model shows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a link\u003C/span\u003E by hand and type its nominal, its tolerance and whether it adds (+) or takes away (−).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"give-each-link-its-tolerance\"\u003EGive each link its tolerance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach link has an \u003Cstrong\u003Eupper\u003C/strong\u003E and a \u003Cstrong\u003Elower\u003C/strong\u003E deviation (for example +0.1 and −0.05). \u003Cstrong\u003ETolerance from\u003C/strong\u003E fills them in from the part:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGeneral tolerance\u003C/strong\u003E: the part&#039;s title block Tolerance field (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/drawing-pdf\" data-topic=\"partcraft/drawing-pdf\"\u003EDrawing to PDF\u003C/a\u003E), when it reads ±0.1, +0.2/-0.1 or ISO 2768-f, -m, -c or -v (ISO 2768-1 linear tolerances, by the link&#039;s size). A part&#039;s general tolerance is used straight away when the chain is picked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGD&amp;T\u003C/strong\u003E: any feature control frame on the part&#039;s drawing. Position, profile and runout zones give ± half the tolerance. An orientation or form tolerance usually lies inside the size tolerance already: add it only where the chain meets the controlled surface. With Ⓜ or Ⓛ the zone is taken at its stated size; the bonus tolerance is not counted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyped\u003C/strong\u003E: any numbers you type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-results\"\u003ERead the results\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGap, nominal\u003C/strong\u003E and, when the tolerances are unequal, \u003Cstrong\u003EGap, mean\u003C/strong\u003E — +0.2/−0 and +0/−0.1 move the middle of the range.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWorst case\u003C/strong\u003E: every tolerance at its limit at once. The gap can never be outside this.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERSS\u003C/strong\u003E (root sum square): each tolerance taken as three standard deviations of a normal spread, added as the square root of the sum of their squares. Most assemblies fall well inside the worst case; this is the usual estimate for parts made in quantity.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive \u003Cstrong\u003EGap at least\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EGap at most\u003C/strong\u003E to see whether the worst case always stays inside, and how many assemblies in a million RSS expects outside.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003E#\u003C/strong\u003E column ranks the links by their \u003Cstrong\u003Eshare\u003C/strong\u003E of the RSS: tighten the first ones to make the biggest difference.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave CSV\u003C/span\u003E writes the links, their shares and the results. The stack-ups are saved in the assembly file, and every change is one step for \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA stack-up is one direction. It does not follow angles or turns along the chain, and it does not read size tolerances from the sketches: give those as typed tolerances or through the title block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"A stack-up adds up the dimensions along a chain through several parts to see how much a result — usually a gap — can vary when each part is made anywhere inside its tolerances. Pick the chain Open an assembly and choose Stack-up in the Assembly group. A card opens over the view. Choose what the chain is measured along: X, Y or Z of the assembly, or The first face's normal. Press Pick the chain and click the faces the chain runs through, in order: start at one side of the gap, go through the parts, and end at the other side of the gap. Press Done or Enter. Backspace takes the last click back; Esc cancels. Two clicks on the same part make that part's dimension (its distance along the chain). A click on another part makes an interface between them, where the parts meet; it has no tolerance unless you type one. The links add or take away by the way the chain runs, and they close the loop: the nominal gap is what the model shows. You can also Add a link by hand and type its nominal, its tolerance and whether it adds (+) or takes away (−). Give each link its tolerance Each link has an upper and a lower deviation (for example +0.1 and −0.05). Tolerance from fills them in from the part: General tolerance: the part's title block Tolerance field (see Drawing to PDF), when it reads ±0.1, +0.2/-0.1 or ISO 2768-f, -m, -c or -v (ISO 2768-1 linear tolerances, by the link's size). A part's general tolerance is used straight away when the chain is picked. GD&T: any feature control frame on the part's drawing. Position, profile and runout zones give ± half the tolerance. An orientation or form tolerance usually lies inside the size tolerance already: add it only where the chain meets the controlled surface. With Ⓜ or Ⓛ the zone is taken at its stated size; the bonus tolerance is not counted. Typed: any numbers you type. Read the results Gap, nominal and, when the tolerances are unequal, Gap, mean — +0.2/−0 and +0/−0.1 move the middle of the range. Worst case: every tolerance at its limit at once. The gap can never be outside this. RSS (root sum square): each tolerance taken as three standard deviations of a normal spread, added as the square root of the sum of their squares. Most assemblies fall well inside the worst case; this is the usual estimate for parts made in quantity. Give Gap at least and Gap at most to see whether the worst case always stays inside, and how many assemblies in a million RSS expects outside. The # column ranks the links by their share of the RSS: tighten the first ones to make the biggest difference. Save CSV writes the links, their shares and the results. The stack-ups are saved in the assembly file, and every change is one step for Ctrl+Z. A stack-up is one direction. It does not follow angles or turns along the chain, and it does not read size tolerances from the sketches: give those as typed tolerances or through the title block."},{"slug":"sweep-loft-draft","scope":"partcraft","title":"Sweep, loft and draft","summary":"Sweep a closed profile along a path of sketch curves or model edges, loft through sections on different planes, and add draft to faces; with the options, the exact results, and the messages.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["sweep","swept boss","swept cut","pipe","path","loft","lofted boss","blend sections","ruled","draft","draft angle","taper faces","neutral plane","pull direction","molding"],"headings":[{"id":"sweep","text":"Sweep","level":2},{"id":"loft","text":"Loft","level":2},{"id":"guide-rails","text":"Guide rails","level":3},{"id":"draft","text":"Draft","level":2},{"id":"messages","text":"Messages","level":2}],"html":"\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn this version\u003C/strong\u003E sweep and loft are not built yet: they need an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E says what to do instead; the Sweep and Loft sections describe them as they work with an exact kernel. \u003Cstrong\u003EDraft works\u003C/strong\u003E on flat faces: the mesh kernel builds it, with the same result an exact kernel gives.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three are in the Features group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E). Each can join material, cut it, keep only the overlap (\u003Cstrong\u003EIntersect\u003C/strong\u003E) or make a \u003Cstrong\u003ENew body\u003C/strong\u003E (sweep and loft: \u003Cstrong\u003EOperation\u003C/strong\u003E in the panel).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sweep\"\u003ESweep\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sweep moves a closed profile along a path: pipes, handles, springs of wire, rails.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the \u003Cstrong\u003Epath\u003C/strong\u003E in one sketch: lines and arcs joined end to end (or use model edges, below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the \u003Cstrong\u003Eprofile\u003C/strong\u003E in another sketch whose plane passes through one end of the path, for example a circle on the top plane at the start of a path drawn on the front plane.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the profile sketch and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESweep\u003C/span\u003E. The most recent other sketch becomes the path.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPath\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETick the sketches that make the path (several sketches join end to end into one 3D path); \u003Cstrong\u003EPick model edges\u003C/strong\u003E adds edges of the part\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOrientation\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFollow the path (default), Frenet, Fixed (sections stay parallel to the profile), Keep a direction, Along a guide curve\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnd scale\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe profile grows or shrinks linearly to this size at the end (1 = constant)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESharp corners in the path are mitred. A circle of radius 5 swept along a 100 mm line is a cylinder of exactly 2500π mm³; along a quarter arc of radius 50, a quarter torus of 625π² mm³.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"loft\"\u003ELoft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA loft blends through two or more sections on different planes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw each section in its own sketch (one closed loop each), on base planes, reference planes (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/reference-geometry\" data-topic=\"partcraft/reference-geometry\"\u003EReference planes, axes and points\u003C/a\u003E) or faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoft\u003C/span\u003E. Every unused one-loop sketch becomes a section, in history order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel lists the sections in order (\u003Cstrong\u003EUp\u003C/strong\u003E reorders, \u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E drops one); \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd a sketch\u003C/strong\u003E adds another, \u003Cstrong\u003EPick a face\u003C/strong\u003E adds a flat face of the part. \u003Cstrong\u003ERuled\u003C/strong\u003E makes flat faces between sections instead of a smooth blend. Between two squares, both are the same frustum (a 40 mm square to a 20 mm square over 30 mm: exactly 28000 mm³).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESections with different numbers of sides (a square to a triangle) are matched by OpenCascade, which may twist the side faces; the feature warns you. Draw sections with the same number of sides, starting from matching corners, for the cleanest result.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"guide-rails\"\u003EGuide rails\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuide rails are curves the loft&#039;s sides must follow from the first section to the last, for a bulge, a waist or a flare you draw yourself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw two or more rails: open curves (lines, arcs, splines) that run from the first section to the last and cross every section once. One sketch can hold several rails: every separate chain in it is its own rail. Draw a rail so it really meets each section: snap its ends to the sections&#039; points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the Loft panel, under \u003Cstrong\u003EGuide rails\u003C/strong\u003E, tick the sketches that hold them, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick model edges as rails\u003C/span\u003E and click edges of the part (Esc ends).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith rails the loft is a Gordon surface through the sections and the rails (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/gordon-surfaces\" data-topic=\"partcraft/gordon-surfaces\"\u003ESurfaces through a curve network\u003C/a\u003E), capped flat at both ends into a solid. The panel says how closely it follows them. Every section must then be a sketch (a point or a face cannot take a rail), and \u003Cstrong\u003ERuled\u003C/strong\u003E does not apply. A rail that misses a section by more than 0.01 mm is refused, with the gap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChecked: two circles (20 mm and 10 mm across 40 mm) with four straight rails give the cone frustum&#039;s volume to within 0.000002 %; arcs that bulge out 5 mm give a barrel that follows every rail within 0.00001 mm.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draft\"\u003EDraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraft tilts faces so a part comes out of a mold.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click faces first). With nothing picked you are in pick mode: click each face to tilt, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cstrong\u003ENeutral plane\u003C/strong\u003E (where the faces keep their size) and the \u003Cstrong\u003EPull direction\u003C/strong\u003E (the neutral plane&#039;s normal by default).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA positive angle leans the face inward along the pull and removes material; a negative one adds it. The neutral plane starts as the Top plane. In this version only flat faces can be drafted: a cylinder, cone or freeform face says &quot;Draft: in this version flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).&quot; The four sides of a 100 × 100 × 50 block drafted 5° about its bottom become a frustum of exactly h/3·(a² + b² + ab), with b = a − 2h·tan 5°.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"messages\"\u003EMessages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The profile&#039;s plane has to pass through one end of the path …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the profile sketch to the start (or end) of the path\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The path has a gap: its curves do not join end to end …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoin the path&#039;s curves; in separate sketches their ends must meet exactly\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A loft section needs exactly one closed loop&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESplit the sketch, or remove the extra loop\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;… cannot be drafted about this neutral plane …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe face is parallel to the neutral plane or does not meet it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Draft: in this version flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picked face is curved. Remove it; draft only flat faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot built yet: twisting sweeps, thin-wall and surface sweeps, loft guide rails and end tangency, draft on cylinders, cones and freeform faces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"In this version sweep and loft are not built yet: they need an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have. What is not built yet says what to do instead; the Sweep and Loft sections describe them as they work with an exact kernel. Draft works on flat faces: the mesh kernel builds it, with the same result an exact kernel gives. All three are in the Features group's More menu (see The mesh kernel). Each can join material, cut it, keep only the overlap (Intersect) or make a New body (sweep and loft: Operation in the panel). Sweep A sweep moves a closed profile along a path: pipes, handles, springs of wire, rails. Draw the path in one sketch: lines and arcs joined end to end (or use model edges, below). Draw the profile in another sketch whose plane passes through one end of the path, for example a circle on the top plane at the start of a path drawn on the front plane. Select the profile sketch and choose More, then Sweep. The most recent other sketch becomes the path. In the panel: Setting What it does Path Tick the sketches that make the path (several sketches join end to end into one 3D path); Pick model edges adds edges of the part Orientation Follow the path (default), Frenet, Fixed (sections stay parallel to the profile), Keep a direction, Along a guide curve End scale The profile grows or shrinks linearly to this size at the end (1 = constant) Sharp corners in the path are mitred. A circle of radius 5 swept along a 100 mm line is a cylinder of exactly 2500π mm³; along a quarter arc of radius 50, a quarter torus of 625π² mm³. Loft A loft blends through two or more sections on different planes. Draw each section in its own sketch (one closed loop each), on base planes, reference planes (see Reference planes, axes and points) or faces. Choose More, then Loft. Every unused one-loop sketch becomes a section, in history order. The panel lists the sections in order (Up reorders, Remove drops one); Add a sketch adds another, Pick a face adds a flat face of the part. Ruled makes flat faces between sections instead of a smooth blend. Between two squares, both are the same frustum (a 40 mm square to a 20 mm square over 30 mm: exactly 28000 mm³). Sections with different numbers of sides (a square to a triangle) are matched by OpenCascade, which may twist the side faces; the feature warns you. Draw sections with the same number of sides, starting from matching corners, for the cleanest result. Guide rails Guide rails are curves the loft's sides must follow from the first section to the last, for a bulge, a waist or a flare you draw yourself. Draw two or more rails: open curves (lines, arcs, splines) that run from the first section to the last and cross every section once. One sketch can hold several rails: every separate chain in it is its own rail. Draw a rail so it really meets each section: snap its ends to the sections' points. In the Loft panel, under Guide rails, tick the sketches that hold them, or click Pick model edges as rails and click edges of the part (Esc ends). With rails the loft is a Gordon surface through the sections and the rails (see Surfaces through a curve network), capped flat at both ends into a solid. The panel says how closely it follows them. Every section must then be a sketch (a point or a face cannot take a rail), and Ruled does not apply. A rail that misses a section by more than 0.01 mm is refused, with the gap. Checked: two circles (20 mm and 10 mm across 40 mm) with four straight rails give the cone frustum's volume to within 0.000002 %; arcs that bulge out 5 mm give a barrel that follows every rail within 0.00001 mm. Draft Draft tilts faces so a part comes out of a mold. Choose More, then Draft (or Shift+click faces first). With nothing picked you are in pick mode: click each face to tilt, then Done. Set Angle (°), the Neutral plane (where the faces keep their size) and the Pull direction (the neutral plane's normal by default). A positive angle leans the face inward along the pull and removes material; a negative one adds it. The neutral plane starts as the Top plane. In this version only flat faces can be drafted: a cylinder, cone or freeform face says \"Draft: in this version flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).\" The four sides of a 100 × 100 × 50 block drafted 5° about its bottom become a frustum of exactly h/3·(a² + b² + ab), with b = a − 2h·tan 5°. Messages Message What to do \"The profile's plane has to pass through one end of the path …\" Move the profile sketch to the start (or end) of the path \"The path has a gap: its curves do not join end to end …\" Join the path's curves; in separate sketches their ends must meet exactly \"A loft section needs exactly one closed loop\" Split the sketch, or remove the extra loop \"… cannot be drafted about this neutral plane …\" The face is parallel to the neutral plane or does not meet it \"Draft: in this version flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).\" A picked face is curved. Remove it; draft only flat faces Not built yet: twisting sweeps, thin-wall and surface sweeps, loft guide rails and end tangency, draft on cylinders, cones and freeform faces."},{"slug":"fillet-chamfer-shell","scope":"partcraft","title":"Round, bevel and hollow with Fillet, Chamfer and Shell","summary":"Pick edges and add constant or variable-radius fillets and equal, two-distance or distance-angle chamfers, hollow a part with Shell by picking the faces to open, change which edges or faces a feature uses, and fix the usual failures.","section":"tasks","order":85,"keywords":["fillet","round","blend","chamfer","bevel","shell","hollow","wall thickness","edges","faces","pick edges","shift click","open face","exact kernel","mesh kernel","not built"],"headings":[{"id":"fillet-or-chamfer-edges","text":"Fillet or chamfer edges","level":2},{"id":"pick-the-edges-first","text":"Pick the edges first","level":3},{"id":"or-add-the-feature-first","text":"Or add the feature first","level":3},{"id":"settings","text":"Settings","level":3},{"id":"variable-fillets-and-chamfer-types","text":"Variable fillets and chamfer types","level":3},{"id":"shell-a-part","text":"Shell a part","level":2},{"id":"order-matters","text":"Order matters","level":2},{"id":"when-it-fails","text":"When it fails","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn this version\u003C/strong\u003E PartCraft builds fillets and chamfers with the mesh kernel, on \u003Cstrong\u003Estraight edges between two flat faces\u003C/strong\u003E only: box edges and the straight edges of extrudes and cuts, outside or inside corners (an inside corner gets material added). Round edges such as hole rims, and a radius that changes along the edge, are not built yet. Where two rounded edges meet at a corner the rounds are mitred (they meet in a sharp crease), not blended. \u003Cstrong\u003EShell\u003C/strong\u003E works too, built by the mesh kernel: it opens flat faces only, shells with different wall thicknesses are not built yet, and it is slow on parts with many round faces. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E rounds edges, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/span\u003E bevels them and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShell\u003C/span\u003E hollows the part. All three work on the solid&#039;s edges and faces and are built by the mesh kernel. Their rounds are faceted like every round face (\u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E sets how finely); the round faces are cylinders you can pick and measure (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fillet-or-chamfer-edges\"\u003EFillet or chamfer edges\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pick-the-edges-first\"\u003EPick the edges first\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an edge of the part. The whole edge highlights, and the right panel says \u003Cstrong\u003EEdge\u003C/strong\u003E: its kind (Straight, Circle or arc, Curve), length and the faces it lies between.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo add more edges, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click them. Each one is marked; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click a marked edge to drop it. The panel heading counts them (&quot;4 edges&quot;) and the status bar says &quot;4 edges picked. Fillet or Chamfer rounds them; Shift+click picks more, Esc clears.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or the same buttons in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe feature is added with all the picked edges: a 2 mm round, or a 1 mm chamfer. The status bar says, for example, &quot;Fillet1 added (4 edges). Change its size on the right; Pick edges changes which.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"or-add-the-feature-first\"\u003EOr add the feature first\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith nothing picked, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPartCraft goes into picking: the right panel heading says &quot;Fillet1: picking&quot;, the part shows as it was before the fillet (the panel says so), and the toolbar shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each edge to add it; click it again to drop it. The status bar counts them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone picking\u003C/span\u003E in the panel), or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFillet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESize\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERadius (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E, default 2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDistance (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E back from the edge on both faces, default 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdges\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list below names each edge by the two faces it joins, such as &quot;Edge 1: Extrude1 end face × Extrude1 side&quot;. \u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E drops one. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick edges\u003C/span\u003E goes back to picking. Double-clicking the feature in the history does the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEdges are remembered by the two faces they join, so they stay on the right edge when you change a dimension earlier in the history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"variable-fillets-and-chamfer-types\"\u003EVariable fillets and chamfer types\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVariable radius\u003C/strong\u003E (fillet): not built yet in this version (&quot;A fillet whose radius changes along the edge: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel). Give it one radius.&quot;). With an exact kernel: tick it and set \u003Cstrong\u003EEnd radius (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E. The radius changes linearly from \u003Cstrong\u003ERadius\u003C/strong\u003E at one end of each edge to \u003Cstrong\u003EEnd radius\u003C/strong\u003E at the other; \u003Cstrong\u003ESwap the ends\u003C/strong\u003E turns it round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E (chamfer): \u003Cstrong\u003EEqual distance\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETwo distances\u003C/strong\u003E (a \u003Cstrong\u003ESecond distance\u003C/strong\u003E on the other face) or \u003Cstrong\u003EDistance and angle\u003C/strong\u003E (the distance on one face, the bevel at \u003Cstrong\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/strong\u003E to that face). \u003Cstrong\u003EFlip sides\u003C/strong\u003E puts the first distance on the other face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shell-a-part\"\u003EShell a part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the flat face to leave open, for example the top of a box. Or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click several faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShell\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShell, opening this face\u003C/span\u003E in the face&#039;s panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe part becomes a 2 mm wall with the picked faces open. Settings:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWall (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe wall thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWalls grow outward (the faces stay inside)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETicked: the walls are added outside, so the inside keeps the part&#039;s size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff: the walls go inward and the outside keeps its size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpen faces\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many faces are removed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInward, the walls are exactly the wall thickness: outside corners stay sharp, inside corners come out rounded (radius = the wall), and a hole through the part gets a tube of wall round it. Outward, the walls are added outside the original surface, which becomes the hollow, and the outer edges come out rounded. The inner faces are named after the faces they offset, so you can pick them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly flat faces can be opened: picking a curved one says &quot;Shell: in this version it opens flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).&quot; A shell is slow on parts with many round faces (several seconds per thousand triangles): use a coarser \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E setting while you design.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick faces\u003C/span\u003E changes which faces are open, the same way as picking edges. The list names each one, such as &quot;Face 1: Extrude1 end face&quot;. A shell with no open faces is not allowed: &quot;Pick the faces to remove (open), then shell.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"order-matters\"\u003EOrder matters\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFeatures build top to bottom. A fillet before a shell is shelled with the part, so the inside gets rounded corners too. A fillet after a shell rounds only the edges you pick. Move features with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove up\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove down\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/edit-history\" data-topic=\"partcraft/edit-history\"\u003EChange a part through its history\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPatterns and mirrors cannot copy a fillet, chamfer or shell: &quot;Fillet1 changes the body&#039;s edges or walls; it cannot be copied. Copy the features that make solids (extrudes, revolves, holes, imports).&quot; Pattern the holes or cuts first, then fillet all the copies&#039; edges in one fillet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-it-fails\"\u003EWhen it fails\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A 5 mm round does not fit here (it is too big for a face next to one of the edges, or the edges meet in a corner the kernel cannot blend).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a smaller radius, or fillet fewer edges at a time (several fillet features, smallest last)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;An edge it rounds is gone: the edge between Extrude1 and Cut2.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn earlier change removed that edge. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick edges\u003C/span\u003E, remove the red edge and pick the new one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The shell could not be made with this thickness (…).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe wall is thicker than a narrow part of the model, or than a fillet&#039;s radius. Make the wall thinner, or the fillets larger than the wall\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;That face has no name the history can follow; pick another.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick a face made by a feature (most faces are)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Fillet: in this version only straight edges between two flat faces (the mesh kernel builds them). Round or bevel curved edges in the sketch.&quot; (also Chamfer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picked edge is round, such as a hole rim, or lies on a curved face. Remove it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick edges\u003C/span\u003E, and round or bevel that corner in the sketch instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A fillet whose radius changes along the edge: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel). Give it one radius.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUntick \u003Cstrong\u003EVariable radius\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Fillet by the mesh kernel: where rounded edges meet they are mitred, not blended.&quot; (⚠ in the history)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA warning, not an error: the part is built. Picked edges meet at a corner, and the rounds meet there in a sharp crease. Fillet fewer edges, or accept the crease\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Shell: in this version it opens flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn open face is curved. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick faces\u003C/span\u003E and remove it; open only flat faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn this version fillets and chamfers work only on straight edges between two flat faces, with one radius, and rounds that meet at a corner are mitred. Shells open flat faces only. With an exact kernel, a variable fillet changes linearly along each edge (one law per feature; no radius set at points along it). Face fillets, full-round fillets and shells with different wall thicknesses are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"In this version PartCraft builds fillets and chamfers with the mesh kernel, on straight edges between two flat faces only: box edges and the straight edges of extrudes and cuts, outside or inside corners (an inside corner gets material added). Round edges such as hole rims, and a radius that changes along the edge, are not built yet. Where two rounded edges meet at a corner the rounds are mitred (they meet in a sharp crease), not blended. Shell works too, built by the mesh kernel: it opens flat faces only, shells with different wall thicknesses are not built yet, and it is slow on parts with many round faces. See What is not built yet. Fillet rounds edges, Chamfer bevels them and Shell hollows the part. All three work on the solid's edges and faces and are built by the mesh kernel. Their rounds are faceted like every round face (Curves sets how finely); the round faces are cylinders you can pick and measure (see The mesh kernel). Fillet or chamfer edges Pick the edges first Click an edge of the part. The whole edge highlights, and the right panel says Edge: its kind (Straight, Circle or arc, Curve), length and the faces it lies between. To add more edges, Shift+click them. Each one is marked; Shift+click a marked edge to drop it. The panel heading counts them (\"4 edges\") and the status bar says \"4 edges picked. Fillet or Chamfer rounds them; Shift+click picks more, Esc clears.\" Choose Fillet or Chamfer in the toolbar, or the same buttons in the right panel. The feature is added with all the picked edges: a 2 mm round, or a 1 mm chamfer. The status bar says, for example, \"Fillet1 added (4 edges). Change its size on the right; Pick edges changes which.\" Or add the feature first With nothing picked, choose Fillet or Chamfer. PartCraft goes into picking: the right panel heading says \"Fillet1: picking\", the part shows as it was before the fillet (the panel says so), and the toolbar shows Done. Click each edge to add it; click it again to drop it. The status bar counts them. Choose Done (or Done picking in the panel), or press Esc or Enter. Settings Setting Fillet Chamfer Size Radius (mm), default 2 Distance (mm) back from the edge on both faces, default 1 Edges How many How many The list below names each edge by the two faces it joins, such as \"Edge 1: Extrude1 end face × Extrude1 side\". Remove drops one. Pick edges goes back to picking. Double-clicking the feature in the history does the same. Edges are remembered by the two faces they join, so they stay on the right edge when you change a dimension earlier in the history. Variable fillets and chamfer types Variable radius (fillet): not built yet in this version (\"A fillet whose radius changes along the edge: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel). Give it one radius.\"). With an exact kernel: tick it and set End radius (mm). The radius changes linearly from Radius at one end of each edge to End radius at the other; Swap the ends turns it round. Type (chamfer): Equal distance, Two distances (a Second distance on the other face) or Distance and angle (the distance on one face, the bevel at Angle (°) to that face). Flip sides puts the first distance on the other face. Shell a part Click the flat face to leave open, for example the top of a box. Or Shift+click several faces. Choose Shell in the toolbar, or Shell, opening this face in the face's panel. The part becomes a 2 mm wall with the picked faces open. Settings: Setting What it does Default Wall (mm) The wall thickness 2 Walls grow outward (the faces stay inside) Ticked: the walls are added outside, so the inside keeps the part's size Off: the walls go inward and the outside keeps its size Open faces How many faces are removed Inward, the walls are exactly the wall thickness: outside corners stay sharp, inside corners come out rounded (radius = the wall), and a hole through the part gets a tube of wall round it. Outward, the walls are added outside the original surface, which becomes the hollow, and the outer edges come out rounded. The inner faces are named after the faces they offset, so you can pick them. Only flat faces can be opened: picking a curved one says \"Shell: in this version it opens flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).\" A shell is slow on parts with many round faces (several seconds per thousand triangles): use a coarser Curves setting while you design. Pick faces changes which faces are open, the same way as picking edges. The list names each one, such as \"Face 1: Extrude1 end face\". A shell with no open faces is not allowed: \"Pick the faces to remove (open), then shell.\" Order matters Features build top to bottom. A fillet before a shell is shelled with the part, so the inside gets rounded corners too. A fillet after a shell rounds only the edges you pick. Move features with Move up and Move down (see Change a part through its history). Patterns and mirrors cannot copy a fillet, chamfer or shell: \"Fillet1 changes the body's edges or walls; it cannot be copied. Copy the features that make solids (extrudes, revolves, holes, imports).\" Pattern the holes or cuts first, then fillet all the copies' edges in one fillet. When it fails Message What to do \"A 5 mm round does not fit here (it is too big for a face next to one of the edges, or the edges meet in a corner the kernel cannot blend).\" Use a smaller radius, or fillet fewer edges at a time (several fillet features, smallest last) \"An edge it rounds is gone: the edge between Extrude1 and Cut2.\" An earlier change removed that edge. Choose Pick edges, remove the red edge and pick the new one \"The shell could not be made with this thickness (…).\" The wall is thicker than a narrow part of the model, or than a fillet's radius. Make the wall thinner, or the fillets larger than the wall \"That face has no name the history can follow; pick another.\" Pick a face made by a feature (most faces are) \"Fillet: in this version only straight edges between two flat faces (the mesh kernel builds them). Round or bevel curved edges in the sketch.\" (also Chamfer) A picked edge is round, such as a hole rim, or lies on a curved face. Remove it with Pick edges, and round or bevel that corner in the sketch instead \"A fillet whose radius changes along the edge: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel). Give it one radius.\" Untick Variable radius \"Fillet by the mesh kernel: where rounded edges meet they are mitred, not blended.\" (⚠ in the history) A warning, not an error: the part is built. Picked edges meet at a corner, and the rounds meet there in a sharp crease. Fillet fewer edges, or accept the crease \"Shell: in this version it opens flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).\" An open face is curved. Pick faces and remove it; open only flat faces Limits In this version fillets and chamfers work only on straight edges between two flat faces, with one radius, and rounds that meet at a corner are mitred. Shells open flat faces only. With an exact kernel, a variable fillet changes linearly along each edge (one law per feature; no radius set at points along it). Face fillets, full-round fillets and shells with different wall thicknesses are not built yet."},{"slug":"import-step","scope":"partcraft","title":"Open a STEP file and build on it","summary":"Bring a supplier's or another CAD program's STEP solid into PartCraft, as a new part or added to the current one, set which way is up, and add sketches and features to it.","section":"tasks","order":87,"keywords":["step","stp","import","open step","ap203","ap214","ap242","supplier model","dumb solid","imported solid","up axis","z up","y up"],"headings":[{"id":"open-a-step-file","text":"Open a STEP file","level":2},{"id":"set-which-way-is-up","text":"Set which way is up","level":2},{"id":"build-on-it","text":"Build on it","level":2},{"id":"where-it-is-kept","text":"Where it is kept","level":2},{"id":"messages","text":"Messages","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot built yet in this version.\u003C/strong\u003E Opening STEP files needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes it as it works with an exact kernel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft opens STEP files (\u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.stp\u003C/code\u003E) as \u003Cstrong\u003Eimported solids\u003C/strong\u003E: an exact solid you can sketch on, cut, drill, fillet and combine with your own features. It has no history of its own: you cannot change the dimensions it was made with, only add to it or cut from it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESTEP import needs the exact kernel. If it is not ready yet, PartCraft waits for it (&quot;Loading the exact kernel to read STEP…&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-a-step-file\"\u003EOpen a STEP file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and pick the \u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.stp\u003C/code\u003E file. Or drop it on the 3D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the part on screen is empty, the STEP solid becomes a new part named after the file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the part already has features, PartCraft asks: &quot;Import bracket.step: 1 solid, 64 faces. Add it to Part1, or start a new part with it?&quot; Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd to this part\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew part\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003EImported1\u003C/strong\u003E feature appears in the history and the status bar says, for example, &quot;Imported bracket.step: 1 solid, 64 faces, 15200.512 mm³. Sketch on its flat faces and add features; it is kept inside the part file.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-which-way-is-up\"\u003ESet which way is up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost CAD programs use Z up; PartCraft uses Y up. By default PartCraft turns the file so its Z points up here.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the part arrives lying on its side, select \u003Cstrong\u003EImported1\u003C/strong\u003E and set \u003Cstrong\u003EUp in the file\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003EY up\u003C/strong\u003E. The panel also shows the file name and how much STEP text is stored.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"build-on-it\"\u003EBuild on it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a flat face of the imported solid and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch on this face\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd cuts, holes, bosses, fillets, chamfers or a shell as on any part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircular\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMirror\u003C/span\u003E with the imported solid as a feature to copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck its size in the \u003Cstrong\u003EPart\u003C/strong\u003E box: volume, surface area, size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-it-is-kept\"\u003EWhere it is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe STEP text is stored \u003Cstrong\u003Einside\u003C/strong\u003E the \u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E file, so the part file is self-contained: you do not need the STEP file again. The part file gets bigger by the size of the STEP file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"messages\"\u003EMessages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&#039;&quot;part.stp&quot; is not a STEP file (it does not start with ISO-10303-21).&#039;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is not plain STEP text. Ask the sender for an AP203, AP214 or AP242 STEP file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;That STEP file is too big (over 60 MB) to import here.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESimplify the model at the source, or send parts one by one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This STEP file could not be read (fail).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is damaged or uses parts of STEP the kernel cannot read\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This STEP file has no closed solid (only surfaces or wires), so PartCraft cannot use it as a body.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model is surfaces only. Ask for a solid\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;STEP needs the exact kernel (OpenCascade), which did not load: …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe imported solid keeps its shape exactly, but has no editable features. Changing a hole size in it means cutting or filling it with your own features.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EColors, names of bodies, assemblies and PMI (tolerances in the file) are not read. An assembly STEP file comes in as all its solids in one imported feature (the \u003Cstrong\u003EBodies\u003C/strong\u003E line of the Part box counts them), not as separate parts. A closed surface model is sewn into a solid where it can be.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESTEP is the only other format PartCraft opens. STL, IGES and other programs&#039; native files are not supported.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet in this version. Opening STEP files needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. What is not built yet says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes it as it works with an exact kernel. PartCraft opens STEP files (.step or .stp) as imported solids: an exact solid you can sketch on, cut, drill, fillet and combine with your own features. It has no history of its own: you cannot change the dimensions it was made with, only add to it or cut from it. STEP import needs the exact kernel. If it is not ready yet, PartCraft waits for it (\"Loading the exact kernel to read STEP…\"). Open a STEP file Choose Open or press Ctrl+O, and pick the .step or .stp file. Or drop it on the 3D view. If the part on screen is empty, the STEP solid becomes a new part named after the file. If the part already has features, PartCraft asks: \"Import bracket.step: 1 solid, 64 faces. Add it to Part1, or start a new part with it?\" Choose Add to this part, New part or Cancel. An Imported1 feature appears in the history and the status bar says, for example, \"Imported bracket.step: 1 solid, 64 faces, 15200.512 mm³. Sketch on its flat faces and add features; it is kept inside the part file.\" Set which way is up Most CAD programs use Z up; PartCraft uses Y up. By default PartCraft turns the file so its Z points up here. If the part arrives lying on its side, select Imported1 and set Up in the file to Y up. The panel also shows the file name and how much STEP text is stored. Build on it Click a flat face of the imported solid and choose Sketch on this face. Add cuts, holes, bosses, fillets, chamfers or a shell as on any part. Use Linear, Circular and Mirror with the imported solid as a feature to copy. Check its size in the Part box: volume, surface area, size. Where it is kept The STEP text is stored inside the .partcraft.json file, so the part file is self-contained: you do not need the STEP file again. The part file gets bigger by the size of the STEP file. Messages Message What it means '\"part.stp\" is not a STEP file (it does not start with ISO-10303-21).' The file is not plain STEP text. Ask the sender for an AP203, AP214 or AP242 STEP file \"That STEP file is too big (over 60 MB) to import here.\" Simplify the model at the source, or send parts one by one \"This STEP file could not be read (fail).\" The file is damaged or uses parts of STEP the kernel cannot read \"This STEP file has no closed solid (only surfaces or wires), so PartCraft cannot use it as a body.\" The model is surfaces only. Ask for a solid \"STEP needs the exact kernel (OpenCascade), which did not load: …\" See The mesh kernel Limits The imported solid keeps its shape exactly, but has no editable features. Changing a hole size in it means cutting or filling it with your own features. Colors, names of bodies, assemblies and PMI (tolerances in the file) are not read. An assembly STEP file comes in as all its solids in one imported feature (the Bodies line of the Part box counts them), not as separate parts. A closed surface model is sewn into a solid where it can be. STEP is the only other format PartCraft opens. STL, IGES and other programs' native files are not supported."},{"slug":"solidworks-exchange","scope":"partcraft","title":"Move parts to and from other part modelers","summary":"Send a part to another part modeler as a macro that rebuilds it with a live feature tree. The named STEP and opening the feature list another modeler wrote out are not built yet in this version.","section":"tasks","order":88,"keywords":["macro","not built","swb","vba","feature tree","round trip","export macro","cadcraft-sw.json","named step","ap214","global variables","equations","native part"],"headings":[{"id":"send-a-part-as-a-macro","text":"Send a part as a macro","level":2},{"id":"what-is-left-out","text":"What is left out","level":3},{"id":"send-a-named-step","text":"Send a named STEP","level":2},{"id":"bring-a-part-back","text":"Bring a part back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft exchanges parts with SolidWorks both ways, without a file format nobody else can read:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOut:\u003C/strong\u003E a macro (\u003Ccode\u003E.swb\u003C/code\u003E, plain text) that SolidWorks runs to build the part itself, feature by feature, with its sketches, relations, dimensions, global variables and equations. You get a native part with a live feature tree, not a dead solid.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBack:\u003C/strong\u003E a macro you run inside SolidWorks writes the part&#039;s features and equations to a \u003Ccode\u003E.cadcraft-sw.json\u003C/code\u003E file. Opening that file in PartCraft is not built yet in this version (it needs an exact kernel).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENone of this has been tested in SolidWorks yet. SolidWorks runs only on Windows with a licence, and PartCraft has never run these macros in it. The macros were written from the SolidWorks API documentation and checked for structure here. Expect to report problems; the log the macro writes says which step failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft cannot write a \u003Ccode\u003E.sldprt\u003C/code\u003E file: that format is closed. The macro is the way to get a native part.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-part-as-a-macro\"\u003ESend a part as a macro\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMacro for SolidWorks (.swb)\u003C/span\u003E. PartCraft downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;part&gt;.swb\u003C/code\u003E; the status bar says it was &quot;written without the exact kernel&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA dialog says how many features, global variables and equations the macro builds, and lists anything it leaves out. No STEP goes beside it in this version: use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E if you need one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn SolidWorks, choose \u003Cstrong\u003ETools &gt; Macro &gt; Run\u003C/strong\u003E, pick the \u003Ccode\u003E.swb\u003C/code\u003E file and wait. It makes a new part from your default part template, sets millimetres and builds every feature.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt the end a message says how many steps were done, how many failed and how many matched PartCraft&#039;s body. The full log, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;part&gt; macro log.txt\u003C/code\u003E, is written next to the macro.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat the macro builds:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPartCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the new part\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch on the Front, Top or Right plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA sketch on that plane, with its lines, arcs, circles, points, relations and dimensions (named as in PartCraft, such as \u003Ccode\u003Ek7\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch on a face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA sketch on the same face, picked by a point on it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch on a fixed plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA reference plane through three points, and the sketch on it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude, cut (blind, through all, mid-plane, two directions, taper)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoss-extrude or cut-extrude\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERevolve, revolved cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERevolve about the same centerline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHole (simple, counterbore, countersink, tapped)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne or two cuts with their own sketches (a tapped hole is its tap drill; no thread)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear and circular pattern, mirror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear pattern, circular pattern, mirror (features or the body)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet, chamfer, shell\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet, chamfer and shell on the same edges and faces, picked by points\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParameters and equations\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGlobal variables and equations on the renamed dimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESuppressed features, the rollback bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESuppressed the same way; the rollback bar in the same place\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter each solid feature the macro compares the body&#039;s volume and size with PartCraft&#039;s. When they differ, it turns that feature&#039;s direction round once and checks again; if that is no better it turns it back and writes &quot;Check&quot; in the log.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-is-left-out\"\u003EWhat is left out\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog and the top of the macro list everything not carried over, with the reason. Today that is: imported solids (insert the STEP instead), sweeps, lofts, drafts, reference axes and points, &quot;up to&quot; end conditions, extruding only some contours, and anything that depends on them. Splines become curves through their points. This version has no exact kernel, so sketches on faces and the features that pick edges or faces are left out too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-named-step\"\u003ESend a named STEP\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot built yet in this version: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP for SolidWorks (named, AP214)\u003C/span\u003E needs an exact kernel, and choosing it says &quot;STEP for SolidWorks: not built yet. …&quot;. With an exact kernel, the file carries the part&#039;s name, millimetres and the part colour, and stands Y up, the way SolidWorks shows parts (the plain \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP214 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E export stands Z up). Letters with accents lose them in the name, and other characters outside plain ASCII become \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bring-a-part-back\"\u003EBring a part back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpening the file in PartCraft (step 3) is not built yet in this version: it says &quot;Opening a SolidWorks export: not built yet. …&quot;. The CadCraft drafting app still opens the file and takes its sketches.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E &gt; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E &gt; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport macro to bring parts back (.swb)\u003C/span\u003E to download \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraftExport.swb\u003C/code\u003E, and put it next to your parts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn SolidWorks, open the part, choose \u003Cstrong\u003ETools &gt; Macro &gt; Run\u003C/strong\u003E and pick \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraftExport.swb\u003C/code\u003E. It writes \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;part&gt;.cadcraft-sw.json\u003C/code\u003E next to the part and changes nothing in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn PartCraft, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E and pick that file, or drop it on the 3D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith an exact kernel, PartCraft rebuilds the part feature by feature and shows what it did: for example &quot;5 of 5 features carried over from SolidWorks (Flange.SLDPRT)&quot;, each feature it could not carry (&quot;Not carried over: Sweep1 (Sweep).&quot;) and whether the volume matches the one SolidWorks wrote.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it as a PartCraft part with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESketches land on a base plane when they sit on one, on the matching flat face of the part when there is one (so they follow later edits), and on a fixed plane otherwise. Edges and faces of fillets, chamfers and shells are found from points and kept as named references. Global variables become parameters (names with spaces get underscores: &quot;Bolt count&quot; becomes \u003Ccode\u003EBolt_count\u003C/code\u003E) and equations become equations on the same dimensions. Nothing is guessed: what has no counterpart is listed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe CadCraft drafting app opens the same file and takes its sketches.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe format is \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft-sw-export\u003C/code\u003E version 1, plain JSON in millimetres and degrees. A file from a newer macro, or one that is not valid, is refused with the reason.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"PartCraft exchanges parts with SolidWorks both ways, without a file format nobody else can read: Out: a macro (.swb, plain text) that SolidWorks runs to build the part itself, feature by feature, with its sketches, relations, dimensions, global variables and equations. You get a native part with a live feature tree, not a dead solid. Back: a macro you run inside SolidWorks writes the part's features and equations to a .cadcraft-sw.json file. Opening that file in PartCraft is not built yet in this version (it needs an exact kernel). None of this has been tested in SolidWorks yet. SolidWorks runs only on Windows with a licence, and PartCraft has never run these macros in it. The macros were written from the SolidWorks API documentation and checked for structure here. Expect to report problems; the log the macro writes says which step failed. PartCraft cannot write a .sldprt file: that format is closed. The macro is the way to get a native part. Send a part as a macro Open the part. Choose Export, then Macro for SolidWorks (.swb). PartCraft downloads \u003Cpart\u003E.swb; the status bar says it was \"written without the exact kernel\". A dialog says how many features, global variables and equations the macro builds, and lists anything it leaves out. No STEP goes beside it in this version: use Export, then STEP (faceted, mesh kernel) if you need one. In SolidWorks, choose Tools \u003E Macro \u003E Run, pick the .swb file and wait. It makes a new part from your default part template, sets millimetres and builds every feature. At the end a message says how many steps were done, how many failed and how many matched PartCraft's body. The full log, \u003Cpart\u003E macro log.txt, is written next to the macro. What the macro builds: PartCraft In the new part Sketch on the Front, Top or Right plane A sketch on that plane, with its lines, arcs, circles, points, relations and dimensions (named as in PartCraft, such as k7) Sketch on a face A sketch on the same face, picked by a point on it Sketch on a fixed plane A reference plane through three points, and the sketch on it Extrude, cut (blind, through all, mid-plane, two directions, taper) Boss-extrude or cut-extrude Revolve, revolved cut Revolve about the same centerline Hole (simple, counterbore, countersink, tapped) One or two cuts with their own sketches (a tapped hole is its tap drill; no thread) Linear and circular pattern, mirror Linear pattern, circular pattern, mirror (features or the body) Fillet, chamfer, shell Fillet, chamfer and shell on the same edges and faces, picked by points Parameters and equations Global variables and equations on the renamed dimensions Suppressed features, the rollback bar Suppressed the same way; the rollback bar in the same place After each solid feature the macro compares the body's volume and size with PartCraft's. When they differ, it turns that feature's direction round once and checks again; if that is no better it turns it back and writes \"Check\" in the log. What is left out The dialog and the top of the macro list everything not carried over, with the reason. Today that is: imported solids (insert the STEP instead), sweeps, lofts, drafts, reference axes and points, \"up to\" end conditions, extruding only some contours, and anything that depends on them. Splines become curves through their points. This version has no exact kernel, so sketches on faces and the features that pick edges or faces are left out too. Send a named STEP Not built yet in this version: STEP for SolidWorks (named, AP214) needs an exact kernel, and choosing it says \"STEP for SolidWorks: not built yet. …\". With an exact kernel, the file carries the part's name, millimetres and the part colour, and stands Y up, the way SolidWorks shows parts (the plain STEP AP214 (exact solid) export stands Z up). Letters with accents lose them in the name, and other characters outside plain ASCII become _. Bring a part back Opening the file in PartCraft (step 3) is not built yet in this version: it says \"Opening a SolidWorks export: not built yet. …\". The CadCraft drafting app still opens the file and takes its sketches. Choose File \u003E Export \u003E Export macro to bring parts back (.swb) to download CadCraftExport.swb, and put it next to your parts. In SolidWorks, open the part, choose Tools \u003E Macro \u003E Run and pick CadCraftExport.swb. It writes \u003Cpart\u003E.cadcraft-sw.json next to the part and changes nothing in it. In PartCraft, choose Open and pick that file, or drop it on the 3D view. With an exact kernel, PartCraft rebuilds the part feature by feature and shows what it did: for example \"5 of 5 features carried over from SolidWorks (Flange.SLDPRT)\", each feature it could not carry (\"Not carried over: Sweep1 (Sweep).\") and whether the volume matches the one SolidWorks wrote. Save it as a PartCraft part with Save. Sketches land on a base plane when they sit on one, on the matching flat face of the part when there is one (so they follow later edits), and on a fixed plane otherwise. Edges and faces of fillets, chamfers and shells are found from points and kept as named references. Global variables become parameters (names with spaces get underscores: \"Bolt count\" becomes Bolt_count) and equations become equations on the same dimensions. Nothing is guessed: what has no counterpart is listed. The CadCraft drafting app opens the same file and takes its sketches. The format is cadcraft-sw-export version 1, plain JSON in millimetres and degrees. A file from a newer macro, or one that is not valid, is refused with the reason."},{"slug":"edit-history","scope":"partcraft","title":"Change a part through its history","summary":"Edit features and sketches after the fact, suppress, roll back, insert features earlier, reorder, rename and delete, and undo any of it.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["history","feature tree","design tree","edit feature","edit sketch","suppress","unsuppress","rollback","roll back","roll forward","reorder","move feature","delete","rename","undo","redo","rebuild","regenerate","parent child"],"headings":[{"id":"edit-a-feature","text":"Edit a feature","level":2},{"id":"change-which-edges-or-faces-a-fillet-chamfer-or-shell-uses","text":"Change which edges or faces a fillet, chamfer or shell uses","level":2},{"id":"edit-a-sketch","text":"Edit a sketch","level":2},{"id":"when-a-feature-fails","text":"When a feature fails","level":2},{"id":"suppress-a-feature","text":"Suppress a feature","level":2},{"id":"roll-back","text":"Roll back","level":2},{"id":"reorder-features","text":"Reorder features","level":2},{"id":"delete-a-feature","text":"Delete a feature","level":2},{"id":"rename","text":"Rename","level":2},{"id":"undo-and-redo","text":"Undo and redo","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA PartCraft part is its history: the list of planes, sketches and features on the left. PartCraft rebuilds the solid from the top of that list every time something changes. Changing a number early in the list changes everything built on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-a-feature\"\u003EEdit a feature\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the feature in the history (or the sketch indented under it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange its settings in the right panel. Each change rebuilds at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no OK or Cancel: every change is applied. If you do not like it, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-which-edges-or-faces-a-fillet-chamfer-or-shell-uses\"\u003EChange which edges or faces a fillet, chamfer or shell uses\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDouble-click the feature in the history, or select it and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick edges\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick faces\u003C/span\u003E). Click edges or faces to add or drop them, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\" data-topic=\"partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\"\u003ERound, bevel and hollow\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-a-sketch\"\u003EEdit a sketch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click the sketch in the history, or select it and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit sketch\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe part goes back to how it was when the sketch was made: features after it are hidden while you edit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange the geometry, relations or dimensions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose the sketch. The whole part rebuilds with the change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change only a size, you do not need to open the sketch. Select the feature or the sketch and change the number under \u003Cstrong\u003ESketch dimensions\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003EDriving dimensions\u003C/strong\u003E) in the right panel. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/change-sizes\" data-topic=\"partcraft/change-sizes\"\u003EChange sizes without opening the sketch\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-feature-fails\"\u003EWhen a feature fails\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a change makes a feature impossible, PartCraft builds everything else and marks the problem:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe feature shows \u003Cstrong\u003E⚠ error\u003C/strong\u003E in the history, and its message is at the top of its right panel (hover the row to read it too);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Efeatures that need it show \u003Cstrong\u003E⚠ not built\u003C/strong\u003E, with &quot;Needs Extrude2, which is failing.&quot;;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe status bar chip says &quot;1 feature failing&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFix the cause (or undo), and the part builds again. Common messages and what to do are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/troubleshooting\" data-topic=\"partcraft/troubleshooting\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"suppress-a-feature\"\u003ESuppress a feature\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESuppressing switches a feature off without deleting it: useful to try a part without a hole or a pattern.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the feature.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESuppress\u003C/span\u003E. Its row shows \u003Cstrong\u003Eoff\u003C/strong\u003E. Features that need it are not built either.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnsuppress\u003C/span\u003E to switch it back on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"roll-back\"\u003ERoll back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERolling back builds the part only up to a point, so you can see it as it was, or add a feature earlier in the history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a feature and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoll back to here\u003C/span\u003E. That feature and every one below it are grayed and not built. A \u003Cstrong\u003ERollback bar · roll forward\u003C/strong\u003E row marks the place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAny feature you add now goes in \u003Cstrong\u003Eat the bar\u003C/strong\u003E, before the grayed ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003ERollback bar · roll forward\u003C/strong\u003E to build everything again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rollback position is saved in the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reorder-features\"\u003EReorder features\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the feature.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove up\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove down\u003C/span\u003E, or, with its history row focused, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↑\"\u003E⌥↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↓\"\u003E⌥↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA feature can never move above something it uses. The button is grayed, and hovering it (or trying the key) gives the reason, for example &quot;Extrude2 uses Sketch2, so Sketch2 has to come first.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat a feature uses: its sketch; the face its sketch sits on; the features a pattern or mirror copies; a sketch line or revolve used as a direction or axis.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delete-a-feature\"\u003EDelete a feature\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the feature and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel or the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf other features use it, PartCraft asks: &quot;Extrude3, Hole1 use Extrude1 and will be deleted too.&quot; Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete all\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDeleting a feature does not delete its sketch: the sketch reappears in the view as an unused sketch, ready for another feature. Delete the sketch separately if you do not need it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rename\"\u003ERename\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the feature and type a new \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E at the bottom of the right panel (up to 60 characters). The part&#039;s own name is in the right panel when nothing is selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-and-redo\"\u003EUndo and redo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E undoes the last change: a feature, a setting, a sketch edit, a drag. The status bar says what, for example &quot;Undid change dimension.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E redoes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPartCraft keeps the last 200 steps. Changes on the drawing sheet (a section, a moved GD&amp;T symbol) are in the same list. Opening a file or starting a new part clears it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac, use ⇧⌘Z to redo. In Safari, ⌘Y may open the browser&#039;s history instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E","text":"A PartCraft part is its history: the list of planes, sketches and features on the left. PartCraft rebuilds the solid from the top of that list every time something changes. Changing a number early in the list changes everything built on it. Edit a feature Click the feature in the history (or the sketch indented under it). Change its settings in the right panel. Each change rebuilds at once. There is no OK or Cancel: every change is applied. If you do not like it, press Ctrl+Z. Change which edges or faces a fillet, chamfer or shell uses Double-click the feature in the history, or select it and choose Pick edges (or Pick faces). Click edges or faces to add or drop them, then Done or Esc. See Round, bevel and hollow. Edit a sketch Double-click the sketch in the history, or select it and choose Edit sketch in the right panel (or Sketch in the toolbar). The part goes back to how it was when the sketch was made: features after it are hidden while you edit. Change the geometry, relations or dimensions. Close the sketch. The whole part rebuilds with the change. To change only a size, you do not need to open the sketch. Select the feature or the sketch and change the number under Sketch dimensions (or Driving dimensions) in the right panel. See Change sizes without opening the sketch. When a feature fails If a change makes a feature impossible, PartCraft builds everything else and marks the problem: the feature shows ⚠ error in the history, and its message is at the top of its right panel (hover the row to read it too); features that need it show ⚠ not built, with \"Needs Extrude2, which is failing.\"; the status bar chip says \"1 feature failing\". Fix the cause (or undo), and the part builds again. Common messages and what to do are in Troubleshooting. Suppress a feature Suppressing switches a feature off without deleting it: useful to try a part without a hole or a pattern. Select the feature. Choose Suppress. Its row shows off. Features that need it are not built either. Choose Unsuppress to switch it back on. Roll back Rolling back builds the part only up to a point, so you can see it as it was, or add a feature earlier in the history. Select a feature and choose Roll back to here. That feature and every one below it are grayed and not built. A Rollback bar · roll forward row marks the place. Any feature you add now goes in at the bar, before the grayed ones. Click Rollback bar · roll forward to build everything again. The rollback position is saved in the file. Reorder features Select the feature. Choose Move up or Move down, or, with its history row focused, press Alt+↑ or Alt+↓. A feature can never move above something it uses. The button is grayed, and hovering it (or trying the key) gives the reason, for example \"Extrude2 uses Sketch2, so Sketch2 has to come first.\" What a feature uses: its sketch; the face its sketch sits on; the features a pattern or mirror copies; a sketch line or revolve used as a direction or axis. Delete a feature Select the feature and choose Delete in the right panel or the toolbar, or press Delete. If other features use it, PartCraft asks: \"Extrude3, Hole1 use Extrude1 and will be deleted too.\" Choose Delete all or Cancel. Deleting a feature does not delete its sketch: the sketch reappears in the view as an unused sketch, ready for another feature. Delete the sketch separately if you do not need it. Rename Select the feature and type a new Name at the bottom of the right panel (up to 60 characters). The part's own name is in the right panel when nothing is selected. Undo and redo Ctrl+Z or Undo undoes the last change: a feature, a setting, a sketch edit, a drag. The status bar says what, for example \"Undid change dimension.\" Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+Shift+Z or Redo redoes it. PartCraft keeps the last 200 steps. Changes on the drawing sheet (a section, a moved GD&T symbol) are in the same list. Opening a file or starting a new part clears it. On a Mac, use ⇧⌘Z to redo. In Safari, ⌘Y may open the browser's history instead."},{"slug":"form-surfaces","scope":"partcraft","title":"Freeform shapes with Form, and surface features","summary":"Push and pull a smooth control cage in the Form workspace, crease edges, use symmetry, check and finish it into an exact solid or surface, then patch, loft, stitch, thicken, offset, extend and trim surfaces in Design. What is exact and what is approximated.","section":"tasks","order":92,"keywords":["form","freeform","t-spline","subdivision","catmull-clark","cage","quadball","crease","symmetry","zebra","curvature","surface","patch","fill","loft surface","stitch","sew","thicken","offset surface","extend","trim"],"headings":[{"id":"start-a-cage","text":"Start a cage","level":2},{"id":"pick-and-move","text":"Pick and move","level":2},{"id":"change-the-cage","text":"Change the cage","level":2},{"id":"sharp-edges-symmetry-and-display","text":"Sharp edges, symmetry and display","level":2},{"id":"check-and-finish","text":"Check and finish","level":2},{"id":"what-is-exact-and-what-is-approximated","text":"What is exact and what is approximated","level":2},{"id":"surface-features-in-design","text":"Surface features in Design","level":2}],"html":"\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot built yet in this version.\u003C/strong\u003E Finishing a form into the part, and the surface features, needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes them as they work with an exact kernel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EForm\u003C/strong\u003E workspace makes organic shapes. You edit a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecage\u003C/strong\u003E, a coarse mesh of corners, edges and faces. The shape is the smooth surface the cage controls (its Catmull–Clark limit surface), so moving one corner bends the surface gently around it. When it looks right, \u003Cstrong\u003EFinish\u003C/strong\u003E adds it to the part history as a \u003Cem\u003EForm\u003C/em\u003E feature, and the exact kernel turns it into true B-spline surfaces. You can then fillet it, cut it, join it to other features and export it as STEP.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EForm\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EWorkspace\u003C/strong\u003E list at the left of the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-a-cage\"\u003EStart a cage\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003ESize\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EDivisions\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel. Divisions is how many faces each side gets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBox\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuadball\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlane\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. The panel also has \u003Cstrong\u003ECylinder\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETorus\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EFaces by clicks\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Equadball\u003C/strong\u003E is a box cage fitted so its corner points lie on a sphere. Between those points the surface sits a little inside the sphere: about 0.4 % less volume with 2 divisions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Eplane\u003C/strong\u003E is open, so it becomes a surface body (see &quot;Surface features in Design&quot; below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFaces by clicks\u003C/strong\u003E: click 4 points on the top (ground) plane or on corners of the cage to make one face. Faces that share corners join up. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after 3 points for a triangle, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour unfinished cage is saved with the part file, so you can close the file and carry on later.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-and-move\"\u003EPick and move\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a corner, an edge or a face of the cage. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click adds or drops items. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E clears the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA gizmo appears at the middle of the selection. Drag an arrow to move along it, or a square to move in that plane. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to move, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to rotate and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to scale, or use the buttons in the panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGizmo\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar steps through them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESoft falloff\u003C/strong\u003E (mm) also pulls the corners within that distance, fading smoothly to nothing at the edge of that distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery drag is one undo step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Z\"\u003E⌃Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Y\"\u003E⌃Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E work as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-the-cage\"\u003EChange the cage\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EModify\u003C/strong\u003E buttons in the panel work on the selection:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPicked faces move out by \u003Cstrong\u003EExtrude distance\u003C/strong\u003E, along their normals, with new side faces. Picked open edges grow new faces outward.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdge loop\u003C/span\u003E / Insert edge loop\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESplits the ring of four-sided faces that crosses the picked edge, halfway along. More loops give tighter curves.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInsert point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdds a corner in the middle of the picked edge.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESubdivide\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESplits each picked face into four-sided faces around its middle.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBridge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoins two picked faces, or two openings (pick one open edge on each), with a tube of faces. Both need the same number of edges.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFill hole\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloses the opening through the picked open edge with one face.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeld\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMerges the picked corners into one, at their middle.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlatten\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoves the picked corners onto the plane that fits them best.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemoves the picked faces, edges (two faces merge) or corners.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sharp-edges-symmetry-and-display\"\u003ESharp edges, symmetry and display\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECrease\u003C/span\u003E makes the picked edges sharp. \u003Cstrong\u003ECrease weight\u003C/strong\u003E 10 is fully sharp. A smaller weight stays sharp for that many refinement steps and then softens, which gives a tight round instead of a hard fold. \u003Cstrong\u003EUncrease\u003C/strong\u003E makes them smooth again. \u003Cstrong\u003ESharp vertex\u003C/strong\u003E pins a corner so the surface passes through it with a point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymmetry\u003C/strong\u003E mirrors the cage across X, Y or Z = 0. It keeps the positive side, mirrors it, and joins the two halves. After that, an edit on either side happens on both. Corners on the mirror plane stay on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDisplay\u003C/span\u003E switches between \u003Cstrong\u003ESmooth\u003C/strong\u003E (the surface the feature will be) and \u003Cstrong\u003EBox\u003C/strong\u003E (the cage faces themselves).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAnalysis\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EZebra stripes\u003C/strong\u003E reflect a striped light from the current view. They bend smoothly across a smooth surface and break at a crease. \u003Cstrong\u003EMean curvature\u003C/strong\u003E shades convex areas red, concave areas blue and flat areas white. It is measured on the displayed mesh with the cotangent formula, so it is a guide, not a measurement.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-and-finish\"\u003ECheck and finish\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECheck exact\u003C/strong\u003E converts the cage in the exact kernel without changing your part. It reports how many B-spline patches the cage makes, how many are exact, and whether the result is a closed, valid solid and its volume.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinish\u003C/span\u003E adds a \u003Cem\u003EForm\u003C/em\u003E feature to the history, or updates the form you were editing, and takes you back to Design. A closed cage becomes a solid. Its \u003Cstrong\u003EOperation\u003C/strong\u003E in the Design panel can be New body, Join, Cut or Intersect. An open cage becomes a surface body.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo change a form later, select it in the history and click \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit in Form\u003C/strong\u003E. The built body is hidden while you edit its cage.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDiscard\u003C/strong\u003E throws the cage away without touching the history.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-exact-and-what-is-approximated\"\u003EWhat is exact and what is approximated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach four-sided face of the cage, refined when needed, becomes one bicubic B-spline patch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere every corner of a face is \u003Cem\u003Eregular\u003C/em\u003E (four edges meet inside the surface, or three on an open edge or along a crease), the patch is \u003Cstrong\u003Eexactly\u003C/strong\u003E the subdivision surface. The tests check this to 1e-9 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAround an \u003Cstrong\u003Eextraordinary point\u003C/strong\u003E (three, five or more edges meeting, a corner of a three- or five-sided face, or the end of a crease) there is no exact polynomial patch. There the patch takes the exact point and tangent plane at that corner and the neighbors&#039; edge curves, so the patches still meet with no gap. Tangent continuity across the edges that meet at the point is approximate. The Form panel and the feature&#039;s panel give the largest angle, typically under 2°. A higher \u003Cstrong\u003ELevel\u003C/strong\u003E in the feature&#039;s panel makes these patches smaller and closer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESharp creases are real edges of the solid, so you can fillet them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach patch is its own face, so a form has many faces and lines are drawn between them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"surface-features-in-design\"\u003ESurface features in Design\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe surface commands are in Design&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, under the features. They need the exact kernel. Surface bodies are drawn see-through in blue. They are not part of the solid until you thicken or stitch them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECommand\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPatch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFills a closed loop of edges. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click the edges of the solid first, or select a surface body to fill its opening. \u003Cstrong\u003ETangent (G1)\u003C/strong\u003E makes the patch meet that surface smoothly.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoft surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA surface through one chain of curves per sketch (open or closed), in history order.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStitch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESews surface bodies along their shared edges. If they close up, the result is a solid, with an Operation like other features.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThicken\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGives a surface body a thickness: along its normal, against it, or half each way. The result is a solid.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA copy of a surface body moved along its normals. The original stays.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtend surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrows a one-face surface past the edges you choose.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrim surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECuts a surface body where another surface body, or the solid, crosses it. Click \u003Cstrong\u003EPick a point on the piece\u003C/strong\u003E to choose which piece to keep, or keep everything else.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStitch, Thicken, Extend and Trim use up the surface body they start from. Later features use the new one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELimits today: forms with several separate closed pieces do not convert into one solid; regular areas are not merged into larger surfaces; and see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Not built yet in this version. Finishing a form into the part, and the surface features, needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. What is not built yet says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes them as they work with an exact kernel. The Form workspace makes organic shapes. You edit a cage, a coarse mesh of corners, edges and faces. The shape is the smooth surface the cage controls (its Catmull–Clark limit surface), so moving one corner bends the surface gently around it. When it looks right, Finish adds it to the part history as a Form feature, and the exact kernel turns it into true B-spline surfaces. You can then fillet it, cut it, join it to other features and export it as STEP. Choose Form in the Workspace list at the left of the toolbar. Start a cage Set Size and Divisions in the right panel. Divisions is how many faces each side gets. Click Box, Quadball or Plane in the toolbar. The panel also has Cylinder, Torus and Faces by clicks. A quadball is a box cage fitted so its corner points lie on a sphere. Between those points the surface sits a little inside the sphere: about 0.4 % less volume with 2 divisions. A plane is open, so it becomes a surface body (see \"Surface features in Design\" below). Faces by clicks: click 4 points on the top (ground) plane or on corners of the cage to make one face. Faces that share corners join up. Press Enter after 3 points for a triangle, or Esc to stop. Your unfinished cage is saved with the part file, so you can close the file and carry on later. Pick and move Click a corner, an edge or a face of the cage. Shift+click adds or drops items. Esc clears the selection. A gizmo appears at the middle of the selection. Drag an arrow to move along it, or a square to move in that plane. Press W to move, E to rotate and R to scale, or use the buttons in the panel. Gizmo in the toolbar steps through them. Soft falloff (mm) also pulls the corners within that distance, fading smoothly to nothing at the edge of that distance. Every drag is one undo step: Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y work as usual. Change the cage The Modify buttons in the panel work on the selection: Button What it does Extrude Picked faces move out by Extrude distance, along their normals, with new side faces. Picked open edges grow new faces outward. Edge loop / Insert edge loop Splits the ring of four-sided faces that crosses the picked edge, halfway along. More loops give tighter curves. Insert point Adds a corner in the middle of the picked edge. Subdivide Splits each picked face into four-sided faces around its middle. Bridge Joins two picked faces, or two openings (pick one open edge on each), with a tube of faces. Both need the same number of edges. Fill hole Closes the opening through the picked open edge with one face. Weld Merges the picked corners into one, at their middle. Flatten Moves the picked corners onto the plane that fits them best. Delete Removes the picked faces, edges (two faces merge) or corners. Sharp edges, symmetry and display Crease makes the picked edges sharp. Crease weight 10 is fully sharp. A smaller weight stays sharp for that many refinement steps and then softens, which gives a tight round instead of a hard fold. Uncrease makes them smooth again. Sharp vertex pins a corner so the surface passes through it with a point. Symmetry mirrors the cage across X, Y or Z = 0. It keeps the positive side, mirrors it, and joins the two halves. After that, an edit on either side happens on both. Corners on the mirror plane stay on it. Display switches between Smooth (the surface the feature will be) and Box (the cage faces themselves). Analysis: Zebra stripes reflect a striped light from the current view. They bend smoothly across a smooth surface and break at a crease. Mean curvature shades convex areas red, concave areas blue and flat areas white. It is measured on the displayed mesh with the cotangent formula, so it is a guide, not a measurement. Check and finish Check exact converts the cage in the exact kernel without changing your part. It reports how many B-spline patches the cage makes, how many are exact, and whether the result is a closed, valid solid and its volume. Finish adds a Form feature to the history, or updates the form you were editing, and takes you back to Design. A closed cage becomes a solid. Its Operation in the Design panel can be New body, Join, Cut or Intersect. An open cage becomes a surface body. To change a form later, select it in the history and click Edit in Form. The built body is hidden while you edit its cage. Discard throws the cage away without touching the history. What is exact and what is approximated Each four-sided face of the cage, refined when needed, becomes one bicubic B-spline patch. Where every corner of a face is regular (four edges meet inside the surface, or three on an open edge or along a crease), the patch is exactly the subdivision surface. The tests check this to 1e-9 mm. Around an extraordinary point (three, five or more edges meeting, a corner of a three- or five-sided face, or the end of a crease) there is no exact polynomial patch. There the patch takes the exact point and tangent plane at that corner and the neighbors' edge curves, so the patches still meet with no gap. Tangent continuity across the edges that meet at the point is approximate. The Form panel and the feature's panel give the largest angle, typically under 2°. A higher Level in the feature's panel makes these patches smaller and closer. Sharp creases are real edges of the solid, so you can fillet them. Each patch is its own face, so a form has many faces and lines are drawn between them. Surface features in Design The surface commands are in Design's More menu, under the features. They need the exact kernel. Surface bodies are drawn see-through in blue. They are not part of the solid until you thicken or stitch them. Command What it does Patch Fills a closed loop of edges. Shift+click the edges of the solid first, or select a surface body to fill its opening. Tangent (G1) makes the patch meet that surface smoothly. Loft surface A surface through one chain of curves per sketch (open or closed), in history order. Stitch Sews surface bodies along their shared edges. If they close up, the result is a solid, with an Operation like other features. Thicken Gives a surface body a thickness: along its normal, against it, or half each way. The result is a solid. Offset surface A copy of a surface body moved along its normals. The original stays. Extend surface Grows a one-face surface past the edges you choose. Trim surface Cuts a surface body where another surface body, or the solid, crosses it. Click Pick a point on the piece to choose which piece to keep, or keep everything else. Stitch, Thicken, Extend and Trim use up the surface body they start from. Later features use the new one. Limits today: forms with several separate closed pieces do not convert into one solid; regular areas are not merged into larger surfaces; and see What is not built yet."},{"slug":"rib","scope":"partcraft","title":"Ribs and gussets","summary":"Stiffen a part with a rib drawn as one line: it fills from the line to the walls, a set thickness across the sketch plane, on the side you choose.","section":"tasks","order":92,"keywords":["rib","gusset","web","stiffener","brace","bracket","corner support","thin wall","strengthen"],"headings":[{"id":"add-a-rib","text":"Add a rib","level":2},{"id":"settings","text":"Settings","level":2},{"id":"messages","text":"Messages","level":2}],"html":"\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot built yet in this version.\u003C/strong\u003E The rib needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes it as it works with an exact kernel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Erib\u003C/strong\u003E (or gusset) is a thin wall that stiffens a part, such as the triangle inside an L-bracket&#039;s corner. You draw only its free edge: PartCraft fills the space between that line and the walls. It needs the \u003Cstrong\u003Eexact kernel\u003C/strong\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-rib\"\u003EAdd a rib\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake a plane through the middle of where the rib goes (for an L-bracket 40 deep, a plane 20 in front of its sketch: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/reference-geometry\" data-topic=\"partcraft/reference-geometry\"\u003EReference planes, axes and points\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESketch on it \u003Cstrong\u003Eone line\u003C/strong\u003E from wall to wall — or a few lines end to end for a bent rib edge. The line may stop short of the walls: its ends are carried on until they meet them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the sketch and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERib\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rib fills from the line to the walls on the side toward the middle of the part, so a gusset lands in the inside corner. If that side is open and the other is closed, PartCraft flips it for you and says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThickness (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E is the rib&#039;s thickness across the sketch plane. It can be driven by a parameter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThickness\u003C/strong\u003E (the choice below it): \u003Cstrong\u003ECentred on the sketch plane\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EIn front of the plane\u003C/strong\u003E (along its normal) or \u003Cstrong\u003EBehind the plane\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFlip\u003C/strong\u003E fills the other side of the line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"messages\"\u003EMessages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn this side the rib&#039;s edge never meets the body\u003C/strong\u003E — nothing closes in the space on that side: tick or untick \u003Cstrong\u003EFlip\u003C/strong\u003E, or move the line so walls bound it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA rib needs one open chain of lines in its sketch\u003C/strong\u003E — the sketch has a closed shape, an arc or a spline, or lines that do not join end to end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe rib&#039;s edge runs inside the body there\u003C/strong\u003E — the line lies inside the solid; draw it in the open space between the walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet in this version. The rib needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. What is not built yet says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes it as it works with an exact kernel. A rib (or gusset) is a thin wall that stiffens a part, such as the triangle inside an L-bracket's corner. You draw only its free edge: PartCraft fills the space between that line and the walls. It needs the exact kernel (see The mesh kernel). Add a rib Make a plane through the middle of where the rib goes (for an L-bracket 40 deep, a plane 20 in front of its sketch: see Reference planes, axes and points). Sketch on it one line from wall to wall — or a few lines end to end for a bent rib edge. The line may stop short of the walls: its ends are carried on until they meet them. Select the sketch and choose More › Rib. The rib fills from the line to the walls on the side toward the middle of the part, so a gusset lands in the inside corner. If that side is open and the other is closed, PartCraft flips it for you and says so. Settings Thickness (mm) is the rib's thickness across the sketch plane. It can be driven by a parameter. Thickness (the choice below it): Centred on the sketch plane, In front of the plane (along its normal) or Behind the plane. Flip fills the other side of the line. Messages On this side the rib's edge never meets the body — nothing closes in the space on that side: tick or untick Flip, or move the line so walls bound it. A rib needs one open chain of lines in its sketch — the sketch has a closed shape, an arc or a spline, or lines that do not join end to end. The rib's edge runs inside the body there — the line lies inside the solid; draw it in the open space between the walls."},{"slug":"rollback","scope":"partcraft","title":"Roll back and forward through the history","summary":"Step the rollback bar up and down one feature at a time to watch the part being built, insert features in the middle, and return to the end.","section":"tasks","order":92,"keywords":["rollback","roll back","roll forward","rollback bar","step through history","insert feature","freeze","history"],"headings":[{"id":"step-one-feature-at-a-time","text":"Step one feature at a time","level":2},{"id":"insert-a-feature-in-the-middle","text":"Insert a feature in the middle","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe rollback bar decides how much of the history is built. Features below it are grayed and not built; new features go in at the bar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"step-one-feature-at-a-time\"\u003EStep one feature at a time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith the part on screen (no sketch or drawing open):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EResult\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"[\"\u003E[\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E[\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the up arrow button next to the part name\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe bar moves up one feature\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"]\"\u003E]\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E]\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the down arrow button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe bar moves down one feature\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the bar-to-the-end button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery feature is built again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says where the bar is, for example &quot;Rollback bar after Sketch1: 1 of 2 features built&quot;. The buttons are off when the bar cannot move further that way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoll back to here\u003C/span\u003E on any feature, or click \u003Cstrong\u003ERollback bar · roll forward\u003C/strong\u003E in the history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"insert-a-feature-in-the-middle\"\u003EInsert a feature in the middle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStep the bar back until it sits where the new feature belongs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the feature: it goes in at the bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to build the rest of the history on top of it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery move of the bar is a step you can undo with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The bar&#039;s place is saved in the file, so a part can be sent rolled back. The command line tool moves it too: \u003Ccode\u003Epartcraft rollback part.json\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Epartcraft forward part.json --end\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The rollback bar decides how much of the history is built. Features below it are grayed and not built; new features go in at the bar. Step one feature at a time With the part on screen (no sketch or drawing open): Do Result [ or the up arrow button next to the part name The bar moves up one feature ] or the down arrow button The bar moves down one feature End or the bar-to-the-end button Every feature is built again The status bar says where the bar is, for example \"Rollback bar after Sketch1: 1 of 2 features built\". The buttons are off when the bar cannot move further that way. You can also choose Roll back to here on any feature, or click Rollback bar · roll forward in the history. Insert a feature in the middle Step the bar back until it sits where the new feature belongs. Add the feature: it goes in at the bar. Press End to build the rest of the history on top of it. Every move of the bar is a step you can undo with Ctrl+Z. The bar's place is saved in the file, so a part can be sent rolled back. The command line tool moves it too: partcraft rollback part.json and partcraft forward part.json --end."},{"slug":"gordon-surfaces","scope":"partcraft","title":"Surfaces through a curve network (Gordon surface)","summary":"Make one smooth surface through a grid of profile and guide curves drawn in sketches or taken from model edges, close it to a solid when the profiles are closed, and what is exact, what is fitted, and the messages when a network does not fit.","section":"tasks","order":93,"keywords":["tangent","g1","tangency","tangent continuity","mirror plane","gordon surface","curve network","boundary surface","net surface","network surface","profiles","guides","guide curves","loft with guides","guide rails","skin","transfinite","coons","b-spline surface","freeform surface"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-the-network","text":"Draw the network","level":2},{"id":"make-the-surface","text":"Make the surface","level":2},{"id":"close-it-to-a-solid","text":"Close it to a solid","level":3},{"id":"tangent-to-a-plane-along-an-edge","text":"Tangent to a plane along an edge","level":2},{"id":"what-is-exact-and-what-is-fitted","text":"What is exact and what is fitted","level":2},{"id":"when-a-network-does-not-fit","text":"When a network does not fit","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot built yet in this version.\u003C/strong\u003E The Gordon surface needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes it as it works with an exact kernel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003EGordon surface\u003C/strong\u003E is one surface that passes through every curve of a network: a set of \u003Cstrong\u003Eprofiles\u003C/strong\u003E running one way and a set of \u003Cstrong\u003Eguides\u003C/strong\u003E running the other, crossing like the threads of a net. It is how you shape a hull, a fender or a handle from a few section curves you control directly. It needs the \u003Cstrong\u003Eexact kernel\u003C/strong\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-network\"\u003EDraw the network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the \u003Cstrong\u003Eprofiles\u003C/strong\u003E: two or more curves running side by side, for example sections on parallel planes. Lines, arcs, circles and splines all work, and a chain of curves joined end to end counts as one curve.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the \u003Cstrong\u003Eguides\u003C/strong\u003E: two or more curves running across the profiles. Each guide has to cross every profile once, and each profile every guide once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake the curves really meet. Draw a guide&#039;s sketch so its curve passes through the profiles&#039; points (snap to projected points, or type the coordinates). The network accepts gaps up to the \u003Cstrong\u003ETolerance\u003C/strong\u003E (0.01 mm to start with).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne sketch can hold several curves: every separate chain in it is its own curve. So you can draw all the guides in one sketch on the top plane, for instance.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-the-surface\"\u003EMake the surface\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E in the Features group, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGordon surface\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPartCraft takes the sketches nobody uses yet: those in planes parallel to the first become \u003Cstrong\u003Eprofiles\u003C/strong\u003E, the others \u003Cstrong\u003Eguides\u003C/strong\u003E. Check the list on the right and set each sketch to \u003Cstrong\u003EProfiles\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EGuides\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ENot used\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo use edges of the part, click \u003Cstrong\u003EPick edges as profiles\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003Eas guides\u003C/strong\u003E), click the edges in the view, then press Esc.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe result is a \u003Cstrong\u003Esurface body\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThicken\u003C/span\u003E turns it into a solid, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStitch\u003C/span\u003E sews it to other surfaces, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim surface\u003C/span\u003E cuts it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"close-it-to-a-solid\"\u003EClose it to a solid\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the profiles are closed curves (circles, closed splines, closed chains) and the first and last profile each lie flat in a plane, tick \u003Cstrong\u003EClose to a solid\u003C/strong\u003E. The ends are capped with flat faces and the closed shape joins, cuts, intersects or becomes a new body like any other feature. This is how you make a loft that follows guide curves. The same works with closed guides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tangent-to-a-plane-along-an-edge\"\u003ETangent to a plane along an edge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA Gordon surface passes through its curves, but on its own it does not match the slope of what it meets. To make it leave an edge \u003Cstrong\u003Etangent\u003C/strong\u003E to a plane (G1), such as a mirror plane, so the mirrored copy joins without a crease, or a flat face it grows from:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the Gordon feature. Under \u003Cstrong\u003ETangent to\u003C/strong\u003E, choose a plane in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a plane…\u003C/span\u003E (Front, Top, Right or a reference plane), or pick a flat face in the view and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd the picked flat face\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPartCraft finds the boundary curve of the network that lies in that plane, and the surface leaves it tangent to the plane.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a plane holds two boundary curves (a floor under both ends), add it twice: each copy takes the next one. Up to four planes, one per edge.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe report says along which curve each plane made it tangent and the steepest angle the surface met that plane at before. Guides that already leave that curve tangent to the plane are followed exactly. Guides that climb away from it are bent in a narrow band near the edge, and the report&#039;s &quot;follows every curve within&quot; figure says by how much: draw the guides leaving the edge flat for an exact fit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-exact-and-what-is-fitted\"\u003EWhat is exact and what is fitted\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel says which of these applies, and how far the surface strays from your curves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExact\u003C/strong\u003E: when every curve is polynomial (lines, splines and chains of them) and the guides cross all the profiles at the same parameters, the surface is exactly the Gordon surface of those curves. A network of iso curves cut from a bicubic surface gives that surface back, and four straight lines give a flat or twisted (bilinear) patch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFitted\u003C/strong\u003E: arcs, circles and edges of the part are not polynomial, and curves that cross at different parameters have to be re-timed so the crossings line up. Those curves are fitted by cubic B-splines within one ten-millionth of the network&#039;s size (about 0.00001 mm for a 100 mm part). A cylinder patch made from arcs and lines stays within 0.00001 mm of the true cylinder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBetween the curves the surface is whatever the method gives: the profiles blended across, plus the guides blended across, minus the blend of their crossing points. It is smooth everywhere except where a curve has a sharp corner. Where two Gordon surfaces share a boundary curve they meet edge to edge (G0), not tangent.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-network-does-not-fit\"\u003EWhen a network does not fit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;… do not meet: they pass 0.4 mm apart&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake that profile and guide cross, or raise the Tolerance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;… meets … 2 times&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA guide may cross each profile only once: split a looping curve, or use fewer profiles\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;… in a different order&quot; or &quot;folds over itself&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo guides cross each other (or two profiles do). The families must run side by side\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;at least two profiles and two guides&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd curves, or set more sketches to Profiles or Guides\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;mix open and closed curves&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake all the profiles open or all closed (the same for the guides)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;An end … is not flat, so it cannot be capped&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the first and last closed profile in one plane each, or leave Close to a solid off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne B-spline face per network: no G1 or G2 tangency to neighbouring surfaces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA closed network&#039;s seam, at the first guide, is joined edge to edge but not tangent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe Loft feature itself has no guide rails; use a Gordon surface closed to a solid instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVery wiggly curves, or crossings spaced very unevenly along one curve compared to another, can need too many points to fit; the feature then says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet in this version. The Gordon surface needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. What is not built yet says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes it as it works with an exact kernel. A Gordon surface is one surface that passes through every curve of a network: a set of profiles running one way and a set of guides running the other, crossing like the threads of a net. It is how you shape a hull, a fender or a handle from a few section curves you control directly. It needs the exact kernel (see The mesh kernel). Draw the network Draw the profiles: two or more curves running side by side, for example sections on parallel planes. Lines, arcs, circles and splines all work, and a chain of curves joined end to end counts as one curve. Draw the guides: two or more curves running across the profiles. Each guide has to cross every profile once, and each profile every guide once. Make the curves really meet. Draw a guide's sketch so its curve passes through the profiles' points (snap to projected points, or type the coordinates). The network accepts gaps up to the Tolerance (0.01 mm to start with). One sketch can hold several curves: every separate chain in it is its own curve. So you can draw all the guides in one sketch on the top plane, for instance. Make the surface Choose More in the Features group, then Gordon surface. PartCraft takes the sketches nobody uses yet: those in planes parallel to the first become profiles, the others guides. Check the list on the right and set each sketch to Profiles, Guides or Not used. To use edges of the part, click Pick edges as profiles (or as guides), click the edges in the view, then press Esc. The result is a surface body. Thicken turns it into a solid, Stitch sews it to other surfaces, and Trim surface cuts it. Close it to a solid When the profiles are closed curves (circles, closed splines, closed chains) and the first and last profile each lie flat in a plane, tick Close to a solid. The ends are capped with flat faces and the closed shape joins, cuts, intersects or becomes a new body like any other feature. This is how you make a loft that follows guide curves. The same works with closed guides. Tangent to a plane along an edge A Gordon surface passes through its curves, but on its own it does not match the slope of what it meets. To make it leave an edge tangent to a plane (G1), such as a mirror plane, so the mirrored copy joins without a crease, or a flat face it grows from: Select the Gordon feature. Under Tangent to, choose a plane in Add a plane… (Front, Top, Right or a reference plane), or pick a flat face in the view and press Add the picked flat face. PartCraft finds the boundary curve of the network that lies in that plane, and the surface leaves it tangent to the plane. If a plane holds two boundary curves (a floor under both ends), add it twice: each copy takes the next one. Up to four planes, one per edge. The report says along which curve each plane made it tangent and the steepest angle the surface met that plane at before. Guides that already leave that curve tangent to the plane are followed exactly. Guides that climb away from it are bent in a narrow band near the edge, and the report's \"follows every curve within\" figure says by how much: draw the guides leaving the edge flat for an exact fit. What is exact and what is fitted The panel says which of these applies, and how far the surface strays from your curves. Exact: when every curve is polynomial (lines, splines and chains of them) and the guides cross all the profiles at the same parameters, the surface is exactly the Gordon surface of those curves. A network of iso curves cut from a bicubic surface gives that surface back, and four straight lines give a flat or twisted (bilinear) patch. Fitted: arcs, circles and edges of the part are not polynomial, and curves that cross at different parameters have to be re-timed so the crossings line up. Those curves are fitted by cubic B-splines within one ten-millionth of the network's size (about 0.00001 mm for a 100 mm part). A cylinder patch made from arcs and lines stays within 0.00001 mm of the true cylinder. Between the curves the surface is whatever the method gives: the profiles blended across, plus the guides blended across, minus the blend of their crossing points. It is smooth everywhere except where a curve has a sharp corner. Where two Gordon surfaces share a boundary curve they meet edge to edge (G0), not tangent. When a network does not fit Message What to do \"… do not meet: they pass 0.4 mm apart\" Make that profile and guide cross, or raise the Tolerance \"… meets … 2 times\" A guide may cross each profile only once: split a looping curve, or use fewer profiles \"… in a different order\" or \"folds over itself\" Two guides cross each other (or two profiles do). The families must run side by side \"at least two profiles and two guides\" Add curves, or set more sketches to Profiles or Guides \"mix open and closed curves\" Make all the profiles open or all closed (the same for the guides) \"An end … is not flat, so it cannot be capped\" Draw the first and last closed profile in one plane each, or leave Close to a solid off Limits One B-spline face per network: no G1 or G2 tangency to neighbouring surfaces. A closed network's seam, at the first guide, is joined edge to edge but not tangent. The Loft feature itself has no guide rails; use a Gordon surface closed to a solid instead. Very wiggly curves, or crossings spaced very unevenly along one curve compared to another, can need too many points to fit; the feature then says so."},{"slug":"sheet-metal","scope":"partcraft","title":"Sheet metal: flanges and the flat pattern","summary":"Start a sheet metal part from a sketch (a flat plate or a bent profile), set its thickness, bend radius and K-factor once, bend flanges up from its edges with bend reliefs, and lay it flat as a DXF for cutting.","section":"tasks","order":94,"keywords":["flat pattern","unfold","development","developed length","bend allowance","bend line","dxf for laser","laser cutting","sheet metal","base flange","edge flange","flange","bend","bend radius","k-factor","k factor","thickness","gauge","relief","bend relief","channel","bracket","enclosure","bent sheet"],"headings":[{"id":"start-with-a-base-flange","text":"Start with a base flange","level":2},{"id":"bend-an-edge-flange","text":"Bend an edge flange","level":2},{"id":"lay-it-flat","text":"Lay it flat","level":2},{"id":"messages","text":"Messages","level":2}],"html":"\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot built yet in this version.\u003C/strong\u003E Sheet metal needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes it as it works with an exact kernel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheet metal parts start with a \u003Cstrong\u003EBase flange\u003C/strong\u003E. It sets the sheet for the whole part: its \u003Cstrong\u003Ethickness\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cstrong\u003Einside bend radius\u003C/strong\u003E and the \u003Cstrong\u003EK-factor\u003C/strong\u003E. Every \u003Cstrong\u003EEdge flange\u003C/strong\u003E after it bends with those same numbers, so the part stays one sheet that can be unfolded. Both need the \u003Cstrong\u003Eexact kernel\u003C/strong\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-with-a-base-flange\"\u003EStart with a base flange\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a sketch. Use a \u003Cstrong\u003Eclosed profile\u003C/strong\u003E for a flat plate (a rectangle, or any closed outline with holes). Use \u003Cstrong\u003Eone open chain of lines\u003C/strong\u003E for a bent sheet, such as an L, a U or a Z drawn as the sheet&#039;s edge view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the sketch in the feature history.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E in the Features group and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBase flange\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the panel on the right:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThickness (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E is the sheet&#039;s gauge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBend radius (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E is the inside radius of every bend. 0 gives a sharp inside corner; the outside still turns with a radius of one thickness.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EK-factor\u003C/strong\u003E says where the neutral layer sits inside the sheet, from 0 (the inside face) to 0.5 (the middle). It decides how long the sheet is when laid flat. 0.44 is the default; your shop or material supplier can give you theirs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDepth (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E is how far a bent profile runs out of the sketch plane. A plate ignores it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThickness outside the bends\u003C/strong\u003E: an open profile is one face of the sheet. By default the thickness goes on the inside of the first bend, so the lines you drew are the outside of the channel. Tick it to put the lines on the inside instead. The answer is the same whichever end you drew from.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery corner of an open chain becomes a bend. A line too short to hold its bends says so, with the length it needs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bend-an-edge-flange\"\u003EBend an edge flange\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a straight edge of the sheet&#039;s top or bottom face (the long edges, not the thin ones).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdge flange\u003C/span\u003E. With no edge picked, PartCraft waits for you to click one; Esc cancels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe flange bends up from that edge&#039;s face around the sheet&#039;s inside radius, then runs straight. In the panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/strong\u003E is how far it bends, from 1 to 180. 90 is a square flange.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELength (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E is the straight leg after the bend.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBend the other way\u003C/strong\u003E bends it from the other face (down instead of up).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOffset at the start\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EOffset at the end\u003C/strong\u003E shorten the flange from each end of the edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBend relief\u003C/strong\u003E: when an end is offset, a slot is cut into the sheet beside it so the bend does not tear the metal there. \u003Cstrong\u003ERelief width\u003C/strong\u003E sets the slot&#039;s width; its depth is the bend radius plus the thickness. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E to leave the corner uncut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change the edge later, press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick\u003C/span\u003E in the panel and click another one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse parameters for the thickness and radius (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/parameters\" data-topic=\"partcraft/parameters\"\u003EParameters and equations\u003C/a\u003E) so a change of gauge updates every flange at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lay-it-flat\"\u003ELay it flat\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth panels end with \u003Cstrong\u003EFlat pattern\u003C/strong\u003E. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELay flat\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA preview shows the sheet unfolded, with each bend&#039;s centre line dashed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESize\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EArea\u003C/strong\u003E are those of the flat blank.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach bend is listed with its direction (\u003Cstrong\u003Eup\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003Edown\u003C/strong\u003E, seen from the base flange&#039;s sketch side), angle and inside radius.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery bend takes the length of the sheet&#039;s neutral layer through it, the \u003Cstrong\u003Ebend allowance\u003C/strong\u003E: angle (in radians) × (inside radius + K-factor × thickness). So a 90° bend of a 2 mm sheet with a 2 mm radius and K-factor 0.44 adds 4.52 mm of flat sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a flat plate, holes, cuts and bend reliefs show in the flat where they really are, including holes through a flange.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo cut many, press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENest on sheets…\u003C/span\u003E under the flat pattern: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/nest-flat-parts\" data-topic=\"partcraft/nest-flat-parts\"\u003ENest flat parts on sheets\u003C/a\u003E. A bent base flange lays out as one rectangle: its developed length × its depth, with a bend line per corner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave DXF\u003C/span\u003E saves the flat pattern for a laser, punch or waterjet, in millimetres:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe outline on layer \u003Cstrong\u003EOUTLINE\u003C/strong\u003E, as lines, arcs and circles;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe bend lines on layer \u003Cstrong\u003EBEND\u003C/strong\u003E, dashed;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea note on each bend on layer \u003Cstrong\u003EBEND_NOTES\u003C/strong\u003E, such as &quot;UP 90° R2&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange anything and the flat goes away; press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELay flat\u003C/span\u003E again for the new one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECheck the K-factor with your shop before cutting: the flat&#039;s length depends on it, and it varies with material, thickness and tooling.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"messages\"\u003EMessages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESheet metal needs a base flange before it\u003C/strong\u003E — an edge flange was added before any base flange. Move it below one, or add a base flange first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn edge flange needs a straight edge\u003C/strong\u003E — flanges bend along lines only; curved edges cannot take one yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThat edge is not on a face of the sheet&#039;s thickness\u003C/strong\u003E — the edge belongs to a face that is not the sheet&#039;s top or bottom (for example a hole&#039;s wall). Pick an edge of the top or bottom face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe offsets leave nothing of the edge\u003C/strong\u003E — the two offsets add up to the whole edge or more.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdge flanges on a bent base flange cannot be laid flat yet\u003C/strong\u003E — start from a flat plate and bend everything up with edge flanges, which unfold.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECuts and holes made after a bent base flange are not shown in its flat yet\u003C/strong\u003E — the flat of a bent profile is its developed rectangle only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet in this version. Sheet metal needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have: it builds every part with the mesh kernel. What is not built yet says what to do instead. The rest of this page describes it as it works with an exact kernel. Sheet metal parts start with a Base flange. It sets the sheet for the whole part: its thickness, the inside bend radius and the K-factor. Every Edge flange after it bends with those same numbers, so the part stays one sheet that can be unfolded. Both need the exact kernel (see The mesh kernel). Start with a base flange Draw a sketch. Use a closed profile for a flat plate (a rectangle, or any closed outline with holes). Use one open chain of lines for a bent sheet, such as an L, a U or a Z drawn as the sheet's edge view. Select the sketch in the feature history. Open More in the Features group and choose Base flange. In the panel on the right: Thickness (mm) is the sheet's gauge. Bend radius (mm) is the inside radius of every bend. 0 gives a sharp inside corner; the outside still turns with a radius of one thickness. K-factor says where the neutral layer sits inside the sheet, from 0 (the inside face) to 0.5 (the middle). It decides how long the sheet is when laid flat. 0.44 is the default; your shop or material supplier can give you theirs. Depth (mm) is how far a bent profile runs out of the sketch plane. A plate ignores it. Thickness outside the bends: an open profile is one face of the sheet. By default the thickness goes on the inside of the first bend, so the lines you drew are the outside of the channel. Tick it to put the lines on the inside instead. The answer is the same whichever end you drew from. Every corner of an open chain becomes a bend. A line too short to hold its bends says so, with the length it needs. Bend an edge flange Click a straight edge of the sheet's top or bottom face (the long edges, not the thin ones). Choose More › Edge flange. With no edge picked, PartCraft waits for you to click one; Esc cancels. The flange bends up from that edge's face around the sheet's inside radius, then runs straight. In the panel: Angle (°) is how far it bends, from 1 to 180. 90 is a square flange. Length (mm) is the straight leg after the bend. Bend the other way bends it from the other face (down instead of up). Offset at the start and Offset at the end shorten the flange from each end of the edge. Bend relief: when an end is offset, a slot is cut into the sheet beside it so the bend does not tear the metal there. Relief width sets the slot's width; its depth is the bend radius plus the thickness. Choose None to leave the corner uncut. To change the edge later, press Pick in the panel and click another one. Use parameters for the thickness and radius (see Parameters and equations) so a change of gauge updates every flange at once. Lay it flat Both panels end with Flat pattern. Press Lay flat: A preview shows the sheet unfolded, with each bend's centre line dashed. Size and Area are those of the flat blank. Each bend is listed with its direction (up or down, seen from the base flange's sketch side), angle and inside radius. Every bend takes the length of the sheet's neutral layer through it, the bend allowance: angle (in radians) × (inside radius + K-factor × thickness). So a 90° bend of a 2 mm sheet with a 2 mm radius and K-factor 0.44 adds 4.52 mm of flat sheet. On a flat plate, holes, cuts and bend reliefs show in the flat where they really are, including holes through a flange. To cut many, press Nest on sheets… under the flat pattern: see Nest flat parts on sheets. A bent base flange lays out as one rectangle: its developed length × its depth, with a bend line per corner. Save DXF saves the flat pattern for a laser, punch or waterjet, in millimetres: the outline on layer OUTLINE, as lines, arcs and circles; the bend lines on layer BEND, dashed; a note on each bend on layer BEND_NOTES, such as \"UP 90° R2\". Change anything and the flat goes away; press Lay flat again for the new one. Check the K-factor with your shop before cutting: the flat's length depends on it, and it varies with material, thickness and tooling. Messages Sheet metal needs a base flange before it — an edge flange was added before any base flange. Move it below one, or add a base flange first. An edge flange needs a straight edge — flanges bend along lines only; curved edges cannot take one yet. That edge is not on a face of the sheet's thickness — the edge belongs to a face that is not the sheet's top or bottom (for example a hole's wall). Pick an edge of the top or bottom face. The offsets leave nothing of the edge — the two offsets add up to the whole edge or more. Edge flanges on a bent base flange cannot be laid flat yet — start from a flat plate and bend everything up with edge flanges, which unfold. Cuts and holes made after a bent base flange are not shown in its flat yet — the flat of a bent profile is its developed rectangle only."},{"slug":"copy-paste","scope":"partcraft","title":"Copy, paste and duplicate","summary":"Copy sketch geometry, features and assembly parts; paste where you click or in place; duplicate in one step; and what comes along with a copy and what does not.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["copy","paste","cut","duplicate","clipboard","paste in place","select all","select none","delete","ctrl c","ctrl v","ctrl d","instance","component","copy sketch","copy feature","copy part"],"headings":[{"id":"in-a-sketch","text":"In a sketch","level":2},{"id":"in-the-history","text":"In the history","level":2},{"id":"in-an-assembly","text":"In an assembly","level":2},{"id":"the-clipboard","text":"The clipboard","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group in the toolbar holds \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E, each with its key. What they copy depends on where you are: geometry in an open sketch, a feature in the history, or a part in an assembly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA grayed-out item cannot act right now. Hover over it: its tooltip says why, for example &quot;Select sketch geometry first&quot; or &quot;The clipboard holds Sketch2 and Extrude2: features paste into the history, so exit the sketch first&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn a sketch\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the history\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn an assembly\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selected points and curves\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selected sketch, extrude, revolve or hole (with its sketch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selected part\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy, then delete them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy, then delete the feature\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy, then remove the part\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe copy follows the pointer; click where it goes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdds the copy at the end of the history\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA new copy beside the other parts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAt the same coordinates\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame as Paste\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn the same place as the copied part\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy and move in one go; the clipboard is left alone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA second copy right after you\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA second copy beside the other parts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe feature (asks if others use it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe part or joint\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect all\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEverything in the sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect none\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClears the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClears the selection and picks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClears the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery paste, duplicate and cut is one step in the undo list: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-a-sketch\"\u003EIn a sketch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what to copy: click it, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click for more, drag a window, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for everything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E. The status bar says what was copied.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E. A dashed copy follows the pointer, held by its middle. It snaps to points: put a copied circle&#039;s center on a point by clicking that point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it goes. The pasted geometry stays selected, so you can drag it or add relations to it straight away. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels the paste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat comes along:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA copied line, circle or arc brings its own end points and center.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERelations and dimensions \u003Cstrong\u003Ebetween copied items\u003C/strong\u003E come along: a copied rectangle is still a rectangle, with its sizes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERelations \u003Cstrong\u003Eto geometry you did not copy\u003C/strong\u003E, and to the origin, are dropped. The status bar counts them: &quot;2 relations to geometry outside the copy will not come along&quot;. So a pasted copy is free to move until you tie it in with relations or dimensions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA fixed point stays fixed, at its new position.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E puts the copy exactly on the original&#039;s coordinates, in this sketch or in another one: handy to bring a profile from one sketch into another. In the same sketch the copy lies on top of the original; drag it away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) copies the selection and starts placing it at once, without touching what is on the clipboard.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo repeat a hole center or a slot several times, copy it once and paste it several times: every \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E starts a new copy that follows the pointer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-history\"\u003EIn the history\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a sketch, an extrude, a revolve or a hole in the history.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies it. A sketch-based feature is copied together with its sketch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E adds the copy at the end of the history (at the rollback bar when you have rolled back), with new names such as Sketch3 and Extrude2.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy sits on the same plane or face as the original, so it builds on top of it and the part looks the same. Select the new sketch and change its plane in the right panel, or open it and drag or re-dimension the profile, to move the copy.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPaste into \u003Cstrong\u003Eanother part\u003C/strong\u003E: open that part and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. When the copy&#039;s sketch was on a face that part does not have, it goes on the front plane, and the status bar says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E does copy and paste in one step, inside the same part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E copies the feature, then deletes it (asking first if other features use it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPatterns, mirrors, fillets, chamfers, shells and imported solids cannot be copied: they hang on edges, faces or features picked in this part. Copy the sketch or the feature they are built on instead, or pattern it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircular\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-an-assembly\"\u003EIn an assembly\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a part in the view or in the list on the left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe new copy is the \u003Cstrong\u003Esame part\u003C/strong\u003E: edit one and every copy changes. It appears 20 mm to the right of everything already in the assembly, turned the same way as the copy, and stays there until a joint places it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) puts it exactly where the copied part is; join it to move it (a slider joint&#039;s offset pushes it along).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPasting a part copied from another assembly brings the part along, the same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E from its file. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd copy\u003C/span\u003E in the Assembly group does the same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, and works with nothing selected (it copies the last part).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-clipboard\"\u003EThe clipboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt is PartCraft&#039;s own clipboard, not the computer&#039;s: you cannot paste PartCraft geometry into another program, or text into PartCraft&#039;s views.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt is kept in this browser, so another PartCraft tab can paste what you copied. It is read back and checked like an opened file; anything damaged or from another version is refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA part with a large imported STEP file may be too big for the browser to keep: it then pastes only in this tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a number box or the help search, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copy and paste text as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the drawing sheet, copy and paste are not available yet; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E work on the selected symbol. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Edit group in the toolbar holds Undo, Redo, Copy, Paste and a More menu with Cut, Copy, Paste, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Select all and Select none, each with its key. What they copy depends on where you are: geometry in an open sketch, a feature in the history, or a part in an assembly. A grayed-out item cannot act right now. Hover over it: its tooltip says why, for example \"Select sketch geometry first\" or \"The clipboard holds Sketch2 and Extrude2: features paste into the history, so exit the sketch first\". Action Key In a sketch In the history In an assembly Copy Ctrl+C The selected points and curves The selected sketch, extrude, revolve or hole (with its sketch) The selected part Cut Ctrl+X Copy, then delete them Copy, then delete the feature Copy, then remove the part Paste Ctrl+V The copy follows the pointer; click where it goes Adds the copy at the end of the history A new copy beside the other parts Paste in place Ctrl+Shift+V At the same coordinates Same as Paste In the same place as the copied part Duplicate Ctrl+D Copy and move in one go; the clipboard is left alone A second copy right after you A second copy beside the other parts Delete Delete The selection The feature (asks if others use it) The part or joint Select all Ctrl+A Everything in the sketch Not available Not available Select none Esc Clears the selection Clears the selection and picks Clears the selection Every paste, duplicate and cut is one step in the undo list: Ctrl+Z takes it back, Ctrl+Y puts it back. In a sketch Select what to copy: click it, Shift+click for more, drag a window, or press Ctrl+A for everything. Press Ctrl+C or choose Copy. The status bar says what was copied. Press Ctrl+V or choose Paste. A dashed copy follows the pointer, held by its middle. It snaps to points: put a copied circle's center on a point by clicking that point. Click where it goes. The pasted geometry stays selected, so you can drag it or add relations to it straight away. Esc cancels the paste. What comes along: A copied line, circle or arc brings its own end points and center. Relations and dimensions between copied items come along: a copied rectangle is still a rectangle, with its sizes. Relations to geometry you did not copy, and to the origin, are dropped. The status bar counts them: \"2 relations to geometry outside the copy will not come along\". So a pasted copy is free to move until you tie it in with relations or dimensions. A fixed point stays fixed, at its new position. Paste in place puts the copy exactly on the original's coordinates, in this sketch or in another one: handy to bring a profile from one sketch into another. In the same sketch the copy lies on top of the original; drag it away. Duplicate (Ctrl+D) copies the selection and starts placing it at once, without touching what is on the clipboard. To repeat a hole center or a slot several times, copy it once and paste it several times: every Ctrl+V starts a new copy that follows the pointer. In the history Click a sketch, an extrude, a revolve or a hole in the history. Ctrl+C copies it. A sketch-based feature is copied together with its sketch. Ctrl+V adds the copy at the end of the history (at the rollback bar when you have rolled back), with new names such as Sketch3 and Extrude2. The copy sits on the same plane or face as the original, so it builds on top of it and the part looks the same. Select the new sketch and change its plane in the right panel, or open it and drag or re-dimension the profile, to move the copy. Paste into another part: open that part and press Ctrl+V. When the copy's sketch was on a face that part does not have, it goes on the front plane, and the status bar says so. Duplicate does copy and paste in one step, inside the same part. Cut copies the feature, then deletes it (asking first if other features use it). Patterns, mirrors, fillets, chamfers, shells and imported solids cannot be copied: they hang on edges, faces or features picked in this part. Copy the sketch or the feature they are built on instead, or pattern it with Linear or Circular. In an assembly Click a part in the view or in the list on the left. Choose Duplicate (Ctrl+D), or Ctrl+C then Ctrl+V. The new copy is the same part: edit one and every copy changes. It appears 20 mm to the right of everything already in the assembly, turned the same way as the copy, and stays there until a joint places it. Paste in place (Ctrl+Shift+V) puts it exactly where the copied part is; join it to move it (a slider joint's offset pushes it along). Pasting a part copied from another assembly brings the part along, the same as Insert from its file. Add copy in the Assembly group does the same as Duplicate, and works with nothing selected (it copies the last part). The clipboard It is PartCraft's own clipboard, not the computer's: you cannot paste PartCraft geometry into another program, or text into PartCraft's views. It is kept in this browser, so another PartCraft tab can paste what you copied. It is read back and checked like an opened file; anything damaged or from another version is refused. A part with a large imported STEP file may be too big for the browser to keep: it then pastes only in this tab. In a number box or the help search, Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V copy and paste text as usual. On the drawing sheet, copy and paste are not available yet; Delete and Select none work on the selected symbol. See Not built yet."},{"slug":"nest-flat-parts","scope":"partcraft","title":"Nest flat parts on sheets","summary":"Lay flat parts on plates or a roll with as little waste as PartCraft can find, never closer than the spacing you give, and save the layout as a DXF for the laser, plasma, waterjet or router.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["nesting","nest","sheet","plate","roll","laser","plasma","waterjet","router","cutting","yield","material use","scrap","waste","spacing","kerf","grain","rolling direction","dxf","flat pattern","outlines"],"headings":[{"id":"open-it","text":"Open it","level":2},{"id":"set-it-up","text":"Set it up","level":2},{"id":"save-it","text":"Save it","level":2},{"id":"what-to-check","text":"What to check","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENesting\u003C/strong\u003E places many flat parts on a sheet so that as little material as possible is left over. PartCraft lays the parts one by one, each where it keeps the used length of the sheet shortest, tries several orders and turns in a few seconds, and keeps the best. Parts can sit in each other&#039;s holes and in the hollow of an L or a U.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-it\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENest flat parts on sheets…\u003C/span\u003E opens it with the part&#039;s flat pattern, or — in an assembly — with the flat pattern of every sheet metal part, as many as there are copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a sheet metal part, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELay flat\u003C/span\u003E and then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENest on sheets…\u003C/span\u003E under the flat pattern.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd outlines from a DXF…\u003C/span\u003E adds every closed outline in a DXF file as a part: a drawing from CadCraft saved as DXF, or a file from your cutter&#039;s own software. An outline inside another one is a hole in it. Lines and arcs that join end to end count as one outline. Files in inches, centimetres or metres are changed to millimetres.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELaying sheet metal flat needs an exact geometry kernel (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sheet-metal\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sheet-metal\"\u003ESheet metal\u003C/a\u003E). Outlines from a DXF file always work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-it-up\"\u003ESet it up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor each part give \u003Cstrong\u003Ehow many\u003C/strong\u003E you need and its \u003Cstrong\u003Egrain\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAny direction\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlong the sheet (as drawn)\u003C/span\u003E for brushed or rolled sheet that must run one way, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAcross the sheet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ESheets\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStock\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlates\u003C/span\u003E of a given length and width (and how many you have; 0 = as many as needed), or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA roll\u003C/span\u003E of a given width, as long as needed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdge margin\u003C/strong\u003E is kept clear at every edge of the sheet (for clamps, or a ragged edge).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpace between parts\u003C/strong\u003E is the least distance between two cut lines: at least your cutter&#039;s kerf, plus what the material needs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETurns\u003C/strong\u003E: the angles a part may be turned to, every 90° by default. Fewer turns run faster; more can use the sheet better.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParts may be flipped\u003C/strong\u003E lets a part be laid upside down. Leave it off for sheet metal: a flat pattern laid flipped bends the other way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENest\u003C/span\u003E. The preview shows every sheet; the facts say how many plates (or how much of the roll) it used and how much of the material the parts take up. Anything that did not fit is named.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-it\"\u003ESave it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave DXF\u003C/span\u003E writes the layout as a DXF (R12, millimetres), one sheet under the next: the sheets&#039; outlines on the layer SHEET, the parts on PARTS (arcs kept as arcs), each part&#039;s name on LABELS.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMixing several parts usually nests better than many copies of one: small parts fill the gaps between big ones.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-to-check\"\u003EWhat to check\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft checks every layout again on the true shapes (not the grid it searched with): no two parts closer than the spacing, nothing in the margins. It does not plan the cut: lead-ins, the cutting order, cutting shared edges once and keeping the leftover sheet are your cutter&#039;s software&#039;s job. Check the DXF there before cutting.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Nesting places many flat parts on a sheet so that as little material as possible is left over. PartCraft lays the parts one by one, each where it keeps the used length of the sheet shortest, tries several orders and turns in a few seconds, and keeps the best. Parts can sit in each other's holes and in the hollow of an L or a U. Open it Export › Nest flat parts on sheets… opens it with the part's flat pattern, or — in an assembly — with the flat pattern of every sheet metal part, as many as there are copies. On a sheet metal part, Lay flat and then Nest on sheets… under the flat pattern. Add outlines from a DXF… adds every closed outline in a DXF file as a part: a drawing from CadCraft saved as DXF, or a file from your cutter's own software. An outline inside another one is a hole in it. Lines and arcs that join end to end count as one outline. Files in inches, centimetres or metres are changed to millimetres. Laying sheet metal flat needs an exact geometry kernel (see Sheet metal). Outlines from a DXF file always work. Set it up For each part give how many you need and its grain: Any direction, Along the sheet (as drawn) for brushed or rolled sheet that must run one way, or Across the sheet. Under Sheets: Stock: Plates of a given length and width (and how many you have; 0 = as many as needed), or A roll of a given width, as long as needed. Edge margin is kept clear at every edge of the sheet (for clamps, or a ragged edge). Space between parts is the least distance between two cut lines: at least your cutter's kerf, plus what the material needs. Turns: the angles a part may be turned to, every 90° by default. Fewer turns run faster; more can use the sheet better. Parts may be flipped lets a part be laid upside down. Leave it off for sheet metal: a flat pattern laid flipped bends the other way. Press Nest. The preview shows every sheet; the facts say how many plates (or how much of the roll) it used and how much of the material the parts take up. Anything that did not fit is named. Save it Save DXF writes the layout as a DXF (R12, millimetres), one sheet under the next: the sheets' outlines on the layer SHEET, the parts on PARTS (arcs kept as arcs), each part's name on LABELS. Mixing several parts usually nests better than many copies of one: small parts fill the gaps between big ones. What to check PartCraft checks every layout again on the true shapes (not the grid it searched with): no two parts closer than the spacing, nothing in the margins. It does not plan the cut: lead-ins, the cutting order, cutting shared edges once and keeping the leftover sheet are your cutter's software's job. Check the DXF there before cutting."},{"slug":"draft-analysis","scope":"partcraft","title":"Check draft for moulding and casting","summary":"Colour the part by its draft angle against the pull direction of a mould, see which faces release, which need more draft and which release from the other half, with each one's share of the area.","section":"tasks","order":96,"keywords":["draft analysis","draft","taper","mould","mold","die casting","injection moulding","pull direction","parting line","undercut","release","check draft"],"headings":[{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EParts made in a mould, a die or a sand casting need \u003Cstrong\u003Edraft\u003C/strong\u003E: walls tilted a little so the part slides out. \u003Cstrong\u003EDraft analysis\u003C/strong\u003E colours every face by how much it tilts away from the direction the mould opens (the \u003Cstrong\u003Epull\u003C/strong\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the Design workspace, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft analysis\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EPull\u003C/strong\u003E: the direction the mould opens, along X, Y or Z either way, or \u003Cstrong\u003ESquare to a picked face\u003C/strong\u003E: press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a face\u003C/span\u003E and click a flat face of the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EMinimum draft\u003C/strong\u003E, in degrees: the least tilt your process needs (often 1° to 3°).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe part is coloured:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColour\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGreen: \u003Cstrong\u003EPositive draft\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETilted toward the pull by at least the minimum: releases from this half of the mould\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOchre: \u003Cstrong\u003ENeeds draft\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWithin the minimum either way, such as a vertical wall: add draft here\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed: \u003Cstrong\u003ENegative draft\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETilted away from the pull by at least the minimum: releases from the other half, or is an undercut if the part is to come out of one half\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach class&#039;s share of the part&#039;s surface area is shown beside it. \u003Cstrong\u003EReverse the pull\u003C/strong\u003E looks from the other half of the mould: green and red swap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECurved faces are judged point by point, so a cylinder lying across the pull shows a band that needs draft along its sides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe colours follow the part as you change it. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E turns the analysis off; nothing is saved in the part.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo add draft, use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E feature on the ochre faces (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sweep-loft-draft\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sweep-loft-draft\"\u003ESweep, loft and draft\u003C/a\u003E), then check again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Parts made in a mould, a die or a sand casting need draft: walls tilted a little so the part slides out. Draft analysis colours every face by how much it tilts away from the direction the mould opens (the pull). Turn it on In the Design workspace, open Views and choose Draft analysis. Set Pull: the direction the mould opens, along X, Y or Z either way, or Square to a picked face: press Pick a face and click a flat face of the part. Set Minimum draft, in degrees: the least tilt your process needs (often 1° to 3°). The part is coloured: Colour Means Green: Positive draft Tilted toward the pull by at least the minimum: releases from this half of the mould Ochre: Needs draft Within the minimum either way, such as a vertical wall: add draft here Red: Negative draft Tilted away from the pull by at least the minimum: releases from the other half, or is an undercut if the part is to come out of one half Each class's share of the part's surface area is shown beside it. Reverse the pull looks from the other half of the mould: green and red swap. Curved faces are judged point by point, so a cylinder lying across the pull shows a band that needs draft along its sides. The colours follow the part as you change it. Esc or Close turns the analysis off; nothing is saved in the part. To add draft, use the Draft feature on the ochre faces (see Sweep, loft and draft), then check again."},{"slug":"surface-analysis","scope":"partcraft","title":"Zebra stripes and curvature","summary":"Check how smoothly the faces of any part join, with zebra stripes that follow the view or a colour map of mean curvature, from the Views menu.","section":"tasks","order":97,"keywords":["zebra","zebra stripes","reflection lines","curvature","curvature map","mean curvature","surface quality","continuity","tangent","g1","g2","smooth","crease","highlight lines"],"headings":[{"id":"zebra-stripes","text":"Zebra stripes","level":2},{"id":"curvature","text":"Curvature","level":2},{"id":"the-card","text":"The card","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETwo quick looks at how smooth a part&#039;s faces are, on any part in the Design workspace (the Form workspace has its own, for the form you are editing: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/form-surfaces\" data-topic=\"partcraft/form-surfaces\"\u003EFreeform shapes with Form\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"zebra-stripes\"\u003EZebra stripes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZebra stripes\u003C/span\u003E. The part shows the reflection of a striped light, as a polished surface would:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere two faces join \u003Cstrong\u003Etangentially\u003C/strong\u003E (a fillet on a flat, a smooth Gordon surface), the stripes run on across the join with a bend.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere they meet at a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecrease\u003C/strong\u003E, the stripes break and jump.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere the curvature also matches (G2), the stripes flow on without even a kink.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn the view (drag, or a view-cube face): the stripes are drawn again from the new direction. \u003Cstrong\u003EStripes\u003C/strong\u003E sets how many there are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"curvature\"\u003ECurvature\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurvature\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu. The part is coloured by its \u003Cstrong\u003Emean curvature\u003C/strong\u003E: blue where it is hollow (concave), pale where it is flat, red where it bulges (convex). The card says at what curvature the colours reach full strength, in 1/mm and as a radius. Sharp edges count as very curved, so they show strongly; look at the faces between them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-card\"\u003EThe card\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth open the analysis card at the bottom left of the view, which also holds the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/draft-analysis\" data-topic=\"partcraft/draft-analysis\"\u003Edraft analysis\u003C/a\u003E. Switch between them with \u003Cstrong\u003EShow\u003C/strong\u003E. Choosing the open one again in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E turns it off. The colours follow the part as you change it; nothing is saved in the part.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Two quick looks at how smooth a part's faces are, on any part in the Design workspace (the Form workspace has its own, for the form you are editing: see Freeform shapes with Form). Zebra stripes Open Views and choose Zebra stripes. The part shows the reflection of a striped light, as a polished surface would: Where two faces join tangentially (a fillet on a flat, a smooth Gordon surface), the stripes run on across the join with a bend. Where they meet at a crease, the stripes break and jump. Where the curvature also matches (G2), the stripes flow on without even a kink. Turn the view (drag, or a view-cube face): the stripes are drawn again from the new direction. Stripes sets how many there are. Curvature Choose Curvature in the same menu. The part is coloured by its mean curvature: blue where it is hollow (concave), pale where it is flat, red where it bulges (convex). The card says at what curvature the colours reach full strength, in 1/mm and as a radius. Sharp edges count as very curved, so they show strongly; look at the faces between them. The card Both open the analysis card at the bottom left of the view, which also holds the draft analysis. Switch between them with Show. Choosing the open one again in Views, Close or Esc turns it off. The colours follow the part as you change it; nothing is saved in the part."},{"slug":"change-sizes","scope":"partcraft","title":"Change sizes without opening the sketch","summary":"Drive a part from the numbers in the right panel: sketch dimensions, depths, counts and angles, how to plan a part so the right numbers drive it, and where parameters and equations take over.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["parameters","driving dimensions","design intent","change size","equations","global variables","design table","configurations","resize","modify dimension"],"headings":[{"id":"change-a-sketch-dimension-from-the-panel","text":"Change a sketch dimension from the panel","level":2},{"id":"type-the-main-number-straight-away","text":"Type the main number straight away","level":2},{"id":"change-the-number-in-the-history","text":"Change the number in the history","level":2},{"id":"drag-it-in-the-view","text":"Drag it in the view","level":2},{"id":"the-numbers-that-drive-a-part","text":"The numbers that drive a part","level":2},{"id":"plan-the-part-so-the-right-numbers-drive-it","text":"Plan the part so the right numbers drive it","level":2},{"id":"beyond-single-numbers","text":"Beyond single numbers","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery size in a PartCraft part is a number you can change later: the dimensions in its sketches and the settings of its features. You do not need to open a sketch to change its dimensions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-sketch-dimension-from-the-panel\"\u003EChange a sketch dimension from the panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a feature in the history, for example &quot;Extrude1&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder the extrude&#039;s own settings, \u003Cstrong\u003ESketch dimensions (Sketch1)\u003C/strong\u003E lists every dimension of its sketch: &quot;Distance&quot;, &quot;Diameter&quot;, &quot;Angle (°)&quot; and so on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a new value in one box and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sketch solves with the new value, and the whole part rebuilds. Selecting the sketch itself shows the same list as \u003Cstrong\u003EDriving dimensions\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA radius or diameter must be more than zero, and an angle is between 0 and 180 degrees; the status bar says so if not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhich box is which? The boxes are listed in the order the dimensions were made. If you are not sure, open the sketch (double-click it), double-click the dimension on the canvas, and change it there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-the-main-number-straight-away\"\u003EType the main number straight away\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a feature in the history (or just add it) and type a number: it goes into the feature&#039;s main box, the one you change most (an Extrude&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EDepth\u003C/strong\u003E, a Revolve&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EAngle\u003C/strong\u003E, a Hole&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EDiameter\u003C/strong\u003E, a pattern&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003ECount\u003C/strong\u003E, a Fillet&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003ERadius\u003C/strong\u003E, a Shell&#039;s wall). Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to set it, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the old one. Adding an Extrude says so in the status bar: &quot;Extrude1 added. Change its numbers on the right, or type a number now for its depth.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-the-number-in-the-history\"\u003EChange the number in the history\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach feature&#039;s row in the history shows its main number beside its name: &quot;Extrude1 25 mm&quot;, &quot;Hole1 Ø6.6 thru&quot;, &quot;Fillet1 R2&quot;, &quot;LPattern1 4 × 20 mm&quot;. Double-click the number (or select the row and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F2\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and a box opens right there. Type the new value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps the old one. It works exactly like the feature&#039;s box on the right: the same limits, \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E to type an equation such as \u003Ccode\u003E=width/2\u003C/code\u003E, and one step to undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn an Extrude, a Hole or a pattern, a double-click anywhere on the row opens the box. On a Fillet, Chamfer, Shell, Draft or Thread a double-click on the name still goes to picking its edges or faces, so double-click the number itself. Sketches have no number in the row: a double-click opens the sketch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drag-it-in-the-view\"\u003EDrag it in the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect an Extrude in the history (or click it in the view, then its row) and an orange arrow stands on its end face, with the depth in a box beside it. Drag the arrow: the part follows as you drag, and letting go sets the new depth as one step (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back). Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging to go back to where you started. The depth moves in steps of a tenth of its size: 1 mm on a 25 mm extrude, 0.1 mm on a 3 mm one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA Revolve gets a curved arrow at the end of its turn: drag it round the axis to change the angle, in whole degrees from 1° to 360°.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr click into the box beside the arrow, type a value (or \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E and an equation) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. There is no arrow on an Extrude that goes \u003Cstrong\u003EThrough all\u003C/strong\u003E or up to a face, or on a value an equation drives: change those on the right.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-numbers-that-drive-a-part\"\u003EThe numbers that drive a part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhere\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you can change\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery dimension: lengths, distances, radii, diameters, angles\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude and Cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDepth, end condition, direction, boss or cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERevolve and revolved cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle, axis, boss or cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHole\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDiameter, depth, through all, counterbore diameter and depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet, Chamfer, Shell\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadius, distance, wall thickness and direction, and which edges or faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear and Circular\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount, spacing or angle, direction or axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPart\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECurves (how finely round shapes are faceted)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere the section cuts, the detail&#039;s size and scale, GD&amp;T values\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plan-the-part-so-the-right-numbers-drive-it\"\u003EPlan the part so the right numbers drive it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe numbers you give are the ones you can change. A few habits make later changes behave:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDimension what matters for the job.\u003C/strong\u003E If a hole must stay 15 mm from an edge, dimension it from that edge, not from the origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFully define sketches.\u003C/strong\u003E Then a change moves only what it should (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/relations-dimensions\" data-topic=\"partcraft/relations-dimensions\"\u003EFully define a sketch\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUse relations for intent.\u003C/strong\u003E Equal on two holes means one diameter drives both. Symmetric shapes: put the origin on the center line, or use Midpoint.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESketch on faces made by features.\u003C/strong\u003E A sketch on an extrude&#039;s end face moves when the depth changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPattern instead of copying.\u003C/strong\u003E One hole patterned eight times has one diameter to change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"beyond-single-numbers\"\u003EBeyond single numbers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne number driving several:\u003C/strong\u003E use parameters and equations: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/parameters\" data-topic=\"partcraft/parameters\"\u003EDrive a part with parameters\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESeveral sizes of one part:\u003C/strong\u003E save each as a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/design-tables\" data-topic=\"partcraft/design-tables\"\u003Econfiguration\u003C/a\u003E; they are kept in the part file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArithmetic in a number box:\u003C/strong\u003E type \u003Ccode\u003E=40/2\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E=width / 2\u003C/code\u003E. That makes an equation; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/equations-two-way\" data-topic=\"partcraft/equations-two-way\"\u003EEquations both ways\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInch units:\u003C/strong\u003E the part shows millimeters. In a number box with \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E, or in a parameter, \u003Ccode\u003E2in\u003C/code\u003E is turned into 50.8.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every size in a PartCraft part is a number you can change later: the dimensions in its sketches and the settings of its features. You do not need to open a sketch to change its dimensions. Change a sketch dimension from the panel Click a feature in the history, for example \"Extrude1\". Under the extrude's own settings, Sketch dimensions (Sketch1) lists every dimension of its sketch: \"Distance\", \"Diameter\", \"Angle (°)\" and so on. Type a new value in one box and press Enter or Tab. The sketch solves with the new value, and the whole part rebuilds. Selecting the sketch itself shows the same list as Driving dimensions. A radius or diameter must be more than zero, and an angle is between 0 and 180 degrees; the status bar says so if not. Which box is which? The boxes are listed in the order the dimensions were made. If you are not sure, open the sketch (double-click it), double-click the dimension on the canvas, and change it there. Type the main number straight away Select a feature in the history (or just add it) and type a number: it goes into the feature's main box, the one you change most (an Extrude's Depth, a Revolve's Angle, a Hole's Diameter, a pattern's Count, a Fillet's Radius, a Shell's wall). Press Enter to set it, or Esc to keep the old one. Adding an Extrude says so in the status bar: \"Extrude1 added. Change its numbers on the right, or type a number now for its depth.\" Change the number in the history Each feature's row in the history shows its main number beside its name: \"Extrude1 25 mm\", \"Hole1 Ø6.6 thru\", \"Fillet1 R2\", \"LPattern1 4 × 20 mm\". Double-click the number (or select the row and press F2) and a box opens right there. Type the new value and press Enter; Esc keeps the old one. It works exactly like the feature's box on the right: the same limits, = to type an equation such as =width/2, and one step to undo. On an Extrude, a Hole or a pattern, a double-click anywhere on the row opens the box. On a Fillet, Chamfer, Shell, Draft or Thread a double-click on the name still goes to picking its edges or faces, so double-click the number itself. Sketches have no number in the row: a double-click opens the sketch. Drag it in the view Select an Extrude in the history (or click it in the view, then its row) and an orange arrow stands on its end face, with the depth in a box beside it. Drag the arrow: the part follows as you drag, and letting go sets the new depth as one step (Ctrl+Z puts it back). Press Esc while dragging to go back to where you started. The depth moves in steps of a tenth of its size: 1 mm on a 25 mm extrude, 0.1 mm on a 3 mm one. A Revolve gets a curved arrow at the end of its turn: drag it round the axis to change the angle, in whole degrees from 1° to 360°. Or click into the box beside the arrow, type a value (or = and an equation) and press Enter. There is no arrow on an Extrude that goes Through all or up to a face, or on a value an equation drives: change those on the right. The numbers that drive a part Where What you can change Sketch Every dimension: lengths, distances, radii, diameters, angles Extrude and Cut Depth, end condition, direction, boss or cut Revolve and revolved cut Angle, axis, boss or cut Hole Diameter, depth, through all, counterbore diameter and depth Fillet, Chamfer, Shell Radius, distance, wall thickness and direction, and which edges or faces Linear and Circular Count, spacing or angle, direction or axis Part Curves (how finely round shapes are faceted) Drawing Where the section cuts, the detail's size and scale, GD&T values Plan the part so the right numbers drive it The numbers you give are the ones you can change. A few habits make later changes behave: Dimension what matters for the job. If a hole must stay 15 mm from an edge, dimension it from that edge, not from the origin. Fully define sketches. Then a change moves only what it should (see Fully define a sketch). Use relations for intent. Equal on two holes means one diameter drives both. Symmetric shapes: put the origin on the center line, or use Midpoint. Sketch on faces made by features. A sketch on an extrude's end face moves when the depth changes. Pattern instead of copying. One hole patterned eight times has one diameter to change. Beyond single numbers One number driving several: use parameters and equations: Drive a part with parameters. Several sizes of one part: save each as a configuration; they are kept in the part file. Arithmetic in a number box: type =40/2 or =width / 2. That makes an equation; see Equations both ways. Inch units: the part shows millimeters. In a number box with =, or in a parameter, 2in is turned into 50.8. See What is not built yet."},{"slug":"parameters","scope":"partcraft","title":"Drive a part with parameters","summary":"Name the sizes that matter (width, thickness, bolt size), link any feature number or sketch dimension to them with =, and change the whole part from one table.","section":"tasks","order":102,"keywords":["parameters","where used","used by","global variables","variables","equations","design intent","driving dimensions","link","expression","formula","name a dimension","linked values"],"headings":[{"id":"add-parameters","text":"Add parameters","level":2},{"id":"link-a-number-to-a-parameter","text":"Link a number to a parameter","level":2},{"id":"change-a-parameter","text":"Change a parameter","level":2},{"id":"see-where-a-parameter-is-used","text":"See where a parameter is used","level":2},{"id":"unlink-rename-delete","text":"Unlink, rename, delete","level":2},{"id":"expressions","text":"Expressions","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA parameter is a named number, such as \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth = 120\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Emargin = bolt * 1.5\u003C/code\u003E. Link a depth, a hole diameter, a pattern count or a sketch dimension to it, and changing the parameter changes all of them in one step. This is how you keep design intent in the part instead of in your head.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-parameters\"\u003EAdd parameters\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, click the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EVariables\u003C/strong\u003E, type a name in the &quot;name&quot; box, for example \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType its value in the next box, for example \u003Ccode\u003E120\u003C/code\u003E, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA value can be a number or an expression of other parameters: \u003Ccode\u003Ebolt * 1.5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth / 2 - 10\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eif(width &gt; 100, 6, 4)\u003C/code\u003E. The \u003Cstrong\u003EValue\u003C/strong\u003E column shows what it works out to. A name starts with a letter or \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E and has only letters, digits and \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E; names of functions and units (\u003Ccode\u003Esin\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Epi\u003C/code\u003E) are refused, and so is a loop such as \u003Ccode\u003Ea = b\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003Eb = a\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"link-a-number-to-a-parameter\"\u003ELink a number to a parameter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a feature in the history, for example &quot;Extrude1&quot;. Its settings show on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a number box, such as \u003Cstrong\u003EDepth (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E and an expression: \u003Ccode\u003E=thick\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E=width / 4\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe box now shows the result, and an \u003Cstrong\u003Eƒ\u003C/strong\u003E button appears next to its label. Point at it to see the equation. Sketch dimensions work the same way, both in the \u003Cstrong\u003ESketch dimensions\u003C/strong\u003E list of a feature and in the dimension box of an open sketch (double-click a dimension, type \u003Ccode\u003E=width\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENumbers you can link: extrude depth, revolve angle, hole diameter, depth and counterbore sizes, pattern count, spacing and angle, fillet radius, chamfer distance, shell wall thickness, every sketch dimension, and the sizes of the newer features (second depth, taper, countersink, reference plane distance, draft angle and others).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-parameter\"\u003EChange a parameter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E tab, type a new value or expression in the \u003Cstrong\u003EExpression\u003C/strong\u003E column and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Everything linked to it changes and the part rebuilds, as one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change a number from the other end, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/equations-two-way\" data-topic=\"partcraft/equations-two-way\"\u003EEquations both ways\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-where-a-parameter-is-used\"\u003ESee where a parameter is used\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder each parameter on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E tab, a line says what uses it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe numbers it drives, such as \u003Cstrong\u003EExtrude1 depth\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ESketch1 D2 (distance)\u003C/strong\u003E. Click one to select its feature and show its properties;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe numbers it drives \u003Cstrong\u003Ethrough\u003C/strong\u003E other parameters, such as \u003Cstrong\u003EHole1 diameter (through h, d)\u003C/strong\u003E when \u003Ccode\u003Ed = h + 2\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eh = w / 2\u003C/code\u003E;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Eparameters\u003C/strong\u003E whose expressions use it, the \u003Cstrong\u003Econfigurations\u003C/strong\u003E that set it, and the \u003Cstrong\u003Escripts\u003C/strong\u003E that read it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript\"\u003EWrite and run scripts\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;Not used yet.&quot; means nothing uses it: you can delete it without asking.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"unlink-rename-delete\"\u003EUnlink, rename, delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnlink a number:\u003C/strong\u003E click its \u003Cstrong\u003Eƒ\u003C/strong\u003E button, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlink\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EEquations\u003C/strong\u003E list. It keeps its current value and no parameter changes it any more.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERename a parameter:\u003C/strong\u003E type a new name in its \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E box. Every expression that uses it is rewritten.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete a parameter:\u003C/strong\u003E click × on its row. If something uses it, PartCraft asks whether to write its current value into those expressions first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDeleting a feature\u003C/strong\u003E drops the equations of its numbers; the status bar says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EParameters are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E file (as file version 3). A part without parameters is saved exactly as before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"expressions\"\u003EExpressions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWrite\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E120\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12.5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1e3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea number (millimetres, or degrees for an angle)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2in\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.5cm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0.2m\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea length in other units, turned into millimetres\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E30deg\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0.5rad\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ean angle, turned into degrees\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E+ - * / %\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E^\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Earithmetic; \u003Ccode\u003E^\u003C/code\u003E is a power, \u003Ccode\u003E-2^2\u003C/code\u003E is −4\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esin cos tan asin acos atan atan2\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etrigonometry in degrees\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esqrt pow exp ln log abs sign\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eother functions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil floor int round(x, digits) min max\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erounding and limits\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eif(test, a, b)\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003E&lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;= == !=\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea choice: \u003Ccode\u003Eif(bolt &gt;= 16, 6, 8)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epi\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3.14159…\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENames are case sensitive: \u003Ccode\u003EWidth\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth\u003C/code\u003E are two parameters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/design-tables\" data-topic=\"partcraft/design-tables\"\u003EConfigurations and design tables\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/scripts-and-cli\" data-topic=\"partcraft/scripts-and-cli\"\u003EScripts and the command line\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A parameter is a named number, such as width = 120 or margin = bolt * 1.5. Link a depth, a hole diameter, a pattern count or a sketch dimension to it, and changing the parameter changes all of them in one step. This is how you keep design intent in the part instead of in your head. Add parameters In the right panel, click the Parameters tab. Under Variables, type a name in the \"name\" box, for example width. Type its value in the next box, for example 120, and press Enter (or click Add). A value can be a number or an expression of other parameters: bolt * 1.5, width / 2 - 10, if(width \u003E 100, 6, 4). The Value column shows what it works out to. A name starts with a letter or _ and has only letters, digits and _; names of functions and units (sin, mm, pi) are refused, and so is a loop such as a = b with b = a. Link a number to a parameter Click a feature in the history, for example \"Extrude1\". Its settings show on the Properties tab. In a number box, such as Depth (mm), type = and an expression: =thick or =width / 4. Press Enter. The box now shows the result, and an ƒ button appears next to its label. Point at it to see the equation. Sketch dimensions work the same way, both in the Sketch dimensions list of a feature and in the dimension box of an open sketch (double-click a dimension, type =width, press Enter). Numbers you can link: extrude depth, revolve angle, hole diameter, depth and counterbore sizes, pattern count, spacing and angle, fillet radius, chamfer distance, shell wall thickness, every sketch dimension, and the sizes of the newer features (second depth, taper, countersink, reference plane distance, draft angle and others). Change a parameter On the Parameters tab, type a new value or expression in the Expression column and press Enter. Everything linked to it changes and the part rebuilds, as one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. To change a number from the other end, see Equations both ways. See where a parameter is used Under each parameter on the Parameters tab, a line says what uses it: the numbers it drives, such as Extrude1 depth or Sketch1 D2 (distance). Click one to select its feature and show its properties; the numbers it drives through other parameters, such as Hole1 diameter (through h, d) when d = h + 2 and h = w / 2; the parameters whose expressions use it, the configurations that set it, and the scripts that read it (see Write and run scripts). \"Not used yet.\" means nothing uses it: you can delete it without asking. Unlink, rename, delete Unlink a number: click its ƒ button, or Unlink in the Equations list. It keeps its current value and no parameter changes it any more. Rename a parameter: type a new name in its Name box. Every expression that uses it is rewritten. Delete a parameter: click × on its row. If something uses it, PartCraft asks whether to write its current value into those expressions first. Deleting a feature drops the equations of its numbers; the status bar says which. Parameters are saved in the .partcraft.json file (as file version 3). A part without parameters is saved exactly as before. Expressions Write Means 120, 12.5, 1e3 a number (millimetres, or degrees for an angle) 2in, 1ft, 3\", 1.5cm, 0.2m a length in other units, turned into millimetres 30deg, 0.5rad an angle, turned into degrees + - * / % and ^ arithmetic; ^ is a power, -2^2 is −4 sin cos tan asin acos atan atan2 trigonometry in degrees sqrt pow exp ln log abs sign other functions ceil floor int round(x, digits) min max rounding and limits if(test, a, b) with \u003C \u003C= \u003E \u003E= == != a choice: if(bolt \u003E= 16, 6, 8) pi 3.14159… Names are case sensitive: Width and width are two parameters. See also Configurations and design tables and Scripts and the command line."},{"slug":"equations-two-way","scope":"partcraft","title":"Equations both ways","summary":"Change a parameter and the part follows; type a new size into a driven number and PartCraft works out the parameter behind it, asking which one when several could change.","section":"tasks","order":104,"keywords":["equations","solve backward","driven dimension","two way","reverse","goal seek","parameters","linked values","which parameter"],"headings":[{"id":"forward-change-the-parameter","text":"Forward: change the parameter","level":2},{"id":"backward-change-the-driven-number","text":"Backward: change the driven number","level":2},{"id":"when-it-cannot","text":"When it cannot","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn equation such as \u003Cstrong\u003EExtrude1 depth = thick + extra\u003C/strong\u003E works in both directions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"forward-change-the-parameter\"\u003EForward: change the parameter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E tab, change \u003Ccode\u003Ethick\u003C/code\u003E from 20 to 30. Extrude1&#039;s depth becomes 30 + extra, and the part rebuilds.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"backward-change-the-driven-number\"\u003EBackward: change the driven number\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick &quot;Extrude1&quot; in the history. Its \u003Cstrong\u003EDepth (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E box shows \u003Cstrong\u003Eƒ\u003C/strong\u003E: an equation drives it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the size you want, for example \u003Ccode\u003E40\u003C/code\u003E, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft solves for the parameter behind the equation, through as many steps as it takes (\u003Ccode\u003Edepth = hub_h\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ehub_h = disc_t + 50\u003C/code\u003E: typing a height changes \u003Ccode\u003Edisc_t\u003C/code\u003E). The status bar says what changed, for example that extra went from 5 to 15.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen two or more parameters drive the number, a box asks \u003Cstrong\u003EWhich parameter should change?\u003C/strong\u003E and lists them. Click one; the others keep their values. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E changes nothing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same works in a sketch: double-click a driven dimension, type a value, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. It also works in the \u003Cstrong\u003EValue\u003C/strong\u003E column of the parameter table, for a parameter that is itself an expression.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-it-cannot\"\u003EWhen it cannot\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA number that must be whole (a pattern count) or stay in range (an angle up to 360°) is refused before anything changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn equation of fixed numbers only (\u003Ccode\u003E=40\u003C/code\u003E) has no parameter to change: the number you type replaces it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf no value of the parameter gives the size you typed, the status bar says so and nothing changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA sketch that cannot be solved with the new value keeps its previous values, and the status bar names the sketch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery change, either way, is one step for \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"An equation such as Extrude1 depth = thick + extra works in both directions. Forward: change the parameter On the Parameters tab, change thick from 20 to 30. Extrude1's depth becomes 30 + extra, and the part rebuilds. Backward: change the driven number Click \"Extrude1\" in the history. Its Depth (mm) box shows ƒ: an equation drives it. Type the size you want, for example 40, and press Enter. PartCraft solves for the parameter behind the equation, through as many steps as it takes (depth = hub_h, hub_h = disc_t + 50: typing a height changes disc_t). The status bar says what changed, for example that extra went from 5 to 15. When two or more parameters drive the number, a box asks Which parameter should change? and lists them. Click one; the others keep their values. Cancel changes nothing. The same works in a sketch: double-click a driven dimension, type a value, press Enter. It also works in the Value column of the parameter table, for a parameter that is itself an expression. When it cannot A number that must be whole (a pattern count) or stay in range (an angle up to 360°) is refused before anything changes. An equation of fixed numbers only (=40) has no parameter to change: the number you type replaces it. If no value of the parameter gives the size you typed, the status bar says so and nothing changes. A sketch that cannot be solved with the new value keeps its previous values, and the status bar names the sketch. Every change, either way, is one step for Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y."},{"slug":"design-tables","scope":"partcraft","title":"Configurations and design tables","summary":"Keep several sizes and versions of one part as named configurations saved in the part file, with features turned on or off in each, switch between them with one click, bring them in or out as a CSV design table, or build them all from the command line.","section":"tasks","order":106,"keywords":["design table","configurations","configuration","sizes","family of parts","csv","spreadsheet","variants","table driven","iparts","part family","suppress","suppressed","turn off a feature","options","with or without","configurator"],"headings":[{"id":"save-a-configuration","text":"Save a configuration","level":2},{"id":"switch-update-rename-delete","text":"Switch, update, rename, delete","level":2},{"id":"turn-features-on-and-off-in-a-configuration","text":"Turn features on and off in a configuration","level":2},{"id":"bring-a-design-table-in-or-out","text":"Bring a design table in or out","level":2},{"id":"every-size-at-once","text":"Every size at once","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Econfiguration\u003C/strong\u003E is one size of the part: a named set of values for its parameters. The part keeps its configurations in its own file, so they travel with it. A \u003Cstrong\u003Edesign table\u003C/strong\u003E is the same thing as a CSV file, one row per configuration.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConfigurations set \u003Cstrong\u003Eparameters\u003C/strong\u003E, so add those first (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/parameters\" data-topic=\"partcraft/parameters\"\u003EDrive a part with parameters\u003C/a\u003E). A configuration stores the parameters that are plain numbers; parameters worked out from others (such as \u003Ccode\u003Earea = w * d\u003C/code\u003E) follow on their own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-a-configuration\"\u003ESave a configuration\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E tab, set the parameters for one size of the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EConfigurations\u003C/strong\u003E, type a name (for example \u003Ccode\u003ELarge\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave current values\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe new configuration is marked \u003Cstrong\u003Eactive\u003C/strong\u003E. Change a parameter afterwards and it says \u003Cstrong\u003Eactive, changed since\u003C/strong\u003E, so you know the part no longer matches it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"switch-update-rename-delete\"\u003ESwitch, update, rename, delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E sets the parameters to that configuration&#039;s values and rebuilds the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate\u003C/span\u003E saves the current values into that configuration.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo rename one, type the new name in its box and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E removes the configuration. The parameters keep their values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach of these is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E goes back. If you rename a parameter, every configuration follows the new name; if you delete one, it drops out of them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-features-on-and-off-in-a-configuration\"\u003ETurn features on and off in a configuration\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA configuration also keeps which features are turned off, so one part can have versions \u003Cstrong\u003Ewith\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Ewithout\u003C/strong\u003E something: mounting holes, a chamfer, a boss.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn the feature off: click it in the history, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESuppress\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave the configuration as usual, for example \u003Ccode\u003ENo holes\u003C/code\u003E. Its row lists what it turns off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn the feature back on (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnsuppress\u003C/span\u003E) and save another configuration, for example \u003Ccode\u003EWith holes\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnce any configuration turns a feature off, applying a configuration turns its features off and \u003Cstrong\u003Eevery other feature on\u003C/strong\u003E, together with its parameter values, in one step. A configuration saved before features were turned off in any of them counts as &quot;everything on&quot;. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate\u003C/span\u003E saves the features as they are now, with the values.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA part whose configurations turn features off is saved as a newer file version: an older PartCraft says the file is from a newer version instead of opening it without them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe CSV design table carries parameter values only. Importing a table over a configuration changes its values and keeps the features it turns off; the command line&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003E--table\u003C/code\u003E builds the values only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe part file is saved with the configurations and with whichever one was active. The part opens with the values it was saved with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bring-a-design-table-in-or-out\"\u003EBring a design table in or out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA design table is a CSV file with a column for each parameter and a row for each configuration:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ename,width,thick,bolt\nsmall,100,6,6.6\nmedium,140,8,8.5\nlarge,200,12,13\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first column may be called \u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Econfig\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Econfiguration\u003C/code\u003E; every other column must be the name of a parameter of the part. Cells are numbers, with units if you like (\u003Ccode\u003E8in\u003C/code\u003E). An empty cell leaves that parameter out of the row.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport CSV\u003C/span\u003E adds each row as a configuration. A row with the name of an existing configuration replaces it. Importing is one step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport CSV\u003C/span\u003E writes the configurations out. With none yet, it writes one row of the current values, a starting point for a spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA configuration name that a spreadsheet would read as a formula (starting with \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E) is written with a leading \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"every-size-at-once\"\u003EEvery size at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe command line tool builds one file per row of a CSV design table:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Enode tools/partcraft-cli/partcraft.mjs build bracket.pcscript --table sizes.csv --out-dir sizes --format step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExport the configurations as CSV first, then pass that file with \u003Ccode\u003E--table\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/scripts-and-cli\" data-topic=\"partcraft/scripts-and-cli\"\u003EScripts and the command line\u003C/a\u003E. A part saved as a script keeps its configurations in a \u003Ccode\u003Econfigs json …\u003C/code\u003E line.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A configuration is one size of the part: a named set of values for its parameters. The part keeps its configurations in its own file, so they travel with it. A design table is the same thing as a CSV file, one row per configuration. Configurations set parameters, so add those first (see Drive a part with parameters). A configuration stores the parameters that are plain numbers; parameters worked out from others (such as area = w * d) follow on their own. Save a configuration On the Parameters tab, set the parameters for one size of the part. Under Configurations, type a name (for example Large) and press Save current values or Enter. The new configuration is marked active. Change a parameter afterwards and it says active, changed since, so you know the part no longer matches it. Switch, update, rename, delete Apply sets the parameters to that configuration's values and rebuilds the part. Update saves the current values into that configuration. To rename one, type the new name in its box and press Enter. Delete removes the configuration. The parameters keep their values. Each of these is one step: Ctrl+Z goes back. If you rename a parameter, every configuration follows the new name; if you delete one, it drops out of them. Turn features on and off in a configuration A configuration also keeps which features are turned off, so one part can have versions with and without something: mounting holes, a chamfer, a boss. Turn the feature off: click it in the history, then Suppress in Properties. Save the configuration as usual, for example No holes. Its row lists what it turns off. Turn the feature back on (Unsuppress) and save another configuration, for example With holes. Once any configuration turns a feature off, applying a configuration turns its features off and every other feature on, together with its parameter values, in one step. A configuration saved before features were turned off in any of them counts as \"everything on\". Update saves the features as they are now, with the values. A part whose configurations turn features off is saved as a newer file version: an older PartCraft says the file is from a newer version instead of opening it without them. The CSV design table carries parameter values only. Importing a table over a configuration changes its values and keeps the features it turns off; the command line's --table builds the values only. The part file is saved with the configurations and with whichever one was active. The part opens with the values it was saved with. Bring a design table in or out A design table is a CSV file with a column for each parameter and a row for each configuration: name,width,thick,bolt small,100,6,6.6 medium,140,8,8.5 large,200,12,13 The first column may be called name, config or configuration; every other column must be the name of a parameter of the part. Cells are numbers, with units if you like (8in). An empty cell leaves that parameter out of the row. Import CSV adds each row as a configuration. A row with the name of an existing configuration replaces it. Importing is one step. Export CSV writes the configurations out. With none yet, it writes one row of the current values, a starting point for a spreadsheet. A configuration name that a spreadsheet would read as a formula (starting with =, +, - or @) is written with a leading '. Every size at once The command line tool builds one file per row of a CSV design table: node tools/partcraft-cli/partcraft.mjs build bracket.pcscript --table sizes.csv --out-dir sizes --format step Export the configurations as CSV first, then pass that file with --table. See Scripts and the command line. A part saved as a script keeps its configurations in a configs json … line."},{"slug":"standard-parts","scope":"partcraft","title":"Open a standard part","summary":"Start a part from a bolt, screw, nut, washer, pin, bearing or key sized from its standard, with every size of the standard's table as a configuration.","section":"tasks","order":107,"keywords":["standard part","standard parts","bolt","screw","hex","nut","washer","pin","dowel","bearing","ball bearing","key","parallel key","fastener","iso 4017","iso 4014","iso 4032","iso 7089","iso 2338","iso 15","din 6885","asme","template","library"],"headings":[{"id":"open-one","text":"Open one","level":2},{"id":"change-the-size","text":"Change the size","level":2},{"id":"what-is-modelled","text":"What is modelled","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft opens common mechanical parts ready made: the dimensions come from the standards&#039; tables, and every size in the table is a configuration you can switch to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-one\"\u003EOpen one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStandard part\u003C/span\u003E in the File group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPart\u003C/span\u003E, choose the kind, for example \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHex head screw (ISO 4017)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E, choose the size, for example \u003Ccode\u003EM10 × 40\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe part opens as a new, unsaved part. Save it to keep it (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPart\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStandard\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EParameters\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHex head screw, hex bolt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO 4017, ISO 4014, ASME B18.2.1 (inch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ed\u003C/code\u003E thread size, \u003Ccode\u003Es\u003C/code\u003E across flats, \u003Ccode\u003Ek\u003C/code\u003E head height, \u003Ccode\u003El\u003C/code\u003E length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHex nut\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO 4032, ASME B18.2.2 (inch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ed\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Es\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eheight\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlain washer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO 7089\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ed1\u003C/code\u003E inside, \u003Ccode\u003Ed2\u003C/code\u003E outside, \u003Ccode\u003Eh\u003C/code\u003E thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParallel pin\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO 2338\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ed\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003El\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDeep groove ball bearing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO 15\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ed\u003C/code\u003E bore, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E outside, \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E width\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParallel key\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDIN 6885-1, form A\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eb\u003C/code\u003E width, \u003Ccode\u003Eh\u003C/code\u003E height, \u003Ccode\u003El\u003C/code\u003E length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-the-size\"\u003EChange the size\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach size is a configuration (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/design-tables\" data-topic=\"partcraft/design-tables\"\u003EConfigurations and design tables\u003C/a\u003E): open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E and pick another one, or change a parameter, such as \u003Ccode\u003El\u003C/code\u003E for a longer screw, and save it as a configuration of your own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-modelled\"\u003EWhat is modelled\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScrews and bolts: the hexagon head and a plain shank. Nuts: the hexagon with a hole of the thread&#039;s nominal size. Threads are not modelled; add one with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThread\u003C/span\u003E if you need it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/threads\" data-topic=\"partcraft/threads\"\u003EThreads\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPins: a plain cylinder; the end chamfers are left out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBearings: the outside envelope only (a ring of the bore, the outside diameter and the width), which is what you need to place one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeys: the round-ended key.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe part&#039;s material is steel, so the mass panel shows its weight. Gears are not here yet: PartCraft has no tooth feature, and a disc is not a gear. CadCraft draws gears by the drawing convention, and the library has them as drawings.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"PartCraft opens common mechanical parts ready made: the dimensions come from the standards' tables, and every size in the table is a configuration you can switch to. Open one Click Standard part in the File group. In Part, choose the kind, for example Hex head screw (ISO 4017). In Size, choose the size, for example M10 × 40. Click Open. The part opens as a new, unsaved part. Save it to keep it (Ctrl+S). Part Standard Parameters Hex head screw, hex bolt ISO 4017, ISO 4014, ASME B18.2.1 (inch) d thread size, s across flats, k head height, l length Hex nut ISO 4032, ASME B18.2.2 (inch) d, s, height Plain washer ISO 7089 d1 inside, d2 outside, h thickness Parallel pin ISO 2338 d, l Deep groove ball bearing ISO 15 d bore, D outside, B width Parallel key DIN 6885-1, form A b width, h height, l length Change the size Each size is a configuration (see Configurations and design tables): open Parameters and pick another one, or change a parameter, such as l for a longer screw, and save it as a configuration of your own. What is modelled Screws and bolts: the hexagon head and a plain shank. Nuts: the hexagon with a hole of the thread's nominal size. Threads are not modelled; add one with Thread if you need it (see Threads). Pins: a plain cylinder; the end chamfers are left out. Bearings: the outside envelope only (a ring of the bore, the outside diameter and the width), which is what you need to place one. Keys: the round-ended key. The part's material is steel, so the mass panel shows its weight. Gears are not here yet: PartCraft has no tooth feature, and a disc is not a gear. CadCraft draws gears by the drawing convention, and the library has them as drawings."},{"slug":"build-assembly","scope":"partcraft","title":"Build an assembly with joints","summary":"Start an assembly from a part, insert copies and other part files, join them with rigid, revolute, slider, cylindrical, planar, ball and pin-in-slot joints on flat faces, center joints on axes, edit a part in place and save the assembly.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["assembly","mate","mates","joint","rigid","revolute","slider","cylindrical","planar","ball","ball joint","spherical","pin slot","pin in slot","insert component","grounded","fixed","concentric","coincident","edit part","in context","partcraft-asm"],"headings":[{"id":"start-an-assembly","text":"Start an assembly","level":2},{"id":"insert-parts","text":"Insert parts","level":2},{"id":"join-two-parts","text":"Join two parts","level":2},{"id":"joint-settings","text":"Joint settings","level":3},{"id":"example-two-flanges-bolted-face-to-face","text":"Example: two flanges bolted face to face","level":3},{"id":"work-with-the-parts","text":"Work with the parts","level":2},{"id":"edit-a-part-in-place","text":"Edit a part in place","level":3},{"id":"undo-save-and-close","text":"Undo, save and close","level":2},{"id":"joint-problems","text":"Joint problems","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn assembly puts parts together. In PartCraft, \u003Cstrong\u003Ejoints\u003C/strong\u003E place the parts: each joint sits on a flat face of each of two parts and puts them face to face. A revolute joint lets one part turn, a slider lets it slide, a rigid joint holds it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-an-assembly\"\u003EStart an assembly\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBuild a part with a solid (an empty part cannot go into an assembly: &quot;Build a solid first, then put it into an assembly.&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Assembly started with Part1 as the grounded part. Insert more parts (Add copy, Insert, or Edit › Duplicate), then Joint them.&quot; The left panel now lists \u003Cstrong\u003EParts\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EJoints\u003C/strong\u003E, and the toolbar shows the assembly tools. The sketch and feature tools hide.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first part is \u003Cstrong\u003Egrounded\u003C/strong\u003E: it never moves, and everything else is placed relative to it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"insert-parts\"\u003EInsert parts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd copy\u003C/span\u003E inserts another copy of the selected part (or of the last one). Copies share one part: edit it and every copy changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) in the Edit group does the same for the selected part. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copy a part and paste it beside the others, in this assembly or another one; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes it in the same place. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"partcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E inserts a part from a \u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E file on your computer. The part is copied into the assembly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew parts appear 20 mm to the right of everything already there, and stay there until a joint places them. A part with no joint can also be moved by hand: typed values or arrows you drag (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/assembly-bom-explode\" data-topic=\"partcraft/assembly-bom-explode\"\u003EParts list, exploded views and moving parts\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-two-parts\"\u003EJoin two parts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoint\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"J\"\u003EJ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EJ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The right panel says \u003Cstrong\u003ENew joint\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a flat face on the first part. It highlights.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a flat face on the second part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe second part jumps so its face lies against the first part&#039;s face, facing it. The joint appears under \u003Cstrong\u003EJoints\u003C/strong\u003E, for example &quot;Revolute1&quot;, and the right panel shows its settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf both faces were made by revolves, the joint starts as \u003Cstrong\u003Erevolute\u003C/strong\u003E; otherwise \u003Cstrong\u003Erigid\u003C/strong\u003E. Change it after.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECurved faces cannot be picked: &quot;That face is curved. Pick a flat face (to turn about a hole or a revolve&#039;s axis, choose it in the joint afterwards).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels a joint you have started (or click a part in the list on the left).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"joint-settings\"\u003EJoint settings\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne of the joints in the table below\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns the second part about the joint&#039;s axis (square to the faces)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOffset (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoves the second part along the axis, away from the face (negative values push into it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlong X\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EAlong Y\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETilt about X\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETilt about Y\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EAlong the slot\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESlot direction\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe other values, shown for the joints that use them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECentre on\u003C/strong\u003E (for each side)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere the joint&#039;s axis crosses the face: &quot;The face&#039;s own origin&quot;, the axis of a revolve (&quot;Axis of Revolve1&quot;) or one hole&#039;s axis (&quot;Hole1, hole 2&quot;)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery value a joint lets move has a drag slider under its box; the others are set once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EJoint\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat moves\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESet once\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERigid\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle, Offset\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERevolute\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle (a hinge)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESlider\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset (a drawer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECylindrical\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle and Offset (a shaft in a plain bearing)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlanar\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlong X, Along Y across the face, and Angle (a part sliding on a table)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBall\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle (spin) and Tilt about X and Y, about the joint&#039;s centre (a ball joint)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPin in slot\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlong the slot and Angle (a pin in a straight slot)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESlot direction: the slot&#039;s angle from the face&#039;s X\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe X and Y of a face are those of the first part&#039;s face; the joint&#039;s centre is set by \u003Cstrong\u003ECentre on\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;The face&#039;s own origin&quot; is the part&#039;s origin projected onto the face, which is often not where you want the parts to line up. For round parts and pins, set \u003Cstrong\u003ECentre on\u003C/strong\u003E to the revolve&#039;s or hole&#039;s axis on both sides: the axes then line up, like a concentric mate.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"example-two-flanges-bolted-face-to-face\"\u003EExample: two flanges bolted face to face\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the flange part (a revolve with a circular pattern of holes) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd copy\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"J\"\u003EJ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EJ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click the flat face of the first flange&#039;s disc (the face the bolt holes start from), then the same face on the second flange. The faces were made by the revolve, so the joint is revolute.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003ECentre on\u003C/strong\u003E to &quot;Axis of Revolve1&quot; for both sides. The flanges now share an axis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E22.5\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Cstrong\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/strong\u003E to line up the bolt holes, or drag the slider.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"work-with-the-parts\"\u003EWork with the parts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a part in the view or in the list to select it. The right panel shows its part name, how many copies there are, its volume, its joints, and whether it is grounded, set by its joints, or &quot;Not joined: stays where it was put&quot;. Buttons:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit this part\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit part\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or double-click the part in the list): opens the part for editing;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert a copy\u003C/span\u003E;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake it the grounded part\u003C/span\u003E;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E: also removes its joints, after asking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn assembly keeps at least one part: &quot;An assembly keeps at least one part. Close it instead.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"edit-a-part-in-place\"\u003EEdit a part in place\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a part and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit part\u003C/span\u003E. The part opens with its history; the heading says &quot;Bracket (in Bracket assembly)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange it like any part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. The assembly updates: every copy of that part changes, and joints on faces made by sketch edges follow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBringing the change back is one step in the assembly&#039;s undo list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-save-and-close\"\u003EUndo, save and close\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe assembly has its own undo list: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E work on joints, inserts and settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;assembly name&gt;.partcraft-asm.json\u003C/code\u003E, with every part inside it. Open it later with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E closes the assembly and returns to the part you started from. If the assembly is unsaved it asks: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard the assembly\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave first\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo rename the assembly, click empty space so nothing is selected and change \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"joint-problems\"\u003EJoint problems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA part no joint reaches is listed as \u003Cstrong\u003Efree\u003C/strong\u003E and the panel says &quot;Bracket2 is not joined to the grounded part, so it stays where it was put.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EJoints must form a tree from the grounded part. A joint that closes a loop is not solved: &quot;Revolute3 closes a loop and is off by 2.000 mm. Joints in a loop are not solved yet; remove one.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf a face or axis a joint uses is gone after editing a part: &quot;The face this joint uses is gone from Bracket.&quot; Remove the joint and make it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENext: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/check-assembly\" data-topic=\"partcraft/check-assembly\"\u003ECheck an assembly for clashes and motion\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"An assembly puts parts together. In PartCraft, joints place the parts: each joint sits on a flat face of each of two parts and puts them face to face. A revolute joint lets one part turn, a slider lets it slide, a rigid joint holds it. Start an assembly Build a part with a solid (an empty part cannot go into an assembly: \"Build a solid first, then put it into an assembly.\"). Choose Assembly in the toolbar. The status bar says \"Assembly started with Part1 as the grounded part. Insert more parts (Add copy, Insert, or Edit › Duplicate), then Joint them.\" The left panel now lists Parts and Joints, and the toolbar shows the assembly tools. The sketch and feature tools hide. The first part is grounded: it never moves, and everything else is placed relative to it. Insert parts Add copy inserts another copy of the selected part (or of the last one). Copies share one part: edit it and every copy changes. Duplicate (Ctrl+D) in the Edit group does the same for the selected part. Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V copy a part and paste it beside the others, in this assembly or another one; Ctrl+Shift+V pastes it in the same place. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Insert inserts a part from a .partcraft.json file on your computer. The part is copied into the assembly. New parts appear 20 mm to the right of everything already there, and stay there until a joint places them. A part with no joint can also be moved by hand: typed values or arrows you drag (see Parts list, exploded views and moving parts). Join two parts Choose Joint or press J. The right panel says New joint. Click a flat face on the first part. It highlights. Click a flat face on the second part. The second part jumps so its face lies against the first part's face, facing it. The joint appears under Joints, for example \"Revolute1\", and the right panel shows its settings. If both faces were made by revolves, the joint starts as revolute; otherwise rigid. Change it after. Curved faces cannot be picked: \"That face is curved. Pick a flat face (to turn about a hole or a revolve's axis, choose it in the joint afterwards).\" Esc cancels a joint you have started (or click a part in the list on the left). Joint settings Setting What it does Type One of the joints in the table below Angle (°) Turns the second part about the joint's axis (square to the faces) Offset (mm) Moves the second part along the axis, away from the face (negative values push into it) Along X, Along Y, Tilt about X, Tilt about Y, Along the slot, Slot direction The other values, shown for the joints that use them Centre on (for each side) Where the joint's axis crosses the face: \"The face's own origin\", the axis of a revolve (\"Axis of Revolve1\") or one hole's axis (\"Hole1, hole 2\") Every value a joint lets move has a drag slider under its box; the others are set once. Joint What moves Set once Rigid nothing Angle, Offset Revolute Angle (a hinge) Offset Slider Offset (a drawer) Angle Cylindrical Angle and Offset (a shaft in a plain bearing) Planar Along X, Along Y across the face, and Angle (a part sliding on a table) Ball Angle (spin) and Tilt about X and Y, about the joint's centre (a ball joint) Pin in slot Along the slot and Angle (a pin in a straight slot) Slot direction: the slot's angle from the face's X The X and Y of a face are those of the first part's face; the joint's centre is set by Centre on. \"The face's own origin\" is the part's origin projected onto the face, which is often not where you want the parts to line up. For round parts and pins, set Centre on to the revolve's or hole's axis on both sides: the axes then line up, like a concentric mate. Example: two flanges bolted face to face Open the flange part (a revolve with a circular pattern of holes) and choose Assembly. Choose Add copy. Press J, click the flat face of the first flange's disc (the face the bolt holes start from), then the same face on the second flange. The faces were made by the revolve, so the joint is revolute. Set Centre on to \"Axis of Revolve1\" for both sides. The flanges now share an axis. Type 22.5 in Angle (°) to line up the bolt holes, or drag the slider. Work with the parts Click a part in the view or in the list to select it. The right panel shows its part name, how many copies there are, its volume, its joints, and whether it is grounded, set by its joints, or \"Not joined: stays where it was put\". Buttons: Edit this part (or Edit part in the toolbar, or double-click the part in the list): opens the part for editing; Insert a copy; Make it the grounded part; Remove: also removes its joints, after asking. An assembly keeps at least one part: \"An assembly keeps at least one part. Close it instead.\" Edit a part in place Select a part and choose Edit part. The part opens with its history; the heading says \"Bracket (in Bracket assembly)\". Change it like any part. Choose Back in the toolbar. The assembly updates: every copy of that part changes, and joints on faces made by sketch edges follow. Bringing the change back is one step in the assembly's undo list. Undo, save and close The assembly has its own undo list: Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y work on joints, inserts and settings. Save or Ctrl+S downloads \u003Cassembly name\u003E.partcraft-asm.json, with every part inside it. Open it later with Open. Close closes the assembly and returns to the part you started from. If the assembly is unsaved it asks: Cancel, Discard the assembly or Save first. To rename the assembly, click empty space so nothing is selected and change Name in the right panel. Joint problems A part no joint reaches is listed as free and the panel says \"Bracket2 is not joined to the grounded part, so it stays where it was put.\" Joints must form a tree from the grounded part. A joint that closes a loop is not solved: \"Revolute3 closes a loop and is off by 2.000 mm. Joints in a loop are not solved yet; remove one.\" If a face or axis a joint uses is gone after editing a part: \"The face this joint uses is gone from Bracket.\" Remove the joint and make it again. Next: Check an assembly for clashes and motion."},{"slug":"assembly-bom-explode","scope":"partcraft","title":"Parts list, exploded views and moving parts","summary":"Give assembly parts a number, description and material, read and download the bill of materials, make exploded views with steps or automatically, move parts that are not joined by hand, set joint limits, and export the whole assembly as STL, 3MF or a bill of materials.","section":"tasks","order":115,"keywords":["bom","bill of materials","parts list","part number","material","density","csv","exploded view","explode","collapse","trail lines","move part","drag","gizmo","translate","rotate","joint limits","assembly export","assembly step","assembly drawing","balloons"],"headings":[{"id":"part-properties","text":"Part properties","level":2},{"id":"bill-of-materials","text":"Bill of materials","level":2},{"id":"exploded-views","text":"Exploded views","level":2},{"id":"move-a-part-by-hand","text":"Move a part by hand","level":2},{"id":"joint-limits","text":"Joint limits","level":2},{"id":"export-the-whole-assembly","text":"Export the whole assembly","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThese tools are in the right panel of an assembly (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/build-assembly\" data-topic=\"partcraft/build-assembly\"\u003EBuild an assembly\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"part-properties\"\u003EPart properties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a part in the list on the left. Under \u003Cstrong\u003EPart properties\u003C/strong\u003E set:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPart number\u003C/strong\u003E (up to 40 characters) and \u003Cstrong\u003EDescription\u003C/strong\u003E (up to 200).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial\u003C/strong\u003E: a common material with its density, or \u003Cstrong\u003ECustom density…\u003C/strong\u003E to type one in g/cm³. The panel then shows the part&#039;s mass.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EProperties belong to the part, so every copy of it shares them. They are saved in the assembly file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bill-of-materials\"\u003EBill of materials\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith nothing selected (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), the panel shows the \u003Cstrong\u003EBill of materials\u003C/strong\u003E: one row per part with its item number, quantity (how many copies), part number, name, material and mass each. Item numbers follow the order the parts first appear in the list. Masses use each part&#039;s volume (of the faceted solid the mesh kernel builds, a little low on round parts) and its material; a part with no material has no mass, and then there is no total.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E saves the list as a CSV file (also \u003Cstrong\u003EExport › Bill of materials (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E). It opens in any spreadsheet; text that starts with = or + gets a leading apostrophe so a spreadsheet does not run it as a formula.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"exploded-views\"\u003EExploded views\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn exploded view pulls the parts apart for pictures. It never changes the joints or where the parts really are: saving, clash checks and exports use the real positions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith nothing selected, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew view\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EExploded views\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd a step\u003C/strong\u003E, tick the parts the step moves, choose a \u003Cstrong\u003EDirection\u003C/strong\u003E (+X … −Z, the normal of a face you click, or \u003Cstrong\u003EDrag an arrow in the view\u003C/strong\u003E) and a \u003Cstrong\u003EDistance\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd step\u003C/span\u003E. With \u003Cstrong\u003EDrag\u003C/strong\u003E, drag the arrow and let go: the step takes the direction and distance you dragged.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd more steps. They play one after another.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the slider between \u003Cstrong\u003EAssembled\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EExploded\u003C/strong\u003E to animate the view. Dashed trail lines run from each part&#039;s rest position to where it is now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto (radial)\u003C/span\u003E moves every part except the grounded one away from the assembly&#039;s middle. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto (joints)\u003C/span\u003E moves each joined part (with everything joined below it) out along its joint&#039;s face normal. You can keep several named views; pick one in \u003Cstrong\u003EView\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003EAssembled\u003C/strong\u003E to put the parts back. Views are saved in the assembly file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-part-by-hand\"\u003EMove a part by hand\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly a part with no joint to the grounded part moves by hand (the grounded part is fixed; joints place the rest). Select it and, under \u003Cstrong\u003EMove by hand\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Cstrong\u003EΔX, ΔY, ΔZ\u003C/strong\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, or choose an axis and an \u003Cstrong\u003EAngle\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn\u003C/span\u003E (about the part&#039;s middle).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrag to move\u003C/span\u003E shows arrows on the part: drag an arrow to move along that axis, or a square to move in a plane. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrag to turn\u003C/span\u003E shows rings to turn it. Each drag is one undo step. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E hides the arrows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"joint-limits\"\u003EJoint limits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a joint that moves and, under \u003Cstrong\u003ELimits\u003C/strong\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003EHold … between limits\u003C/strong\u003E for any of its values, then set its \u003Cstrong\u003EMinimum\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EMaximum\u003C/strong\u003E. Each value is held inside its own limits, its slider covers just that range, and \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the motion for clashes\u003C/strong\u003E sweeps only between them. A cylindrical joint can limit its angle, its offset or both. Changing the joint&#039;s type drops limits on values the new type does not have.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-the-whole-assembly\"\u003EExport the whole assembly\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn an assembly the \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E menu writes the whole assembly with every part where it is placed:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESTEP AP242 / AP214\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EDrawing (PDF)\u003C/strong\u003E are not built yet in this version: they need an exact kernel, so they are grayed. Export each part&#039;s faceted STEP from the part instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESTL\u003C/strong\u003E: one mesh of all the parts. \u003Cstrong\u003E3MF\u003C/strong\u003E: each part once, placed once per copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBill of materials (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe assembly drawing and the assembly STEP are not built yet in this version (they need an exact kernel). Exploded views are for pictures on screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"These tools are in the right panel of an assembly (see Build an assembly). Part properties Select a part in the list on the left. Under Part properties set: Part number (up to 40 characters) and Description (up to 200). Material: a common material with its density, or Custom density… to type one in g/cm³. The panel then shows the part's mass. Properties belong to the part, so every copy of it shares them. They are saved in the assembly file. Bill of materials With nothing selected (Esc), the panel shows the Bill of materials: one row per part with its item number, quantity (how many copies), part number, name, material and mass each. Item numbers follow the order the parts first appear in the list. Masses use each part's volume (of the faceted solid the mesh kernel builds, a little low on round parts) and its material; a part with no material has no mass, and then there is no total. Download CSV saves the list as a CSV file (also Export › Bill of materials (CSV)). It opens in any spreadsheet; text that starts with = or + gets a leading apostrophe so a spreadsheet does not run it as a formula. Exploded views An exploded view pulls the parts apart for pictures. It never changes the joints or where the parts really are: saving, clash checks and exports use the real positions. With nothing selected, choose New view under Exploded views. Under Add a step, tick the parts the step moves, choose a Direction (+X … −Z, the normal of a face you click, or Drag an arrow in the view) and a Distance, then Add step. With Drag, drag the arrow and let go: the step takes the direction and distance you dragged. Add more steps. They play one after another. Drag the slider between Assembled and Exploded to animate the view. Dashed trail lines run from each part's rest position to where it is now. Auto (radial) moves every part except the grounded one away from the assembly's middle. Auto (joints) moves each joined part (with everything joined below it) out along its joint's face normal. You can keep several named views; pick one in View, or Assembled to put the parts back. Views are saved in the assembly file. Move a part by hand Only a part with no joint to the grounded part moves by hand (the grounded part is fixed; joints place the rest). Select it and, under Move by hand: Type ΔX, ΔY, ΔZ and choose Move, or choose an axis and an Angle and Turn (about the part's middle). Drag to move shows arrows on the part: drag an arrow to move along that axis, or a square to move in a plane. Drag to turn shows rings to turn it. Each drag is one undo step. Esc hides the arrows. Joint limits Select a joint that moves and, under Limits, tick Hold … between limits for any of its values, then set its Minimum and Maximum. Each value is held inside its own limits, its slider covers just that range, and Check the motion for clashes sweeps only between them. A cylindrical joint can limit its angle, its offset or both. Changing the joint's type drops limits on values the new type does not have. Export the whole assembly In an assembly the Export menu writes the whole assembly with every part where it is placed: STEP AP242 / AP214 and Drawing (PDF) are not built yet in this version: they need an exact kernel, so they are grayed. Export each part's faceted STEP from the part instead. STL: one mesh of all the parts. 3MF: each part once, placed once per copy. Bill of materials (CSV). Limits The assembly drawing and the assembly STEP are not built yet in this version (they need an exact kernel). Exploded views are for pictures on screen."},{"slug":"check-assembly","scope":"partcraft","title":"Check an assembly for clashes and motion","summary":"Find parts that overlap with Clash, drag any joint's values to watch the motion, and sweep a joint through its range to find where parts collide.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["interference","interference detection","clash","collision","overlap","thread fit","thread depth mismatch","touching faces","hide","filter","motion","motion study","sweep","range of motion","check"],"headings":[{"id":"check-for-interference","text":"Check for interference","level":2},{"id":"kinds-of-results","text":"Kinds of results","level":3},{"id":"hide-what-you-do-not-need-to-see","text":"Hide what you do not need to see","level":3},{"id":"watch-the-motion","text":"Watch the motion","level":2},{"id":"sweep-a-joint-for-clashes","text":"Sweep a joint for clashes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETwo checks run on the parts as they are placed: \u003Cstrong\u003EClash\u003C/strong\u003E looks for overlapping material right now, and the \u003Cstrong\u003Emotion check\u003C/strong\u003E moves one joint through its range and looks for overlaps along the way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-for-interference\"\u003ECheck for interference\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen or build an assembly (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/build-assembly\" data-topic=\"partcraft/build-assembly\"\u003EBuild an assembly\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClash\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar reports the result:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;No interference between 3 parts (40 ms).&quot; Nothing overlaps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Interference: Flange1 and Flange2: Overlap, 2.5 mm³.&quot; Each pair, what kind of result it is and the volume they share.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EAssembly\u003C/strong\u003E box on the left shows \u003Cstrong\u003EInterference\u003C/strong\u003E: &quot;None&quot;, &quot;2 results&quot; (the ones shown), or &quot;Not checked&quot;. With nothing selected, the right panel lists each result under \u003Cstrong\u003EInterference\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe result is cleared whenever the assembly changes. Run \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClash\u003C/span\u003E again after moving a joint. Every pair of parts is checked against every other, so large assemblies take longer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"kinds-of-results\"\u003EKinds of results\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKind\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOverlap\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe parts share volume: a real clash.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThread depth mismatch\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe shared volume is inside one part&#039;s thread but beyond the other&#039;s: a bolt threaded deeper than its hole, or a plain shank in a tapped hole.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThread fit (cosmetic threads)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe shared volume is inside both parts&#039; threads: a bolt in a tapped hole. Cosmetic threads are drawn at the thread&#039;s full size, so they always overlap a little.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETouching faces\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo shared volume, but faces in contact (within 0.001 mm).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelled threads (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/threads\" data-topic=\"partcraft/threads\"\u003EThreads on shafts and bores, drawn or modelled\u003C/a\u003E) are real geometry: what they share is an \u003Cstrong\u003EOverlap\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"hide-what-you-do-not-need-to-see\"\u003EHide what you do not need to see\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EInterference\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EShow\u003C/strong\u003E has a box for each kind: clear \u003Cstrong\u003EThread fit\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ETouching faces\u003C/strong\u003E to see only the clashes that matter. \u003Cstrong\u003EHide this pair\u003C/strong\u003E beside a result hides that pair of parts whatever kind it is. Hidden results are counted (&quot;2 results hidden by these filters&quot;), and \u003Cstrong\u003EShow the hidden pairs again\u003C/strong\u003E brings the pairs back. The status bar counts hidden results too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe filters are saved with the assembly, and each change is one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E step. \u003Cstrong\u003ESweep a joint for clashes\u003C/strong\u003E (below) uses them too. An assembly with filters is saved as version 4, which earlier PartCraft versions refuse to open instead of losing the filters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"watch-the-motion\"\u003EWatch the motion\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a joint that moves (any but rigid) in the list on the left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the slider under one of its values, such as \u003Cstrong\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EOffset (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E. The part moves as you drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELet go. The new position is kept and can be undone with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also type an angle or offset in the box and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sweep-a-joint-for-clashes\"\u003ESweep a joint for clashes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a joint that moves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a joint with several values (cylindrical, planar, ball, pin in slot), choose which one to sweep in \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck moves\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck the motion for clashes\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft moves that value through 25 positions in 24 equal steps, the others staying as they are: an angle from −180° to 180° (a tilt from −90° to 90°), a length from minus to plus the size of the biggest part. It reports:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;No clash anywhere for Angle (°) from -180 to 180 (25 positions checked).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;6 of 25 positions clash, first at Angle (°) 45 (12.4 mm³).&quot; The first position that clashes and the overlap there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe button is grayed for rigid joints, which do not move. The sweep checks the positions only, not the path between them: a thin part could pass through another between two steps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EContact and gravity are not built. Without limits the sweep covers the whole range; give the value \u003Cstrong\u003ELimits\u003C/strong\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/assembly-bom-explode\" data-topic=\"partcraft/assembly-bom-explode\"\u003EParts list, exploded views and moving parts\u003C/a\u003E) and the sweep covers only those, so a hinge that opens 90° is checked over 90°.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Two checks run on the parts as they are placed: Clash looks for overlapping material right now, and the motion check moves one joint through its range and looks for overlaps along the way. Check for interference Open or build an assembly (see Build an assembly). Choose Clash. The status bar reports the result: \"No interference between 3 parts (40 ms).\" Nothing overlaps. \"Interference: Flange1 and Flange2: Overlap, 2.5 mm³.\" Each pair, what kind of result it is and the volume they share. The Assembly box on the left shows Interference: \"None\", \"2 results\" (the ones shown), or \"Not checked\". With nothing selected, the right panel lists each result under Interference. The result is cleared whenever the assembly changes. Run Clash again after moving a joint. Every pair of parts is checked against every other, so large assemblies take longer. Kinds of results Kind Means Overlap The parts share volume: a real clash. Thread depth mismatch The shared volume is inside one part's thread but beyond the other's: a bolt threaded deeper than its hole, or a plain shank in a tapped hole. Thread fit (cosmetic threads) The shared volume is inside both parts' threads: a bolt in a tapped hole. Cosmetic threads are drawn at the thread's full size, so they always overlap a little. Touching faces No shared volume, but faces in contact (within 0.001 mm). Modelled threads (see Threads on shafts and bores, drawn or modelled) are real geometry: what they share is an Overlap. Hide what you do not need to see Under Interference, Show has a box for each kind: clear Thread fit and Touching faces to see only the clashes that matter. Hide this pair beside a result hides that pair of parts whatever kind it is. Hidden results are counted (\"2 results hidden by these filters\"), and Show the hidden pairs again brings the pairs back. The status bar counts hidden results too. The filters are saved with the assembly, and each change is one Ctrl+Z step. Sweep a joint for clashes (below) uses them too. An assembly with filters is saved as version 4, which earlier PartCraft versions refuse to open instead of losing the filters. Watch the motion Select a joint that moves (any but rigid) in the list on the left. Drag the slider under one of its values, such as Angle (°) or Offset (mm). The part moves as you drag. Let go. The new position is kept and can be undone with Ctrl+Z. You can also type an angle or offset in the box and press Enter. Sweep a joint for clashes Select a joint that moves. For a joint with several values (cylindrical, planar, ball, pin in slot), choose which one to sweep in Check moves. Choose Check the motion for clashes. PartCraft moves that value through 25 positions in 24 equal steps, the others staying as they are: an angle from −180° to 180° (a tilt from −90° to 90°), a length from minus to plus the size of the biggest part. It reports: \"No clash anywhere for Angle (°) from -180 to 180 (25 positions checked).\" \"6 of 25 positions clash, first at Angle (°) 45 (12.4 mm³).\" The first position that clashes and the overlap there. The button is grayed for rigid joints, which do not move. The sweep checks the positions only, not the path between them: a thin part could pass through another between two steps. Contact and gravity are not built. Without limits the sweep covers the whole range; give the value Limits (see Parts list, exploded views and moving parts) and the sweep covers only those, so a hinge that opens 90° is checked over 90°."},{"slug":"measure","scope":"partcraft","title":"Measure distances, angles and mass","summary":"Click two things in the view for the minimum distance, angle and ΔX ΔY ΔZ; read an edge's length, a hole's diameter, a face's area or a point's coordinates; get volume, mass, centre of mass and inertia of a part or an assembly.","section":"tasks","order":125,"keywords":["measure","distance","minimum distance","angle","length","radius","diameter","area","coordinates","delta","inspect","mass properties","mass","weight","center of mass","centre of mass","inertia","moment of inertia","density","material"],"headings":[{"id":"measure-one-thing","text":"Measure one thing","level":2},{"id":"measure-between-two-things","text":"Measure between two things","level":2},{"id":"how-exact-it-is","text":"How exact it is","level":3},{"id":"mass-properties","text":"Mass properties","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeasure\u003C/strong\u003E works on the model in every workspace (Design, Form, Simulation, Generative, Manufacture) and in an assembly. It is in the \u003Cstrong\u003EView\u003C/strong\u003E group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A card opens over the top left of the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measure-one-thing\"\u003EMeasure one thing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a vertex, an edge or a face. The card shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA point\u003C/strong\u003E (a vertex, an edge midpoint or a circle centre): its X, Y, Z.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn edge\u003C/strong\u003E: straight, circle or curve, and its length; for a circle also the radius, the diameter and the centre.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA face\u003C/strong\u003E: flat, cylinder, cone and so on, and its area; a flat face&#039;s normal; a cylinder&#039;s radius and diameter (a hole&#039;s size). Areas are of the facets the mesh kernel builds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe whole body\u003C/strong\u003E: hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click the part. You get its volume, area, mass and inertia (see below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pointer snaps to vertices, edge midpoints and circle centres within 10 pixels; a small blue dot shows the snap before you click. To pick a hole&#039;s centre, point at the middle of the hole; to pick its rim (the circle), point at the rim.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measure-between-two-things\"\u003EMeasure between two things\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a second item. The card adds:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMinimum distance\u003C/strong\u003E and the two closest points, drawn in the view with a line between them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EΔX, ΔY, ΔZ\u003C/strong\u003E from the first closest point to the second.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAngle\u003C/strong\u003E between two flat faces (between their normals, 0–180°), between two straight edges or axes (0–90°), or between an edge and a face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGap between the planes\u003C/strong\u003E for two parallel flat faces, \u003Cstrong\u003ECentre to centre\u003C/strong\u003E for two circles or holes, \u003Cstrong\u003EAxis to axis\u003C/strong\u003E for two parallel holes or cylinders.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA third click starts a new pair. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E clears the picks; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E) closes Measure. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E puts the results on the clipboard as tab-separated text, ready to paste into a spreadsheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"how-exact-it-is\"\u003EHow exact it is\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft builds the part with the mesh kernel, so distances are worked out on the facets of the mesh and the card says \u003Cstrong\u003Eapproximate: of the facets\u003C/strong\u003E: right for flat faces and between vertices, slightly off for curved ones. A finer \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E setting brings them closer. Exact measuring on the true surfaces needs an exact kernel: not built yet in this version.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mass-properties\"\u003EMass properties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMass properties\u003C/span\u003E in the card or in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E menu. On a part, pick a \u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial\u003C/strong\u003E in the card (steel, aluminum, plastics and others with their usual densities). In an assembly each part uses the material set in its properties (select the part, then \u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial\u003C/strong\u003E), and the result is for all the parts together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe card shows mass, volume, area, centre of mass, the inertia tensor (Ixx, Iyy, Izz and the products Ixy, Iyz, Ixz, in kg·mm², about the centre of mass along the world axes) and the principal moments. Without a material it shows the same per unit density (mm⁵) and no mass.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe numbers are exact for the tessellated solid the mesh kernel builds (curved faces as facets), so a round part comes out a little low, and the card says so. Material densities are typical handbook values; a real alloy or grade can differ by a few per cent.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnaps can find a point hidden behind other geometry; check the card says what you meant.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMeasure is closed when you open a sketch or the drawing sheet, or switch workspace.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Measure works on the model in every workspace (Design, Form, Simulation, Generative, Manufacture) and in an assembly. It is in the View group's Views menu, or press M. A card opens over the top left of the view. Measure one thing Click a vertex, an edge or a face. The card shows: A point (a vertex, an edge midpoint or a circle centre): its X, Y, Z. An edge: straight, circle or curve, and its length; for a circle also the radius, the diameter and the centre. A face: flat, cylinder, cone and so on, and its area; a flat face's normal; a cylinder's radius and diameter (a hole's size). Areas are of the facets the mesh kernel builds. The whole body: hold Shift and click the part. You get its volume, area, mass and inertia (see below). The pointer snaps to vertices, edge midpoints and circle centres within 10 pixels; a small blue dot shows the snap before you click. To pick a hole's centre, point at the middle of the hole; to pick its rim (the circle), point at the rim. Measure between two things Click a second item. The card adds: Minimum distance and the two closest points, drawn in the view with a line between them. ΔX, ΔY, ΔZ from the first closest point to the second. Angle between two flat faces (between their normals, 0–180°), between two straight edges or axes (0–90°), or between an edge and a face. Gap between the planes for two parallel flat faces, Centre to centre for two circles or holes, Axis to axis for two parallel holes or cylinders. A third click starts a new pair. Esc clears the picks; Esc again (or Close) closes Measure. Copy puts the results on the clipboard as tab-separated text, ready to paste into a spreadsheet. How exact it is PartCraft builds the part with the mesh kernel, so distances are worked out on the facets of the mesh and the card says approximate: of the facets: right for flat faces and between vertices, slightly off for curved ones. A finer Curves setting brings them closer. Exact measuring on the true surfaces needs an exact kernel: not built yet in this version. Mass properties Choose Mass properties in the card or in the Views menu. On a part, pick a Material in the card (steel, aluminum, plastics and others with their usual densities). In an assembly each part uses the material set in its properties (select the part, then Material), and the result is for all the parts together. The card shows mass, volume, area, centre of mass, the inertia tensor (Ixx, Iyy, Izz and the products Ixy, Iyz, Ixz, in kg·mm², about the centre of mass along the world axes) and the principal moments. Without a material it shows the same per unit density (mm⁵) and no mass. The numbers are exact for the tessellated solid the mesh kernel builds (curved faces as facets), so a round part comes out a little low, and the card says so. Material densities are typical handbook values; a real alloy or grade can differ by a few per cent. Limits Snaps can find a point hidden behind other geometry; check the card says what you meant. Measure is closed when you open a sketch or the drawing sheet, or switch workspace."},{"slug":"section-view","scope":"partcraft","title":"Look inside with a section view","summary":"Cut the part or an assembly with a plane to see inside it, with the cut faces filled. Move the plane with a slider, cut on a flat face you click, flip which side is cut away.","section":"tasks","order":126,"keywords":["section","section view","cut","cutaway","cross section","clipping","inside","slice","plane","caps","hatch","assembly section"],"headings":[{"id":"cut-through-the-middle","text":"Cut through the middle","level":2},{"id":"cut-on-a-face","text":"Cut on a face","level":2},{"id":"clicks-and-the-cut","text":"Clicks and the cut","level":2},{"id":"close-it","text":"Close it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003Esection view\u003C/strong\u003E cuts the model with a plane so you can see inside: holes, pockets, wall thicknesses, how parts in an assembly sit together. It works in every workspace and in an assembly. Open it from the \u003Cstrong\u003EView\u003C/strong\u003E group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E menu, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection view\u003C/span\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A card opens at the bottom left of the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the view is cut. The part, its volume, measurements and exports stay whole, and nothing is saved in the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-through-the-middle\"\u003ECut through the middle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The part is cut by the front plane through its middle, and the cut faces are filled with a tinted colour.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPosition\u003C/span\u003E slider, or type a distance in mm, to move the cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose another \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlane\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront (XY)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop (XZ)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight (YZ)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut away the other side\u003C/span\u003E to keep the half that was cut away instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-on-a-face\"\u003ECut on a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a face\u003C/span\u003E on the card.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a flat face in the view. The cut starts flush with it, with the plane on that face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPosition\u003C/span\u003E to go deeper. The number is the distance along the face&#039;s outward direction.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clicks-and-the-cut\"\u003EClicks and the cut\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicks in the view go to what is left after the cut, so you can pick a face or an edge inside the part, start a sketch on it, or measure across it. Untick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill the cut faces\u003C/span\u003E to see through the cut into the hollow parts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"close-it\"\u003EClose it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E. Opening a sketch or the drawing sheet closes it too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a section on the drawing sheet (hatched, with its cutting-plane line), use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/drawing-pdf\" data-topic=\"partcraft/drawing-pdf\"\u003EMake a drawing (PDF)\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The section view cuts the model with a plane so you can see inside: holes, pockets, wall thicknesses, how parts in an assembly sit together. It works in every workspace and in an assembly. Open it from the View group's Views menu, Section view, or press X. A card opens at the bottom left of the view. Only the view is cut. The part, its volume, measurements and exports stay whole, and nothing is saved in the file. Cut through the middle Press X. The part is cut by the front plane through its middle, and the cut faces are filled with a tinted colour. Drag the Position slider, or type a distance in mm, to move the cut. Choose another Plane: Front (XY), Top (XZ) or Right (YZ). Tick Cut away the other side to keep the half that was cut away instead. Cut on a face Click Pick a face on the card. Click a flat face in the view. The cut starts flush with it, with the plane on that face. Move the Position to go deeper. The number is the distance along the face's outward direction. Clicks and the cut Clicks in the view go to what is left after the cut, so you can pick a face or an edge inside the part, start a sketch on it, or measure across it. Untick Fill the cut faces to see through the cut into the hollow parts. Close it Press Esc, press X again, or click Close. Opening a sketch or the drawing sheet closes it too. For a section on the drawing sheet (hatched, with its cutting-plane line), use Drawing: see Make a drawing (PDF)."},{"slug":"export-files","scope":"partcraft","title":"Export for printing, STEP and DXF","summary":"Export the part as STL or 3MF for 3D printing, as a faceted STEP solid for other CAD programs, and a sketch or a flat face as DXF for laser and waterjet cutting.","section":"tasks","order":130,"keywords":["export","save as","ifc","bim","property sets","stl","3mf","3d print","slicer","step","stp","dxf","laser","waterjet","cnc","flat pattern","profile","mesh","faceted"],"headings":[{"id":"for-3d-printing-stl-or-3mf","text":"For 3D printing: STL or 3MF","level":2},{"id":"for-other-cad-programs-step","text":"For other CAD programs: STEP","level":2},{"id":"for-bim-programs-ifc-with-every-property","text":"For BIM programs: IFC with every property","level":2},{"id":"for-laser-waterjet-or-cnc-profiles-dxf","text":"For laser, waterjet or CNC profiles: DXF","level":2},{"id":"a-sketch","text":"A sketch","level":3},{"id":"a-flat-face-of-the-solid","text":"A flat face of the solid","level":3},{"id":"a-drawing","text":"A drawing","level":2},{"id":"formats-not-available","text":"Formats not available","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EExports are copies in other formats. Keep the \u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E file too: it is the only file PartCraft can open again and edit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll exports work on the part on screen, in millimeters. In an assembly, STL, 3MF, STEP and Drawing (PDF) export the whole assembly instead: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/assembly-bom-explode\" data-topic=\"partcraft/assembly-bom-explode\"\u003EParts list, exploded views and moving parts\u003C/a\u003E. If there is no solid yet: &quot;There is no solid to export yet.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"for-3d-printing-stl-or-3mf\"\u003EFor 3D printing: STL or 3MF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOptional: for smooth round surfaces, click empty space so nothing is selected and set \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003EFine (128 per turn)\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EVery fine (256 per turn)\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel. STL and 3MF are always made of triangles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTL, for 3D printing\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3MF, for 3D printing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe file downloads. The status bar says, for example, &quot;Exported Part1.stl (binary, mm, Z up, 1536 triangles).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth formats are \u003Cstrong\u003EZ up\u003C/strong\u003E, as printers and slicers expect. PartCraft&#039;s own world is Y up, so the export turns the part: what points up in PartCraft (square to the Top plane) points up in the slicer, and a part sketched on the Top plane lies flat on the bed. Set the final orientation in your slicer if needed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E3MF is a newer, smaller format that slicers read with units included. Use STL if a program does not take 3MF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo check first that the part is closed, fits your printer&#039;s bed and stands on a flat face, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/print-3d\" data-topic=\"partcraft/print-3d\"\u003EGet a part ready for 3D printing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"for-other-cad-programs-step\"\u003EFor other CAD programs: STEP\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E. It writes the part as a STEP AP242 solid of flat faces. Flat faces come through as single faces, but every round surface arrives as many narrow facets, and the file keeps PartCraft&#039;s own axes (Y up). The other program sees the right shape and volume, but cannot pick a hole as a cylinder. Set \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E finer first if round faces matter. It holds the solid only: no history, no sketches, no drawing. If the other program reads only AP214, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP214 (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E instead: the same solid, written in that older schema.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP242 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP214 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E are not built yet in this version: they need an exact kernel, so they are grayed in the menu and the status bar says &quot;Exact STEP: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel).&quot; See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"for-bim-programs-ifc-with-every-property\"\u003EFor BIM programs: IFC with every property\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC 4 (with every property)\u003C/span\u003E. It writes the part as an IFC 4 element for building and plant models, its shape as triangles (Z up, millimetres), with what PartCraft knows about it as property sets:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EProperty set\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHolds\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPartCraft_Part\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName, material and its density, the active configuration, how many bodies; in an assembly also the part number and description\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPartCraft_Parameters\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery parameter of the part with its value; a formula&#039;s expression and a note are in the property&#039;s description\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPartCraft_TitleBlock\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing&#039;s title block fields you filled in (title, drawing number, revision, company, drawn, checked and approved by, date, finish, general tolerance) and the sheet size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EQto_BuildingElementProxyQuantities\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPartCraft_Quantities\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVolume, surface area, mass (with a material) and the overall size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe material is attached as an IFC material, and the drawing number is the element&#039;s tag. In an assembly, each part is written once as a type holding its properties, and each copy is placed where the assembly puts it with its own values in \u003Cstrong\u003EPartCraft_Instance\u003C/strong\u003E: its name, its item number in the bill of materials, and whether it is the grounded part.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"for-laser-waterjet-or-cnc-profiles-dxf\"\u003EFor laser, waterjet or CNC profiles: DXF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo kinds of DXF, both version R12, which every CAD and CAM program opens:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"a-sketch\"\u003EA sketch\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the sketch in the history (or have it open).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF of the sketch or flat face\u003C/span\u003E. (The sketch&#039;s right panel also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport as DXF\u003C/span\u003E.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou get \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;part&gt; - &lt;sketch&gt;.dxf\u003C/code\u003E in the sketch&#039;s own coordinates: lines, true arcs and circles on layer \u003Cstrong\u003EPROFILE\u003C/strong\u003E, construction geometry on \u003Cstrong\u003ECONSTRUCTION\u003C/strong\u003E, loose points on \u003Cstrong\u003EPOINTS\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"a-flat-face-of-the-solid\"\u003EA flat face of the solid\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a flat face in the view (for example the top of a plate with holes).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport face as DXF\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF of the sketch or flat face\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou get \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;part&gt; - face.dxf\u003C/code\u003E: the face&#039;s outline and every hole in it as closed polylines. The status bar says how many outlines and the face&#039;s area. Round edges are their facets, so set \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E to Fine first if the cutter follows them closely.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf neither a sketch nor a flat face is selected: &quot;Open or select a sketch, or pick a flat face, to export DXF.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-drawing\"\u003EA drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe PDF drawing has its own walkthrough: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/drawing-pdf\" data-topic=\"partcraft/drawing-pdf\"\u003EMake a drawing sheet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"formats-not-available\"\u003EFormats not available\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EglTF, OBJ, IGES, Parasolid and DWG are not exported in this build. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/file-formats\" data-topic=\"partcraft/file-formats\"\u003EFile formats\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Exports are copies in other formats. Keep the .partcraft.json file too: it is the only file PartCraft can open again and edit. All exports work on the part on screen, in millimeters. In an assembly, STL, 3MF, STEP and Drawing (PDF) export the whole assembly instead: see Parts list, exploded views and moving parts. If there is no solid yet: \"There is no solid to export yet.\" For 3D printing: STL or 3MF Optional: for smooth round surfaces, click empty space so nothing is selected and set Curves to Fine (128 per turn) or Very fine (256 per turn) in the right panel. STL and 3MF are always made of triangles. Choose Export, then STL, for 3D printing or 3MF, for 3D printing. The file downloads. The status bar says, for example, \"Exported Part1.stl (binary, mm, Z up, 1536 triangles).\" Both formats are Z up, as printers and slicers expect. PartCraft's own world is Y up, so the export turns the part: what points up in PartCraft (square to the Top plane) points up in the slicer, and a part sketched on the Top plane lies flat on the bed. Set the final orientation in your slicer if needed. 3MF is a newer, smaller format that slicers read with units included. Use STL if a program does not take 3MF. To check first that the part is closed, fits your printer's bed and stands on a flat face, use Export › Check for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…: see Get a part ready for 3D printing. For other CAD programs: STEP Choose Export, then STEP (faceted, mesh kernel). It writes the part as a STEP AP242 solid of flat faces. Flat faces come through as single faces, but every round surface arrives as many narrow facets, and the file keeps PartCraft's own axes (Y up). The other program sees the right shape and volume, but cannot pick a hole as a cylinder. Set Curves finer first if round faces matter. It holds the solid only: no history, no sketches, no drawing. If the other program reads only AP214, choose STEP AP214 (faceted, mesh kernel) instead: the same solid, written in that older schema. STEP AP242 (exact solid) and STEP AP214 (exact solid) are not built yet in this version: they need an exact kernel, so they are grayed in the menu and the status bar says \"Exact STEP: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel).\" See What is not built yet. For BIM programs: IFC with every property Choose Export, then IFC 4 (with every property). It writes the part as an IFC 4 element for building and plant models, its shape as triangles (Z up, millimetres), with what PartCraft knows about it as property sets: Property set Holds PartCraft_Part Name, material and its density, the active configuration, how many bodies; in an assembly also the part number and description PartCraft_Parameters Every parameter of the part with its value; a formula's expression and a note are in the property's description PartCraft_TitleBlock The drawing's title block fields you filled in (title, drawing number, revision, company, drawn, checked and approved by, date, finish, general tolerance) and the sheet size Qto_BuildingElementProxyQuantities, PartCraft_Quantities Volume, surface area, mass (with a material) and the overall size The material is attached as an IFC material, and the drawing number is the element's tag. In an assembly, each part is written once as a type holding its properties, and each copy is placed where the assembly puts it with its own values in PartCraft_Instance: its name, its item number in the bill of materials, and whether it is the grounded part. For laser, waterjet or CNC profiles: DXF Two kinds of DXF, both version R12, which every CAD and CAM program opens: A sketch Select the sketch in the history (or have it open). Choose Export, then DXF of the sketch or flat face. (The sketch's right panel also has Export as DXF.) You get \u003Cpart\u003E - \u003Csketch\u003E.dxf in the sketch's own coordinates: lines, true arcs and circles on layer PROFILE, construction geometry on CONSTRUCTION, loose points on POINTS. A flat face of the solid Click a flat face in the view (for example the top of a plate with holes). Choose Export face as DXF in the right panel, or Export, then DXF of the sketch or flat face. You get \u003Cpart\u003E - face.dxf: the face's outline and every hole in it as closed polylines. The status bar says how many outlines and the face's area. Round edges are their facets, so set Curves to Fine first if the cutter follows them closely. If neither a sketch nor a flat face is selected: \"Open or select a sketch, or pick a flat face, to export DXF.\" A drawing The PDF drawing has its own walkthrough: Make a drawing sheet. Formats not available glTF, OBJ, IGES, Parasolid and DWG are not exported in this build. See File formats."},{"slug":"print-3d","scope":"partcraft","title":"Get a part ready for 3D printing","summary":"Check that a part or assembly is closed, fits your printer and stands on a base, then download it as STL or 3MF for your slicer.","section":"tasks","order":131,"keywords":["3d print","3d printing","printer","print","slicer","stl","3mf","watertight","closed","manifold","bed","bed size","build plate","overhang","supports","base","millimeters","export","threads","clearance"],"headings":[{"id":"check-and-download","text":"Check and download","level":2},{"id":"what-the-dialog-can-say","text":"What the dialog can say","level":2},{"id":"threads-that-print","text":"Threads that print","level":2},{"id":"smooth-round-faces","text":"Smooth round faces","level":2},{"id":"not-checked","text":"Not checked","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA slicer needs a closed shape: no holes, every face pointing out, nothing passing through itself. Parts PartCraft builds are closed by construction, so the check mostly tells you the size, whether the part fits your printer&#039;s bed, whether it stands on a flat face, and how much of it overhangs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-and-download\"\u003ECheck and download\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOptional, once: set your printer&#039;s bed in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › PartCraft › \u003Cstrong\u003E3D printer\u003C/strong\u003E: width, depth and height in millimeters. Leave them empty to skip that check.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog says &quot;Ready to print&quot; or &quot;Not ready to print&quot;, lists anything wrong, and gives the size on the bed (X × Y × Z, in millimeters), the volume and the number of triangles.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload 3MF\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload STL\u003C/span\u003E. The file is the same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3MF, for 3D printing\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTL, for 3D printing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn an assembly, the check and the files take the whole assembly, every copy where it is placed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe part is checked standing as the export writes it: what points up in PartCraft (square to the Top plane) points up on the bed. A part sketched on the Top plane lies flat.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-dialog-can-say\"\u003EWhat the dialog can say\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot ready to print\u003C/strong\u003E (a slicer may refuse it or print it wrong): holes (edges with a face on one side only), edges shared by three or more faces, faces facing opposite ways, a body inside out, or a body passing through itself. A part built in PartCraft should never show these; if one does, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/troubleshooting\" data-topic=\"partcraft/troubleshooting\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWarnings\u003C/strong\u003E (it prints, but have a look first):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELarger than your printer&#039;s bed\u003C/strong\u003E, even turned 90° on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing lies flat at the bottom\u003C/strong\u003E: turn the part in your slicer so a face rests on the bed, or it will need supports or a raft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeans out more than 45° from upright\u003C/strong\u003E: those faces need support, standing as it is. Another way up may need less.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESeparate bodies overlap\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003Emeet face to face\u003C/strong\u003E: slicers usually merge them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECombine\u003C/span\u003E in Features › More joins them into one body. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/bodies\" data-topic=\"partcraft/bodies\"\u003EBodies\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnder 1 mm across\u003C/strong\u003E: check the part&#039;s sizes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"threads-that-print\"\u003EThreads that print\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA modelled thread prints as a real thread. Give it some \u003Cstrong\u003EClearance\u003C/strong\u003E so a printed bolt goes into a printed nut: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/threads\" data-topic=\"partcraft/threads\"\u003EThreads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"smooth-round-faces\"\u003ESmooth round faces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESTL and 3MF are made of triangles. For smoother round faces, click empty space so nothing is selected and set \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003EFine (128 per turn)\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EVery fine (256 per turn)\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel before you export. More triangles make bigger files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-checked\"\u003ENot checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWall thickness, details smaller than your nozzle can draw, and how the slicer will place supports. Your slicer shows those.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A slicer needs a closed shape: no holes, every face pointing out, nothing passing through itself. Parts PartCraft builds are closed by construction, so the check mostly tells you the size, whether the part fits your printer's bed, whether it stands on a flat face, and how much of it overhangs. Check and download Optional, once: set your printer's bed in Settings › PartCraft › 3D printer: width, depth and height in millimeters. Leave them empty to skip that check. Choose Export, then Check for 3D printing (STL or 3MF)…. The dialog says \"Ready to print\" or \"Not ready to print\", lists anything wrong, and gives the size on the bed (X × Y × Z, in millimeters), the volume and the number of triangles. Click Download 3MF or Download STL. The file is the same as Export › 3MF, for 3D printing or STL, for 3D printing. In an assembly, the check and the files take the whole assembly, every copy where it is placed. The part is checked standing as the export writes it: what points up in PartCraft (square to the Top plane) points up on the bed. A part sketched on the Top plane lies flat. What the dialog can say Not ready to print (a slicer may refuse it or print it wrong): holes (edges with a face on one side only), edges shared by three or more faces, faces facing opposite ways, a body inside out, or a body passing through itself. A part built in PartCraft should never show these; if one does, see Troubleshooting. Warnings (it prints, but have a look first): Larger than your printer's bed, even turned 90° on it. Nothing lies flat at the bottom: turn the part in your slicer so a face rests on the bed, or it will need supports or a raft. Leans out more than 45° from upright: those faces need support, standing as it is. Another way up may need less. Separate bodies overlap or meet face to face: slicers usually merge them. Combine in Features › More joins them into one body. See Bodies. Under 1 mm across: check the part's sizes. Threads that print A modelled thread prints as a real thread. Give it some Clearance so a printed bolt goes into a printed nut: see Threads. Smooth round faces STL and 3MF are made of triangles. For smoother round faces, click empty space so nothing is selected and set Curves to Fine (128 per turn) or Very fine (256 per turn) in the right panel before you export. More triangles make bigger files. Not checked Wall thickness, details smaller than your nozzle can draw, and how the slicer will place supports. Your slicer shows those."},{"slug":"drawing-pdf","scope":"partcraft","title":"Make a drawing with views, a section and GD&T","summary":"Open the drawing sheet, add a section and a detail view, place datums, feature control frames and surface finish symbols, choose the sheet size, fill in the title block and the revisions, and export the sheet as a PDF.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["hole table","hole chart","hole positions","tags","drawing","2d drawing","sheet","sheet size","a4","a3","a2","a1","a0","ansi","title block","revision","revision block","drawing number","material","pdf","views","orthographic","third angle","isometric","section view","detail view","gd&t","datum","feature control frame","tolerance","position","flatness","surface finish","roughness","title block","print"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-drawing-sheet","text":"Open the drawing sheet","level":2},{"id":"what-is-on-the-sheet","text":"What is on the sheet","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-sheet-size","text":"Choose the sheet size","level":2},{"id":"fill-in-the-title-block","text":"Fill in the title block","level":2},{"id":"add-revisions","text":"Add revisions","level":2},{"id":"add-a-section-view","text":"Add a section view","level":2},{"id":"add-a-detail-view","text":"Add a detail view","level":2},{"id":"add-a-hole-table","text":"Add a hole table","level":2},{"id":"place-gd-t-and-surface-finish-symbols","text":"Place GD&T and surface finish symbols","level":2},{"id":"datum","text":"Datum","level":3},{"id":"feature-control-frame","text":"Feature control frame","level":3},{"id":"surface-finish","text":"Surface finish","level":3},{"id":"export-the-pdf","text":"Export the PDF","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft makes a drawing sheet from the part: front, top and right views in third-angle projection, an isometric view, overall sizes and a title block on a sheet from A4 to A0 or ANSI A to E. On top of that you can add a section, a detail view and GD&amp;T symbols. The sheet updates when the part changes, and its settings are saved in the part file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-drawing-sheet\"\u003EOpen the drawing sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBuild the part. (With nothing built: &quot;Build a solid first, then make its drawing.&quot;)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe 3D view is replaced by the sheet. The toolbar shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDatum\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrame\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinish\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetail\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, and the right panel is headed \u003Cstrong\u003EDrawing\u003C/strong\u003E. The status bar says how the views were made: &quot;Drawing (curves are drawn as facets). Datum, Frame and Finish place GD&amp;T on a view; the panel sets a section and a detail view.&quot; The views are drawn from the mesh kernel&#039;s facets, so round edges are short straight pieces; true arcs need an exact kernel, which this version does not have.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove round the sheet: roll the wheel to zoom, drag with the right or middle button to pan, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to fit the sheet. With the sheet focused, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E zoom and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the arrow keys pans.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the model\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (with nothing selected and no tool active) to return to the 3D model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-on-the-sheet\"\u003EWhat is on the sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe sheet you choose\u003C/strong\u003E, landscape (A3 unless you pick another), with a border and a \u003Cstrong\u003Etitle block\u003C/strong\u003E: title, company, drawing number, revision, material, drawn / checked / approved by, date, scale, sheet size, &quot;Units: millimetres · Third-angle projection&quot;, and whether the views are exact or faceted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Erevision block\u003C/strong\u003E in the top-right corner once you add a revision.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFront, top and right views\u003C/strong\u003E: the top view above the front view, the right view to its right. Hidden edges dashed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003Eisometric view\u003C/strong\u003E where there is room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOverall sizes\u003C/strong\u003E: width and height on the front view, depth on the top view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne \u003Cstrong\u003Escale\u003C/strong\u003E for the main views from the ISO series (1:1, 1:2, 1:5 … 2:1, 5:1, 10:1), picked so they fit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-sheet-size\"\u003EChoose the sheet size\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the drawing sheet, with nothing selected, pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel: \u003Cstrong\u003EA4, A3, A2, A1, A0\u003C/strong\u003E (ISO 216) or \u003Cstrong\u003EANSI A, B, C, D, E\u003C/strong\u003E (11 × 8.5 to 44 × 34 in). The views are laid out again at the largest ISO scale that fits, so a bigger sheet can show the part bigger.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-in-the-title-block\"\u003EFill in the title block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe right panel&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003ETitle block\u003C/strong\u003E fields go straight onto the sheet: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETitle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing no.\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevision\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompany\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawn by\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecked by\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApproved by\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDate\u003C/span\u003E (year-month-day), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral tolerance\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinish\u003C/span\u003E (the last two print just above the title block). Type in a box and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click away. An empty box leaves its cell blank, except:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETitle\u003C/strong\u003E shows the part&#039;s name until you type one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDate\u003C/strong\u003E shows today until you type one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERevision\u003C/strong\u003E shows the newest revision in the list until you type one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial\u003C/strong\u003E comes from the part: click empty space in the model, then pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E in the Part panel. The same material gives Measure&#039;s mass properties their density.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-revisions\"\u003EAdd revisions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ERevisions\u003C/strong\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd revision\u003C/span\u003E. Each new row takes the next letter (A, B, C …, after Z comes AA) and today&#039;s date. Type what changed and who made the change. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E removes a row. The revision block in the top-right corner of the sheet lists them, oldest first, up to 20.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheet size, title block, revisions and material are saved in the part file, and each change is one step of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-section-view\"\u003EAdd a section view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe part is cut by a plane and the cut is hatched. Like the other views, the section is drawn from facets, so round edges are short straight pieces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, set \u003Cstrong\u003ESection A-A\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003EOf the front view\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EOf the top view\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EOf the right view\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe section appears on the sheet, hatched where the cut passes through material, with the cutting line and its &quot;A&quot; arrows on the view it is taken from.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003ECut at (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E to move the cutting plane. It starts through the middle of the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003ESection A-A\u003C/strong\u003E back to \u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E to remove it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-detail-view\"\u003EAdd a detail view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EDetail B\u003C/strong\u003E to the view to enlarge (or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetail\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the center of the area to enlarge, on that view. A circle labeled &quot;B&quot; marks it, and the enlarged &quot;Detail B&quot; appears on the sheet with its own scale, for example &quot;Detail B (2:1)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EDetail radius (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E for the size of the circle and \u003Cstrong\u003EDetail scale\u003C/strong\u003E for the enlargement: 2, 4, 5 or 10 × the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA detail can be taken from the front, top, right or section view, not from the isometric. If there is no room for every view, the panel says &quot;No room on the sheet for: detail. Make the detail smaller or drop a view.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-hole-table\"\u003EAdd a hole table\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA hole table lists the part&#039;s holes with their positions, for drilling or CNC: choose \u003Cstrong\u003EHole table\u003C/strong\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EOf the top view\u003C/strong\u003E (or front, or right) in the panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery hole seen end-on in that view gets a tag beside it: \u003Cstrong\u003EA1\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EA2\u003C/strong\u003E … for the first size, \u003Cstrong\u003EB1\u003C/strong\u003E … for the next.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe table lists each tag, its \u003Cstrong\u003EX\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EY\u003C/strong\u003E in millimetres, and its size: &quot;Ø14 THRU&quot;, &quot;Ø6.6 × 10 DEEP&quot;, a counterbore or countersink, or a tapped hole&#039;s thread callout.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EX and Y are measured from the lower-left corner of the view&#039;s outline, marked \u003Cstrong\u003EX0 Y0\u003C/strong\u003E on the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHoles made by patterns are included. Holes that are not square to the view are counted in the panel and left out of the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe table goes in a free area of the sheet and into the PDF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-gd-t-and-surface-finish-symbols\"\u003EPlace GD&amp;T and surface finish symbols\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDatum\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrame\u003C/span\u003E (a feature control frame) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinish\u003C/span\u003E (a surface finish symbol).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the point on a view where it attaches. It snaps to the nearest drawn line within 2.5 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe symbol appears with a leader, selected, and the right panel shows its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag a symbol to move it; its leader stays on the point. With a symbol selected, the arrow keys nudge it 1 mm. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the panel) removes it; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deselects it. Every change is in the undo list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"datum\"\u003EDatum\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELetter\u003C/strong\u003E: A to Z, without I, O and Q (they read as 1, 0 and O). Each new datum takes the next free letter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"feature-control-frame\"\u003EFeature control frame\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETolerance\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStraightness, Flatness, Circularity, Cylindricity, Profile of a line, Profile of a surface, Perpendicularity, Angularity, Parallelism, Position, Concentricity and Symmetry (both removed in Y14.5-2018, and marked so), Circular runout, Total runout\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZone (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe tolerance value, default 0.1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDiameter zone (⌀)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFor characteristics with a cylindrical zone; on by default for Position\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERFS (none), MMC (Ⓜ), LMC (Ⓛ), where the characteristic allows it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPrimary / Secondary / Tertiary datum\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUp to three datum letters, each with a \u003Cstrong\u003E… modifier\u003C/strong\u003E (MMB, LMB) where allowed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft checks the frame against the rules of ASME Y14.5 and says what is wrong at the top of the panel, for example &quot;Flatness is a form tolerance: it takes no datum references.&quot; or &quot;Position needs at least one datum reference.&quot; The line \u003Cstrong\u003EReads\u003C/strong\u003E spells out the frame.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"surface-finish\"\u003ESurface finish\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERa (µm)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERoughness average\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1.6\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProcess\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eany process, machined, no material removed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emachined\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENote\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUp to 40 characters, such as a lay or a process\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith nothing selected, the panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003ENotes on the sheet\u003C/strong\u003E, each with \u003Cstrong\u003ESelect\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-the-pdf\"\u003EExport the PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport PDF\u003C/span\u003E in the panel, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing (PDF): views, section, detail, GD&amp;T\u003C/span\u003E from the model. The PDF downloads as \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;part name&gt; - drawing.pdf\u003C/code\u003E, and the status bar lists what is on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPrint it at &quot;Actual size&quot; on paper of the sheet&#039;s size to keep the scale true.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensions other than the three overall sizes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd them on the PDF with a markup app, or export a sketch or face as DXF and dimension it in a 2D CAD program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMore than one section or detail; broken-out, auxiliary and projected views\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirst-angle projection, moving views, portrait sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThird angle, automatic layout, landscape\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFree text notes and tables on the sheet (other than the title and revision blocks)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse the finish note, or add text on the PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawings of assemblies\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECharacters outside the Western European set print as &quot;?&quot; (the PDF uses the reader&#039;s built-in Helvetica). The GD&amp;T symbols are drawn as lines, so they print in any reader.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"PartCraft makes a drawing sheet from the part: front, top and right views in third-angle projection, an isometric view, overall sizes and a title block on a sheet from A4 to A0 or ANSI A to E. On top of that you can add a section, a detail view and GD&T symbols. The sheet updates when the part changes, and its settings are saved in the part file. Open the drawing sheet Build the part. (With nothing built: \"Build a solid first, then make its drawing.\") Choose Drawing in the toolbar. The 3D view is replaced by the sheet. The toolbar shows Datum, Frame, Finish, Detail, PDF and Back, and the right panel is headed Drawing. The status bar says how the views were made: \"Drawing (curves are drawn as facets). Datum, Frame and Finish place GD&T on a view; the panel sets a section and a detail view.\" The views are drawn from the mesh kernel's facets, so round edges are short straight pieces; true arcs need an exact kernel, which this version does not have. Move round the sheet: roll the wheel to zoom, drag with the right or middle button to pan, press F to fit the sheet. With the sheet focused, + and − zoom and Shift with the arrow keys pans. Choose Back, Back to the model or press Esc (with nothing selected and no tool active) to return to the 3D model. What is on the sheet The sheet you choose, landscape (A3 unless you pick another), with a border and a title block: title, company, drawing number, revision, material, drawn / checked / approved by, date, scale, sheet size, \"Units: millimetres · Third-angle projection\", and whether the views are exact or faceted. A revision block in the top-right corner once you add a revision. Front, top and right views: the top view above the front view, the right view to its right. Hidden edges dashed. An isometric view where there is room. Overall sizes: width and height on the front view, depth on the top view. One scale for the main views from the ISO series (1:1, 1:2, 1:5 … 2:1, 5:1, 10:1), picked so they fit. Choose the sheet size On the drawing sheet, with nothing selected, pick Sheet in the right panel: A4, A3, A2, A1, A0 (ISO 216) or ANSI A, B, C, D, E (11 × 8.5 to 44 × 34 in). The views are laid out again at the largest ISO scale that fits, so a bigger sheet can show the part bigger. Fill in the title block The right panel's Title block fields go straight onto the sheet: Title, Drawing no., Revision, Company, Drawn by, Checked by, Approved by, Date (year-month-day), General tolerance and Finish (the last two print just above the title block). Type in a box and press Enter or click away. An empty box leaves its cell blank, except: Title shows the part's name until you type one. Date shows today until you type one. Revision shows the newest revision in the list until you type one. Material comes from the part: click empty space in the model, then pick Material in the Part panel. The same material gives Measure's mass properties their density. Add revisions Under Revisions, click Add revision. Each new row takes the next letter (A, B, C …, after Z comes AA) and today's date. Type what changed and who made the change. Delete removes a row. The revision block in the top-right corner of the sheet lists them, oldest first, up to 20. Sheet size, title block, revisions and material are saved in the part file, and each change is one step of Undo. Add a section view The part is cut by a plane and the cut is hatched. Like the other views, the section is drawn from facets, so round edges are short straight pieces. In the right panel, set Section A-A to Of the front view, Of the top view or Of the right view. The section appears on the sheet, hatched where the cut passes through material, with the cutting line and its \"A\" arrows on the view it is taken from. Set Cut at (mm) to move the cutting plane. It starts through the middle of the part. Set Section A-A back to None to remove it. Add a detail view Set Detail B to the view to enlarge (or choose Detail in the toolbar). Click the center of the area to enlarge, on that view. A circle labeled \"B\" marks it, and the enlarged \"Detail B\" appears on the sheet with its own scale, for example \"Detail B (2:1)\". Set Detail radius (mm) for the size of the circle and Detail scale for the enlargement: 2, 4, 5 or 10 × the sheet. A detail can be taken from the front, top, right or section view, not from the isometric. If there is no room for every view, the panel says \"No room on the sheet for: detail. Make the detail smaller or drop a view.\" Add a hole table A hole table lists the part's holes with their positions, for drilling or CNC: choose Hole table › Of the top view (or front, or right) in the panel. Every hole seen end-on in that view gets a tag beside it: A1, A2 … for the first size, B1 … for the next. The table lists each tag, its X and Y in millimetres, and its size: \"Ø14 THRU\", \"Ø6.6 × 10 DEEP\", a counterbore or countersink, or a tapped hole's thread callout. X and Y are measured from the lower-left corner of the view's outline, marked X0 Y0 on the sheet. Holes made by patterns are included. Holes that are not square to the view are counted in the panel and left out of the table. The table goes in a free area of the sheet and into the PDF. Place GD&T and surface finish symbols Choose Datum, Frame (a feature control frame) or Finish (a surface finish symbol). Click the point on a view where it attaches. It snaps to the nearest drawn line within 2.5 mm. The symbol appears with a leader, selected, and the right panel shows its settings. Drag a symbol to move it; its leader stays on the point. With a symbol selected, the arrow keys nudge it 1 mm. Delete or Backspace (or Delete in the panel) removes it; Done or Esc deselects it. Every change is in the undo list. Datum Letter: A to Z, without I, O and Q (they read as 1, 0 and O). Each new datum takes the next free letter. Feature control frame Setting Choices Tolerance Straightness, Flatness, Circularity, Cylindricity, Profile of a line, Profile of a surface, Perpendicularity, Angularity, Parallelism, Position, Concentricity and Symmetry (both removed in Y14.5-2018, and marked so), Circular runout, Total runout Zone (mm) The tolerance value, default 0.1 Diameter zone (⌀) For characteristics with a cylindrical zone; on by default for Position Material RFS (none), MMC (Ⓜ), LMC (Ⓛ), where the characteristic allows it Primary / Secondary / Tertiary datum Up to three datum letters, each with a … modifier (MMB, LMB) where allowed PartCraft checks the frame against the rules of ASME Y14.5 and says what is wrong at the top of the panel, for example \"Flatness is a form tolerance: it takes no datum references.\" or \"Position needs at least one datum reference.\" The line Reads spells out the frame. Surface finish Setting Choices Default Ra (µm) Roughness average 1.6 Process any process, machined, no material removed machined Note Up to 40 characters, such as a lay or a process With nothing selected, the panel lists Notes on the sheet, each with Select. Export the PDF Choose PDF in the toolbar, Export PDF in the panel, or Export, then Drawing (PDF): views, section, detail, GD&T from the model. The PDF downloads as \u003Cpart name\u003E - drawing.pdf, and the status bar lists what is on it. Print it at \"Actual size\" on paper of the sheet's size to keep the scale true. Limits Not built yet Do this today Dimensions other than the three overall sizes Add them on the PDF with a markup app, or export a sketch or face as DXF and dimension it in a 2D CAD program More than one section or detail; broken-out, auxiliary and projected views Not available First-angle projection, moving views, portrait sheets Third angle, automatic layout, landscape Free text notes and tables on the sheet (other than the title and revision blocks) Use the finish note, or add text on the PDF Drawings of assemblies Not available Characters outside the Western European set print as \"?\" (the PDF uses the reader's built-in Helvetica). The GD&T symbols are drawn as lines, so they print in any reader."},{"slug":"manufacture","scope":"partcraft","title":"Make toolpaths and G-code (Manufacture)","summary":"Set up stock and a work origin, choose tools, add face, pocket, contour, drill and chamfer operations by picking faces, simulate the stock for gouges and cycle time, and download G-code for Fanuc-style, GRBL or LinuxCNC controls.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["adaptive","adaptive clearing","constant engagement","trochoidal","hsm","high speed machining","manufacture","CAM","toolpath","G-code","NC","CNC","mill","milling","pocket","contour","profile","facing","drill","peck","G83","G81","G73","chamfer","stock","work offset","G54","post processor","GRBL","LinuxCNC","Fanuc","feeds and speeds","simulation","gouge","cycle time"],"headings":[{"id":"set-up","text":"Set up","level":2},{"id":"tools","text":"Tools","level":2},{"id":"add-operations","text":"Add operations","level":2},{"id":"simulate","text":"Simulate","level":2},{"id":"post-the-g-code","text":"Post the G-code","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe Manufacture workspace turns the part into toolpaths for a 3-axis milling machine and writes them as G-code. It is \u003Cstrong\u003E2.5-axis\u003C/strong\u003E: the tool stays vertical, walls are cut straight down and floors flat. It works on the named faces the mesh kernel gives the part (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELab build: check every program in your control&#039;s own simulator, and run it in the air above the stock first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-up\"\u003ESet up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EManufacture\u003C/strong\u003E in the workspace switcher, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew setup\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the \u003Cstrong\u003ESetup\u003C/strong\u003E tab choose:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETool axis (+Z)\u003C/strong\u003E: which way the tool points into the part. World +Y (PartCraft&#039;s up) is the default; machine X is then world X and machine Y is world −Z.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWork origin (G54)\u003C/strong\u003E: a stock corner or centre, top or bottom, the model&#039;s top centre, or the model origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStock\u003C/strong\u003E: a box or a cylinder, either the model plus offsets (sides, top, bottom) or a fixed size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial\u003C/strong\u003E (sets the starting feeds and speeds), the \u003Cstrong\u003Epost\u003C/strong\u003E (control), the machine&#039;s maximum spindle speed, rapid rate and tool change time, coolant and the program number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tools\"\u003ETools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003ETools\u003C/strong\u003E tab holds the library: flat, ball and bull nose end mills, drills, chamfer mills and face mills, each with a T number, diameter, flutes and flute length (plus corner radius, point or chamfer angle where they apply).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESpindle speed and feed come from the material: n = 1000 × cutting speed / (π × diameter), feed = n × flutes × chip load (drills: n × feed per rev). The numbers are conservative starting values from published ranges, not tuned for your machine; type your tool maker&#039;s cutting speed, chip load, rpm or feed to override them. When the speed passes the machine&#039;s maximum it is held there and the feed follows, keeping the chip load.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-operations\"\u003EAdd operations\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach toolbar button adds an operation and, where it needs geometry, starts picking: click faces or edges in the view (click again to drop one), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone picking\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOperation\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPick\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZig-zag passes over the whole stock, past its edges, down to the model&#039;s top\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPocket\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea flat floor face, a round hole, or a closed chain of edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClears the region in offset passes inside-out, at each step-down; islands (faces standing up inside the floor) are left; a floor open all round (nothing higher outside it) is cleared out to the stock edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdaptive\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe same as Pocket\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClears the same region in passes that each cut a set width (\u003Cstrong\u003EEngagement\u003C/strong\u003E, a fraction of the diameter), so the cutter never takes a full-width slot, even in corners; it goes in by a helix and finishes the walls at each level. Narrow cuts allow a deeper step-down and a higher feed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContour\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea flat face (its outline), a round face, or edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFollows the outline offset by the tool radius, outside or inside, with lead-in and lead-out arcs and optional tabs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrill\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eholes (click a hole&#039;s wall or its rim)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrills each hole with a canned cycle: peck (G83), drill (G81) or chip break (G73); or give a diameter to drill every hole of that size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea face&#039;s outline or edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERuns a chamfer mill along the edge so it cuts the width you set\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect an operation in the list to change its tool, heights (clearance, retract, feed height, top, bottom), step-down, stepover, stock to leave, finishing pass, entry (helix, ramp or plunge), climb milling, tabs or cycle. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUp\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDown\u003C/span\u003E reorder, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESuppress\u003C/span\u003E leaves it out, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E removes it. Everything is in the undo history and saved in the part file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pocket&#039;s stepover is at most half the tool diameter, so every point of its floor lies within one tool radius of a pass. Inside corners keep the tool&#039;s radius.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdaptive clearing\u003C/strong\u003E keeps the cutter&#039;s engagement near one value. An engagement of 0.15 of the diameter is an engagement angle of about 46°: the arc of the cutter&#039;s edge in the material. An offset pocket, by contrast, cuts full width on its first pass and wraps further round in every inside corner. Between passes the tool stays down where it moves through cut air, and lifts and comes down in cut air elsewhere. The passes are worked out on a fine grid over the region (cells about a twelfth of the tool radius), so the engagement can run up to about a cell wider than set; the walls are finished exactly. A big open region makes a long program with many short moves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"simulate\"\u003ESimulate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EToolpaths are made again after every change (the part&#039;s too). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESimulate\u003C/span\u003E also shows the stock left after the whole program. The panel shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECycle time\u003C/strong\u003E: feed moves at their feed, rapids as straight lines at the setup&#039;s rapid rate, plus the tool change time. Real machines accelerate, so they take longer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGouges\u003C/strong\u003E: places where the tool went more than 0.02 mm below the part&#039;s surface. Red marks show them in the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStock left on the part\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe stock is a height map of the top (a Z-map), so it cannot show undercuts. Drag \u003Cstrong\u003EPlayback\u003C/strong\u003E to move the tool along the program. In the view, feed moves are solid and rapids dashed, one colour per operation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"post-the-g-code\"\u003EPost the G-code\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose the control under \u003Cstrong\u003EPost\u003C/strong\u003E and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload G-code\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPost\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EControl\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFanuc-style ISO\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E.nc\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E%, O number, line numbers, T M6 with G43 H, canned cycles\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGRBL\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E.nc\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo tool changer or canned cycles: a tool change pauses with M0, drilling is written as plain moves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinuxCNC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E.ngc\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ET M6 with G43 H, canned cycles, M2\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery file starts with comments for the program, stock, work offset and tools. Coordinates are in millimetres with 3 decimals. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolpath CSV\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolpath JSON\u003C/span\u003E export the moves themselves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E2.5-axis only: no 3D roughing or finishing of curved faces (the flange&#039;s cone is left as steps), no rest machining. Adaptive clearing is 2D (flat floors).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHolders and shanks are not checked for collisions; a warning appears when a cut is deeper than the flute length.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFeeds and speeds are starting values; there is no machine-specific post editor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The Manufacture workspace turns the part into toolpaths for a 3-axis milling machine and writes them as G-code. It is 2.5-axis: the tool stays vertical, walls are cut straight down and floors flat. It works on the named faces the mesh kernel gives the part (see The mesh kernel). Lab build: check every program in your control's own simulator, and run it in the air above the stock first. Set up Choose Manufacture in the workspace switcher, then New setup. On the Setup tab choose: Tool axis (+Z): which way the tool points into the part. World +Y (PartCraft's up) is the default; machine X is then world X and machine Y is world −Z. Work origin (G54): a stock corner or centre, top or bottom, the model's top centre, or the model origin. Stock: a box or a cylinder, either the model plus offsets (sides, top, bottom) or a fixed size. Material (sets the starting feeds and speeds), the post (control), the machine's maximum spindle speed, rapid rate and tool change time, coolant and the program number. Tools The Tools tab holds the library: flat, ball and bull nose end mills, drills, chamfer mills and face mills, each with a T number, diameter, flutes and flute length (plus corner radius, point or chamfer angle where they apply). Spindle speed and feed come from the material: n = 1000 × cutting speed / (π × diameter), feed = n × flutes × chip load (drills: n × feed per rev). The numbers are conservative starting values from published ranges, not tuned for your machine; type your tool maker's cutting speed, chip load, rpm or feed to override them. When the speed passes the machine's maximum it is held there and the feed follows, keeping the chip load. Add operations Each toolbar button adds an operation and, where it needs geometry, starts picking: click faces or edges in the view (click again to drop one), then Done picking or Esc. Operation Pick What it does Face nothing Zig-zag passes over the whole stock, past its edges, down to the model's top Pocket a flat floor face, a round hole, or a closed chain of edges Clears the region in offset passes inside-out, at each step-down; islands (faces standing up inside the floor) are left; a floor open all round (nothing higher outside it) is cleared out to the stock edge Adaptive the same as Pocket Clears the same region in passes that each cut a set width (Engagement, a fraction of the diameter), so the cutter never takes a full-width slot, even in corners; it goes in by a helix and finishes the walls at each level. Narrow cuts allow a deeper step-down and a higher feed Contour a flat face (its outline), a round face, or edges Follows the outline offset by the tool radius, outside or inside, with lead-in and lead-out arcs and optional tabs Drill holes (click a hole's wall or its rim) Drills each hole with a canned cycle: peck (G83), drill (G81) or chip break (G73); or give a diameter to drill every hole of that size Chamfer a face's outline or edges Runs a chamfer mill along the edge so it cuts the width you set Select an operation in the list to change its tool, heights (clearance, retract, feed height, top, bottom), step-down, stepover, stock to leave, finishing pass, entry (helix, ramp or plunge), climb milling, tabs or cycle. Up and Down reorder, Suppress leaves it out, Delete removes it. Everything is in the undo history and saved in the part file. A pocket's stepover is at most half the tool diameter, so every point of its floor lies within one tool radius of a pass. Inside corners keep the tool's radius. Adaptive clearing keeps the cutter's engagement near one value. An engagement of 0.15 of the diameter is an engagement angle of about 46°: the arc of the cutter's edge in the material. An offset pocket, by contrast, cuts full width on its first pass and wraps further round in every inside corner. Between passes the tool stays down where it moves through cut air, and lifts and comes down in cut air elsewhere. The passes are worked out on a fine grid over the region (cells about a twelfth of the tool radius), so the engagement can run up to about a cell wider than set; the walls are finished exactly. A big open region makes a long program with many short moves. Simulate Toolpaths are made again after every change (the part's too). Simulate also shows the stock left after the whole program. The panel shows: Cycle time: feed moves at their feed, rapids as straight lines at the setup's rapid rate, plus the tool change time. Real machines accelerate, so they take longer. Gouges: places where the tool went more than 0.02 mm below the part's surface. Red marks show them in the view. Stock left on the part. The stock is a height map of the top (a Z-map), so it cannot show undercuts. Drag Playback to move the tool along the program. In the view, feed moves are solid and rapids dashed, one colour per operation. Post the G-code Choose the control under Post and press Download G-code (or Post on the toolbar): Control File Notes Fanuc-style ISO .nc %, O number, line numbers, T M6 with G43 H, canned cycles GRBL .nc No tool changer or canned cycles: a tool change pauses with M0, drilling is written as plain moves LinuxCNC .ngc T M6 with G43 H, canned cycles, M2 Every file starts with comments for the program, stock, work offset and tools. Coordinates are in millimetres with 3 decimals. Toolpath CSV and Toolpath JSON export the moves themselves. Limits 2.5-axis only: no 3D roughing or finishing of curved faces (the flange's cone is left as steps), no rest machining. Adaptive clearing is 2D (flat floors). Holders and shanks are not checked for collisions; a warning appears when a cut is deeper than the flute length. Feeds and speeds are starting values; there is no machine-specific post editor."},{"slug":"simulation","scope":"partcraft","title":"Check stress and deflection (Simulation)","summary":"Pick a material, fix faces, add forces, pressures or the part's own weight, solve, and read von Mises stress, displacement, safety factor, reactions and natural frequencies on the model, with what the method can and cannot tell you.","section":"tasks","order":142,"keywords":["simulation","FEA","finite element","stress","strain","von Mises","displacement","deflection","safety factor","factor of safety","yield","reaction","load","force","pressure","gravity","self weight","fixed","frictionless","support","constraint","material","steel","aluminum","titanium","ABS","PLA","nylon","natural frequency","modal","vibration","mesh","element size","tetrahedral","tetrahedra","tet mesh","voxel","mesh quality","principal stress","equivalent strain","error estimate","curved faces","holes","fillets","adaptive","adaptive refinement","automatic refinement","h-adaptive","convergence","mesh convergence","exact surface","sphere","cone","torus","spline","freeform"],"headings":[{"id":"set-up-a-study","text":"Set up a study","level":2},{"id":"refine-automatically","text":"Refine automatically","level":3},{"id":"hold-it-and-load-it","text":"Hold it and load it","level":2},{"id":"solve-and-read-the-results","text":"Solve and read the results","level":2},{"id":"how-far-to-trust-it","text":"How far to trust it","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe Simulation workspace answers &quot;will it bend or break under this load?&quot; for the part you are modelling. It is a \u003Cstrong\u003Elinear static\u003C/strong\u003E study (small deflections, the material stays elastic) or a \u003Cstrong\u003Enatural frequency\u003C/strong\u003E study, solved on a mesh of quadratic tetrahedra that follows the part&#039;s faces (or, if you choose, of small equal cubes). It works on the named faces the mesh kernel gives the part (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003ESimulation\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EWorkspace\u003C/strong\u003E box at the left of the toolbar. Your part is not changed; the study is saved inside the part file and every change to it can be undone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-up-a-study\"\u003ESet up a study\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003ENew study\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial\u003C/strong\u003E. The panel shows its stiffness (E), Poisson&#039;s ratio, density and yield strength. The values are typical handbook figures; your supplier&#039;s certified values may differ.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003EMesh\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETetrahedral (accurate)\u003C/strong\u003E, the default: 10-node tetrahedra whose outer faces lie on the part&#039;s faces, with curved edges on holes, fillets and other round faces. The nodes on flat, cylindrical, conical, spherical and toroidal faces, revolved and extruded faces and freeform (B-spline) faces sit on the exact surface; any other kind of face follows its display mesh, and the results table says how many faces did. The mesh is made finer by itself near curved faces, thin walls and sharp edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVoxel cubes (fast)\u003C/strong\u003E: the part filled with equal cubes. Quick, but curved faces become steps. Studies saved before the tetrahedral mesh existed keep this.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave \u003Cstrong\u003EElement size\u003C/strong\u003E empty for an automatic size (about 12,000 tetrahedra, or 20,000 cubes), or type one in millimetres. The panel shows about how many elements that makes before the finer spots are added. With tetrahedra, \u003Cstrong\u003ESmaller elements on curved faces\u003C/strong\u003E (on by default) refines holes and fillets, where stress usually peaks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"refine-automatically\"\u003ERefine automatically\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith tetrahedra, a static or buckling study (without a thermal load), a natural-frequency study and a steady thermal study offer \u003Cstrong\u003ERefine automatically (adaptive)\u003C/strong\u003E. Tick it and set:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETarget error (%)\u003C/strong\u003E: solving stops when the estimated error is below it (1 % to start with).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMost passes\u003C/strong\u003E: the most solves it may run (5 to start with, at most 10).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach pass solves, finds the elements with the largest estimated error, splits them (and as few neighbours as needed to keep the mesh whole), and solves again. New nodes on curved faces go onto the exact surface. The results then show a table and a chart with, for each pass, the elements, the unknowns, the estimated error and the peak stress (or temperature) and displacement (or heat flux), and a line saying why it stopped: the target was met, the passes ran out, the next mesh would be too big, or a pass failed (you then see the last good pass).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERead the estimate as an estimate. It (Zienkiewicz–Zhu, compared with exact answers it reads about 0.6 to 0.95 of the true error) measures the error over the \u003Cstrong\u003Ewhole part\u003C/strong\u003E, so a peak stress at a hole or a fillet is usually still a little off when the estimate looks small: for a stress concentration, set the target under 1 % and watch the peak settle from pass to pass. At a sharp inside corner or the edge of a fixed face the true stress is infinite, so the estimate never gets there: the passes run out instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENatural frequencies and buckling.\u003C/strong\u003E For a natural-frequency study the estimate covers every mode you asked for, each counted alike; the table and chart follow the first two frequencies. On a part with only flat faces a frequency can only go down from pass to pass (each mesh holds the one before it); curved faces also get closer to their true shape, which can move a frequency a little either way. For buckling the estimate covers the pre-stress and the buckling modes, and the table follows the load factors. Checked on a 100 × 10 × 10 mm steel bar: the first frequency settles from 812.4 to 811.9 Hz over four passes, 0.4 % under Euler–Bernoulli (which ignores shear), and the column&#039;s load factor within 0.5 % of Euler.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hold-it-and-load-it\"\u003EHold it and load it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery study needs at least one support and, for stress, one load.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFixed\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. The new support is selected and waiting for faces: click the faces that are held still (click a face again to drop it). Press \u003Cstrong\u003EDone picking\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when finished. Blue cones mark the held faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForce\u003C/span\u003E and click the faces the force acts on. In the panel type the \u003Cstrong\u003ETotal force\u003C/strong\u003E in newtons and choose its \u003Cstrong\u003EDirection\u003C/strong\u003E: into the face, or along an axis. The force is shared over the picked faces by area. Orange arrows show it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPressure\u003C/span\u003E for a pressure in MPa (N/mm²) pushing into the picked faces; a negative pressure pulls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMore kinds are in the panel&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd\u003C/strong\u003E list: a \u003Cstrong\u003EFrictionless face\u003C/strong\u003E (the face may slide along itself but not move away from it — use it for symmetry planes and smooth supports) and \u003Cstrong\u003EGravity\u003C/strong\u003E (the part&#039;s own weight, 9.81 m/s², in the direction you choose).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA support that leaves the part free to slide or spin is refused, saying which motion is left free.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"solve-and-read-the-results\"\u003ESolve and read the results\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolve\u003C/span\u003E. The work runs in the background; \u003Cstrong\u003EStop\u003C/strong\u003E cancels it. Then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe part is coloured by \u003Cstrong\u003Evon Mises stress\u003C/strong\u003E; choose \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplacement\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ESafety factor\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cstrong\u003EShow\u003C/strong\u003E. The legend gives the scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint at the part to read the values under the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick \u003Cstrong\u003EDeformed shape\u003C/strong\u003E to see the part bent (the motion is enlarged by the \u003Cstrong\u003EScale\u003C/strong\u003E; the real one is the table&#039;s).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe table lists the largest stress and where it is, the largest displacement, the smallest \u003Cstrong\u003Esafety factor\u003C/strong\u003E (yield strength ÷ von Mises stress; below 1 the material yields), the applied load and the \u003Cstrong\u003Ereactions\u003C/strong\u003E at the supports (they balance the load), and the mesh used.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor \u003Cstrong\u003Enatural frequencies\u003C/strong\u003E, choose that \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E, set how many \u003Cstrong\u003EModes\u003C/strong\u003E, and solve. Each mode shows its frequency and shape (shapes are scaled to 1 at their largest motion).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you change the part, the results are marked out of date: solve again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-far-to-trust-it\"\u003EHow far to trust it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft checks its solver against hand formulas (a cantilever&#039;s tip deflection PL³/3EI within 1 %, a bar&#039;s stretch PL/AE exactly, a cantilever&#039;s first frequency within 3 %, the stress around a hole in a plate). Still, keep these limits in mind:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe mesh.\u003C/strong\u003E With tetrahedra, PartCraft checks a cantilever&#039;s deflection and bending stress (within 0.3 %), the stress at a hole in a plate (Kt, within about 1 %), a thick tube under pressure (Lamé, within 0.3 %) and the first frequency (within 0.4 %). The table shows the \u003Cstrong\u003Emesh quality\u003C/strong\u003E (the smallest and largest angles between element faces; below about 10° an element is poor) and an \u003Cstrong\u003Eestimated stress error\u003C/strong\u003E: if it is more than a few percent, solve again with a smaller element size, or let \u003Cstrong\u003ERefine automatically\u003C/strong\u003E do it. On a plate with a hole and a bar with two edge notches, automatic refinement got within 1 % of the textbook Kt with about a sixth of the unknowns that making the whole mesh finer needed. With voxel cubes, a hole or a fillet is a staircase at the cube size, so stress there is rough.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESharp inside corners and the edges of fixed faces have no true peak stress.\u003C/strong\u003E The value there keeps growing as the elements get smaller. Read stresses a little away from such places, or round the corner in the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EResults to read.\u003C/strong\u003E Besides von Mises stress, displacement and safety factor, a tetrahedral study shows the \u003Cstrong\u003Emaximum and minimum principal stress\u003C/strong\u003E (tension and compression) and the \u003Cstrong\u003Eequivalent strain\u003C/strong\u003E. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003EShow mesh\u003C/strong\u003E to see the elements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinear and elastic only.\u003C/strong\u003E No yielding, contact between parts, large deflections or fatigue. Heat, thermal stress and linear buckling are their own study types: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/thermal-and-buckling\" data-topic=\"partcraft/thermal-and-buckling\"\u003EHeat, thermal stress and buckling\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery material is the same in all directions.\u003C/strong\u003E 3D-printed plastics are weaker across their layers, often by a third or more.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis is a lab build: check parts that matter another way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/thermal-and-buckling\" data-topic=\"partcraft/thermal-and-buckling\"\u003EHeat, thermal stress and buckling\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/generative-design\" data-topic=\"partcraft/generative-design\"\u003EGrow a shape from loads (Generative)\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/measure\" data-topic=\"partcraft/measure\"\u003EMeasure distances, angles and mass\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The Simulation workspace answers \"will it bend or break under this load?\" for the part you are modelling. It is a linear static study (small deflections, the material stays elastic) or a natural frequency study, solved on a mesh of quadratic tetrahedra that follows the part's faces (or, if you choose, of small equal cubes). It works on the named faces the mesh kernel gives the part (see The mesh kernel). Choose Simulation in the Workspace box at the left of the toolbar. Your part is not changed; the study is saved inside the part file and every change to it can be undone. Set up a study Press New study in the right panel. Choose the Material. The panel shows its stiffness (E), Poisson's ratio, density and yield strength. The values are typical handbook figures; your supplier's certified values may differ. Choose the Mesh: Tetrahedral (accurate), the default: 10-node tetrahedra whose outer faces lie on the part's faces, with curved edges on holes, fillets and other round faces. The nodes on flat, cylindrical, conical, spherical and toroidal faces, revolved and extruded faces and freeform (B-spline) faces sit on the exact surface; any other kind of face follows its display mesh, and the results table says how many faces did. The mesh is made finer by itself near curved faces, thin walls and sharp edges. Voxel cubes (fast): the part filled with equal cubes. Quick, but curved faces become steps. Studies saved before the tetrahedral mesh existed keep this. Leave Element size empty for an automatic size (about 12,000 tetrahedra, or 20,000 cubes), or type one in millimetres. The panel shows about how many elements that makes before the finer spots are added. With tetrahedra, Smaller elements on curved faces (on by default) refines holes and fillets, where stress usually peaks. Refine automatically With tetrahedra, a static or buckling study (without a thermal load), a natural-frequency study and a steady thermal study offer Refine automatically (adaptive). Tick it and set: Target error (%): solving stops when the estimated error is below it (1 % to start with). Most passes: the most solves it may run (5 to start with, at most 10). Each pass solves, finds the elements with the largest estimated error, splits them (and as few neighbours as needed to keep the mesh whole), and solves again. New nodes on curved faces go onto the exact surface. The results then show a table and a chart with, for each pass, the elements, the unknowns, the estimated error and the peak stress (or temperature) and displacement (or heat flux), and a line saying why it stopped: the target was met, the passes ran out, the next mesh would be too big, or a pass failed (you then see the last good pass). Read the estimate as an estimate. It (Zienkiewicz–Zhu, compared with exact answers it reads about 0.6 to 0.95 of the true error) measures the error over the whole part, so a peak stress at a hole or a fillet is usually still a little off when the estimate looks small: for a stress concentration, set the target under 1 % and watch the peak settle from pass to pass. At a sharp inside corner or the edge of a fixed face the true stress is infinite, so the estimate never gets there: the passes run out instead. Natural frequencies and buckling. For a natural-frequency study the estimate covers every mode you asked for, each counted alike; the table and chart follow the first two frequencies. On a part with only flat faces a frequency can only go down from pass to pass (each mesh holds the one before it); curved faces also get closer to their true shape, which can move a frequency a little either way. For buckling the estimate covers the pre-stress and the buckling modes, and the table follows the load factors. Checked on a 100 × 10 × 10 mm steel bar: the first frequency settles from 812.4 to 811.9 Hz over four passes, 0.4 % under Euler–Bernoulli (which ignores shear), and the column's load factor within 0.5 % of Euler. Hold it and load it Every study needs at least one support and, for stress, one load. Press Fixed in the toolbar. The new support is selected and waiting for faces: click the faces that are held still (click a face again to drop it). Press Done picking or Esc when finished. Blue cones mark the held faces. Press Force and click the faces the force acts on. In the panel type the Total force in newtons and choose its Direction: into the face, or along an axis. The force is shared over the picked faces by area. Orange arrows show it. Press Pressure for a pressure in MPa (N/mm²) pushing into the picked faces; a negative pressure pulls. More kinds are in the panel's Add list: a Frictionless face (the face may slide along itself but not move away from it — use it for symmetry planes and smooth supports) and Gravity (the part's own weight, 9.81 m/s², in the direction you choose). A support that leaves the part free to slide or spin is refused, saying which motion is left free. Solve and read the results Press Solve. The work runs in the background; Stop cancels it. Then: The part is coloured by von Mises stress; choose Displacement or Safety factor in Show. The legend gives the scale. Point at the part to read the values under the pointer. Tick Deformed shape to see the part bent (the motion is enlarged by the Scale; the real one is the table's). The table lists the largest stress and where it is, the largest displacement, the smallest safety factor (yield strength ÷ von Mises stress; below 1 the material yields), the applied load and the reactions at the supports (they balance the load), and the mesh used. For natural frequencies, choose that Type, set how many Modes, and solve. Each mode shows its frequency and shape (shapes are scaled to 1 at their largest motion). If you change the part, the results are marked out of date: solve again. How far to trust it PartCraft checks its solver against hand formulas (a cantilever's tip deflection PL³/3EI within 1 %, a bar's stretch PL/AE exactly, a cantilever's first frequency within 3 %, the stress around a hole in a plate). Still, keep these limits in mind: The mesh. With tetrahedra, PartCraft checks a cantilever's deflection and bending stress (within 0.3 %), the stress at a hole in a plate (Kt, within about 1 %), a thick tube under pressure (Lamé, within 0.3 %) and the first frequency (within 0.4 %). The table shows the mesh quality (the smallest and largest angles between element faces; below about 10° an element is poor) and an estimated stress error: if it is more than a few percent, solve again with a smaller element size, or let Refine automatically do it. On a plate with a hole and a bar with two edge notches, automatic refinement got within 1 % of the textbook Kt with about a sixth of the unknowns that making the whole mesh finer needed. With voxel cubes, a hole or a fillet is a staircase at the cube size, so stress there is rough. Sharp inside corners and the edges of fixed faces have no true peak stress. The value there keeps growing as the elements get smaller. Read stresses a little away from such places, or round the corner in the model. Results to read. Besides von Mises stress, displacement and safety factor, a tetrahedral study shows the maximum and minimum principal stress (tension and compression) and the equivalent strain. Tick Show mesh to see the elements. Linear and elastic only. No yielding, contact between parts, large deflections or fatigue. Heat, thermal stress and linear buckling are their own study types: see Heat, thermal stress and buckling. Every material is the same in all directions. 3D-printed plastics are weaker across their layers, often by a third or more. This is a lab build: check parts that matter another way. Related Heat, thermal stress and buckling Grow a shape from loads (Generative) Measure distances, angles and mass What is not built yet"},{"slug":"thermal-and-buckling","scope":"partcraft","title":"Heat, thermal stress and buckling","summary":"Find the temperatures in a part from fixed temperatures, heat flux, heat power, convection and radiation (steady or over time), load a stress study with a temperature rise, and find the load at which a slender part buckles, with what each method can and cannot tell you.","section":"tasks","order":143,"keywords":["thermal","heat transfer","heat","temperature","conduction","convection","film coefficient","radiation","emissivity","heat flux","heat power","heat generation","heat sink","transient","time","cooling","heating","Crank-Nicolson","implicit Euler","heat balance","thermal stress","thermal stress over time","transient thermal stress","quench","thermal shock","stress history","worst time","thermal expansion","expansion coefficient","buckling","Euler buckling","load factor","critical load","column","slender","eigenvalue"],"headings":[{"id":"thermal-where-does-the-heat-go","text":"Thermal: where does the heat go?","level":2},{"id":"over-time-transient","text":"Over time (transient)","level":3},{"id":"thermal-stress-what-does-a-temperature-change-do-to-the-part","text":"Thermal stress: what does a temperature change do to the part?","level":2},{"id":"buckling-when-does-a-slender-part-give-way","text":"Buckling: when does a slender part give way?","level":2},{"id":"how-far-to-trust-it","text":"How far to trust it","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThese studies live in the Simulation workspace next to static stress and natural frequencies (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/simulation\" data-topic=\"partcraft/simulation\"\u003ECheck stress and deflection\u003C/a\u003E). Pick them in the panel&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E box. They use the same mesh of small cubes and the same material list; every library material has a thermal conductivity, a specific heat and an expansion coefficient (typical handbook values, shown under \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"thermal-where-does-the-heat-go\"\u003EThermal: where does the heat go?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EThermal (heat transfer)\u003C/strong\u003E as the \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd thermal conditions from the panel&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd\u003C/strong\u003E list, then click their faces (as for supports and loads):\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFixed temperature\u003C/strong\u003E: the face is held at a temperature, in °C.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHeat flux\u003C/strong\u003E: W/m² flowing into the face (negative flows out). It acts on the face&#039;s true area, even where the cubes make a staircase.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHeat power\u003C/strong\u003E: a total in watts, shared over the picked faces by area. Use it for a heater or a chip of known power.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConvection\u003C/strong\u003E: air or liquid at a temperature carries heat away at h W/(m²·K). Typical h: still air 5–10, a fan 25–100, water 500–10,000. PartCraft does not compute h: there is no fluid flow in this study.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERadiation\u003C/strong\u003E: the face radiates to surroundings at a temperature, with an emissivity from 0 to 1. It is \u003Cstrong\u003Elinearised\u003C/strong\u003E (the radiation is written as a coefficient that depends on the temperature and re-solved until it settles) and goes only to the surroundings: faces do not exchange heat with each other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHeat generation\u003C/strong\u003E (W/m³) heats the whole part from inside, for example a resistive part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003ESolve\u003C/strong\u003E. The part is coloured by temperature; choose \u003Cstrong\u003EHeat flux\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cstrong\u003EShow\u003C/strong\u003E to see where the heat flows. Point at the part to read both.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESteady heat flow needs somewhere for the heat to go: at least one fixed temperature, convection or radiation. A part that only takes heat in has no steady state; make it transient instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe table lists the lowest and highest temperature and where they are, the largest heat flux, and the \u003Cstrong\u003Eheat balance\u003C/strong\u003E: the heat into or out of each condition (a fixed temperature counts what it must supply or absorb), the heat generated, the totals in and out, and the \u003Cstrong\u003Eimbalance\u003C/strong\u003E. The imbalance should be a tiny fraction of the heat in; a large one means the result is not to be trusted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"over-time-transient\"\u003EOver time (transient)\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETick \u003Cstrong\u003ETransient\u003C/strong\u003E and give a \u003Cstrong\u003EStart temperature\u003C/strong\u003E (the whole part starts there), a \u003Cstrong\u003ETime span\u003C/strong\u003E and a number of \u003Cstrong\u003ETime steps\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EImplicit Euler\u003C/strong\u003E is robust; \u003Cstrong\u003ECrank–Nicolson\u003C/strong\u003E is more accurate for the same steps but can ripple after a sudden change. After solving, drag the \u003Cstrong\u003ETime\u003C/strong\u003E slider to see the temperatures at each moment (the colour scale stays the same for every moment). The heat balance then includes the heat being stored.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"thermal-stress-what-does-a-temperature-change-do-to-the-part\"\u003EThermal stress: what does a temperature change do to the part?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a \u003Cstrong\u003EStatic stress\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003EBuckling\u003C/strong\u003E) study, set \u003Cstrong\u003EThermal load\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUniform temperature change\u003C/strong\u003E: the whole part warms (or cools, with a negative number) by that many kelvin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETemperatures from thermal conditions\u003C/strong\u003E: add thermal conditions to the same study; the steady temperatures are solved first on the same mesh, then the part expands by α·(T − the \u003Cstrong\u003Estress-free temperature\u003C/strong\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETemperatures over time (transient run)\u003C/strong\u003E: add thermal conditions, then give a \u003Cstrong\u003EStart temperature\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETime span\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETime steps\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ETime stepping\u003C/strong\u003E as for a transient thermal study. The temperatures are solved over time on the same mesh (cubes or tetrahedra), and a static solve on each moment&#039;s temperatures gives that moment&#039;s stress. \u003Cstrong\u003EStress at\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe worst time\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): \u003Cstrong\u003EStress solves\u003C/strong\u003E moments spread evenly over the run, the start included (21 unless you change it, at most 51). The results open at the moment with the largest von Mises stress; the table names it, a small chart shows the peak stress against time, and the \u003Cstrong\u003ETime\u003C/strong\u003E slider shows the stress at every moment solved (the colour scale stays that of the worst moment).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne chosen time\u003C/strong\u003E: one moment, the time step nearest the \u003Cstrong\u003ETime (s)\u003C/strong\u003E you give. A buckling study always takes one chosen time (the end of the run unless you set one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA part that is quenched (dipped from hot into cold) or suddenly heated is stressed most while the inside and the surface differ most: often right at the start. A held temperature applies from the first instant, as a sudden change, so the stress at the very start depends on the element size (only a few elements see the change). Read it as &quot;very high&quot;, and look at the slider&#039;s next moments for values the mesh resolves. The stress is quasi-static: no inertia, no vibration from the sudden change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA part that is free to grow has no thermal stress: hold it just enough to stop it moving (for example three frictionless faces at right angles). Held between two walls it is squeezed: a steel bar warmed 50 K between rigid walls carries about 117 MPa. Thermal loads balance themselves, so the reactions still balance only the mechanical loads.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"buckling-when-does-a-slender-part-give-way\"\u003EBuckling: when does a slender part give way?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EBuckling (linear)\u003C/strong\u003E as the \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E, and add supports and loads as for a static study.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003ELoad factors to find\u003C/strong\u003E (how many buckling modes).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003ESolve\u003C/strong\u003E. Each mode has a \u003Cstrong\u003Eload factor\u003C/strong\u003E: the multiple of your loads at which the ideal part buckles. A load factor of 3 means three times your loads; \u003Cstrong\u003Ea load factor below 1 means it buckles under the loads you applied\u003C/strong\u003E. The table also gives the total load at each factor. The mode shape shows how it buckles (its size means nothing).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinear buckling overestimates the real buckling load.\u003C/strong\u003E It assumes a perfect part: perfectly straight, with no residual stress, and elastic. Real parts have small imperfections and can yield first, and thin-walled shells can buckle at a fraction of the linear value. Use a generous margin, and treat a load factor near or below 3 for a thin shell as a warning.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-far-to-trust-it\"\u003EHow far to trust it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft checks these solvers against hand formulas: a bar between two temperatures (exact), a cooling fin (within 0.3 K), a slab heating itself (exact), a cube cooling over time (within 0.1 % with Crank–Nicolson), a bar suddenly heated at one end, a free bar warmed (no stress, ΔL = αLΔT), a bar held between walls (σ = −EαΔT), a quenched plate over time against the classic series solution (within 1 % on cubes, 2.1 % on tetrahedra at the plate&#039;s centre; 0.5 % at its surface), a long run&#039;s last moment against the steady thermal stress (the same to a millionth), and Euler columns (within 1 % fixed–free, 1.5 % pinned–pinned). Keep in mind:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConstant material values.\u003C/strong\u003E Conductivity, specific heat and expansion do not change with temperature here; at a few hundred degrees they can change by 10–30 %. Stiffness does not change either.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStress over time is quasi-static and elastic\u003C/strong\u003E: each moment is a static solve (no inertia), and no yielding, so a quench that would yield the surface shows a stress above yield instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConvection coefficients are your input\u003C/strong\u003E, not computed from an air or water flow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo contact resistance\u003C/strong\u003E: the part is one solid.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinear buckling only\u003C/strong\u003E: no imperfections, no yielding, no behaviour after buckling.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe cube staircase and sharp inside corners affect thermal stresses the same way as other stresses (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/simulation\" data-topic=\"partcraft/simulation\"\u003ECheck stress and deflection\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis is a lab build: check parts that matter another way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/simulation\" data-topic=\"partcraft/simulation\"\u003ECheck stress and deflection (Simulation)\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"These studies live in the Simulation workspace next to static stress and natural frequencies (see Check stress and deflection). Pick them in the panel's Type box. They use the same mesh of small cubes and the same material list; every library material has a thermal conductivity, a specific heat and an expansion coefficient (typical handbook values, shown under Properties). Thermal: where does the heat go? Choose Thermal (heat transfer) as the Type. Add thermal conditions from the panel's Add list, then click their faces (as for supports and loads): Fixed temperature: the face is held at a temperature, in °C. Heat flux: W/m² flowing into the face (negative flows out). It acts on the face's true area, even where the cubes make a staircase. Heat power: a total in watts, shared over the picked faces by area. Use it for a heater or a chip of known power. Convection: air or liquid at a temperature carries heat away at h W/(m²·K). Typical h: still air 5–10, a fan 25–100, water 500–10,000. PartCraft does not compute h: there is no fluid flow in this study. Radiation: the face radiates to surroundings at a temperature, with an emissivity from 0 to 1. It is linearised (the radiation is written as a coefficient that depends on the temperature and re-solved until it settles) and goes only to the surroundings: faces do not exchange heat with each other. Heat generation (W/m³) heats the whole part from inside, for example a resistive part. Press Solve. The part is coloured by temperature; choose Heat flux in Show to see where the heat flows. Point at the part to read both. Steady heat flow needs somewhere for the heat to go: at least one fixed temperature, convection or radiation. A part that only takes heat in has no steady state; make it transient instead. The table lists the lowest and highest temperature and where they are, the largest heat flux, and the heat balance: the heat into or out of each condition (a fixed temperature counts what it must supply or absorb), the heat generated, the totals in and out, and the imbalance. The imbalance should be a tiny fraction of the heat in; a large one means the result is not to be trusted. Over time (transient) Tick Transient and give a Start temperature (the whole part starts there), a Time span and a number of Time steps. Implicit Euler is robust; Crank–Nicolson is more accurate for the same steps but can ripple after a sudden change. After solving, drag the Time slider to see the temperatures at each moment (the colour scale stays the same for every moment). The heat balance then includes the heat being stored. Thermal stress: what does a temperature change do to the part? In a Static stress (or Buckling) study, set Thermal load: Uniform temperature change: the whole part warms (or cools, with a negative number) by that many kelvin. Temperatures from thermal conditions: add thermal conditions to the same study; the steady temperatures are solved first on the same mesh, then the part expands by α·(T − the stress-free temperature). Temperatures over time (transient run): add thermal conditions, then give a Start temperature, Time span, Time steps and Time stepping as for a transient thermal study. The temperatures are solved over time on the same mesh (cubes or tetrahedra), and a static solve on each moment's temperatures gives that moment's stress. Stress at: The worst time (the start): Stress solves moments spread evenly over the run, the start included (21 unless you change it, at most 51). The results open at the moment with the largest von Mises stress; the table names it, a small chart shows the peak stress against time, and the Time slider shows the stress at every moment solved (the colour scale stays that of the worst moment). One chosen time: one moment, the time step nearest the Time (s) you give. A buckling study always takes one chosen time (the end of the run unless you set one). A part that is quenched (dipped from hot into cold) or suddenly heated is stressed most while the inside and the surface differ most: often right at the start. A held temperature applies from the first instant, as a sudden change, so the stress at the very start depends on the element size (only a few elements see the change). Read it as \"very high\", and look at the slider's next moments for values the mesh resolves. The stress is quasi-static: no inertia, no vibration from the sudden change. A part that is free to grow has no thermal stress: hold it just enough to stop it moving (for example three frictionless faces at right angles). Held between two walls it is squeezed: a steel bar warmed 50 K between rigid walls carries about 117 MPa. Thermal loads balance themselves, so the reactions still balance only the mechanical loads. Buckling: when does a slender part give way? Choose Buckling (linear) as the Type, and add supports and loads as for a static study. Set Load factors to find (how many buckling modes). Press Solve. Each mode has a load factor: the multiple of your loads at which the ideal part buckles. A load factor of 3 means three times your loads; a load factor below 1 means it buckles under the loads you applied. The table also gives the total load at each factor. The mode shape shows how it buckles (its size means nothing). Linear buckling overestimates the real buckling load. It assumes a perfect part: perfectly straight, with no residual stress, and elastic. Real parts have small imperfections and can yield first, and thin-walled shells can buckle at a fraction of the linear value. Use a generous margin, and treat a load factor near or below 3 for a thin shell as a warning. How far to trust it PartCraft checks these solvers against hand formulas: a bar between two temperatures (exact), a cooling fin (within 0.3 K), a slab heating itself (exact), a cube cooling over time (within 0.1 % with Crank–Nicolson), a bar suddenly heated at one end, a free bar warmed (no stress, ΔL = αLΔT), a bar held between walls (σ = −EαΔT), a quenched plate over time against the classic series solution (within 1 % on cubes, 2.1 % on tetrahedra at the plate's centre; 0.5 % at its surface), a long run's last moment against the steady thermal stress (the same to a millionth), and Euler columns (within 1 % fixed–free, 1.5 % pinned–pinned). Keep in mind: Constant material values. Conductivity, specific heat and expansion do not change with temperature here; at a few hundred degrees they can change by 10–30 %. Stiffness does not change either. Stress over time is quasi-static and elastic: each moment is a static solve (no inertia), and no yielding, so a quench that would yield the surface shows a stress above yield instead. Convection coefficients are your input, not computed from an air or water flow. No contact resistance: the part is one solid. Linear buckling only: no imperfections, no yielding, no behaviour after buckling. The cube staircase and sharp inside corners affect thermal stresses the same way as other stresses (see Check stress and deflection). This is a lab build: check parts that matter another way. Related Check stress and deflection (Simulation) What is not built yet"},{"slug":"generative-design","scope":"partcraft","title":"Grow a shape from loads (Generative)","summary":"Pick the faces that are held and loaded, regions to keep and to keep clear, target volumes and materials, and generate outcomes: the stiffest shapes found for each volume, checked again as solids, compared in a table and downloaded as STL, STEP or a new part.","section":"tasks","order":144,"keywords":["generative design","topology optimization","topology optimisation","SIMP","lightweight","lattice","organic shape","outcomes","compare","mass","volume fraction","preserve","keep-in","obstacle","keep-out","design space","minimum member size","STL","3D printing"],"headings":[{"id":"set-up-a-study","text":"Set up a study","level":2},{"id":"goals","text":"Goals","level":2},{"id":"generate-and-compare","text":"Generate and compare","level":2},{"id":"use-an-outcome","text":"Use an outcome","level":2},{"id":"how-far-to-trust-it","text":"How far to trust it","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe Generative workspace removes material the loads do not need. You say where the part is held and loaded, what must stay and what must stay clear; PartCraft finds the stiffest shape for each target volume (\u003Cstrong\u003Etopology optimisation\u003C/strong\u003E), checks each one again as a solid with the real material, and shows the outcomes side by side. It works on the named faces the mesh kernel gives the part (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EGenerative\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EWorkspace\u003C/strong\u003E box. Your part is not changed; the study is saved inside the part file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-up-a-study\"\u003ESet up a study\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003ENew study\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpace\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cem\u003EThe part as it is\u003C/em\u003E removes material from your part; \u003Cem\u003EA box around the part\u003C/em\u003E grows a shape inside a box larger than the part by the \u003Cstrong\u003EBox margin\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElement size\u003C/strong\u003E: leave it empty for an automatic size (about 8,000 cubes), or type one. Finer sizes give finer shapes and take longer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMinimum member\u003C/strong\u003E: members thinner than about twice this do not form. Empty means one and a half cubes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick one to six \u003Cstrong\u003ETarget volumes\u003C/strong\u003E (the share of the design space the shape may fill) and one to four \u003Cstrong\u003EMaterials\u003C/strong\u003E. Every volume is generated once; each material is checked on that shape (the stiffest shape does not depend on the material, but its mass, stress and safety factor do).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"goals\"\u003EGoals\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFixed\u003C/span\u003E: click the faces that are held. A \u003Cstrong\u003EFrictionless face\u003C/strong\u003E from the panel&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd\u003C/strong\u003E list holds a face only across itself (use it for symmetry planes).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForce\u003C/span\u003E: click the loaded faces, then type the total force and choose its direction in the panel. \u003Cstrong\u003EPressure\u003C/strong\u003E is in the \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd\u003C/strong\u003E list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreserve\u003C/span\u003E: click faces whose surroundings must stay solid, and set how deep (\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep solid within\u003C/strong\u003E) — for example around bolt holes. The faces you fix or load are always kept.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EObstacle\u003C/span\u003E: click faces whose surroundings must stay empty, and set how far (\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep empty within\u003C/strong\u003E) — room for a shaft or a tool.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003EDone picking\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after clicking faces. Green and red tints show the regions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"generate-and-compare\"\u003EGenerate and compare\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerate\u003C/span\u003E. The panel shows progress and the time left; \u003Cstrong\u003EStop\u003C/strong\u003E cancels. Each outcome appears in the \u003Cstrong\u003EOutcomes\u003C/strong\u003E table: target volume, material, \u003Cstrong\u003Emass\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Elargest stress\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Elargest displacement\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Esafety factor\u003C/strong\u003E (yield ÷ stress), all from re-solving the finished shape as solid cubes with the real material.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose an outcome to see it in the view (the part is hidden; tick \u003Cstrong\u003EShow the part through it\u003C/strong\u003E to see both). Below the table: how many iterations it took and whether it settled, its stiffness as a solid against the optimiser&#039;s own figure, and its surface.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-an-outcome\"\u003EUse an outcome\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESTL\u003C/strong\u003E: a closed triangle mesh, ready for 3D printing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESTEP (faceted)\u003C/strong\u003E: the same surface as a STEP solid made of flat faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENew part file\u003C/strong\u003E: a PartCraft part whose one feature is the outcome as an imported solid. Open it to sketch on it, cut holes or check it in Simulation. It is faceted (thousands of flat faces), not an editable shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdd to this part\u003C/strong\u003E joins the outcome to the part; it is on only for a box design space (with the part itself as the space, the outcome lies inside the part).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENew part file\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd to this part\u003C/strong\u003E are not built yet in this version: the outcome becomes an imported solid, which needs an exact kernel. The STL and STEP (faceted) downloads work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-far-to-trust-it\"\u003EHow far to trust it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt optimises \u003Cstrong\u003Estiffness\u003C/strong\u003E for the given volume. There are no stress limits and no manufacturing rules (overhangs, milling directions, minimum hole sizes) yet: check a chosen outcome in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/simulation\" data-topic=\"partcraft/simulation\"\u003ESimulation\u003C/a\u003E and adjust.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThin members one or two cubes wide are approximations: use a smaller element size for slender parts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe method is SIMP with a density filter, a threshold projection and optimality-criteria updates; the reported stress and deflection are of the outcome as cubes, which is honest but coarse at sharp corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/simulation\" data-topic=\"partcraft/simulation\"\u003ECheck stress and deflection (Simulation)\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/export-files\" data-topic=\"partcraft/export-files\"\u003EExport for printing, STEP and DXF\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The Generative workspace removes material the loads do not need. You say where the part is held and loaded, what must stay and what must stay clear; PartCraft finds the stiffest shape for each target volume (topology optimisation), checks each one again as a solid with the real material, and shows the outcomes side by side. It works on the named faces the mesh kernel gives the part (see The mesh kernel). Choose Generative in the Workspace box. Your part is not changed; the study is saved inside the part file. Set up a study Press New study in the right panel. Space: The part as it is removes material from your part; A box around the part grows a shape inside a box larger than the part by the Box margin. Element size: leave it empty for an automatic size (about 8,000 cubes), or type one. Finer sizes give finer shapes and take longer. Minimum member: members thinner than about twice this do not form. Empty means one and a half cubes. Tick one to six Target volumes (the share of the design space the shape may fill) and one to four Materials. Every volume is generated once; each material is checked on that shape (the stiffest shape does not depend on the material, but its mass, stress and safety factor do). Goals Fixed: click the faces that are held. A Frictionless face from the panel's Add list holds a face only across itself (use it for symmetry planes). Force: click the loaded faces, then type the total force and choose its direction in the panel. Pressure is in the Add list. Preserve: click faces whose surroundings must stay solid, and set how deep (Keep solid within) — for example around bolt holes. The faces you fix or load are always kept. Obstacle: click faces whose surroundings must stay empty, and set how far (Keep empty within) — room for a shaft or a tool. Press Done picking or Esc after clicking faces. Green and red tints show the regions. Generate and compare Press Generate. The panel shows progress and the time left; Stop cancels. Each outcome appears in the Outcomes table: target volume, material, mass, largest stress, largest displacement and safety factor (yield ÷ stress), all from re-solving the finished shape as solid cubes with the real material. Choose an outcome to see it in the view (the part is hidden; tick Show the part through it to see both). Below the table: how many iterations it took and whether it settled, its stiffness as a solid against the optimiser's own figure, and its surface. Use an outcome STL: a closed triangle mesh, ready for 3D printing. STEP (faceted): the same surface as a STEP solid made of flat faces. New part file: a PartCraft part whose one feature is the outcome as an imported solid. Open it to sketch on it, cut holes or check it in Simulation. It is faceted (thousands of flat faces), not an editable shape. Add to this part joins the outcome to the part; it is on only for a box design space (with the part itself as the space, the outcome lies inside the part). New part file and Add to this part are not built yet in this version: the outcome becomes an imported solid, which needs an exact kernel. The STL and STEP (faceted) downloads work. How far to trust it It optimises stiffness for the given volume. There are no stress limits and no manufacturing rules (overhangs, milling directions, minimum hole sizes) yet: check a chosen outcome in Simulation and adjust. Thin members one or two cubes wide are approximations: use a smaller element size for slender parts. The method is SIMP with a density filter, a threshold projection and optimality-criteria updates; the reported stress and deflection are of the outcome as cubes, which is honest but coarse at sharp corners. Related Check stress and deflection (Simulation) Export for printing, STEP and DXF What is not built yet"},{"slug":"flow-simulation","scope":"partcraft","title":"Simulate fluid flow (Flow study)","summary":"Flow through the channels inside a part or around it in a box: inlets, outlets, air, water or your own fluid, then speed and pressure on a movable cut plane, streamlines, surface pressure, pressure drop, mass balance, drag and lift, with what the laminar method can and cannot tell you.","section":"tasks","order":145,"keywords":["flow","CFD","fluid","computational fluid dynamics","flow simulation","air","water","viscosity","density","Reynolds number","laminar","turbulent","pressure drop","head loss","inlet","outlet","lid","cap","velocity","flow rate","volume flow","litres per minute","static pressure","streamlines","cut plane","section plane","drag","lift","drag coefficient","lift coefficient","frontal area","external flow","internal flow","pipe","channel","manifold","nozzle","lattice Boltzmann","residual","convergence"],"headings":[{"id":"pick-the-fluid-and-the-cells","text":"Pick the fluid and the cells","level":2},{"id":"flow-inside-the-part","text":"Flow inside the part","level":2},{"id":"flow-around-the-part","text":"Flow around the part","level":2},{"id":"solve-and-read-the-results","text":"Solve and read the results","level":2},{"id":"turbulent-flow-experimental","text":"Turbulent flow (experimental)","level":2},{"id":"what-it-can-and-cannot-tell-you","text":"What it can and cannot tell you","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003EFlow\u003C/strong\u003E study works out how a fluid moves through the channels inside your part (\u003Cstrong\u003Einternal\u003C/strong\u003E) or around it (\u003Cstrong\u003Eexternal\u003C/strong\u003E). By default it is \u003Cstrong\u003Elaminar, incompressible, isothermal and Newtonian\u003C/strong\u003E: smooth flow of a fluid whose density and viscosity stay fixed, with no heat, no turbulence and no gravity. An \u003Cstrong\u003Eexperimental\u003C/strong\u003E turbulent model is also there, for qualitative patterns only (see below). It works on the named faces the mesh kernel gives the part (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003ESimulation\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EWorkspace\u003C/strong\u003E box, press \u003Cstrong\u003ENew study\u003C/strong\u003E if there is none, and set \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003EFlow (CFD)\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EFlow model\u003C/strong\u003E is \u003Cstrong\u003ELaminar\u003C/strong\u003E (the default) or \u003Cstrong\u003ETurbulent (Spalart–Allmaras, experimental)\u003C/strong\u003E: see \u003Cstrong\u003ETurbulent flow (experimental)\u003C/strong\u003E below. The structural settings of the study are kept, so you can switch back. The flow settings are saved in the part file and every change can be undone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-the-fluid-and-the-cells\"\u003EPick the fluid and the cells\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFluid\u003C/strong\u003E: air or water at 20 °C, or \u003Cstrong\u003ECustom\u003C/strong\u003E with your own density (kg/m³) and dynamic viscosity (Pa·s). Air uses the ideal gas law and Sutherland&#039;s law (1.204 kg/m³, 1.813 × 10⁻⁵ Pa·s); water uses the IAPWS values (998.21 kg/m³, 1.0016 mPa·s).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECell size\u003C/strong\u003E: the fluid space is filled with equal cubes. Leave it empty for an automatic size; the panel shows the grid. Every opening and channel should be \u003Cstrong\u003Eat least 8 to 10 cells across\u003C/strong\u003E for useful numbers; smaller cells are more accurate and slower (twice as fine is about 16 times the work).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWalls\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EInterpolated\u003C/strong\u003E follows curved walls between cells (the default); \u003Cstrong\u003EBounce-back\u003C/strong\u003E treats walls as the cube staircase.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"flow-inside-the-part\"\u003EFlow inside the part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe fluid space must be closed. Add an \u003Cstrong\u003EInlet\u003C/strong\u003E and an \u003Cstrong\u003EOutlet\u003C/strong\u003E in the panel, then click their faces in the view:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe flat face around an opening\u003C/strong\u003E — the rim at a pipe end, a port face. PartCraft closes the opening&#039;s hole there with a lid. Every hole in that face belongs to that opening, so when an inlet and an outlet open on the same face, model a thin cap over one of them instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe inner face of a cap\u003C/strong\u003E — as in other CAD tools: model a thin plate (an extrude) over the opening and pick its face that touches the fluid.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor an inlet set a \u003Cstrong\u003Evelocity\u003C/strong\u003E (m/s, uniform across the opening, into the part) or a \u003Cstrong\u003Evolume flow\u003C/strong\u003E (L/min). For an outlet set the \u003Cstrong\u003Estatic pressure\u003C/strong\u003E (Pa, gauge). If an opening is left open, or a wall is thinner than a cell, the inside joins the outside and the solve stops with &quot;No closed fluid space&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"flow-around-the-part\"\u003EFlow around the part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EFlow\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003EExternal\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cstrong\u003ESpeed\u003C/strong\u003E, the direction it flows towards, and how far the box reaches upstream, downstream and to the sides (empty: 1, 3 and 1 times the part&#039;s largest size). The box face the flow enters by is an inflow, the face it leaves by an outflow at 0 Pa, and the faces along the flow slip. A box that is too small squeezes the flow past the part and raises drag. \u003Cstrong\u003EReference area\u003C/strong\u003E is the area drag and lift coefficients use (empty: the part&#039;s frontal area seen along the flow); lift is measured along +Y unless you pick another direction.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"solve-and-read-the-results\"\u003ESolve and read the results\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003ESolve\u003C/strong\u003E. The solve runs in the background with the step count, the residual and a residual plot; \u003Cstrong\u003EStop\u003C/strong\u003E ends it. It stops when the residual (how much the velocity field still changes per step) is below the target and the pressure drop or drag has settled. If it reaches the step limit first the panel says \u003Cstrong\u003ENot converged\u003C/strong\u003E in red: the numbers are the last state, not a steady answer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECut plane\u003C/strong\u003E: speed or static pressure on a plane through the model; choose its normal (X, Y or Z) and drag the slider to move it. Hover it to read the values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStreamlines\u003C/strong\u003E: from the inlets (internal) or from a rake across the inflow end of the box in the cut plane (external).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPart\u003C/strong\u003E: solid, see-through (to see the plane inside), surface pressure, or hidden.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETable\u003C/strong\u003E: Reynolds number, pressure drop (mean static pressure at the inlets minus at the outlets), the flow and mean pressure of each opening, the force on the part, drag and lift with their coefficients and reference area (external), the mass balance (inflow against outflow), the mesh and the solver.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turbulent-flow-experimental\"\u003ETurbulent flow (experimental)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFlow model › Turbulent (Spalart–Allmaras, experimental)\u003C/strong\u003E solves the time-averaged turbulent flow (steady RANS): the Spalart–Allmaras model gives an eddy viscosity in every cell, and the cells next to walls use Spalding&#039;s law of the wall instead of resolving the thin layer there. It is fully turbulent everywhere (no laminar regions, no transition).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt did not pass its accuracy checks.\u003C/strong\u003E The panel, the results and the table say so on every turbulent study, and the warning cannot be closed. Use it for \u003Cstrong\u003Equalitative flow patterns\u003C/strong\u003E — where flow separates, recirculates or speeds up — \u003Cstrong\u003Enot for design numbers\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECheck\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EReference\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EResult\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlat channel friction, first cell at y⁺ 49 / 98\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDean&#039;s correlation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E+3.3 % / +4.7 %\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EResolved-wall channel (y⁺ ≈ 3), Re_τ 180: bulk velocity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoser, Kim &amp; Mansour DNS\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E−1 %\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlat channel, y⁺ 7–15 or 190–380\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDean&#039;s correlation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E+9 to +22 %\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipe friction through the cube cells, fully developed, 10–30 cells across\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EColebrook\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E30 to 34 % too high\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReattachment behind a backward-facing step, 6–14 cells per step height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDriver &amp; Seegmiller, 6.26 step heights\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E21 to 39 % too short\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResults improve only slowly with finer cells. After solving, the table shows the \u003Cstrong\u003Efirst-cell y⁺\u003C/strong\u003E (min, mean and max over the wall cells) and warns outside the checked range of 1 to 400; aim for about 30 to 300. \u003Cstrong\u003ERun time:\u003C/strong\u003E the automatic cells are sized for a few minutes on one laptop core at most (the panel shows the worst case for the grid and step limit; a small pipe settles in under 20 seconds). Halving the cell size costs about 16 times as long. Forces on the part come from a momentum balance over the openings or the box.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWork on it so far:\u003C/strong\u003E the wall cells now match their own shear stress to the wall law, which brought the pipe and step errors down to the figures above without changing the flat-channel results. That was not enough for design numbers. The next step is finer cells near the walls.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-can-and-cannot-tell-you\"\u003EWhat it can and cannot tell you\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe laminar model\u003C/strong\u003E is laminar only. In pipes the flow turns turbulent above a Reynolds number of about 2,000 to 4,000; behind blunt bodies the wake starts to shed vortices above about 50. The panel warns at these points. A laminar result for turbulent flow underestimates pressure drop and gets drag wrong. The experimental turbulent model (above) covers turbulent flow qualitatively; its numbers are not reliable yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMethod:\u003C/strong\u003E the lattice Boltzmann method (D3Q19, two-relaxation-time collision) run to a steady state. It is slightly compressible inside; PartCraft keeps density changes to a few per cent. If the flow is too fast for the cells it refuses to start and says how much smaller the cells must be.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChecked against:\u003C/strong\u003E Hagen–Poiseuille flow in a pipe through a part (pressure gradient within 1 % at 20 cells across), the lid-driven cavity of Ghia et al. (within 0.75 % of the lid speed), and the Schäfer–Turek cylinder benchmark (drag within 1 % at 10 cells per diameter).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot modelled: heat transfer, turbulence with design accuracy (the turbulent model is experimental), free surfaces, cavitation, gravity, moving parts, non-Newtonian fluids, rough walls. Lab build: check important results another way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A Flow study works out how a fluid moves through the channels inside your part (internal) or around it (external). By default it is laminar, incompressible, isothermal and Newtonian: smooth flow of a fluid whose density and viscosity stay fixed, with no heat, no turbulence and no gravity. An experimental turbulent model is also there, for qualitative patterns only (see below). It works on the named faces the mesh kernel gives the part (see The mesh kernel). Choose Simulation in the Workspace box, press New study if there is none, and set Type to Flow (CFD). Flow model is Laminar (the default) or Turbulent (Spalart–Allmaras, experimental): see Turbulent flow (experimental) below. The structural settings of the study are kept, so you can switch back. The flow settings are saved in the part file and every change can be undone. Pick the fluid and the cells Fluid: air or water at 20 °C, or Custom with your own density (kg/m³) and dynamic viscosity (Pa·s). Air uses the ideal gas law and Sutherland's law (1.204 kg/m³, 1.813 × 10⁻⁵ Pa·s); water uses the IAPWS values (998.21 kg/m³, 1.0016 mPa·s). Cell size: the fluid space is filled with equal cubes. Leave it empty for an automatic size; the panel shows the grid. Every opening and channel should be at least 8 to 10 cells across for useful numbers; smaller cells are more accurate and slower (twice as fine is about 16 times the work). Walls: Interpolated follows curved walls between cells (the default); Bounce-back treats walls as the cube staircase. Flow inside the part The fluid space must be closed. Add an Inlet and an Outlet in the panel, then click their faces in the view: The flat face around an opening — the rim at a pipe end, a port face. PartCraft closes the opening's hole there with a lid. Every hole in that face belongs to that opening, so when an inlet and an outlet open on the same face, model a thin cap over one of them instead. The inner face of a cap — as in other CAD tools: model a thin plate (an extrude) over the opening and pick its face that touches the fluid. For an inlet set a velocity (m/s, uniform across the opening, into the part) or a volume flow (L/min). For an outlet set the static pressure (Pa, gauge). If an opening is left open, or a wall is thinner than a cell, the inside joins the outside and the solve stops with \"No closed fluid space\". Flow around the part Set Flow to External, the Speed, the direction it flows towards, and how far the box reaches upstream, downstream and to the sides (empty: 1, 3 and 1 times the part's largest size). The box face the flow enters by is an inflow, the face it leaves by an outflow at 0 Pa, and the faces along the flow slip. A box that is too small squeezes the flow past the part and raises drag. Reference area is the area drag and lift coefficients use (empty: the part's frontal area seen along the flow); lift is measured along +Y unless you pick another direction. Solve and read the results Press Solve. The solve runs in the background with the step count, the residual and a residual plot; Stop ends it. It stops when the residual (how much the velocity field still changes per step) is below the target and the pressure drop or drag has settled. If it reaches the step limit first the panel says Not converged in red: the numbers are the last state, not a steady answer. Cut plane: speed or static pressure on a plane through the model; choose its normal (X, Y or Z) and drag the slider to move it. Hover it to read the values. Streamlines: from the inlets (internal) or from a rake across the inflow end of the box in the cut plane (external). Part: solid, see-through (to see the plane inside), surface pressure, or hidden. Table: Reynolds number, pressure drop (mean static pressure at the inlets minus at the outlets), the flow and mean pressure of each opening, the force on the part, drag and lift with their coefficients and reference area (external), the mass balance (inflow against outflow), the mesh and the solver. Turbulent flow (experimental) Flow model › Turbulent (Spalart–Allmaras, experimental) solves the time-averaged turbulent flow (steady RANS): the Spalart–Allmaras model gives an eddy viscosity in every cell, and the cells next to walls use Spalding's law of the wall instead of resolving the thin layer there. It is fully turbulent everywhere (no laminar regions, no transition). It did not pass its accuracy checks. The panel, the results and the table say so on every turbulent study, and the warning cannot be closed. Use it for qualitative flow patterns — where flow separates, recirculates or speeds up — not for design numbers. Check Reference Result Flat channel friction, first cell at y⁺ 49 / 98 Dean's correlation +3.3 % / +4.7 % Resolved-wall channel (y⁺ ≈ 3), Re_τ 180: bulk velocity Moser, Kim & Mansour DNS −1 % Flat channel, y⁺ 7–15 or 190–380 Dean's correlation +9 to +22 % Pipe friction through the cube cells, fully developed, 10–30 cells across Colebrook 30 to 34 % too high Reattachment behind a backward-facing step, 6–14 cells per step height Driver & Seegmiller, 6.26 step heights 21 to 39 % too short Results improve only slowly with finer cells. After solving, the table shows the first-cell y⁺ (min, mean and max over the wall cells) and warns outside the checked range of 1 to 400; aim for about 30 to 300. Run time: the automatic cells are sized for a few minutes on one laptop core at most (the panel shows the worst case for the grid and step limit; a small pipe settles in under 20 seconds). Halving the cell size costs about 16 times as long. Forces on the part come from a momentum balance over the openings or the box. Work on it so far: the wall cells now match their own shear stress to the wall law, which brought the pipe and step errors down to the figures above without changing the flat-channel results. That was not enough for design numbers. The next step is finer cells near the walls. What it can and cannot tell you The laminar model is laminar only. In pipes the flow turns turbulent above a Reynolds number of about 2,000 to 4,000; behind blunt bodies the wake starts to shed vortices above about 50. The panel warns at these points. A laminar result for turbulent flow underestimates pressure drop and gets drag wrong. The experimental turbulent model (above) covers turbulent flow qualitatively; its numbers are not reliable yet. Method: the lattice Boltzmann method (D3Q19, two-relaxation-time collision) run to a steady state. It is slightly compressible inside; PartCraft keeps density changes to a few per cent. If the flow is too fast for the cells it refuses to start and says how much smaller the cells must be. Checked against: Hagen–Poiseuille flow in a pipe through a part (pressure gradient within 1 % at 20 cells across), the lid-driven cavity of Ghia et al. (within 0.75 % of the lid speed), and the Schäfer–Turek cylinder benchmark (drag within 1 % at 10 cells per diameter). Not modelled: heat transfer, turbulence with design accuracy (the turbulent model is experimental), free surfaces, cavitation, gravity, moving parts, non-Newtonian fluids, rough walls. Lab build: check important results another way."},{"slug":"craftscript","scope":"partcraft","title":"Write and run scripts (Script workspace)","summary":"The Script workspace step by step: write a CraftScript or Lua script, run it with F5, read the console, keep results in the variables for the next script, show meshes, fields and plots, stop a run, and save scripts with the part.","section":"tasks","order":145,"keywords":["script","scripting","script while modelling","pop out","second screen","second monitor","param","params","parameters in scripts","CraftScript","Lua","code","program","editor","console","REPL","command line","run","F5","run selection","stop","variables","workspace","plot","field","mesh","legend","sub-display","analysis","automation","matrix","numerical"],"headings":[{"id":"script-while-you-model","text":"Script while you model","level":2},{"id":"run-your-first-script","text":"Run your first script","level":2},{"id":"find-fold-and-complete","text":"Find, fold and complete","level":2},{"id":"when-something-goes-wrong","text":"When something goes wrong","level":2},{"id":"variables-carry-from-script-to-script","text":"Variables carry from script to script","level":2},{"id":"meshes-fields-and-plots","text":"Meshes, fields and plots","level":2},{"id":"files","text":"Files","level":2},{"id":"read-the-part-s-parameters","text":"Read the part's parameters","level":2},{"id":"scripts-are-saved-in-the-part","text":"Scripts are saved in the part","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPreview.\u003C/strong\u003E Scripting is new in PartCraft 0.3 and is a preview: the language, the library and the way scripts are saved may still change, and scripts written now may need small edits later. Tell us what breaks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Script workspace runs your own calculations next to the part: small sums, whole analyses, or the scripts in the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-library\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-library\"\u003Eanalysis library\u003C/a\u003E. Scripts are written in \u003Cstrong\u003ECraftScript\u003C/strong\u003E (a matrix language much like MATLAB, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-language\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-language\"\u003EThe CraftScript language\u003C/a\u003E) or in \u003Cstrong\u003ELua\u003C/strong\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-lua\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-lua\"\u003EScripts in Lua\u003C/a\u003E) or \u003Cstrong\u003EPython\u003C/strong\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-python\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-python\"\u003EScripts in Python\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EScript\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EWorkspace\u003C/strong\u003E box at the left of the toolbar. The part is not changed. The right panel becomes the script panel (wider than usual; drag its edge to change it, or move it to a second screen with its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EArrange the panels\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"script-while-you-model\"\u003EScript while you model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou do not have to leave the Design workspace to write a script. In Design, the right panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScript\u003C/span\u003E tab holds the same editor, console, variables and library, with the same scripts. The feature tree and the view keep working: click a feature to select it, double-click a sketch to edit it, turn the view, then go back to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScript\u003C/span\u003E tab and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F5\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat a script \u003Cstrong\u003Eshows\u003C/strong\u003E (meshes, fields, plots, tables) is not drawn over your model in Design: the console says so, and the Script workspace has it all when you switch to it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPop out\u003C/span\u003E, beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E, moves the whole panel to a window of its own, for example on a second screen, so the editor and the full view sit side by side. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut back\u003C/span\u003E docks it again. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EArrange the panels\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-your-first-script\"\u003ERun your first script\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in the editor, for example:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E``\u003Ccode\u003E x = linspace(0, 2*pi, 50); y = sin(x); plot(x, y, &#039;title&#039;, &#039;A sine&#039;) disp(max(y)) \u003C/code\u003E``\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F5\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E). The console under the editor shows what the script printed, and &quot;Done in … s&quot;. The plot appears in the \u003Cstrong\u003Esub-displays\u003C/strong\u003E along the bottom of the 3D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA line without a semicolon prints its value, as in MATLAB: \u003Ccode\u003Ea = 3\u003C/code\u003E prints \u003Ccode\u003Ea = 3\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003Ea = 3;\u003C/code\u003E prints nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo run only part of a script, select the lines and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelection\u003C/span\u003E). With nothing selected it runs the line the cursor is on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe line at the bottom of the console (after \u003Ccode\u003E&gt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E) runs one statement as you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E bring back earlier lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-fold-and-complete\"\u003EFind, fold and complete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFind and replace.\u003C/strong\u003E Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command F\"\u003E⌘F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command H\"\u003E⌘H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to replace too). A bar opens above the code: type what to find; the matches light up and the count says where you are. The buttons beside the box match case (\u003Cstrong\u003EAa\u003C/strong\u003E), find whole words only (\u003Cstrong\u003EW\u003C/strong\u003E) and use a regular expression (\u003Cstrong\u003E.\\\u003C/strong\u003E*; in the replacement, \u003Ccode\u003E$1\u003C/code\u003E puts back what the first group matched). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplace\u003C/span\u003E replaces the current match, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll\u003C/span\u003E replaces every match as one step of \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFolding.\u003C/strong\u003E Click the ▾ beside a line number, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command [\"\u003E⇧⌘[\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+[\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the cursor on a \u003Ccode\u003Efunction\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eif\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Efor\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ewhile\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eswitch\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Etry\u003C/code\u003E line (Lua: \u003Ccode\u003Efunction\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edo\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eif\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Erepeat\u003C/code\u003E), to tuck its inner lines away; the line says how many. ▸ or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command ]\"\u003E⇧⌘]\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+]\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens it again. Folding only hides lines: Run, Save, copying and the line numbers all still count every line, and typing on a folded line opens it first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECompletions.\u003C/strong\u003E Type two letters of a name, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Space\"\u003E⌘ Space\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Space\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: a list shows the variables you have, the language&#039;s functions (with their arguments), keywords and names already in the script. Choose with the arrows and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-something-goes-wrong\"\u003EWhen something goes wrong\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn error names the problem and its line: the line is marked red in the editor, and \u003Cstrong\u003ELine n\u003C/strong\u003E in the console takes you there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA loop that never ends: press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E (toolbar or panel). The script stops at once; the variables keep the values they had before that run.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery run also has a step budget (2 billion steps by default; change it under \u003Cstrong\u003ERun options\u003C/strong\u003E in the panel). A run that passes it stops with a message.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"variables-carry-from-script-to-script\"\u003EVariables carry from script to script\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery top-level variable a script makes lands in the \u003Cstrong\u003EVariables\u003C/strong\u003E tab (name, type, size, smallest and largest value) and stays there for the next script, in either language. That is how one script meshes, the next solves, and a third plots.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a variable to show it: meshes, fields and models in the 3D view, arrays as a table (plus a line plot for a vector or a colour map for a matrix) in the sub-displays.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as JSON\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as CSV\u003C/span\u003E (a table of numbers) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as HDF5\u003C/span\u003E download it; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E removes it; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear all\u003C/span\u003E empties the workspace.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick \u003Cstrong\u003ESave the variables with the part\u003C/strong\u003E to keep them in the part file (up to 32 MB; bigger ones stay for this visit only). When you open such a part, the panel offers to load them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"meshes-fields-and-plots\"\u003EMeshes, fields and plots\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eshow(m)\u003C/code\u003E draws a mesh; \u003Ccode\u003Eshow(f)\u003C/code\u003E colours a field on its mesh with a legend. The \u003Cstrong\u003EShown\u003C/strong\u003E list at the top left of the view hides (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eeye\u003C/span\u003E) or removes each item, and hides or shows the part itself. Under the legend, type a range (\u003Cstrong\u003EFrom\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Eto\u003C/strong\u003E) or press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto\u003C/span\u003E; a vector or stress field also offers which component to show.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eshow(f, &#039;deform&#039;, u, &#039;scale&#039;, 50)\u003C/code\u003E draws the mesh moved by a displacement field \u003Ccode\u003Eu\u003C/code\u003E, enlarged 50 times (without \u003Ccode\u003Escale\u003C/code\u003E, about 8 % of the model&#039;s size).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a shown mesh: the nearest node, the element and the field values there appear in the sub-displays (\u003Cstrong\u003EPicked\u003C/strong\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eplot\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eimagesc\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Etable\u003C/code\u003E put a line plot, a colour map of a 2-D array or a table in the sub-displays. A plot with the same \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;id&#039;\u003C/code\u003E (or title) as an earlier one replaces it, so a loop can update one convergence plot; \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;append&#039;, true\u003C/code\u003E adds a curve to it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E empties the sub-displays; the arrow folds them away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith two or more plots, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide by side\u003C/span\u003E shows them all at once (the newest six); clicking a table&#039;s or picture&#039;s tab shows that one alone again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery plot has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPNG\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESVG\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E under it. PNG and SVG print it on white in the paper colours, whatever the theme (PNG 1600 × 1000 pixels; SVG scales without blurring); CSV holds its numbers with every digit: one column of x and a column per curve when the curves share x, otherwise one row per point with the curve&#039;s name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E fits the view to what is shown.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E in the Script group (or drop files on the view or the panel): \u003Ccode\u003E.craft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.lua\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.py\u003C/code\u003E files open as new scripts (they do not run); data files (STL, OBJ, CSV, JSON, NPY, HDF5) load into the variables under their file name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E in the Script group downloads the current script as a file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E in the Script group (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E by the tabs) starts another script. Double-click a tab to rename it; its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E×\u003C/span\u003E removes it (you can undo that).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-part-s-parameters\"\u003ERead the part&#039;s parameters\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA script reads the part&#039;s parameters (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/parameters\" data-topic=\"partcraft/parameters\"\u003EDrive a part with parameters\u003C/a\u003E) by name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ew = param(&#039;width&#039;);     % one parameter&#039;s value, in mm (or degrees, or a plain number)\np = params();           % all of them, as a struct: p.width, p.height\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Lua and Python they are \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.param(&#039;width&#039;)\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.params()\u003C/code\u003E. Scripts only read parameters: change them on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E tab. Under the script&#039;s tab, a line lists the parameters it reads with \u003Ccode\u003Eparam(&#039;…&#039;)\u003C/code\u003E, and marks any the part does not have; the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E tab lists the script under each one. A name built while the script runs, such as \u003Ccode\u003Eparam(name)\u003C/code\u003E, is not listed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scripts-are-saved-in-the-part\"\u003EScripts are saved in the part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery script in the tabs is kept in the part, so \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E in the File group saves them with it and changes to them can be undone. Opening a part \u003Cstrong\u003Enever runs its scripts\u003C/strong\u003E: the console says how many it carries, and they run only when you press Run. A part from someone else can hold any script: read it before you run it. Scripts run in the background on this computer with no network access and cannot touch the page.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-language\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-language\"\u003EThe CraftScript language\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-lua\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-lua\"\u003EScripts in Lua\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-python\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-python\"\u003EScripts in Python\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-library\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-library\"\u003EThe analysis library\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-own-analysis\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-own-analysis\"\u003EWrite your own analysis\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Preview. Scripting is new in PartCraft 0.3 and is a preview: the language, the library and the way scripts are saved may still change, and scripts written now may need small edits later. Tell us what breaks. The Script workspace runs your own calculations next to the part: small sums, whole analyses, or the scripts in the analysis library. Scripts are written in CraftScript (a matrix language much like MATLAB, see The CraftScript language) or in Lua (see Scripts in Lua) or Python (see Scripts in Python). Choose Script in the Workspace box at the left of the toolbar. The part is not changed. The right panel becomes the script panel (wider than usual; drag its edge to change it, or move it to a second screen with its ⋯ menu, see Arrange the panels). Script while you model You do not have to leave the Design workspace to write a script. In Design, the right panel's Script tab holds the same editor, console, variables and library, with the same scripts. The feature tree and the view keep working: click a feature to select it, double-click a sketch to edit it, turn the view, then go back to the Script tab and press F5. What a script shows (meshes, fields, plots, tables) is not drawn over your model in Design: the console says so, and the Script workspace has it all when you switch to it. Pop out, beside Run, moves the whole panel to a window of its own, for example on a second screen, so the editor and the full view sit side by side. Put back docks it again. See Arrange the panels. Run your first script Type in the editor, for example: `` x = linspace(0, 2*pi, 50); y = sin(x); plot(x, y, 'title', 'A sine') disp(max(y)) `` Press F5 (or Run). The console under the editor shows what the script printed, and \"Done in … s\". The plot appears in the sub-displays along the bottom of the 3D view. A line without a semicolon prints its value, as in MATLAB: a = 3 prints a = 3; a = 3; prints nothing. To run only part of a script, select the lines and press Ctrl+Enter (Selection). With nothing selected it runs the line the cursor is on. The line at the bottom of the console (after \u003E\u003E) runs one statement as you press Enter. ↑ and ↓ bring back earlier lines. Find, fold and complete Find and replace. Press Ctrl+F (or Ctrl+H to replace too). A bar opens above the code: type what to find; the matches light up and the count says where you are. The buttons beside the box match case (Aa), find whole words only (W) and use a regular expression (.\\*; in the replacement, $1 puts back what the first group matched). Replace replaces the current match, All replaces every match as one step of Ctrl+Z. Folding. Click the ▾ beside a line number, or press Ctrl+Shift+[ with the cursor on a function, if, for, while, switch or try line (Lua: function, do, if, repeat), to tuck its inner lines away; the line says how many. ▸ or Ctrl+Shift+] opens it again. Folding only hides lines: Run, Save, copying and the line numbers all still count every line, and typing on a folded line opens it first. Completions. Type two letters of a name, or press Ctrl+Space: a list shows the variables you have, the language's functions (with their arguments), keywords and names already in the script. Choose with the arrows and press Enter or Tab. When something goes wrong An error names the problem and its line: the line is marked red in the editor, and Line n in the console takes you there. A loop that never ends: press Stop (toolbar or panel). The script stops at once; the variables keep the values they had before that run. Every run also has a step budget (2 billion steps by default; change it under Run options in the panel). A run that passes it stops with a message. Variables carry from script to script Every top-level variable a script makes lands in the Variables tab (name, type, size, smallest and largest value) and stays there for the next script, in either language. That is how one script meshes, the next solves, and a third plots. Click a variable to show it: meshes, fields and models in the 3D view, arrays as a table (plus a line plot for a vector or a colour map for a matrix) in the sub-displays. Save as JSON, Save as CSV (a table of numbers) and Save as HDF5 download it; Delete removes it; Clear all empties the workspace. Tick Save the variables with the part to keep them in the part file (up to 32 MB; bigger ones stay for this visit only). When you open such a part, the panel offers to load them. Meshes, fields and plots show(m) draws a mesh; show(f) colours a field on its mesh with a legend. The Shown list at the top left of the view hides (eye) or removes each item, and hides or shows the part itself. Under the legend, type a range (From, to) or press Auto; a vector or stress field also offers which component to show. show(f, 'deform', u, 'scale', 50) draws the mesh moved by a displacement field u, enlarged 50 times (without scale, about 8 % of the model's size). Click a shown mesh: the nearest node, the element and the field values there appear in the sub-displays (Picked). plot, imagesc and table put a line plot, a colour map of a 2-D array or a table in the sub-displays. A plot with the same 'id' (or title) as an earlier one replaces it, so a loop can update one convergence plot; 'append', true adds a curve to it. Clear empties the sub-displays; the arrow folds them away. With two or more plots, Side by side shows them all at once (the newest six); clicking a table's or picture's tab shows that one alone again. Every plot has PNG, SVG and CSV under it. PNG and SVG print it on white in the paper colours, whatever the theme (PNG 1600 × 1000 pixels; SVG scales without blurring); CSV holds its numbers with every digit: one column of x and a column per curve when the curves share x, otherwise one row per point with the curve's name. F or Fit fits the view to what is shown. Files Open in the Script group (or drop files on the view or the panel): .craft, .lua and .py files open as new scripts (they do not run); data files (STL, OBJ, CSV, JSON, NPY, HDF5) load into the variables under their file name. Save in the Script group downloads the current script as a file. New in the Script group (or + by the tabs) starts another script. Double-click a tab to rename it; its × removes it (you can undo that). Read the part's parameters A script reads the part's parameters (see Drive a part with parameters) by name: w = param('width'); % one parameter's value, in mm (or degrees, or a plain number) p = params(); % all of them, as a struct: p.width, p.height In Lua and Python they are cs.param('width') and cs.params(). Scripts only read parameters: change them on the Parameters tab. Under the script's tab, a line lists the parameters it reads with param('…'), and marks any the part does not have; the Parameters tab lists the script under each one. A name built while the script runs, such as param(name), is not listed. Scripts are saved in the part Every script in the tabs is kept in the part, so Save in the File group saves them with it and changes to them can be undone. Opening a part never runs its scripts: the console says how many it carries, and they run only when you press Run. A part from someone else can hold any script: read it before you run it. Scripts run in the background on this computer with no network access and cannot touch the page. Related The CraftScript language Scripts in Lua Scripts in Python The analysis library Write your own analysis"},{"slug":"craftscript-library","scope":"partcraft","title":"The analysis library","summary":"The library of readable analysis scripts in the Script workspace: what is in each group, opening a function read-only, its header with method, references and a check, making your own copy, and how a copy takes over from the library function.","section":"tasks","order":146,"keywords":["library","craftlib","analysis","electromagnet","static","modal","thermal","topology optimization","top88","SIMP","electromagnetics","electrostatics","magnetostatics","fluid","lattice Boltzmann","LBM","mesh","voxel","tetrahedra","references","check","verification","copy","shadow"],"headings":[{"id":"start-with-an-example","text":"Start with an example","level":2},{"id":"groups","text":"Groups","level":2},{"id":"how-the-library-is-checked","text":"How the library is checked","level":2},{"id":"read-a-function","text":"Read a function","level":2},{"id":"change-one","text":"Change one","level":2},{"id":"use-one","text":"Use one","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe analysis library is a set of scripts, each a function you can read from top to bottom: how the stiffness matrix is assembled, where the boundary conditions go, which solver runs, how the results are worked out. Nothing in it is hidden in a black box; only the fast inner loops (element matrices, sparse solvers, meshing) are built in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-with-an-example\"\u003EStart with an example\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EExamples\u003C/strong\u003E group holds five short scripts that use the library end to end. Click one, press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen (read-only)\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F5\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: each clears the view, shows a result on the canvas and a plot in the sub-displays.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExample\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it shows\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECantilever stress\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA steel bar 100 × 10 × 10 mm under a 100 N tip load: von Mises stress on the deformed bar, and the tip deflection against beam theory at four element sizes (plotted)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMBB beam topology\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe classic 60 × 20 topology optimization: the design forming, the compliance falling, the final layout (94 iterations, compliance 203.19)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECapacitor field\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo plates 4 mm wide, 1 mm apart: potential and field, the capacitance with fringing against the ideal value and Palmer&#039;s formula\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChannel flow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELattice Boltzmann flow past a post in a channel: the velocity field and the convergence history\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElectromagnet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA C-shaped iron core with an air gap and a coil: the flux density, the field in the gap against the magnetic-circuit formula, the inductance and the pull across the gap. Walked through in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/magnetic-fields-2d\" data-topic=\"partcraft/magnetic-fields-2d\"\u003EMagnetic fields in 2-D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPermanent magnet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same core with a permanent magnet instead of the coil: the magnet&#039;s working point, the field in the gap against the magnetic circuit, how much flux leaks, the pull. In \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/magnetic-fields-2d#permanent-magnets\" data-topic=\"partcraft/magnetic-fields-2d\" data-hash=\"permanent-magnets\"\u003EMagnetic fields in 2-D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"groups\"\u003EGroups\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFunctions\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat they do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeshing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ebox_mesh\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecylinder_mesh\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Evoxel_mesh\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etet_mesh\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emesh_quality\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Enodes_at\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eface_nodes\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoxes and cylinders as hexahedra, cubes or tetrahedra; cubes or tetrahedra of any model (the part, a body, a loaded STL); element volumes and quality; picking nodes on a plane or a face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStatic stress\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Estatic_voxel\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Estatic_tet\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear static stress on cubes or on 10-node tetrahedra: displacements, stresses, von Mises, reactions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrames\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eframe3d\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D frames of beams, columns and braces by the direct stiffness method: end releases, supports and springs, loads along members; displacements, reactions, member end forces. DetailCraft&#039;s and BimCraft&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport as CraftScript\u003C/span\u003E writes a whole model for it. An analysis aid, not a code check\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVibration\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emodal_tet\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENatural frequencies and mode shapes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHeat\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eheat_steady\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eheat_transient\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eheat_system\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESteady and transient conduction with fixed temperatures, heat input, generation, flux and convection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETopology optimization\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etop88\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etop99\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etopopt3d\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe published 88- and 99-line 2-D codes, and the same method in 3-D on cubes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElectromagnetics\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epoisson2d\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emagnetostatic2d\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2-D electrostatics (potential, field, capacitance, charge) and magnetostatics (vector potential, flux density, permanent magnets)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFluid flow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Elbm2d\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elbm3d\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELattice Boltzmann flow: 2-D written out in the script, 3-D on PartCraft&#039;s own flow kernel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery function&#039;s header gives its exact arguments. Units are those of PartCraft&#039;s studies unless the header says otherwise: millimetres, newtons, megapascals, tonnes per cubic millimetre for density, watts and kelvin for heat. The electromagnetic and lattice Boltzmann functions work in any consistent units; their headers say how.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-library-is-checked\"\u003EHow the library is checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach function&#039;s header ends with a \u003Cstrong\u003Echeck\u003C/strong\u003E: a small case with a known answer. PartCraft&#039;s tests run every one of them, and where PartCraft has its own study for the same job the script&#039;s answer is compared with that study on the same mesh:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe cantilever&#039;s tip deflection against beam theory (within 0.2 % with cubes, 0.05 % with tetrahedra), and against the \u003Cstrong\u003ESimulation\u003C/strong\u003E workspace&#039;s solvers on the same mesh (the same to many digits);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe cantilever&#039;s first frequency against beam theory (0.24 % lower, as expected for a short beam) and against \u003Cstrong\u003ESimulation\u003C/strong\u003E&#039;s modal solver;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eheat: a heated plane wall exactly, a small body cooling as the lumped-capacity formula says, a slab&#039;s temperature dying away as theory says;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Etopology optimization: \u003Ccode\u003Etop88\u003C/code\u003E reproduces the paper&#039;s 94 iterations and compliance 203.1925 (and 218.8032 with the density filter); \u003Ccode\u003Etopopt3d\u003C/code\u003E ends at the same compliance as the \u003Cstrong\u003EGenerative\u003C/strong\u003E workspace with the same settings;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea capacitor&#039;s capacitance and a long solenoid&#039;s field against their textbook values, the electromagnet&#039;s gap field against the magnetic-circuit formula, and permanent magnets against a round magnet in air and a magnet closed by an iron keeper;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Echannel flow against the exact parabolic profile.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-a-function\"\u003ERead a function\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary\u003C/strong\u003E tab of the script panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a function. Its header shows: what it computes, the method, units, assumptions and limits, the \u003Cstrong\u003Ereferences\u003C/strong\u003E (book or paper, with equation numbers) and a \u003Cstrong\u003Echeck\u003C/strong\u003E — a small case with a known answer that PartCraft&#039;s own tests run.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen (read-only)\u003C/span\u003E opens the whole script in a tab with a dashed border. You can run it, select and run parts of it, but not change it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-one\"\u003EChange one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake a copy\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003EMake a copy to edit\u003C/strong\u003E above a read-only tab). The copy is a script in your part with the same name, so it is saved with the part, and \u003Cstrong\u003Eevery call to that function now uses your copy\u003C/strong\u003E — the editor says so above the script. Rename the copy to keep both.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-one\"\u003EUse one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECall a library function from any script by its name, like a built-in function:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ExPhys = top88(60, 20, 0.5, 3.0, 1.5, 1);\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResults come back as values in the workspace; plots and fields it shows appear in the view and the sub-displays.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA static analysis of the open part, for example, takes a few lines:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ev = voxel_mesh(part(), 2);                   % 2 mm cubes\nX = meshNodes(v);\nbc = struct(&#039;fixed&#039;, nodes_at(v, 2, min(X(:, 2))));   % hold the bottom\ntop = nodes_at(v, 2, max(X(:, 2)));\nbc.loads = {struct(&#039;nodes&#039;, top, &#039;force&#039;, [0 -500 0])};   % 500 N down on the top\nr = static_voxel(v, 210000, 0.3, bc);       % steel\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Er\u003C/code\u003E holds the displacements, stresses and reactions; the von Mises stress is drawn on the deformed part. For curved parts use \u003Ccode\u003Etet_mesh\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Estatic_tet\u003C/code\u003E, which can also load and hold whole faces (\u003Ccode\u003Eface_nodes\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe library&#039;s functions are written in CraftScript, and a Lua script calls them through \u003Ccode\u003Ecs\u003C/code\u003E like the built-in functions: \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.static_tet(t, 200000, 0.3, bc)\u003C/code\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-lua\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-lua\"\u003ELua scripts\u003C/a\u003E). Each function solves one material and one load case; the headers list what else is not covered.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The analysis library is a set of scripts, each a function you can read from top to bottom: how the stiffness matrix is assembled, where the boundary conditions go, which solver runs, how the results are worked out. Nothing in it is hidden in a black box; only the fast inner loops (element matrices, sparse solvers, meshing) are built in. Start with an example The Examples group holds five short scripts that use the library end to end. Click one, press Open (read-only), then F5: each clears the view, shows a result on the canvas and a plot in the sub-displays. Example What it shows Cantilever stress A steel bar 100 × 10 × 10 mm under a 100 N tip load: von Mises stress on the deformed bar, and the tip deflection against beam theory at four element sizes (plotted) MBB beam topology The classic 60 × 20 topology optimization: the design forming, the compliance falling, the final layout (94 iterations, compliance 203.19) Capacitor field Two plates 4 mm wide, 1 mm apart: potential and field, the capacitance with fringing against the ideal value and Palmer's formula Channel flow Lattice Boltzmann flow past a post in a channel: the velocity field and the convergence history Electromagnet A C-shaped iron core with an air gap and a coil: the flux density, the field in the gap against the magnetic-circuit formula, the inductance and the pull across the gap. Walked through in Magnetic fields in 2-D Permanent magnet The same core with a permanent magnet instead of the coil: the magnet's working point, the field in the gap against the magnetic circuit, how much flux leaks, the pull. In Magnetic fields in 2-D Groups Group Functions What they do Meshing box_mesh, cylinder_mesh, voxel_mesh, tet_mesh, mesh_quality, nodes_at, face_nodes Boxes and cylinders as hexahedra, cubes or tetrahedra; cubes or tetrahedra of any model (the part, a body, a loaded STL); element volumes and quality; picking nodes on a plane or a face Static stress static_voxel, static_tet Linear static stress on cubes or on 10-node tetrahedra: displacements, stresses, von Mises, reactions Frames frame3d 3D frames of beams, columns and braces by the direct stiffness method: end releases, supports and springs, loads along members; displacements, reactions, member end forces. DetailCraft's and BimCraft's Export as CraftScript writes a whole model for it. An analysis aid, not a code check Vibration modal_tet Natural frequencies and mode shapes Heat heat_steady, heat_transient, heat_system Steady and transient conduction with fixed temperatures, heat input, generation, flux and convection Topology optimization top88, top99, topopt3d The published 88- and 99-line 2-D codes, and the same method in 3-D on cubes Electromagnetics poisson2d, magnetostatic2d 2-D electrostatics (potential, field, capacitance, charge) and magnetostatics (vector potential, flux density, permanent magnets) Fluid flow lbm2d, lbm3d Lattice Boltzmann flow: 2-D written out in the script, 3-D on PartCraft's own flow kernel Every function's header gives its exact arguments. Units are those of PartCraft's studies unless the header says otherwise: millimetres, newtons, megapascals, tonnes per cubic millimetre for density, watts and kelvin for heat. The electromagnetic and lattice Boltzmann functions work in any consistent units; their headers say how. How the library is checked Each function's header ends with a check: a small case with a known answer. PartCraft's tests run every one of them, and where PartCraft has its own study for the same job the script's answer is compared with that study on the same mesh: the cantilever's tip deflection against beam theory (within 0.2 % with cubes, 0.05 % with tetrahedra), and against the Simulation workspace's solvers on the same mesh (the same to many digits); the cantilever's first frequency against beam theory (0.24 % lower, as expected for a short beam) and against Simulation's modal solver; heat: a heated plane wall exactly, a small body cooling as the lumped-capacity formula says, a slab's temperature dying away as theory says; topology optimization: top88 reproduces the paper's 94 iterations and compliance 203.1925 (and 218.8032 with the density filter); topopt3d ends at the same compliance as the Generative workspace with the same settings; a capacitor's capacitance and a long solenoid's field against their textbook values, the electromagnet's gap field against the magnetic-circuit formula, and permanent magnets against a round magnet in air and a magnet closed by an iron keeper; channel flow against the exact parabolic profile. Read a function Open the Library tab of the script panel. Click a function. Its header shows: what it computes, the method, units, assumptions and limits, the references (book or paper, with equation numbers) and a check — a small case with a known answer that PartCraft's own tests run. Open (read-only) opens the whole script in a tab with a dashed border. You can run it, select and run parts of it, but not change it. Change one Press Make a copy (or Make a copy to edit above a read-only tab). The copy is a script in your part with the same name, so it is saved with the part, and every call to that function now uses your copy — the editor says so above the script. Rename the copy to keep both. Use one Call a library function from any script by its name, like a built-in function: xPhys = top88(60, 20, 0.5, 3.0, 1.5, 1); Results come back as values in the workspace; plots and fields it shows appear in the view and the sub-displays. A static analysis of the open part, for example, takes a few lines: v = voxel_mesh(part(), 2); % 2 mm cubes X = meshNodes(v); bc = struct('fixed', nodes_at(v, 2, min(X(:, 2)))); % hold the bottom top = nodes_at(v, 2, max(X(:, 2))); bc.loads = {struct('nodes', top, 'force', [0 -500 0])}; % 500 N down on the top r = static_voxel(v, 210000, 0.3, bc); % steel r holds the displacements, stresses and reactions; the von Mises stress is drawn on the deformed part. For curved parts use tet_mesh and static_tet, which can also load and hold whole faces (face_nodes). The library's functions are written in CraftScript, and a Lua script calls them through cs like the built-in functions: cs.static_tet(t, 200000, 0.3, bc) (see Lua scripts). Each function solves one material and one load case; the headers list what else is not covered."},{"slug":"magnetic-fields-2d","scope":"partcraft","title":"Magnetic fields in 2-D (tutorial)","summary":"Work out the magnetic field of an electromagnet or a permanent magnet with the analysis library: run the examples, read the flux density, the gap field, the inductance and the pull, change the design, and build your own model of coils, magnets and iron.","section":"tasks","order":147,"keywords":["magnetic","magnetostatics","magnetic field","flux density","tesla","electromagnet","solenoid","coil","ampere turns","iron core","air gap","permeability","inductance","magnetic force","reluctance","magnetic circuit","vector potential","electromagnetics","finite element","fem","motor","actuator","transformer","magnetostatic2d","permanent magnet","neodymium","ndfeb","ferrite","remanence","working point","recoil","leakage","holding magnet"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-modelled","text":"What is modelled","level":2},{"id":"run-the-example","text":"Run the example","level":2},{"id":"read-the-results","text":"Read the results","level":2},{"id":"change-the-design","text":"Change the design","level":2},{"id":"model-your-own-shape","text":"Model your own shape","level":2},{"id":"permanent-magnets","text":"Permanent magnets","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-do-yet","text":"What it does not do yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft&#039;s analysis library can work out the magnetic field of coils and iron in 2-D: a cross-section of something that is long and the same along its length, such as an electromagnet, a transformer core, a busbar or a motor&#039;s cross-section. This tutorial runs the library&#039;s electromagnet example, reads what it says, changes it, shows how to model your own shape, and then adds a permanent magnet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt uses the Script workspace and the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-library\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-library\"\u003Eanalysis library\u003C/a\u003E. You do not need to know the CraftScript language to follow it; the example says what each line does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-modelled\"\u003EWhat is modelled\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA C-shaped iron core, 100 × 80 mm outside and 20 mm thick, with a 2 mm air gap in its right leg. A coil of 100 turns carrying 10 A (1000 ampere-turns) is wound round its left leg. Everything is long in the direction out of the screen, so the answers are \u003Cstrong\u003Eper metre of depth\u003C/strong\u003E: multiply by the real depth for a real magnet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe library&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003Emagnetostatic2d\u003C/code\u003E solves for the magnetic vector potential on a grid of 1 mm squares in a box of air round the magnet, then works out the flux density \u003Cstrong\u003EB\u003C/strong\u003E (in tesla) from it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-the-example\"\u003ERun the example\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EScript\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EWorkspace\u003C/strong\u003E box at the left of the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary\u003C/strong\u003E tab of the script panel, then \u003Cstrong\u003EExamples\u003C/strong\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EElectromagnet: field, gap, inductance\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen (read-only)\u003C/span\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F5\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a second or two the canvas shows the flux density, and the console prints:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EB in the gap 0.5427 T; magnetic circuit 0.5516 T (-1.6 %: fringing and leakage)\ninductance 0.1539 H per metre of depth; the circuit gives 0.1103 H/m, missing the flux that leaks past the gap\nstored energy 7.694 J/m; |B| peaks at 1.97 T in the window&#039;s inner corners (real steel would saturate there)\npull across the gap about 2343 N per metre of depth (117.2 N for 50 mm deep)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe plot below the canvas is the vertical flux density B_y along the core&#039;s middle line, from left to right: about −0.55 T in the left leg (the flux runs down it), almost nothing in the window, and +0.55 T in the right leg and across the gap (the flux comes back up). The same flux passes through the iron and the gap, so the two legs and the gap show nearly the same value.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-results\"\u003ERead the results\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe field in the gap\u003C/strong\u003E is 0.543 T. A hand calculation treats the core as one magnetic circuit, B = μ0 N I / (g + l / μr), where g is the gap, l the length of the path through the iron and μr the iron&#039;s relative permeability; it gives 0.552 T. The field is a little lower than the hand calculation because the flux bulges out round the gap (fringing) and some flux takes a short cut across the window (leakage). With a smaller gap or a finer grid the two come closer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe inductance\u003C/strong\u003E of the 100-turn coil is 0.154 H per metre of depth, from the energy stored in the field. The circuit formula gives less (0.110 H/m) because it counts only the flux through the gap, not the flux that leaks past it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe pull\u003C/strong\u003E that closes the gap is B² w / (2 μ0) for a gap face w wide: about 2300 N per metre, or 117 N for a magnet 50 mm deep.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe peak\u003C/strong\u003E of 1.97 T sits in the inner corners of the window, where the flux turns sharply. The model treats the iron as linear; real electrical steel saturates at about 1.5 to 2 T, so treat values above that as a warning, not an answer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe library&#039;s own tests run this example and check the gap field against the magnetic-circuit formula on every change to PartCraft. The method, its references and its other checks (a long solenoid, an iron-cored solenoid) are in the header of \u003Ccode\u003Emagnetostatic2d\u003C/code\u003E: click it in the library.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-the-design\"\u003EChange the design\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake a copy\u003C/span\u003E above the read-only script. The copy is saved with your part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange a value near the top and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F5\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. Try:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eg = 0.001;\u003C/code\u003E — half the gap: the gap field nearly doubles, and so does the pull (it goes with B²).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emur = 200;\u003C/code\u003E — a poorer core: more of the ampere-turns are spent in the iron.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN = 200;\u003C/code\u003E — twice the turns: twice the field, four times the inductance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eh = 0.0005;\u003C/code\u003E — a finer grid: closer answers, about four times the time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach run replaces the canvas. The variables the script made (such as \u003Ccode\u003EBgap\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E) stay in the \u003Cstrong\u003EVariables\u003C/strong\u003E list for your own sums.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"model-your-own-shape\"\u003EModel your own shape\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe example builds everything from three lists, one value per grid square, which you can make for any shape drawn with straight lines along the grid:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhere the iron is:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Eiron\u003C/code\u003E is true for the squares inside the core. The example builds it from rectangles: \u003Ccode\u003Eframe &amp; ~window &amp; ~gap\u003C/code\u003E means &quot;in the frame, not in the window, not in the gap&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe permeability:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E1 + (mur - 1) * iron\u003C/code\u003E is 1 in air and \u003Ccode\u003Emur\u003C/code\u003E in the iron. Give several materials their own values the same way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe current:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E is the current density in amperes per square metre, positive out of the screen, negative into it. A coil has two sides with opposite signs; the example works out \u003Ccode\u003EJ0\u003C/code\u003E so that one side carries N I.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThen \u003Ccode\u003Er = magnetostatic2d(m, permeability, J)\u003C/code\u003E solves it. \u003Ccode\u003Er.B\u003C/code\u003E is the flux density in each square (two columns, x and y), \u003Ccode\u003Er.Bmag\u003C/code\u003E its size, \u003Ccode\u003Er.energy\u003C/code\u003E the stored energy per metre. \u003Ccode\u003Eplot(x, y)\u003C/code\u003E draws a line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe library works in metres, and the view in millimetres, so a field shown as it is would be drawn a thousand times too small. The example shows it on a copy of the grid in millimetres (\u003Ccode\u003Emm = quadGrid(…)\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003Eshow(field(mm, …))\u003C/code\u003E): copy those two lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBy default the edge of the box holds the field in: no flux leaves it. Make the box at least two or three times the size of the magnet so that this does not squeeze the field. For a symmetric magnet you can model half of it: the header of \u003Ccode\u003Emagnetostatic2d\u003C/code\u003E says which edge condition to use on a line of symmetry.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"permanent-magnets\"\u003EPermanent magnets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe second example, \u003Cstrong\u003EExamples\u003C/strong\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EPermanent magnet in an iron core: working point, leakage, pull\u003C/strong\u003E, is the same core with the coil taken away and a 10 mm slice of the left leg replaced by a magnet, magnetised upward. Run it with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F5\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. It prints:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ethe magnet works at B = 1.036 T, H = -124 kA/m (on its recoil line B = Br + μ0 μr H)\nB in the gap 0.6778 T; the magnetic circuit without leakage 0.9692 T (-30.1 %)\nleakage factor 1.53: of the flux leaving the magnet, 65 % crosses the gap between the pole faces\npull across the gap about 3656 N per metre of depth (182.8 N for 50 mm deep)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe working point.\u003C/strong\u003E A magnet is described by its \u003Cstrong\u003Eremanence\u003C/strong\u003E Br (the field it keeps when it is shorted by iron: about 1.2 T for a sintered NdFeB magnet, 0.4 T for ferrite) and its recoil permeability (about 1.05 and 1.1). Opening a gap pushes it down its recoil line: here to 1.04 T, with H pointing against B inside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeakage.\u003C/strong\u003E The hand calculation with no leakage gives 0.97 T in the gap; the model gives 0.68 T. A magnet drives its flux with a strong push over a short length, so a lot of it goes straight back across the window instead of through the gap. The leakage factor says how much: 1.53 here. Set \u003Ccode\u003Eg = 0;\u003C/code\u003E for a closed keeper: the magnet then works within half a percent of the hand calculation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMagnets in your own model.\u003C/strong\u003E Give \u003Ccode\u003Emagnetostatic2d\u003C/code\u003E the magnets as \u003Ccode\u003Estruct(&#039;Br&#039;, Br)\u003C/code\u003E, where \u003Ccode\u003EBr\u003C/code\u003E has one row per grid square with the remanence along x and y in tesla (zero outside the magnets), and give the magnet squares their recoil permeability in the permeability list:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E``\u003Ccode\u003E Brem = [zeros(numel(mag), 1), 1.2 * mag];     % 1.2 T upward in the squares where mag is true r = magnetostatic2d(m, 1 + (mur - 1) * iron + 0.05 * mag, 0, struct(), struct(&#039;Br&#039;, Brem)); \u003C/code\u003E``\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a magnet magnetised at an angle, put \u003Ccode\u003EBr·cos(angle)\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EBr·sin(angle)\u003C/code\u003E in the two columns; for a ring magnetised outward, work out each square&#039;s direction from its position. \u003Ccode\u003Er.H\u003C/code\u003E gives the field strength H in each square.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe model keeps a magnet on its straight recoil line however hard it is driven. A real magnet driven past its knee (by a large gap, a strong coil or heat) loses part of its strength for good; check the working point against the maker&#039;s curve for the grade and temperature.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe library&#039;s tests check magnets against two hand results: a round magnet in open air (inside it, B = Br / (1 + μr)) and a magnet closed by an iron keeper (the magnetic circuit), both within 1 %.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-do-yet\"\u003EWhat it does not do yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaturation.\u003C/strong\u003E The iron is linear: double the current, double the field, without limit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDemagnetisation.\u003C/strong\u003E Magnets stay on their straight recoil line (see above).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEddy currents\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Elosses\u003C/strong\u003E (iron loss, copper loss).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMoving parts and torque.\u003C/strong\u003E No rotor turning in a stator, no torque against angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERound shapes.\u003C/strong\u003E The grid is made of equal squares, so curved outlines come out stepped.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E3-D.\u003C/strong\u003E Everything is a cross-section of something long.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMotor design (a rotating rotor, torque and losses in 2-D) is being looked at for a later version. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"PartCraft's analysis library can work out the magnetic field of coils and iron in 2-D: a cross-section of something that is long and the same along its length, such as an electromagnet, a transformer core, a busbar or a motor's cross-section. This tutorial runs the library's electromagnet example, reads what it says, changes it, shows how to model your own shape, and then adds a permanent magnet. It uses the Script workspace and the analysis library. You do not need to know the CraftScript language to follow it; the example says what each line does. What is modelled A C-shaped iron core, 100 × 80 mm outside and 20 mm thick, with a 2 mm air gap in its right leg. A coil of 100 turns carrying 10 A (1000 ampere-turns) is wound round its left leg. Everything is long in the direction out of the screen, so the answers are per metre of depth: multiply by the real depth for a real magnet. The library's magnetostatic2d solves for the magnetic vector potential on a grid of 1 mm squares in a box of air round the magnet, then works out the flux density B (in tesla) from it. Run the example Choose Script in the Workspace box at the left of the toolbar. Open the Library tab of the script panel, then Examples › Electromagnet: field, gap, inductance. Click Open (read-only), then press F5. In a second or two the canvas shows the flux density, and the console prints: B in the gap 0.5427 T; magnetic circuit 0.5516 T (-1.6 %: fringing and leakage) inductance 0.1539 H per metre of depth; the circuit gives 0.1103 H/m, missing the flux that leaks past the gap stored energy 7.694 J/m; |B| peaks at 1.97 T in the window's inner corners (real steel would saturate there) pull across the gap about 2343 N per metre of depth (117.2 N for 50 mm deep) The plot below the canvas is the vertical flux density B_y along the core's middle line, from left to right: about −0.55 T in the left leg (the flux runs down it), almost nothing in the window, and +0.55 T in the right leg and across the gap (the flux comes back up). The same flux passes through the iron and the gap, so the two legs and the gap show nearly the same value. Read the results The field in the gap is 0.543 T. A hand calculation treats the core as one magnetic circuit, B = μ0 N I / (g + l / μr), where g is the gap, l the length of the path through the iron and μr the iron's relative permeability; it gives 0.552 T. The field is a little lower than the hand calculation because the flux bulges out round the gap (fringing) and some flux takes a short cut across the window (leakage). With a smaller gap or a finer grid the two come closer. The inductance of the 100-turn coil is 0.154 H per metre of depth, from the energy stored in the field. The circuit formula gives less (0.110 H/m) because it counts only the flux through the gap, not the flux that leaks past it. The pull that closes the gap is B² w / (2 μ0) for a gap face w wide: about 2300 N per metre, or 117 N for a magnet 50 mm deep. The peak of 1.97 T sits in the inner corners of the window, where the flux turns sharply. The model treats the iron as linear; real electrical steel saturates at about 1.5 to 2 T, so treat values above that as a warning, not an answer. The library's own tests run this example and check the gap field against the magnetic-circuit formula on every change to PartCraft. The method, its references and its other checks (a long solenoid, an iron-cored solenoid) are in the header of magnetostatic2d: click it in the library. Change the design Click Make a copy above the read-only script. The copy is saved with your part. Change a value near the top and press F5 again. Try: g = 0.001; — half the gap: the gap field nearly doubles, and so does the pull (it goes with B²). mur = 200; — a poorer core: more of the ampere-turns are spent in the iron. N = 200; — twice the turns: twice the field, four times the inductance. h = 0.0005; — a finer grid: closer answers, about four times the time. Each run replaces the canvas. The variables the script made (such as Bgap, L, F) stay in the Variables list for your own sums. Model your own shape The example builds everything from three lists, one value per grid square, which you can make for any shape drawn with straight lines along the grid: Where the iron is: iron is true for the squares inside the core. The example builds it from rectangles: frame & ~window & ~gap means \"in the frame, not in the window, not in the gap\". The permeability: 1 + (mur - 1) * iron is 1 in air and mur in the iron. Give several materials their own values the same way. The current: J is the current density in amperes per square metre, positive out of the screen, negative into it. A coil has two sides with opposite signs; the example works out J0 so that one side carries N I. Then r = magnetostatic2d(m, permeability, J) solves it. r.B is the flux density in each square (two columns, x and y), r.Bmag its size, r.energy the stored energy per metre. plot(x, y) draws a line. The library works in metres, and the view in millimetres, so a field shown as it is would be drawn a thousand times too small. The example shows it on a copy of the grid in millimetres (mm = quadGrid(…), then show(field(mm, …))): copy those two lines. By default the edge of the box holds the field in: no flux leaves it. Make the box at least two or three times the size of the magnet so that this does not squeeze the field. For a symmetric magnet you can model half of it: the header of magnetostatic2d says which edge condition to use on a line of symmetry. Permanent magnets The second example, Examples › Permanent magnet in an iron core: working point, leakage, pull, is the same core with the coil taken away and a 10 mm slice of the left leg replaced by a magnet, magnetised upward. Run it with F5. It prints: the magnet works at B = 1.036 T, H = -124 kA/m (on its recoil line B = Br + μ0 μr H) B in the gap 0.6778 T; the magnetic circuit without leakage 0.9692 T (-30.1 %) leakage factor 1.53: of the flux leaving the magnet, 65 % crosses the gap between the pole faces pull across the gap about 3656 N per metre of depth (182.8 N for 50 mm deep) The working point. A magnet is described by its remanence Br (the field it keeps when it is shorted by iron: about 1.2 T for a sintered NdFeB magnet, 0.4 T for ferrite) and its recoil permeability (about 1.05 and 1.1). Opening a gap pushes it down its recoil line: here to 1.04 T, with H pointing against B inside it. Leakage. The hand calculation with no leakage gives 0.97 T in the gap; the model gives 0.68 T. A magnet drives its flux with a strong push over a short length, so a lot of it goes straight back across the window instead of through the gap. The leakage factor says how much: 1.53 here. Set g = 0; for a closed keeper: the magnet then works within half a percent of the hand calculation. Magnets in your own model. Give magnetostatic2d the magnets as struct('Br', Br), where Br has one row per grid square with the remanence along x and y in tesla (zero outside the magnets), and give the magnet squares their recoil permeability in the permeability list: `` Brem = [zeros(numel(mag), 1), 1.2 * mag]; % 1.2 T upward in the squares where mag is true r = magnetostatic2d(m, 1 + (mur - 1) * iron + 0.05 * mag, 0, struct(), struct('Br', Brem)); `` For a magnet magnetised at an angle, put Br·cos(angle) and Br·sin(angle) in the two columns; for a ring magnetised outward, work out each square's direction from its position. r.H gives the field strength H in each square. The model keeps a magnet on its straight recoil line however hard it is driven. A real magnet driven past its knee (by a large gap, a strong coil or heat) loses part of its strength for good; check the working point against the maker's curve for the grade and temperature. The library's tests check magnets against two hand results: a round magnet in open air (inside it, B = Br / (1 + μr)) and a magnet closed by an iron keeper (the magnetic circuit), both within 1 %. What it does not do yet Saturation. The iron is linear: double the current, double the field, without limit. Demagnetisation. Magnets stay on their straight recoil line (see above). Eddy currents and losses (iron loss, copper loss). Moving parts and torque. No rotor turning in a stator, no torque against angle. Round shapes. The grid is made of equal squares, so curved outlines come out stepped. 3-D. Everything is a cross-section of something long. Motor design (a rotating rotor, torque and losses in 2-D) is being looked at for a later version. See Not built yet."},{"slug":"craftscript-own-analysis","scope":"partcraft","title":"Write your own analysis","summary":"Build an analysis as scripts in the Script workspace: mesh the part, assemble and solve, show the field and a convergence plot, check the result against a hand calculation, and keep the steps as separate scripts that share variables.","section":"tasks","order":147,"keywords":["own analysis","custom analysis","FEA script","finite element","assemble","stiffness","boundary conditions","solve","pcg","sparse","field","post-process","convergence","verification","hand calculation","check","workflow"],"headings":[{"id":"1-a-mesh","text":"1. A mesh","level":2},{"id":"2-assemble-and-solve","text":"2. Assemble and solve","level":2},{"id":"3-show-the-result","text":"3. Show the result","level":2},{"id":"4-check-it","text":"4. Check it","level":2},{"id":"5-keep-it","text":"5. Keep it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn analysis in the Script workspace is a few short scripts that hand variables to each other. Start from a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-library\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-library\"\u003Elibrary\u003C/a\u003E function that is close to what you need, make a copy, and change it; or build one up as below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"1-a-mesh\"\u003E1. A mesh\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emodel = part();                  % the part you are modelling, as a model\nm = tetmesh(model, 4);           % tetrahedra about 4 mm across\nshow(m)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECheck the mesh in the view before going on: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fits it, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eeye\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cstrong\u003EShown\u003C/strong\u003E hides the part.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"2-assemble-and-solve\"\u003E2. Assemble and solve\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWrite the element loop in the script (it reads like the textbook), and leave the heavy parts to the built-in functions: element matrices, sparse assembly from triplets, and the conjugate gradient solver with a preconditioner. A library function such as \u003Ccode\u003Estatic_tet\u003C/code\u003E (once the library has it) is a complete example to copy from.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3-show-the-result\"\u003E3. Show the result\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ef = field(m, vm, &#039;node&#039;, &#039;von Mises&#039;, &#039;MPa&#039;);  % one value per node\nshow(f, &#039;deform&#039;, u, &#039;scale&#039;, 100)\nplot(residuals, &#039;title&#039;, &#039;Solver residual&#039;, &#039;id&#039;, &#039;conv&#039;)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe legend&#039;s range can be typed in or set back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the mesh to read the values at a node.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA plot that keeps the same \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;id&#039;\u003C/code\u003E is updated in place, so print and plot inside your loop to watch it converge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"4-check-it\"\u003E4. Check it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECompare with something you know: a cantilever&#039;s tip deflection against PL³/3EI, a plate with a hole against its stress concentration factor, the reactions against the loads. Write the check as its own script so it runs again after every change:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etip = max(abs(u(:)));\nexpected = P * L^3 / (3 * E * I);\nfprintf(&#039;tip %g mm, beam theory %g mm (%.1f %%)\\n&#039;, tip, expected, 100 * (tip / expected - 1))\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"5-keep-it\"\u003E5. Keep it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach step can be its own script tab: they share the variables, so run them one after the other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScripts are saved in the part. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003ESave the variables with the part\u003C/strong\u003E on the Variables tab to keep the results too (up to 32 MB).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E in the Script group downloads a script to share it; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E brings one in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"An analysis in the Script workspace is a few short scripts that hand variables to each other. Start from a library function that is close to what you need, make a copy, and change it; or build one up as below. 1. A mesh model = part(); % the part you are modelling, as a model m = tetmesh(model, 4); % tetrahedra about 4 mm across show(m) Check the mesh in the view before going on: F fits it, the eye in Shown hides the part. 2. Assemble and solve Write the element loop in the script (it reads like the textbook), and leave the heavy parts to the built-in functions: element matrices, sparse assembly from triplets, and the conjugate gradient solver with a preconditioner. A library function such as static_tet (once the library has it) is a complete example to copy from. 3. Show the result f = field(m, vm, 'node', 'von Mises', 'MPa'); % one value per node show(f, 'deform', u, 'scale', 100) plot(residuals, 'title', 'Solver residual', 'id', 'conv') The legend's range can be typed in or set back to Auto. Click the mesh to read the values at a node. A plot that keeps the same 'id' is updated in place, so print and plot inside your loop to watch it converge. 4. Check it Compare with something you know: a cantilever's tip deflection against PL³/3EI, a plate with a hole against its stress concentration factor, the reactions against the loads. Write the check as its own script so it runs again after every change: tip = max(abs(u(:))); expected = P * L^3 / (3 * E * I); fprintf('tip %g mm, beam theory %g mm (%.1f %%)\\n', tip, expected, 100 * (tip / expected - 1)) 5. Keep it Each step can be its own script tab: they share the variables, so run them one after the other. Scripts are saved in the part. Tick Save the variables with the part on the Variables tab to keep the results too (up to 32 MB). Save in the Script group downloads a script to share it; Open brings one in."},{"slug":"save-reopen","scope":"partcraft","title":"Save, reopen and keep working","summary":"Save parts and assemblies to your computer, name them, reopen them by button, key or drag, keep versions, and avoid losing work in a browser tab.","section":"tasks","order":150,"keywords":["save","open","reopen","load","download","versions","backup","rename","file name","lost work","leave site","partcraft.json","partcraft-asm.json","new part"],"headings":[{"id":"save-a-part","text":"Save a part","level":2},{"id":"save-an-assembly","text":"Save an assembly","level":2},{"id":"reopen","text":"Reopen","level":2},{"id":"start-over","text":"Start over","level":2},{"id":"keep-versions","text":"Keep versions","level":2},{"id":"do-not-lose-work","text":"Do not lose work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft keeps nothing between visits. Your part lives in the browser tab until you save it as a file, and comes back when you open that file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-a-part\"\u003ESave a part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EName the part: click empty space in the view so nothing is selected, then type the \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The history heading changes to match.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says &quot;Saved Bracket.partcraft.json (the full history; reopen it here to keep editing).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a sketch is open, saving closes it first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file holds every sketch, relation, dimension and feature, plus suppressed features, the rollback position, the drawing sheet&#039;s settings and GD&amp;T notes, and the full text of any imported STEP solid. Your undo list is not saved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-an-assembly\"\u003ESave an assembly\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn an assembly, the same \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;assembly name&gt;.partcraft-asm.json\u003C/code\u003E. Every part is stored inside it: you do not need the separate part files to reopen it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you edit a part from inside an assembly, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E saves that part on its own as a \u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E first, then save, to save the whole assembly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reopen\"\u003EReopen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAny of these:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Echoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then pick the file;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Edrag the file from \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E onto the 3D view (&quot;Drop a PartCraft part to open it&quot; appears).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft tells parts from assemblies by what is inside, so both open the same way. A \u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.stp\u003C/code\u003E file opens as an imported solid: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/import-step\" data-topic=\"partcraft/import-step\"\u003EOpen a STEP file and build on it\u003C/a\u003E. The status bar confirms: &quot;Opened Bracket.partcraft.json: 7 features.&quot; or &quot;Opened Hinge.partcraft-asm.json: 3 parts, 2 joints.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the part on screen has unsaved changes, PartCraft first asks: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave first\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClose an assembly (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E) before opening a part file. If you open a part while an assembly is on screen, the part loads behind the assembly and you only see it after closing the assembly. Opening an assembly file also replaces an unsaved assembly without asking, so save it first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-over\"\u003EStart over\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E starts an empty &quot;Part1&quot;. It asks first if there are unsaved changes, and closes an open assembly (asking if it is unsaved).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keep-versions\"\u003EKeep versions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach save is a new download, so you can keep versions simply by keeping the files:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPut a version or date in the name before saving: &quot;Bracket rev B&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYour browser may add &quot; (1)&quot;, &quot; (2)&quot; when a file of the same name is already in the downloads folder. The newest is the one with the highest number, or check the date in \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove finished files out of the downloads folder into your project folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E for where downloads go on each system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"do-not-lose-work\"\u003EDo not lose work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave before closing the tab, reloading, or letting the computer sleep for long. The browser asks &quot;Leave site?&quot; if you try to close with unsaved changes, but a crash or a closed laptop gives no warning.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate or incognito windows work, but nothing else is different: the file is still the only copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud saving and version history are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"PartCraft keeps nothing between visits. Your part lives in the browser tab until you save it as a file, and comes back when you open that file. Save a part Name the part: click empty space in the view so nothing is selected, then type the Name in the right panel and press Enter. The history heading changes to match. Press Ctrl+S or choose Save. The browser downloads \u003Cname\u003E.partcraft.json. The status bar says \"Saved Bracket.partcraft.json (the full history; reopen it here to keep editing).\" If a sketch is open, saving closes it first. The file holds every sketch, relation, dimension and feature, plus suppressed features, the rollback position, the drawing sheet's settings and GD&T notes, and the full text of any imported STEP solid. Your undo list is not saved. Save an assembly In an assembly, the same Save or Ctrl+S downloads \u003Cassembly name\u003E.partcraft-asm.json. Every part is stored inside it: you do not need the separate part files to reopen it. While you edit a part from inside an assembly, Ctrl+S saves that part on its own as a .partcraft.json. Choose Back first, then save, to save the whole assembly. Reopen Any of these: choose Open or press Ctrl+O, then pick the file; drag the file from Finder / File Explorer / your file manager onto the 3D view (\"Drop a PartCraft part to open it\" appears). PartCraft tells parts from assemblies by what is inside, so both open the same way. A .step or .stp file opens as an imported solid: see Open a STEP file and build on it. The status bar confirms: \"Opened Bracket.partcraft.json: 7 features.\" or \"Opened Hinge.partcraft-asm.json: 3 parts, 2 joints.\" If the part on screen has unsaved changes, PartCraft first asks: Cancel, Discard changes or Save first. Close an assembly (Close) before opening a part file. If you open a part while an assembly is on screen, the part loads behind the assembly and you only see it after closing the assembly. Opening an assembly file also replaces an unsaved assembly without asking, so save it first. Start over New starts an empty \"Part1\". It asks first if there are unsaved changes, and closes an open assembly (asking if it is unsaved). Keep versions Each save is a new download, so you can keep versions simply by keeping the files: Put a version or date in the name before saving: \"Bracket rev B\". Your browser may add \" (1)\", \" (2)\" when a file of the same name is already in the downloads folder. The newest is the one with the highest number, or check the date in Finder / File Explorer / your file manager. Move finished files out of the downloads folder into your project folder. See Where your files go for where downloads go on each system. Do not lose work Save before closing the tab, reloading, or letting the computer sleep for long. The browser asks \"Leave site?\" if you try to close with unsaved changes, but a crash or a closed laptop gives no warning. Private or incognito windows work, but nothing else is different: the file is still the only copy. Cloud saving and version history are not available yet."},{"slug":"work-live","scope":"partcraft","title":"Model one part together, live","summary":"What changes in PartCraft while you are in a CadCraft Studio live session: everyone's features in one tree, each window building the same solid, your own undo, and joining from a browser on the same network.","section":"tasks","order":199,"keywords":["live","studio live","together","collaborate","session","share live","join","feature tree","history","rebuild","undo","host"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"everyone-models-at-once","text":"Everyone models at once","level":2},{"id":"undo-undoes-your-own-work","text":"Undo undoes your own work","level":2},{"id":"join-from-a-browser","text":"Join from a browser","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat happens to a part while several people model it at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the session (Share live, Join); this page is about the part itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everyone-models-at-once\"\u003EEveryone models at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEveryone works on the same feature tree: sketches, extrudes, holes, patterns, fillets, parameters. Your own edits show at once; other people&#039;s appear a moment after they make them. Only the tree travels: each window builds the solid itself, and PartCraft&#039;s kernel builds the same solid from the same tree in every window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo people change one feature:\u003C/strong\u003E different settings (one the depth, the other the end condition) are both kept; the same setting goes to the later change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA sketch\u003C/strong\u003E travels as a whole: when two people edit one sketch at once, the later edit wins. Agree who draws which sketch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA feature built on one someone just deleted\u003C/strong\u003E (an extrude of a removed sketch, a hole on a removed face) is taken back, and the status bar says why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReordering the tree\u003C/strong\u003E is checked: if two people drag features at the same moment, the second move is taken back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-undoes-your-own-work\"\u003EUndo undoes your own work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes your own last edit and leaves everyone else&#039;s alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-from-a-browser\"\u003EJoin from a browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. PartCraft opens in the browser, fills with the host&#039;s part once the host lets you in, and builds it. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the host saves the part. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssemblies aren&#039;t shared in a session yet: open the part itself to work on it together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will learn What happens to a part while several people model it at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the session (Share live, Join); this page is about the part itself. Everyone models at once Everyone works on the same feature tree: sketches, extrudes, holes, patterns, fillets, parameters. Your own edits show at once; other people's appear a moment after they make them. Only the tree travels: each window builds the solid itself, and PartCraft's kernel builds the same solid from the same tree in every window. Two people change one feature: different settings (one the depth, the other the end condition) are both kept; the same setting goes to the later change. A sketch travels as a whole: when two people edit one sketch at once, the later edit wins. Agree who draws which sketch. A feature built on one someone just deleted (an extrude of a removed sketch, a hole on a removed face) is taken back, and the status bar says why. Reordering the tree is checked: if two people drag features at the same moment, the second move is taken back. Undo undoes your own work Ctrl+Z undoes your own last edit and leaves everyone else's alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who. Join from a browser A teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host's code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. PartCraft opens in the browser, fills with the host's part once the host lets you in, and builds it. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet. When the session ends Only the host saves the part. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it. Assemblies aren't shared in a session yet: open the part itself to work on it together."},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"partcraft","title":"Coming from another parametric modeler","summary":"How the habits of desktop parametric part modelers map to PartCraft, and the differences that trip experienced users up in the first hour.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switching","migrate","familiar","feature manager","design tree","smart dimension","boss extrude","cut extrude","hole wizard","mates","interference detection","rollback bar","property manager","mouse","differences"],"headings":[{"id":"habits-mapped","text":"Habits, mapped","level":2},{"id":"differences-that-trip-people-up","text":"Differences that trip people up","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you design parts in SolidWorks, most of PartCraft&#039;s ideas will be familiar: sketches on Front, Top and Right planes, relations and smart dimensions, a feature history with a rollback bar, and assemblies placed by mates. The way you reach them is different in places, and this first build does less. This page maps your habits and lists the surprises.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"habits-mapped\"\u003EHabits, mapped\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou are used to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn PartCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe design tree on the left\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe history on the left: planes, then features, sketches indented under the feature that uses them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFront, Top and Right planes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same three, through the origin. Front is the XY plane, Top is XZ, Right is YZ; Y points up\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch on a plane or face, then the confirmation corner to exit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) on a selected plane or flat face; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExit\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E until it closes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESmart Dimension\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Click one or two items, then where the number goes. Where you place it picks horizontal, vertical or aligned\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd Relations in the property panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect items, then the relation buttons in the right panel (Coincident, Horizontal, Parallel, Tangent, Equal and so on)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAutomatic relations while sketching\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnaps add coincident, point-on-curve, horizontal and vertical relations\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue / black / red sketch colors\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same idea: blue under defined, ink fully defined, red over defined. The chip shows the degrees of freedom left\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenterline for revolves\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECenterline\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolve\u003C/span\u003E uses the first centerline unless you pick another axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch fillet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) in a sketch: click the corner, type the radius\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstruction geometry toggle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstruction\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtruded boss\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E: Blind, Mid-plane, Up to face / next / vertex, Offset from face, second direction, taper, one contour (Pick regions)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtruded cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E: starts as Through all; switch to Blind for a pocket\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERevolved boss / cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolve\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolved cut\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReference plane / axis / point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlane\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/reference-geometry\" data-topic=\"partcraft/reference-geometry\"\u003EReference planes, axes and points\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwept / lofted boss or cut, draft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E on flat faces (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sweep-loft-draft\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sweep-loft-draft\"\u003ESweep, loft and draft\u003C/a\u003E). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESweep\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoft\u003C/span\u003E are not built yet in this version (they need an exact kernel)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHole Wizard\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHole\u003C/span\u003E at every loose sketch point: simple, counterbore, countersink (90° / 82°) or tapped ISO metric M2–M24, through or blind\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear / circular pattern, mirror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircular\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMirror\u003C/span\u003E (features or the whole body)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet and chamfer on edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/span\u003E: click an edge, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click more, then the button. One radius; equal, two-distance or distance-angle chamfers. In this version only straight edges between two flat faces, and rounds that meet at a corner are mitred. Round or bevel curved edges in the sketch (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E in a sketch, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShell\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShell\u003C/span\u003E: click the flat faces to open, then the button. One wall thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a STEP file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet in this version (it needs an exact kernel)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit feature, then OK\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the feature; change numbers on the right; it rebuilds as you type. No OK, no Cancel: use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click the sketch in the history\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERebuild\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAutomatic after every change. There is no rebuild key\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESuppress / unsuppress\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESuppress\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnsuppress\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag the rollback bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoll back to here\u003C/span\u003E on a feature; click &quot;Rollback bar · roll forward&quot; to return\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a feature in the tree to reorder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove up\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove down\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↑\"\u003E⌥↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↓\"\u003E⌥↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; refused with the reason when it would break a dependency\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit dimensions by double-clicking on the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect the feature: its sketch&#039;s dimensions are listed in the right panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMates: coincident, concentric, distance, angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoint\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"J\"\u003EJ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EJ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): pick a flat face on each part; the joint puts them face to face. \u003Cstrong\u003ECentre on\u003C/strong\u003E an axis acts like concentric; \u003Cstrong\u003EOffset\u003C/strong\u003E like distance; \u003Cstrong\u003EAngle\u003C/strong\u003E like angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFixed component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe first part is grounded; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake it the grounded part\u003C/span\u003E changes it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInsert component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd copy\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E (another copy) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E (from a part file)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit part in the assembly\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit part\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInterference detection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClash\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoving a component to see the motion\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag the angle or offset slider of a revolute or slider joint; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck the motion for clashes\u003C/span\u003E sweeps it. A part with no joint: \u003Cstrong\u003EMove by hand\u003C/strong\u003E (arrows to drag, or typed values)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasure / Inspect\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): distances, angles, radii, areas; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMass properties\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExploded view, BOM\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn an assembly with nothing selected: \u003Cstrong\u003EExploded views\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EBill of materials\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMass properties\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EPart\u003C/strong\u003E box: volume, surface area, size, of the faceted solid (a little low on round parts). No mass: there are no materials yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave, Save As STEP\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E downloads a \u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E has STL, 3MF, faceted STEP, DXF and a PDF drawing (exact STEP is not built yet in this version)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake a drawing from the part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E: an automatic A3 sheet with three views, isometric, one section, one detail, datums, feature control frames and finish symbols. Only overall sizes are dimensioned\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMiddle-drag to rotate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeft-drag\u003C/strong\u003E rotates here. Middle- or right-drag pans. The wheel zooms at the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EView orientation, Normal To\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E menu; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENormal to\u003C/span\u003E for a picked face, plane or sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom to fit (F)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E (in a sketch, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is the Fillet tool)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click menus\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENone. Right-drag pans; in a sketch, right-click ends a line chain\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your hands know SolidWorks, pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeature tree\u003C/span\u003E menu style in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPartCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E: a ribbon of Features, Sketch, Surfaces, Evaluate, Drawing, Assembly and View tabs, the middle button orbits, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the middle button pan, and the left button only picks. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"partcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-that-trip-people-up\"\u003EDifferences that trip people up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe whole sketch is the profile.\u003C/strong\u003E Extrude and Revolve use every closed loop in the sketch. You cannot pick one region or one contour of a sketch for a feature. Use one sketch per feature.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhich sketch a feature takes.\u003C/strong\u003E A feature uses the sketch you just closed or the one selected in the history. With nothing selected it takes the newest sketch no feature uses yet. If you get the wrong one, select the right sketch and choose the feature again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChanges apply as you type.\u003C/strong\u003E There is no feature dialog with OK and Cancel. Every number rebuilds at once, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is the way back. The undo list covers sketch edits, features and settings alike.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOver defined is never allowed to stand quietly.\u003C/strong\u003E There are no driven (reference) dimensions. A dimension that the other relations already fix is reported as over defined (&quot;is already implied. Delete it.&quot;). A conflicting one leaves the geometry untouched and turns red. Delete one, or undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESweeps, lofts and exact STEP are not built yet; fillets, chamfers, shells and drafts have limits.\u003C/strong\u003E This version of PartCraft builds every part with a mesh kernel; the status bar chip always says &quot;Mesh kernel&quot;. Fillets and chamfers work on straight edges between two flat faces, and where rounds meet at a corner they are mitred, not blended. Shells open, and drafts tilt, flat faces only. Features that need an exact kernel stay clickable, but say &quot;not built yet&quot; when chosen. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo buttons called Fillet.\u003C/strong\u003E In a sketch, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) rounds a sketch corner. On the part, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E in the Features group rounds solid edges (straight edges between flat faces in this version). There is no sweep or loft in this version: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHoles need loose points.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHole\u003C/span\u003E drills at points made with the Point tool. Circle centers and line ends do not count.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThrough all is for cuts only.\u003C/strong\u003E A boss needs a depth. There are no &quot;up to vertex / surface / next&quot; end conditions yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe solid is faceted.\u003C/strong\u003E The mesh kernel builds round faces from flat facets, so the view, volumes, STL, 3MF and STEP exports are all made of them: \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E in the part&#039;s panel makes them finer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMillimeters only.\u003C/strong\u003E There is no inch setting yet. Multiply inch sizes by 25.4 when you type them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EY is up.\u003C/strong\u003E The Front plane faces you and Top looks down on the part, as you expect, but the world&#039;s up axis is Y. STL and 3MF exports are turned to Z up; the faceted STEP keeps Y up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMates are faces, not geometry pairs.\u003C/strong\u003E A joint always starts from two flat faces put face to face. For a pin in a hole, pick the flat face around the hole and the pin&#039;s shoulder, then set \u003Cstrong\u003ECentre on\u003C/strong\u003E to the hole&#039;s axis and the pin&#039;s revolve axis. Parts not reached by joints stay where they were inserted; you cannot drag them. Joints in a loop are not solved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving is a download.\u003C/strong\u003E There is no file you keep overwriting. Each save is a new file in your downloads folder. Name the part first, and keep your files in a project folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo context menus, no command manager tabs.\u003C/strong\u003E Everything is on the toolbar and the right panel, and the toolbar changes with what you are doing: the sketch tools appear only in a sketch, the assembly tools only in an assembly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeys are single letters inside a sketch.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pick tools with no modifier. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"partcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you design parts in SolidWorks, most of PartCraft's ideas will be familiar: sketches on Front, Top and Right planes, relations and smart dimensions, a feature history with a rollback bar, and assemblies placed by mates. The way you reach them is different in places, and this first build does less. This page maps your habits and lists the surprises. Habits, mapped You are used to In PartCraft The design tree on the left The history on the left: planes, then features, sketches indented under the feature that uses them Front, Top and Right planes The same three, through the origin. Front is the XY plane, Top is XZ, Right is YZ; Y points up Sketch on a plane or face, then the confirmation corner to exit Sketch (S) on a selected plane or flat face; Exit or Esc until it closes Smart Dimension Dimension (D). Click one or two items, then where the number goes. Where you place it picks horizontal, vertical or aligned Add Relations in the property panel Select items, then the relation buttons in the right panel (Coincident, Horizontal, Parallel, Tangent, Equal and so on) Automatic relations while sketching Snaps add coincident, point-on-curve, horizontal and vertical relations Blue / black / red sketch colors The same idea: blue under defined, ink fully defined, red over defined. The chip shows the degrees of freedom left Centerline for revolves Centerline (N); Revolve uses the first centerline unless you pick another axis Sketch fillet Fillet (F) in a sketch: click the corner, type the radius Construction geometry toggle Construction (Q) Extruded boss Extrude: Blind, Mid-plane, Up to face / next / vertex, Offset from face, second direction, taper, one contour (Pick regions) Extruded cut Cut: starts as Through all; switch to Blind for a pocket Revolved boss / cut Revolve / More › Revolved cut Reference plane / axis / point More › Plane, Axis, Point (see Reference planes, axes and points) Swept / lofted boss or cut, draft More › Draft on flat faces (see Sweep, loft and draft). Sweep and Loft are not built yet in this version (they need an exact kernel) Hole Wizard Hole at every loose sketch point: simple, counterbore, countersink (90° / 82°) or tapped ISO metric M2–M24, through or blind Linear / circular pattern, mirror Linear, Circular, Mirror (features or the whole body) Fillet and chamfer on edges Fillet / Chamfer: click an edge, Shift+click more, then the button. One radius; equal, two-distance or distance-angle chamfers. In this version only straight edges between two flat faces, and rounds that meet at a corner are mitred. Round or bevel curved edges in the sketch (Fillet in a sketch, F) Shell Shell: click the flat faces to open, then the button. One wall thickness Open a STEP file Not built yet in this version (it needs an exact kernel) Edit feature, then OK Click the feature; change numbers on the right; it rebuilds as you type. No OK, no Cancel: use Ctrl+Z Edit sketch Double-click the sketch in the history Rebuild Automatic after every change. There is no rebuild key Suppress / unsuppress Suppress / Unsuppress in the right panel Drag the rollback bar Roll back to here on a feature; click \"Rollback bar · roll forward\" to return Drag a feature in the tree to reorder Move up / Move down or Alt+↑ / Alt+↓; refused with the reason when it would break a dependency Edit dimensions by double-clicking on the model Select the feature: its sketch's dimensions are listed in the right panel Mates: coincident, concentric, distance, angle Joint (J): pick a flat face on each part; the joint puts them face to face. Centre on an axis acts like concentric; Offset like distance; Angle like angle Fixed component The first part is grounded; Make it the grounded part changes it Insert component Add copy or Duplicate (another copy) or Insert (from a part file) Edit part in the assembly Edit part, then Back Interference detection Clash Moving a component to see the motion Drag the angle or offset slider of a revolute or slider joint; Check the motion for clashes sweeps it. A part with no joint: Move by hand (arrows to drag, or typed values) Measure / Inspect Views, then Measure (M): distances, angles, radii, areas; Mass properties Exploded view, BOM In an assembly with nothing selected: Exploded views and Bill of materials in the right panel Mass properties The Part box: volume, surface area, size, of the faceted solid (a little low on round parts). No mass: there are no materials yet Save, Save As STEP Save downloads a .partcraft.json; Export has STL, 3MF, faceted STEP, DXF and a PDF drawing (exact STEP is not built yet in this version) Make a drawing from the part Drawing: an automatic A3 sheet with three views, isometric, one section, one detail, datums, feature control frames and finish symbols. Only overall sizes are dimensioned Middle-drag to rotate Left-drag rotates here. Middle- or right-drag pans. The wheel zooms at the pointer View orientation, Normal To Views menu; Normal to for a picked face, plane or sketch Zoom to fit (F) F, or Fit (in a sketch, F is the Fillet tool) Right-click menus None. Right-drag pans; in a sketch, right-click ends a line chain If your hands know SolidWorks, pick the Feature tree menu style in Settings › PartCraft › Menus, keys and mouse: a ribbon of Features, Sketch, Surfaces, Evaluate, Drawing, Assembly and View tabs, the middle button orbits, Shift and the middle button pan, and the left button only picks. See Menus, keys and mouse. Differences that trip people up The whole sketch is the profile. Extrude and Revolve use every closed loop in the sketch. You cannot pick one region or one contour of a sketch for a feature. Use one sketch per feature. Which sketch a feature takes. A feature uses the sketch you just closed or the one selected in the history. With nothing selected it takes the newest sketch no feature uses yet. If you get the wrong one, select the right sketch and choose the feature again. Changes apply as you type. There is no feature dialog with OK and Cancel. Every number rebuilds at once, and Ctrl+Z is the way back. The undo list covers sketch edits, features and settings alike. Over defined is never allowed to stand quietly. There are no driven (reference) dimensions. A dimension that the other relations already fix is reported as over defined (\"is already implied. Delete it.\"). A conflicting one leaves the geometry untouched and turns red. Delete one, or undo. Sweeps, lofts and exact STEP are not built yet; fillets, chamfers, shells and drafts have limits. This version of PartCraft builds every part with a mesh kernel; the status bar chip always says \"Mesh kernel\". Fillets and chamfers work on straight edges between two flat faces, and where rounds meet at a corner they are mitred, not blended. Shells open, and drafts tilt, flat faces only. Features that need an exact kernel stay clickable, but say \"not built yet\" when chosen. See The mesh kernel and What is not built yet. Two buttons called Fillet. In a sketch, Fillet (F) rounds a sketch corner. On the part, the Fillet in the Features group rounds solid edges (straight edges between flat faces in this version). There is no sweep or loft in this version: see What is not built yet. Holes need loose points. Hole drills at points made with the Point tool. Circle centers and line ends do not count. Through all is for cuts only. A boss needs a depth. There are no \"up to vertex / surface / next\" end conditions yet. The solid is faceted. The mesh kernel builds round faces from flat facets, so the view, volumes, STL, 3MF and STEP exports are all made of them: Curves in the part's panel makes them finer. Millimeters only. There is no inch setting yet. Multiply inch sizes by 25.4 when you type them. Y is up. The Front plane faces you and Top looks down on the part, as you expect, but the world's up axis is Y. STL and 3MF exports are turned to Z up; the faceted STEP keeps Y up. Mates are faces, not geometry pairs. A joint always starts from two flat faces put face to face. For a pin in a hole, pick the flat face around the hole and the pin's shoulder, then set Centre on to the hole's axis and the pin's revolve axis. Parts not reached by joints stay where they were inserted; you cannot drag them. Joints in a loop are not solved. Saving is a download. There is no file you keep overwriting. Each save is a new file in your downloads folder. Name the part first, and keep your files in a project folder. No context menus, no command manager tabs. Everything is on the toolbar and the right panel, and the toolbar changes with what you are doing: the sketch tools appear only in a sketch, the assembly tools only in an assembly. Keys are single letters inside a sketch. L, R, C, A, P, N, F, D, Q and S pick tools with no modifier. See Keys and mouse."},{"slug":"menus-keys-and-mouse","scope":"partcraft","title":"Menus, keys and mouse","summary":"Pick a menu style that matches the way you already work, give any command your own key, move commands between ribbon tabs, and set what the wheel and each mouse button do in the 3D view.","section":"reference","order":6,"keywords":["menu style","menus","ribbon","toolbar","feature tree","keyboard shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","customize","remap","rebind","mouse","wheel","invert","zoom direction","middle button","orbit","pan","export","import","switching","familiar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-two-styles","text":"The two styles","level":2},{"id":"make-it-your-own","text":"Make it your own","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"take-it-with-you","text":"Take it with you","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft&#039;s commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A \u003Cstrong\u003Emenu style\u003C/strong\u003E only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPartCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-two-styles\"\u003EThe two styles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStyle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPartCraft\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPartCraft&#039;s own toolbar: the workspace switcher, File, Sketch, Features, Drawing, Assembly, Edit and View. Keys \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sketch, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fit, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E measure, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E section view. The start.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeature tree\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA ribbon laid out the way feature-based mechanical CAD programs lay theirs: a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu (with every export), quick buttons, and the tabs \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeatures\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESketch\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESurfaces\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvaluate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStudies\u003C/span\u003E (the workspaces and their tools) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E. Extra keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E extrude, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E hole, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E then a letter for a view: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E front, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E back, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E top, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E bottom, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E right, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E left, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E isometric, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E normal to. In the 3D view the middle button orbits, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the middle button pan, the right button pans and the left button only picks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery style keeps \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA tool of another workspace switches to it first; a Design tool picked in another workspace goes back to Design first. Sketch tools wait for a sketch, the drawing tools for the drawing sheet, the assembly tools for an assembly: the status bar says what to do first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInside a sketch, on the drawing sheet, in an assembly and in the other workspaces, letters keep their own meaning there (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E line, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rectangle, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"J\"\u003EJ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EJ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E joint …) in every style. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the arrows always keep theirs. A key of PartCraft&#039;s own layout that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status bar says where its command went.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-it-your-own\"\u003EMake it your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the style closest to what you want, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustomize…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys and menus\u003C/span\u003E, find a command (the search box helps). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a key\u003C/span\u003E and press the key, or two keys in a row. Click a key to take it off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith the ribbon, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere it sits\u003C/span\u003E moves the command to another tab and panel, to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu, or to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys only\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMouse\u003C/span\u003E, set the wheel, the buttons that pan a sketch, and what each button does in the 3D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving a built-in style makes your own copy (&quot;My Feature tree&quot;); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands, or one that would run before a two-letter key could be typed, is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms in\u003C/span\u003E (the start) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms out\u003C/span\u003E, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan the sketch with\u003C/strong\u003E: the middle button, the right button, or both. A right click in a sketch still ends the tool&#039;s step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag in the 3D view\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEach view&#039;s own\u003C/span\u003E keeps PartCraft&#039;s setting (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPartCraft\u003C/span\u003E: left orbits, or middle orbits). Or set each button to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the orbit button pans. A click without dragging still picks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-it-with-you\"\u003ETake it with you\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the style in use as a small \u003Ccode\u003E.ccinput.json\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E adds one, from another computer or a colleague. An imported file can only point at PartCraft&#039;s own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status bar. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this style\u003C/span\u003E removes one of your own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese settings stay in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this style in every app\u003C/span\u003E under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, or a ribbon), with the changes you made in each app. Choosing another style in one app changes only that app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelated: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"partcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"partcraft/settings\"\u003EPartCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"partcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from another parametric modeler\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"PartCraft's commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A menu style only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in Settings (Ctrl+,) › PartCraft › Menus, keys and mouse; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it. The two styles Style What you get PartCraft PartCraft's own toolbar: the workspace switcher, File, Sketch, Features, Drawing, Assembly, Edit and View. Keys S sketch, F fit, M measure, X section view. The start. Feature tree A ribbon laid out the way feature-based mechanical CAD programs lay theirs: a File menu (with every export), quick buttons, and the tabs Features, Sketch, Surfaces, Evaluate, Drawing, Assembly, Studies (the workspaces and their tools) and View. Extra keys: E extrude, H hole, and V then a letter for a view: V F front, V B back, V T top, V O bottom, V R right, V L left, V I isometric, V N normal to. In the 3D view the middle button orbits, Shift and the middle button pan, the right button pans and the left button only picks. Every style keeps Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+D and Ctrl+A. A tool of another workspace switches to it first; a Design tool picked in another workspace goes back to Design first. Sketch tools wait for a sketch, the drawing tools for the drawing sheet, the assembly tools for an assembly: the status bar says what to do first. Inside a sketch, on the drawing sheet, in an assembly and in the other workspaces, letters keep their own meaning there (L line, R rectangle, J joint …) in every style. Delete, Esc, Enter, Tab, Space and the arrows always keep theirs. A key of PartCraft's own layout that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status bar says where its command went. Make it your own Pick the style closest to what you want, then click Customize…. On Keys and menus, find a command (the search box helps). Add a key and press the key, or two keys in a row. Click a key to take it off. With the ribbon, Where it sits moves the command to another tab and panel, to the File menu, or to Keys only. On Mouse, set the wheel, the buttons that pan a sketch, and what each button does in the 3D view. Give it a name and click Save. Saving a built-in style makes your own copy (\"My Feature tree\"); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands, or one that would run before a two-letter key could be typed, is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save. Mouse Mouse wheel: Scroll up zooms in (the start) or Scroll up zooms out, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move. Pan the sketch with: the middle button, the right button, or both. A right click in a sketch still ends the tool's step. Drag in the 3D view: Each view's own keeps PartCraft's setting (Settings › PartCraft: left orbits, or middle orbits). Or set each button to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; Shift with the orbit button pans. A click without dragging still picks. Take it with you Export saves the style in use as a small .ccinput.json file; Import… adds one, from another computer or a colleague. An imported file can only point at PartCraft's own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status bar. Delete this style removes one of your own. These settings stay in this browser. Use this style in every app under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, or a ribbon), with the changes you made in each app. Choosing another style in one app changes only that app. Related: Keys and mouse · PartCraft settings · Coming from another parametric modeler"},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"partcraft","title":"Keys and mouse","summary":"Every keyboard shortcut and mouse action in PartCraft, grouped by where it works: everywhere, copy and paste, on the part, in the 3D view, in a sketch, in the history and in an assembly.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","mouse","orbit","rotate","pan","zoom","wheel","right click","undo","redo","save","open","escape","delete","alt arrow","copy","paste","cut","duplicate","select all","middle button"],"headings":[{"id":"everywhere","text":"Everywhere","level":2},{"id":"copy-paste-and-duplicate","text":"Copy, paste and duplicate","level":2},{"id":"on-the-part-no-sketch-open","text":"On the part (no sketch open)","level":2},{"id":"in-the-3d-view","text":"In the 3D view","level":2},{"id":"while-picking-edges-or-faces-for-a-fillet-chamfer-or-shell","text":"While picking edges or faces for a fillet, chamfer or shell","level":2},{"id":"on-the-drawing-sheet","text":"On the drawing sheet","level":2},{"id":"in-a-sketch","text":"In a sketch","level":2},{"id":"in-the-number-box","text":"In the number box","level":3},{"id":"in-the-history-list","text":"In the history list","level":2},{"id":"in-an-assembly","text":"In an assembly","level":2},{"id":"in-the-script-workspace","text":"In the Script workspace","level":2},{"id":"shortcuts-the-browser-may-take","text":"Shortcuts the browser may take","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EKeys do nothing while you are typing in a box in a panel, except \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, which always work. Letter keys work without a modifier.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGeneral notes about keys in the browser, and on keyboards without function keys: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everywhere\"\u003EEverywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave the part (in an assembly: the assembly). Closes an open sketch first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a part or assembly file (asks first if there are unsaved changes)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen or close this help (also \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes it too. On many laptops and Mac keyboards, hold Fn\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScreen mode: normal, full screen, full screen with the bars hidden. Control on a Mac too, not ⌘\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeave full screen (when in full screen)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESettings, including the PartCraft tab (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"partcraft/settings\"\u003EPartCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a panel&#039;s number box, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is the browser&#039;s own text undo. In the number box over a sketch dimension, it undoes in PartCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-paste-and-duplicate\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey act on what you are working in: the selected geometry in an open sketch, the selected feature in the history, or the selected part in an assembly. Details in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"partcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut: copy, then delete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste. In a sketch the copy follows the pointer: click where it goes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place: same coordinates (in an assembly, the same place)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate: copy and paste in one step, leaving the clipboard alone\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect all (in a sketch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect none (and cancel a paste that follows the pointer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a number box, these keys copy and paste text as usual. Selected text in a panel is copied by the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-the-part-no-sketch-open\"\u003EOn the part (no sketch open)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch on the selected plane or flat face; with nothing picked, choose where by clicking\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit the part in the view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasure, in every workspace and in an assembly; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E clears the picks, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again closes it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/measure\" data-topic=\"partcraft/measure\"\u003EMeasure distances, angles and mass\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESection view on or off, in every workspace and in an assembly; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/section-view\" data-topic=\"partcraft/section-view\"\u003ELook inside with a section view\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the selected feature (asks if others use it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"0\"\u003E0\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E0\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E … \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"9\"\u003E9\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E9\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\".\"\u003E.\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E.\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith a feature selected: type its main number (an Extrude&#039;s depth, a Hole&#039;s diameter, a pattern&#039;s count, a fillet&#039;s radius …), then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps the old one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the selected feature, when one is selected\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStop choosing a sketch plane, or clear the selection (including edges or faces picked with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-3d-view\"\u003EIn the 3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick in the view first so it has the keyboard focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the view 15 degrees\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift ←\"\u003E⇧←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the other arrows\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"=\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMouse\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn (orbit) the view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right or middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or ⌘ held while dragging with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERoll the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom at the pointer (away from you zooms in)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger swipe on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the view; with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, pan. Pinch zooms. Set by \u003Cstrong\u003EScrolling\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"partcraft/settings\"\u003EPartCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cstrong\u003ETurning the view: Middle button\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"partcraft/settings\"\u003EPartCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E, the middle button turns the view, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the middle button or the right button pans, and the left button only picks. | Click | Pick a face (it highlights whole), an edge or a plane | | \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click an edge or face | Collect several edges or faces | | Click empty space | Clear the pick |\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"while-picking-edges-or-faces-for-a-fillet-chamfer-or-shell\"\u003EWhile picking edges or faces for a fillet, chamfer or shell\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or mouse\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick an edge or face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd it, or drop it if it is already in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish picking (same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArrow keys, with the view focused\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn; with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-the-drawing-sheet\"\u003EOn the drawing sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit the sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom (with the sheet focused)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift ←\"\u003E⇧←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the other arrows\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan (with the sheet focused)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENudge the selected GD&amp;T symbol 1 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the selected symbol\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStop placing a symbol; else deselect; else back to the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMouse\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick on a view, with Datum, Frame, Finish or Detail chosen\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace it there (snaps to the nearest line)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a symbol\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a symbol\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove it (the leader stays on its point)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERoll the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom at the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right or middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-a-sketch\"\u003EIn a sketch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERectangle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECircle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArc (center, start, end)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenterline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet a corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpline (fit points)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrim\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtend\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while offsetting: both sides)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMirror\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwitch the selected curves to construction and back\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnd a line chain or a spline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect everything in the sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy, cut, paste geometry (paste follows the pointer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste at the same coordinates\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate the selection and place it with a click\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStep back: cancel the number box or a paste, end the line chain, back to Select, clear the selection, then close the sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMouse\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace points with a drawing tool; select with Select\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd to or take from the selection (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click on Windows and Linux, ⌘+click on a Mac, do the same)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag on empty space (Select)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow: selects everything fully inside\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a point, line or circle edge (Select)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove it; relations hold\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a dimension&#039;s number (Select)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the label\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click a dimension\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange its value\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click or right-click while drawing lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnd the chain\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right or middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERoll the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom at the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"in-the-number-box\"\u003EIn the number box\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApply the value\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose without changing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicking away also applies the value.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-history-list\"\u003EIn the history list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReach the list\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove between rows\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect the row\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↑\"\u003E⌥↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↓\"\u003E⌥↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the selected feature up or down, where the history allows\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F2\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange the feature&#039;s main number in its row; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sets it, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps the old one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMouse\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a plane or feature\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click a sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click a feature&#039;s number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange it in the row\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-an-assembly\"\u003EIn an assembly\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"J\"\u003EJ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EJ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart a joint: then click a flat face on each of two parts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancel the joint you started, or clear the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove the selected part or joint\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove the selected part or joint\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo and redo in the assembly&#039;s own list\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate the selected part (same part, beside the others)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy a part, paste it beside the others or in the same place\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMouse in the view: as in the 3D view above. Click a part to select it. Double-click a part in the list to edit it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-script-workspace\"\u003EIn the Script workspace\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F5\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun the script (in the editor, the panel or the view)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun the selected lines, or the line the cursor is on\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Tab\"\u003E⇧ Tab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Tab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIndent / outdent the selected lines. To leave the editor with the keyboard, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command /\"\u003E⌘/\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+/\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EComment the selected lines out, or back in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command F\"\u003E⌘F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command H\"\u003E⌘H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFind / find and replace in the script; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E next match, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Return\"\u003E⇧ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift F3\"\u003E⇧ F3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+F3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E previous, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command [\"\u003E⇧⌘[\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+[\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command ]\"\u003E⇧⌘]\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+]\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFold / unfold the block the cursor is on (a function, if, for, while … end)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Space\"\u003E⌘ Space\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Space\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EList completions for the name you are typing (it also opens after two letters); \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E choose, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes one, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in the line under the console\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun that one statement; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E bring back earlier ones\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit the view to what scripts show\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript\"\u003EWrite and run scripts\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcuts-the-browser-may-take\"\u003EShortcuts the browser may take\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in Safari may open the history: use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to redo.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is the browser&#039;s bookmark key: PartCraft takes it while the app has the focus, and leaves it alone while you type in a box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command +\"\u003E⌘+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl++\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command −\"\u003E⌘−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E are the browser&#039;s full screen and page zoom, not PartCraft&#039;s. Use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for full screen and the wheel for the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFunction keys on many laptops and Macs need the Fn key: press Fn+\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for help, or use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETouch and trackpad gestures: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/trackpad-and-touch\" data-topic=\"partcraft/trackpad-and-touch\"\u003ETrackpad, touch and small screens\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Keys do nothing while you are typing in a box in a panel, except Ctrl+S and Ctrl+O, which always work. Letter keys work without a modifier. General notes about keys in the browser, and on keyboards without function keys: Keys, mice and trackpads. Everywhere Key Action Ctrl+S Save the part (in an assembly: the assembly). Closes an open sketch first Ctrl+O Open a part or assembly file (asks first if there are unsaved changes) Ctrl+Z Undo Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo F1 Open or close this help (also Help in the app bar). Esc closes it too. On many laptops and Mac keyboards, hold Fn Ctrl+Shift+F Screen mode: normal, full screen, full screen with the bars hidden. Control on a Mac too, not ⌘ Esc Leave full screen (when in full screen) Ctrl+, Settings, including the PartCraft tab (see PartCraft settings) In a panel's number box, Ctrl+Z is the browser's own text undo. In the number box over a sketch dimension, it undoes in PartCraft. Copy, paste and duplicate They act on what you are working in: the selected geometry in an open sketch, the selected feature in the history, or the selected part in an assembly. Details in Copy, paste and duplicate. Key Action Ctrl+C Copy the selection Ctrl+X Cut: copy, then delete Ctrl+V Paste. In a sketch the copy follows the pointer: click where it goes Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place: same coordinates (in an assembly, the same place) Ctrl+D Duplicate: copy and paste in one step, leaving the clipboard alone Ctrl+A Select all (in a sketch) Esc Select none (and cancel a paste that follows the pointer) In a number box, these keys copy and paste text as usual. Selected text in a panel is copied by the browser. On the part (no sketch open) Key Action S Sketch on the selected plane or flat face; with nothing picked, choose where by clicking F Fit the part in the view M Measure, in every workspace and in an assembly; Esc clears the picks, Esc again closes it (see Measure distances, angles and mass) X Section view on or off, in every workspace and in an assembly; Esc closes it (see Look inside with a section view) Delete Delete the selected feature (asks if others use it) 0 … 9, . With a feature selected: type its main number (an Extrude's depth, a Hole's diameter, a pattern's count, a fillet's radius …), then Enter. Esc keeps the old one Backspace Delete the selected feature, when one is selected Esc Stop choosing a sketch plane, or clear the selection (including edges or faces picked with Shift) In the 3D view Click in the view first so it has the keyboard focus. Key Action ← → ↑ ↓ Turn the view 15 degrees Shift+← and the other arrows Pan + or = Zoom in − Zoom out Mouse Action Drag with the left button Turn (orbit) the view Drag with the right or middle button Pan Shift, Ctrl or ⌘ held while dragging with the left button Pan Roll the wheel Zoom at the pointer (away from you zooms in) Two-finger swipe on a trackpad Turn the view; with Shift, pan. Pinch zooms. Set by Scrolling in PartCraft settings With Turning the view: Middle button in PartCraft settings, the middle button turns the view, Shift with the middle button or the right button pans, and the left button only picks. | Click | Pick a face (it highlights whole), an edge or a plane | | Shift+click an edge or face | Collect several edges or faces | | Click empty space | Clear the pick | While picking edges or faces for a fillet, chamfer or shell Key or mouse Action Click an edge or face Add it, or drop it if it is already in Esc or Enter Finish picking (same as Done) F Fit Arrow keys, with the view focused Turn; with Shift, pan On the drawing sheet Key Action F Fit the sheet + / − Zoom (with the sheet focused) Shift+← and the other arrows Pan (with the sheet focused) ← → ↑ ↓ Nudge the selected GD&T symbol 1 mm Delete or Backspace Delete the selected symbol Esc Stop placing a symbol; else deselect; else back to the model Mouse Action Click on a view, with Datum, Frame, Finish or Detail chosen Place it there (snaps to the nearest line) Click a symbol Select it Drag a symbol Move it (the leader stays on its point) Roll the wheel Zoom at the pointer Drag with the right or middle button Pan In a sketch Key Action L Line R Rectangle C Circle A Arc (center, start, end) P Point N Centerline F Fillet a corner D Dimension B Spline (fit points) T Trim E Extend O Offset (B while offsetting: both sides) M Mirror S Select Q Switch the selected curves to construction and back Enter End a line chain or a spline Delete or Backspace Delete the selection Ctrl+A Select everything in the sketch Ctrl+C / Ctrl+X / Ctrl+V Copy, cut, paste geometry (paste follows the pointer) Ctrl+Shift+V Paste at the same coordinates Ctrl+D Duplicate the selection and place it with a click Esc Step back: cancel the number box or a paste, end the line chain, back to Select, clear the selection, then close the sketch Mouse Action Click Place points with a drawing tool; select with Select Shift+click Add to or take from the selection (Ctrl+click on Windows and Linux, ⌘+click on a Mac, do the same) Drag on empty space (Select) Window: selects everything fully inside Drag a point, line or circle edge (Select) Move it; relations hold Drag a dimension's number (Select) Move the label Double-click a dimension Change its value Double-click or right-click while drawing lines End the chain Drag with the right or middle button Pan Roll the wheel Zoom at the pointer In the number box Key Action Enter Apply the value Esc Close without changing Clicking away also applies the value. In the history list Key Action Tab Reach the list ↑ / ↓ Move between rows Enter or Space Select the row Alt+↑ / Alt+↓ Move the selected feature up or down, where the history allows F2 Change the feature's main number in its row; Enter sets it, Esc keeps the old one Mouse Action Click Select a plane or feature Double-click a sketch Open it Double-click a feature's number Change it in the row In an assembly Key Action J Start a joint: then click a flat face on each of two parts F Fit Esc Cancel the joint you started, or clear the selection Delete Remove the selected part or joint Backspace Remove the selected part or joint Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y Undo and redo in the assembly's own list Ctrl+D Duplicate the selected part (same part, beside the others) Ctrl+C, then Ctrl+V or Ctrl+Shift+V Copy a part, paste it beside the others or in the same place Mouse in the view: as in the 3D view above. Click a part to select it. Double-click a part in the list to edit it. In the Script workspace Key Action F5 Run the script (in the editor, the panel or the view) Ctrl+Enter Run the selected lines, or the line the cursor is on Tab / Shift+Tab Indent / outdent the selected lines. To leave the editor with the keyboard, press Esc, then Tab Ctrl+/ Comment the selected lines out, or back in Ctrl+F / Ctrl+H Find / find and replace in the script; Enter or F3 next match, Shift+Enter or Shift+F3 previous, Esc closes Ctrl+Shift+[ / Ctrl+Shift+] Fold / unfold the block the cursor is on (a function, if, for, while … end) Ctrl+Space List completions for the name you are typing (it also opens after two letters); ↑ / ↓ choose, Enter or Tab takes one, Esc closes Enter in the line under the console Run that one statement; ↑ and ↓ bring back earlier ones F Fit the view to what scripts show See Write and run scripts. Shortcuts the browser may take Ctrl+Y in Safari may open the history: use Ctrl+Shift+Z to redo. Ctrl+D is the browser's bookmark key: PartCraft takes it while the app has the focus, and leaves it alone while you type in a box. F11 and Ctrl++ / Ctrl+− are the browser's full screen and page zoom, not PartCraft's. Use Ctrl+Shift+F for full screen and the wheel for the model. Function keys on many laptops and Macs need the Fn key: press Fn+F1 for help, or use the Help button. Touch and trackpad gestures: Trackpad, touch and small screens."},{"slug":"settings","scope":"partcraft","title":"PartCraft settings","summary":"Choose which mouse button turns the view, make a trackpad swipe turn and pan the part, turn automatic relations while sketching on or off, and set how many threads the simulation solvers use.","section":"reference","order":15,"keywords":["settings","preferences","options","mouse","middle button","wheel button","orbit","rotate","turn","trackpad","touchpad","scroll","swipe","pinch","relations","constraints","auto constrain","automatic relations","snap","coincident","threads","solver threads","cores","processors","multi-threaded","faster solve","parallel"],"headings":[{"id":"turning-the-view","text":"Turning the view","level":2},{"id":"scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad","text":"Scrolling: mouse wheel or trackpad","level":2},{"id":"sketching-add-relations-while-drawing","text":"Sketching: add relations while drawing","level":2},{"id":"sketching-dimension-as-you-draw","text":"Sketching: dimension as you draw","level":2},{"id":"corner-indicator","text":"Corner indicator","level":2},{"id":"solver-threads","text":"Solver threads","level":2},{"id":"on-your-own-web-server","text":"On your own web server","level":3}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the app bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPartCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). PartCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset PartCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E puts everything back to the start.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turning-the-view\"\u003ETurning the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeft button\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): drag with the left button to turn the part; drag with the right or middle button to pan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle button\u003C/strong\u003E: press the wheel and drag to turn the part; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or ⌘) with the middle button, or drag with the right button, to pan. The left button only picks faces, edges and planes, so a click that moves a little never turns the part. Many CAD programs work this way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe wheel zooms at the pointer either way. In a sketch, the right and middle buttons pan as before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad\"\u003EScrolling: mouse wheel or trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWork it out\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): PartCraft watches the scrolling. Sideways or fine-grained scrolling, as a trackpad sends, turns the part; whole wheel notches zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: scrolling always zooms toward the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E: swipe with two fingers to turn the part, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and swipe to pan, pinch to zoom. In a sketch a two-finger swipe pans the sketch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pinch zooms in every choice. If &quot;Work it out&quot; guesses wrong for your mouse or trackpad, pick the one you have. More in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/trackpad-and-touch\" data-topic=\"partcraft/trackpad-and-touch\"\u003ETrackpad, touch and small screens\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sketching-add-relations-while-drawing\"\u003ESketching: add relations while drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdd relations while drawing\u003C/strong\u003E (on at the start): a click that snaps to a point makes the new point coincident with it, a click on a curve puts the point on that curve, and a line drawn nearly level or upright becomes horizontal or vertical. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/relations-dimensions\" data-topic=\"partcraft/relations-dimensions\"\u003ERelations and dimensions\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn it off when you want to place geometry near other geometry without tying it to it. The pointer still snaps to points and curves, but nothing is joined: lines drawn in a chain do not share their ends, so a drag can pull them apart. Add the relations you want yourself from the right panel. A closed profile still extrudes when its ends meet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe setting changes only what new drawing adds. Relations already in a sketch stay, and the Fillet tool still makes its tangent arc and radius.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sketching-dimension-as-you-draw\"\u003ESketching: dimension as you draw\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDimension as you draw\u003C/strong\u003E (on at the start): while a line, rectangle, circle or arc is being drawn, typing a number fills the size box beside the pointer (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E goes to the next box) and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E draws the shape at that size, with a driving dimension for each size you typed. Off: typing a number while you draw does nothing, and shapes are drawn by clicks only. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sketch-basics#type-the-size-as-you-draw\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sketch-basics\" data-hash=\"type-the-size-as-you-draw\"\u003EType the size as you draw\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-indicator\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat shows in the corner of the 3D view: \u003Cstrong\u003EView cube\u003C/strong\u003E (the start; top right — click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, drag its compass ring to turn the model), \u003Cstrong\u003EAxis icon\u003C/strong\u003E (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left that turns with the view: X red, Y green, Z blue) or \u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E. Neither shows while you sketch or on the drawing sheet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/view-cube\" data-topic=\"partcraft/view-cube\"\u003EThe view cube\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"solver-threads\"\u003ESolver threads\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow many threads the simulation solvers may use. \u003Cstrong\u003EAutomatic\u003C/strong\u003E (the start) takes one fewer than the computer&#039;s logical processors (at most 16), so the page stays responsive while a study solves; or pick a number. Large voxel studies, buckling, stress over time and flow solve faster on several threads (about 2× on 4 threads for 80,000 cubes in our timings, more on an idle computer); models under about 20,000 unknowns stay on one thread, because starting the threads costs more than they save. A threaded solve gives the same answer as one thread (to rounding: the sums are added in a different order). Each study&#039;s results list \u003Cstrong\u003EN threads\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003E1 thread\u003C/strong\u003E with the reason under \u003Cstrong\u003ESolver\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThreads need the page to be \u003Cstrong\u003Ecross-origin isolated\u003C/strong\u003E (the browser lets workers share memory only then). The lab page is; where it is not, the setting says so (&quot;This page is not cross-origin isolated, so solvers run on one thread whatever is chosen here.&quot;) and every solve uses one thread. Everything else works the same, and the answers are the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELaminar flow studies use the threads too (about 2.5× on 4 threads for 54,000 fluid cells, the same answer to the last digit). The quadratic-tetrahedron solver and heat transfer run on one thread: threading them was measured slower on our test computer (their solvers are limited by memory speed and by a step that cannot be split).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"on-your-own-web-server\"\u003EOn your own web server\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you run PartCraft from your own server, it works as served. For the solver threads, have the server send two headers with PartCraft&#039;s page:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin\nCross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn Apache, in the folder&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003E.htaccess\u003C/code\u003E (needs mod_headers):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EHeader set Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy &quot;same-origin&quot;\nHeader set Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy &quot;require-corp&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn nginx, in the \u003Ccode\u003Elocation\u003C/code\u003E that serves PartCraft:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eadd_header Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy &quot;same-origin&quot; always;\nadd_header Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy &quot;require-corp&quot; always;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReload the page: Settings › Solver threads then offers \u003Cstrong\u003EAutomatic\u003C/strong\u003E with your computer&#039;s count. The second header makes the browser refuse anything the page loads from another site that does not allow it, which is fine for PartCraft on its own (it loads everything from its own folder).\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the app bar or Ctrl+,. The first tab, PartCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). PartCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. Reset PartCraft settings puts everything back to the start. Turning the view Left button (the start): drag with the left button to turn the part; drag with the right or middle button to pan. Middle button: press the wheel and drag to turn the part; hold Shift (or Ctrl, or ⌘) with the middle button, or drag with the right button, to pan. The left button only picks faces, edges and planes, so a click that moves a little never turns the part. Many CAD programs work this way. The wheel zooms at the pointer either way. In a sketch, the right and middle buttons pan as before. Scrolling: mouse wheel or trackpad Work it out (the start): PartCraft watches the scrolling. Sideways or fine-grained scrolling, as a trackpad sends, turns the part; whole wheel notches zoom. Mouse wheel: scrolling always zooms toward the pointer. Trackpad: swipe with two fingers to turn the part, hold Shift and swipe to pan, pinch to zoom. In a sketch a two-finger swipe pans the sketch. A pinch zooms in every choice. If \"Work it out\" guesses wrong for your mouse or trackpad, pick the one you have. More in Trackpad, touch and small screens. Sketching: add relations while drawing Add relations while drawing (on at the start): a click that snaps to a point makes the new point coincident with it, a click on a curve puts the point on that curve, and a line drawn nearly level or upright becomes horizontal or vertical. See Relations and dimensions. Turn it off when you want to place geometry near other geometry without tying it to it. The pointer still snaps to points and curves, but nothing is joined: lines drawn in a chain do not share their ends, so a drag can pull them apart. Add the relations you want yourself from the right panel. A closed profile still extrudes when its ends meet. The setting changes only what new drawing adds. Relations already in a sketch stay, and the Fillet tool still makes its tangent arc and radius. Sketching: dimension as you draw Dimension as you draw (on at the start): while a line, rectangle, circle or arc is being drawn, typing a number fills the size box beside the pointer (Tab goes to the next box) and Enter draws the shape at that size, with a driving dimension for each size you typed. Off: typing a number while you draw does nothing, and shapes are drawn by clicks only. See Type the size as you draw. Corner indicator What shows in the corner of the 3D view: View cube (the start; top right — click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, drag its compass ring to turn the model), Axis icon (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left that turns with the view: X red, Y green, Z blue) or None. Neither shows while you sketch or on the drawing sheet. See The view cube. Solver threads How many threads the simulation solvers may use. Automatic (the start) takes one fewer than the computer's logical processors (at most 16), so the page stays responsive while a study solves; or pick a number. Large voxel studies, buckling, stress over time and flow solve faster on several threads (about 2× on 4 threads for 80,000 cubes in our timings, more on an idle computer); models under about 20,000 unknowns stay on one thread, because starting the threads costs more than they save. A threaded solve gives the same answer as one thread (to rounding: the sums are added in a different order). Each study's results list N threads or 1 thread with the reason under Solver. Threads need the page to be cross-origin isolated (the browser lets workers share memory only then). The lab page is; where it is not, the setting says so (\"This page is not cross-origin isolated, so solvers run on one thread whatever is chosen here.\") and every solve uses one thread. Everything else works the same, and the answers are the same. Laminar flow studies use the threads too (about 2.5× on 4 threads for 54,000 fluid cells, the same answer to the last digit). The quadratic-tetrahedron solver and heat transfer run on one thread: threading them was measured slower on our test computer (their solvers are limited by memory speed and by a step that cannot be split). On your own web server If you run PartCraft from your own server, it works as served. For the solver threads, have the server send two headers with PartCraft's page: Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp On Apache, in the folder's .htaccess (needs mod_headers): Header set Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy \"same-origin\" Header set Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy \"require-corp\" On nginx, in the location that serves PartCraft: add_header Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy \"same-origin\" always; add_header Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy \"require-corp\" always; Reload the page: Settings › Solver threads then offers Automatic with your computer's count. The second header makes the browser refuse anything the page loads from another site that does not allow it, which is fine for PartCraft on its own (it loads everything from its own folder)."},{"slug":"sketch-relations","scope":"partcraft","title":"Sketch relations and dimensions","summary":"Every relation and dimension a PartCraft sketch has, what to select for each, its symbol, and how it behaves when solving.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["relations","constraints","coincident","horizontal","vertical","parallel","perpendicular","tangent","equal","concentric","midpoint","fix","distance","horizontal distance","vertical distance","radius","diameter","angle","symbols","glyphs"],"headings":[{"id":"relations","text":"Relations","level":2},{"id":"dimensions","text":"Dimensions","level":2},{"id":"how-solving-behaves","text":"How solving behaves","level":2},{"id":"not-available-yet","text":"Not available yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ERelations fix how geometry sits relative to other geometry. Dimensions fix sizes. To add a relation, select items with the Select tool (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and choose its button in the right panel; to add a dimension, use the Dimension tool (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) or the dimension buttons in the right panel. How to work with them: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/relations-dimensions\" data-topic=\"partcraft/relations-dimensions\"\u003EFully define a sketch\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;Curve&quot; below means a circle or an arc. The origin counts as a point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"relations\"\u003ERelations\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESymbol\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESelect\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEffect\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E• Coincident\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E• (shown only when selected or in trouble)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe points meet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E• Coincident\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E•\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA point and a line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point lies on the line (or its extension)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E• Coincident\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E•\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA point and a curve\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point lies on the circle or arc\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EH Horizontal\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EH\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line, or two points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe line is level, or the two points are at the same height\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EV Vertical\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EV\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line, or two points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe line is upright, or the points are one above the other\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E∥ Parallel\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E∥\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe lines run the same way\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⊥ Perpendicular\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E⊥\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe lines meet at a right angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ET Tangent\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ET\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line and a curve\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe line touches the curve without crossing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ET Tangent\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ET\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo curves\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey touch, outside or inside, as drawn\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ET Tangent\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ET\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA spline and a line, arc or spline that meets one of its ends\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe spline leaves that end in the other curve&#039;s direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E= Equal\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E=\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E= Equal\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E=\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo curves\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame radius\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E◎ Concentric\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E◎\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo curves\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame center\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E◎ Concentric\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E◎\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA point and a curve\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point sits at the curve&#039;s center\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EM Midpoint\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EM\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA point and a line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point sits at the line&#039;s middle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E↔ Symmetric\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E↔\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo points and a line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe points are mirror images about the line (Mirror adds these)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⚓ Fix\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E⚓\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA point, a line, a curve or a spline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHolds it exactly where it is now (a spline: its points)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines here include centerlines. Drawing adds some relations for you: snapping to a point makes it coincident, snapping to a curve puts the point on it, nearly level or upright lines get H or V, and a rectangle comes with its four corners joined, two H and two V.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix\u003C/strong\u003E is a quick way to stop something moving, but the part then cannot follow a dimension change elsewhere. Prefer dimensions to the origin.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESelect\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeasures\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShown as\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts length\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDistance 40\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe straight distance between them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDistance 40\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA point and a line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe distance from the point to the line, square to it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDistance 40\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo parallel lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe gap between them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDistance 40\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHorizontal distance\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo points, or a line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe left-right distance only\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHorizontal distance 40\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVertical distance\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo points, or a line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe up-down distance only\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVertical distance 40\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA circle or arc\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts radius\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadius R10\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiameter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA circle or arc\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDiameter ⌀20\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo lines that are not parallel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe angle between them, 0 to 180 degrees\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle 30°\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith the Dimension tool, PartCraft picks the kind from what you click and where you place the number:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou click\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts length; for a slanted line, horizontal if the number goes above or below it, vertical if to the side\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDiameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne arc\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadius\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDistance; horizontal or vertical as for a slanted line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA point and a line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDistance from the point to the line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle, or the distance if they are parallel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA point and a circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing: &quot;Those items cannot be dimensioned together.&quot; Dimension to the circle&#039;s center point instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOffset\u003C/strong\u003E (shown as the distance) is made by the Offset tool: it holds a copied line parallel at that distance, or a copied arc or circle concentric with a radius that much bigger or smaller. The other pieces of the same offset carry an \u003Cstrong\u003EO\u003C/strong\u003E relation and follow the one Offset dimension. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sketch-tools\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sketch-tools\"\u003ESplines, trim, offset and other sketch tools\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDimension values are millimeters (or degrees), never negative. Radius and diameter must be more than zero. A value of 0 is allowed for distances: two points 0 apart are coincident.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-solving-behaves\"\u003EHow solving behaves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeast movement.\u003C/strong\u003E When a value changes, the solver moves the geometry as little as possible. A line whose length changes grows equally at both ends if nothing holds either end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo flipping.\u003C/strong\u003E Distances from a point to a line, horizontal and vertical distances, angles and tangency remember which side they were made on. Changing a value never flips the geometry to the other side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConflicts leave things alone.\u003C/strong\u003E If relations contradict each other, the sketch stays exactly as it was and the conflicting ones turn red.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERedundant relations\u003C/strong\u003E (implied by others) are named in the status bar (&quot;is already implied. Delete it.&quot;) and the feature still builds, with a warning.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETangent joints.\u003C/strong\u003E Where a fillet arc meets its lines at a shared end point, tangency is solved in a form that stays well defined there, so sketch fillets do not read as over defined.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-available-yet\"\u003ENot available yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECollinear, coradial and pierce relations; a point on a spline; driven (reference) dimensions; ordinate and arc-length dimensions; equations between dimensions. Use Parallel plus a 0 distance for collinear lines.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Relations fix how geometry sits relative to other geometry. Dimensions fix sizes. To add a relation, select items with the Select tool (S) and choose its button in the right panel; to add a dimension, use the Dimension tool (D) or the dimension buttons in the right panel. How to work with them: Fully define a sketch. \"Curve\" below means a circle or an arc. The origin counts as a point. Relations Button Symbol Select Effect • Coincident • (shown only when selected or in trouble) Two points The points meet • Coincident • A point and a line The point lies on the line (or its extension) • Coincident • A point and a curve The point lies on the circle or arc H Horizontal H A line, or two points The line is level, or the two points are at the same height V Vertical V A line, or two points The line is upright, or the points are one above the other ∥ Parallel ∥ Two lines The lines run the same way ⊥ Perpendicular ⊥ Two lines The lines meet at a right angle T Tangent T A line and a curve The line touches the curve without crossing T Tangent T Two curves They touch, outside or inside, as drawn T Tangent T A spline and a line, arc or spline that meets one of its ends The spline leaves that end in the other curve's direction = Equal = Two lines Same length = Equal = Two curves Same radius ◎ Concentric ◎ Two curves Same center ◎ Concentric ◎ A point and a curve The point sits at the curve's center M Midpoint M A point and a line The point sits at the line's middle ↔ Symmetric ↔ Two points and a line The points are mirror images about the line (Mirror adds these) ⚓ Fix ⚓ A point, a line, a curve or a spline Holds it exactly where it is now (a spline: its points) Lines here include centerlines. Drawing adds some relations for you: snapping to a point makes it coincident, snapping to a curve puts the point on it, nearly level or upright lines get H or V, and a rectangle comes with its four corners joined, two H and two V. Fix is a quick way to stop something moving, but the part then cannot follow a dimension change elsewhere. Prefer dimensions to the origin. Dimensions Button Select Measures Shown as Distance A line Its length Distance 40 Distance Two points The straight distance between them Distance 40 Distance A point and a line The distance from the point to the line, square to it Distance 40 Distance Two parallel lines The gap between them Distance 40 Horizontal distance Two points, or a line The left-right distance only Horizontal distance 40 Vertical distance Two points, or a line The up-down distance only Vertical distance 40 Radius A circle or arc Its radius Radius R10 Diameter A circle or arc Its diameter Diameter ⌀20 Angle Two lines that are not parallel The angle between them, 0 to 180 degrees Angle 30° With the Dimension tool, PartCraft picks the kind from what you click and where you place the number: You click You get One line Its length; for a slanted line, horizontal if the number goes above or below it, vertical if to the side One circle Diameter One arc Radius Two points Distance; horizontal or vertical as for a slanted line A point and a line Distance from the point to the line Two lines Angle, or the distance if they are parallel A point and a circle Nothing: \"Those items cannot be dimensioned together.\" Dimension to the circle's center point instead Offset (shown as the distance) is made by the Offset tool: it holds a copied line parallel at that distance, or a copied arc or circle concentric with a radius that much bigger or smaller. The other pieces of the same offset carry an O relation and follow the one Offset dimension. See Splines, trim, offset and other sketch tools. Dimension values are millimeters (or degrees), never negative. Radius and diameter must be more than zero. A value of 0 is allowed for distances: two points 0 apart are coincident. How solving behaves Least movement. When a value changes, the solver moves the geometry as little as possible. A line whose length changes grows equally at both ends if nothing holds either end. No flipping. Distances from a point to a line, horizontal and vertical distances, angles and tangency remember which side they were made on. Changing a value never flips the geometry to the other side. Conflicts leave things alone. If relations contradict each other, the sketch stays exactly as it was and the conflicting ones turn red. Redundant relations (implied by others) are named in the status bar (\"is already implied. Delete it.\") and the feature still builds, with a warning. Tangent joints. Where a fillet arc meets its lines at a shared end point, tangency is solved in a form that stays well defined there, so sketch fillets do not read as over defined. Not available yet Collinear, coradial and pierce relations; a point on a spline; driven (reference) dimensions; ordinate and arc-length dimensions; equations between dimensions. Use Parallel plus a 0 distance for collinear lines."},{"slug":"features","scope":"partcraft","title":"Features and their options","summary":"Every feature in the toolbar with its settings, defaults and limits, from extrude to fillet, shell and imported STEP solids, the actions every feature shares, and the part settings.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["features","not built","extrude","cut","revolve","revolved cut","hole","counterbore","linear pattern","circular pattern","mirror","fillet","chamfer","shell","import","options","defaults","settings","resolution","curves","facets","suppress","rollback"],"headings":[{"id":"sketch","text":"Sketch","level":2},{"id":"extrude-and-cut","text":"Extrude and Cut","level":2},{"id":"revolve-and-revolved-cut","text":"Revolve and revolved cut","level":2},{"id":"hole","text":"Hole","level":2},{"id":"linear-pattern","text":"Linear pattern","level":2},{"id":"circular-pattern","text":"Circular pattern","level":2},{"id":"mirror","text":"Mirror","level":2},{"id":"fillet-and-chamfer","text":"Fillet and Chamfer","level":2},{"id":"shell","text":"Shell","level":2},{"id":"imported-solid","text":"Imported solid","level":2},{"id":"what-every-feature-has","text":"What every feature has","level":2},{"id":"states-in-the-history","text":"States in the history","level":2},{"id":"part-settings","text":"Part settings","level":2},{"id":"curves-resolution","text":"Curves resolution","level":3},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EFeatures are listed in the history in the order they were made and rebuilt top to bottom. Each one is named after its kind with a number: Sketch1, Extrude1, Revolve1, Hole1, LPattern1, CirPattern1, Mirror1, Fillet1, Chamfer1, Shell1, Imported1.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft builds every feature with the mesh kernel. Fillet and Chamfer work on straight edges between two flat faces, and Shell opens flat faces. Imported solids need an exact kernel, which this version does not have: they are not built yet, and their section below describes them as a part made in an earlier build keeps them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sketch\"\u003ESketch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlane\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFront, Top or Right plane, or a flat face. Shown, not changed: to move a sketch, make a new one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EState\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFully defined, Under defined (with the degrees of freedom), Over defined, or Empty\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGeometry, Relations\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many curves, points and relations\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDriving dimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery dimension of the sketch, editable\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EButtons: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit sketch\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport as DXF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"extrude-and-cut\"\u003EExtrude and Cut\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E make the same feature, starting with different settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExtrude starts at\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECut starts at\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOperation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoss (add material), Cut (remove material), Intersect (keep the overlap), New body (not built yet in this version)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoss\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnd\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlind (a depth), Mid-plane (both ways), Through all\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlind\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThrough all\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDepth (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbove 0, up to 1,000,000\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReverse direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff / on\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProfile: every closed loop of solid lines, circles and arcs in the sketch. Loops inside loops are holes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThrough all works only for cuts. It reaches right through the part on the chosen side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA cut goes into the material, away from the sketch plane&#039;s outward side; Reverse flips it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"revolve-and-revolved-cut\"\u003ERevolve and revolved cut\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOperation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoss, Cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoss (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolve\u003C/span\u003E), Cut (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolved cut\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbove 0, up to 360\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E360\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAxis\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirst centerline, any line or centerline of the sketch, or an axis feature lying in the sketch&#039;s plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirst centerline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe profile must stay on one side of the axis. It may touch it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA revolve&#039;s axis can be used by Circular patterns and by assembly joints.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hole\"\u003EHole\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESimple, Counterbore\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESimple\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDiameter (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbove 0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThrough all\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn / off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDepth (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhen Through all is off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECounterbore ⌀\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWider than the hole\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E11\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECounterbore depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShallower than a blind hole\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReverse direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff / on\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne hole at every loose point of the sketch (points made with the Point tool). The panel shows how many.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHoles drill square to the sketch plane, into the material.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach hole&#039;s axis can be used by assembly joints (&quot;Hole1, hole 2&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"linear-pattern\"\u003ELinear pattern\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeatures to copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAny solid features above it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selected feature, else the last solid feature\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDirection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EX axis, Y axis, Z axis; a line of an earlier sketch; the axis of a revolve; a picked edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EX axis (or the edge picked when you added it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpacing (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAny number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E20\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1 to 1000, including the original\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReverse direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff / on\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"circular-pattern\"\u003ECircular pattern\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeatures to copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs for Linear\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs for Linear\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAxis\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs for Direction above\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe last revolve&#039;s axis, else the Y axis (or the edge picked)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbove 0, up to 360. 360 spaces the copies equally all round; less spreads them over the angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E360\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1 to 1000, including the original\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReverse direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff / on\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mirror\"\u003EMirror\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMirror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe whole body (as built so far), or chosen features\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selected feature, else the whole body\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMirror plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFront, Top or Right plane; a flat face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight plane, or the flat face picked when you added it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fillet-and-chamfer\"\u003EFillet and Chamfer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn this version the mesh kernel builds them on straight edges between two flat faces (outside or inside corners), with one radius. A round edge says &quot;Fillet: in this version only straight edges between two flat faces (the mesh kernel builds them). Round or bevel curved edges in the sketch.&quot;; where rounded edges meet at a corner they are mitred, and the feature warns &quot;Fillet by the mesh kernel: where rounded edges meet they are mitred, not blended.&quot; Chamfer types (equal distance, two distances, distance and angle, with \u003Cstrong\u003EFlip sides\u003C/strong\u003E) all work. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\" data-topic=\"partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\"\u003ERound, bevel and hollow\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadius (mm) (Fillet)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbove 0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDistance (mm) (Chamfer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbove 0, the same on both faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe picked edges, each listed by the two faces it joins (&quot;Extrude1 end face × Extrude1 side&quot;), with \u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe edges picked when you added it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick edges before choosing the button (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click for several), or choose it with nothing picked and click edges afterwards.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick edges\u003C/span\u003E (or double-click the feature) changes the edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEdges are kept by the two faces they join, so they survive size changes upstream.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shell\"\u003EShell\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe mesh kernel builds it. Only flat faces can be opened (&quot;Shell: in this version it opens flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).&quot;); walls of different thicknesses are not built yet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\" data-topic=\"partcraft/fillet-chamfer-shell\"\u003ERound, bevel and hollow\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWall (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbove 0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWalls grow outward (the faces stay inside)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff: walls go inward. On: walls go outward\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe faces removed, at least one, with \u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe faces picked when you added it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick faces\u003C/span\u003E changes the open faces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"imported-solid\"\u003EImported solid\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMade by opening a STEP file in an earlier build: opening STEP files is not built yet in this version, and an imported solid in an older part stays in the history marked as not built. It shows the file name and the size of the STEP text kept in the part file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUp in the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ up (most CAD), Y up\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ up\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPatterns and mirrors can copy an imported solid, but not a fillet, chamfer or shell.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-every-feature-has\"\u003EWhat every feature has\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the bottom of each feature&#039;s panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESuppress\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnsuppress\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwitch it off or on. Features that need it are not built while it is off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoll back to here\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuild only the features above this one. New features go in at the rollback bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove up\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReorder, when nothing it uses would end up below it. Grayed with the reason otherwise\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete it, and (after asking) every feature that uses it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERename it (up to 60 characters)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFeatures with a sketch also list \u003Cstrong\u003ESketch dimensions\u003C/strong\u003E, editable in place.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"states-in-the-history\"\u003EStates in the history\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EState\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShown as\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENormal\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAll well\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E⚠\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilt, with something to check: &quot;Over defined: 1 relation is not needed.&quot; or &quot;Nothing is left of the part after this.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EError\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E⚠ error, red\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECould not be built; the message says why\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E⚠ not built\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENeeds a feature that failed, is suppressed or rolled back, or needs an exact kernel, which this version does not have (&quot;Sweep: not built yet. …&quot;)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESuppressed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eoff\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwitched off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERolled back\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Egrayed, below the rollback bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built until you roll forward\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"part-settings\"\u003EPart settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick empty space so nothing is selected. The right panel shows \u003Cstrong\u003EPart\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E: the part&#039;s name, used for file names and the drawing&#039;s title block (up to 120 characters).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E: see below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"curves-resolution\"\u003ECurves resolution\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe mesh kernel builds the solid itself from flat facets, so the view, STL, 3MF, the faceted STEP, the volume and the drawing views all follow \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E: how many facets per full turn.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFacets per turn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EVolume of a round shape\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECoarse (32 per turn)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E32\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbout 0.64 % under the true value\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENormal (64 per turn)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E64\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbout 0.16 % under (the default)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFine (128 per turn)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E128\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbout 0.04 % under\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVery fine (256 per turn)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E256\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbout 0.01 % under\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFiner curves give smoother exports and views, and slower rebuilds. The setting is saved in the part file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVariable-radius fillets, fillets and chamfers on round edges, shells opening curved faces, draft on curved faces, sweep, loft, rib, imported STEP solids, new bodies, tapers, &quot;up to&quot; a face or vertex, and references on the solid&#039;s faces and edges need an exact kernel, which this version does not have. Also not built: tapped and countersunk holes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Features are listed in the history in the order they were made and rebuilt top to bottom. Each one is named after its kind with a number: Sketch1, Extrude1, Revolve1, Hole1, LPattern1, CirPattern1, Mirror1, Fillet1, Chamfer1, Shell1, Imported1. PartCraft builds every feature with the mesh kernel. Fillet and Chamfer work on straight edges between two flat faces, and Shell opens flat faces. Imported solids need an exact kernel, which this version does not have: they are not built yet, and their section below describes them as a part made in an earlier build keeps them. See The mesh kernel and What is not built yet. Sketch Setting Notes Plane Front, Top or Right plane, or a flat face. Shown, not changed: to move a sketch, make a new one State Fully defined, Under defined (with the degrees of freedom), Over defined, or Empty Geometry, Relations How many curves, points and relations Driving dimensions Every dimension of the sketch, editable Buttons: Edit sketch, Export as DXF. Extrude and Cut Extrude and Cut make the same feature, starting with different settings. Setting Choices Extrude starts at Cut starts at Operation Boss (add material), Cut (remove material), Intersect (keep the overlap), New body (not built yet in this version) Boss Cut End Blind (a depth), Mid-plane (both ways), Through all Blind Through all Depth (mm) Above 0, up to 1,000,000 10 10 Reverse direction Off / on Off Off Profile: every closed loop of solid lines, circles and arcs in the sketch. Loops inside loops are holes. Through all works only for cuts. It reaches right through the part on the chosen side. A cut goes into the material, away from the sketch plane's outward side; Reverse flips it. Revolve and revolved cut Setting Choices Default Operation Boss, Cut Boss (Revolve), Cut (More › Revolved cut) Angle (°) Above 0, up to 360 360 Axis First centerline, any line or centerline of the sketch, or an axis feature lying in the sketch's plane First centerline The profile must stay on one side of the axis. It may touch it. A revolve's axis can be used by Circular patterns and by assembly joints. Hole Setting Choices Default Type Simple, Counterbore Simple Diameter (mm) Above 0 6 Through all On / off On Depth (mm) When Through all is off 10 Counterbore ⌀ Wider than the hole 11 Counterbore depth Shallower than a blind hole 6 Reverse direction Off / on Off One hole at every loose point of the sketch (points made with the Point tool). The panel shows how many. Holes drill square to the sketch plane, into the material. Each hole's axis can be used by assembly joints (\"Hole1, hole 2\"). Linear pattern Setting Choices Default Features to copy Any solid features above it The selected feature, else the last solid feature Direction X axis, Y axis, Z axis; a line of an earlier sketch; the axis of a revolve; a picked edge X axis (or the edge picked when you added it) Spacing (mm) Any number 20 Count 1 to 1000, including the original 3 Reverse direction Off / on Off Circular pattern Setting Choices Default Features to copy As for Linear As for Linear Axis As for Direction above The last revolve's axis, else the Y axis (or the edge picked) Angle (°) Above 0, up to 360. 360 spaces the copies equally all round; less spreads them over the angle 360 Count 1 to 1000, including the original 6 Reverse direction Off / on Off Mirror Setting Choices Default Mirror The whole body (as built so far), or chosen features The selected feature, else the whole body Mirror plane Front, Top or Right plane; a flat face Right plane, or the flat face picked when you added it Fillet and Chamfer In this version the mesh kernel builds them on straight edges between two flat faces (outside or inside corners), with one radius. A round edge says \"Fillet: in this version only straight edges between two flat faces (the mesh kernel builds them). Round or bevel curved edges in the sketch.\"; where rounded edges meet at a corner they are mitred, and the feature warns \"Fillet by the mesh kernel: where rounded edges meet they are mitred, not blended.\" Chamfer types (equal distance, two distances, distance and angle, with Flip sides) all work. See Round, bevel and hollow. Setting Choices Default Radius (mm) (Fillet) Above 0 2 Distance (mm) (Chamfer) Above 0, the same on both faces 1 Edges The picked edges, each listed by the two faces it joins (\"Extrude1 end face × Extrude1 side\"), with Remove The edges picked when you added it Pick edges before choosing the button (Shift+click for several), or choose it with nothing picked and click edges afterwards. Pick edges (or double-click the feature) changes the edges. Edges are kept by the two faces they join, so they survive size changes upstream. Shell The mesh kernel builds it. Only flat faces can be opened (\"Shell: in this version it opens flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).\"); walls of different thicknesses are not built yet. See Round, bevel and hollow. Setting Choices Default Wall (mm) Above 0 2 Walls grow outward (the faces stay inside) Off: walls go inward. On: walls go outward Off Open faces The faces removed, at least one, with Remove The faces picked when you added it Pick faces changes the open faces. Imported solid Made by opening a STEP file in an earlier build: opening STEP files is not built yet in this version, and an imported solid in an older part stays in the history marked as not built. It shows the file name and the size of the STEP text kept in the part file. Setting Choices Default Up in the file Z up (most CAD), Y up Z up Patterns and mirrors can copy an imported solid, but not a fillet, chamfer or shell. What every feature has At the bottom of each feature's panel: Button Does Suppress / Unsuppress Switch it off or on. Features that need it are not built while it is off Roll back to here Build only the features above this one. New features go in at the rollback bar Move up / Move down Reorder, when nothing it uses would end up below it. Grayed with the reason otherwise Delete Delete it, and (after asking) every feature that uses it Name Rename it (up to 60 characters) Features with a sketch also list Sketch dimensions, editable in place. States in the history State Shown as Meaning Built Normal All well Warning ⚠ Built, with something to check: \"Over defined: 1 relation is not needed.\" or \"Nothing is left of the part after this.\" Error ⚠ error, red Could not be built; the message says why Not built ⚠ not built Needs a feature that failed, is suppressed or rolled back, or needs an exact kernel, which this version does not have (\"Sweep: not built yet. …\") Suppressed off Switched off Rolled back grayed, below the rollback bar Not built until you roll forward Part settings Click empty space so nothing is selected. The right panel shows Part: Name: the part's name, used for file names and the drawing's title block (up to 120 characters). Curves: see below. Curves resolution The mesh kernel builds the solid itself from flat facets, so the view, STL, 3MF, the faceted STEP, the volume and the drawing views all follow Curves: how many facets per full turn. Setting Facets per turn Volume of a round shape Coarse (32 per turn) 32 About 0.64 % under the true value Normal (64 per turn) 64 About 0.16 % under (the default) Fine (128 per turn) 128 About 0.04 % under Very fine (256 per turn) 256 About 0.01 % under Finer curves give smoother exports and views, and slower rebuilds. The setting is saved in the part file. Not built yet Variable-radius fillets, fillets and chamfers on round edges, shells opening curved faces, draft on curved faces, sweep, loft, rib, imported STEP solids, new bodies, tapers, \"up to\" a face or vertex, and references on the solid's faces and edges need an exact kernel, which this version does not have. Also not built: tapped and countersunk holes. See What is not built yet."},{"slug":"file-formats","scope":"partcraft","title":"File formats","summary":"Every file PartCraft reads and writes, with extensions, units, axes and what each one keeps or loses.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["formats","file types","extensions","ifc","bim","partcraft.json","partcraft-asm.json","stl","3mf","step","stp","ap242","dxf","r12","pdf","gltf","obj","iges","units","z up"],"headings":[{"id":"the-partcraft-files","text":"The PartCraft files","level":2},{"id":"facets-and-exactness","text":"Facets and exactness","level":2},{"id":"not-supported-in-this-build","text":"Not supported in this build","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExtension\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOpen\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESave or export\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUnits\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUp axis\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKeeps\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPartCraft part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EY\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe full history: sketches, relations, dimensions, features, suppression, rollback, curves setting, drawing settings and GD&amp;T, STEP text imported in an earlier build\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPartCraft assembly\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft-asm.json\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E in an assembly\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EY\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery part (embedded), the copies and their positions, the joints\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTL (binary)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.stl\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTL, for 3D printing\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe solid&#039;s triangles only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3MF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.3mf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3MF, for 3D printing\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm (stated in the file)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe solid as one mesh object\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTEP (AP203, AP214, AP242)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.stp\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet in this version (needs an exact kernel)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTEP AP242, exact\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP242 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet in this version (needs an exact kernel)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTEP AP214, exact\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP214 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet in this version (needs an exact kernel)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTEP AP242, faceted\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EY\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA closed solid of flat faces: round surfaces as facets. The STEP to use in this version\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTEP AP214, faceted\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP214 (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EY\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same solid in the older AP214 schema, for programs that read only that\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC 4\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC 4 (with every property)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe shape as triangles, the material, every parameter, the title block, quantities; an assembly&#039;s parts as types and its copies placed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDXF R12, sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF of the sketch or flat face\u003C/span\u003E with a sketch selected\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch&#039;s own x, y\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines, true arcs and circles, points; layers PROFILE, CONSTRUCTION, POINTS\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDXF R12, face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF of the sketch or flat face\u003C/span\u003E with a flat face picked\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFace&#039;s own x, y\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe face&#039;s outlines as closed polylines (round edges as facets)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPDF drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.pdf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing (PDF): views, section, detail, GD&amp;T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm on an A3 sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFront, top, right, isometric, section and detail views drawn from facets, hidden lines, overall sizes, GD&amp;T symbols, title block\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-partcraft-files\"\u003EThe PartCraft files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth are plain text (JSON). The solid is never stored: PartCraft rebuilds it from the history, so files stay small and always match the history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach file has a version number. This build writes part files as version 2 and assembly files as version 1, and opens every older version. A file from a newer PartCraft is refused with a message naming both versions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery value is checked when a file opens. Names lose control characters; numbers out of range, references to missing features and unknown feature kinds are refused with a plain reason.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDo not edit these files by hand unless you know the format. A file that does not pass the checks will not open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA part with a large STEP solid imported in an earlier build can pass 50 MB, the most PartCraft opens as a part file (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/troubleshooting#file-problems\" data-topic=\"partcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"file-problems\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"facets-and-exactness\"\u003EFacets and exactness\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESTL, 3MF and the face DXF are always made of facets, at the \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E setting. The sketch DXF has true arcs and circles, because it comes straight from the sketch. This version builds every part with the mesh kernel, so the STEP export and the drawing&#039;s views (sections included) are faceted too. Exact STEP and true curves need an exact kernel: not built yet in this version.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-supported-in-this-build\"\u003ENot supported in this build\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStatus\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EglTF, OBJ, IGES, Parasolid, ACIS (in or out)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTL or 3MF import\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built. Export DXF; every CAD program opens R12 DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly STEP and the drawing of an assembly\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet (needs an exact kernel). STL, 3MF and the bill of materials export from an assembly\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOther programs&#039; native part and assembly files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Format Extension Open Save or export Units Up axis Keeps PartCraft part .partcraft.json Yes Save mm Y The full history: sketches, relations, dimensions, features, suppression, rollback, curves setting, drawing settings and GD&T, STEP text imported in an earlier build PartCraft assembly .partcraft-asm.json Yes Save in an assembly mm Y Every part (embedded), the copies and their positions, the joints STL (binary) .stl No STL, for 3D printing mm Z The solid's triangles only 3MF .3mf No 3MF, for 3D printing mm (stated in the file) Z The solid as one mesh object STEP (AP203, AP214, AP242) .step, .stp Not built yet in this version (needs an exact kernel) mm STEP AP242, exact .step STEP AP242 (exact solid) mm Z Not built yet in this version (needs an exact kernel) STEP AP214, exact .step STEP AP214 (exact solid) mm Z Not built yet in this version (needs an exact kernel) STEP AP242, faceted .step STEP (faceted, mesh kernel) mm Y A closed solid of flat faces: round surfaces as facets. The STEP to use in this version STEP AP214, faceted .step STEP AP214 (faceted, mesh kernel) mm Y The same solid in the older AP214 schema, for programs that read only that IFC 4 .ifc No IFC 4 (with every property) mm Z The shape as triangles, the material, every parameter, the title block, quantities; an assembly's parts as types and its copies placed DXF R12, sketch .dxf No DXF of the sketch or flat face with a sketch selected mm Sketch's own x, y Lines, true arcs and circles, points; layers PROFILE, CONSTRUCTION, POINTS DXF R12, face .dxf No DXF of the sketch or flat face with a flat face picked mm Face's own x, y The face's outlines as closed polylines (round edges as facets) PDF drawing .pdf No Drawing (PDF): views, section, detail, GD&T mm on an A3 sheet Front, top, right, isometric, section and detail views drawn from facets, hidden lines, overall sizes, GD&T symbols, title block The PartCraft files Both are plain text (JSON). The solid is never stored: PartCraft rebuilds it from the history, so files stay small and always match the history. Each file has a version number. This build writes part files as version 2 and assembly files as version 1, and opens every older version. A file from a newer PartCraft is refused with a message naming both versions. Every value is checked when a file opens. Names lose control characters; numbers out of range, references to missing features and unknown feature kinds are refused with a plain reason. Do not edit these files by hand unless you know the format. A file that does not pass the checks will not open. A part with a large STEP solid imported in an earlier build can pass 50 MB, the most PartCraft opens as a part file (see Troubleshooting). Facets and exactness STL, 3MF and the face DXF are always made of facets, at the Curves setting. The sketch DXF has true arcs and circles, because it comes straight from the sketch. This version builds every part with the mesh kernel, so the STEP export and the drawing's views (sections included) are faceted too. Exact STEP and true curves need an exact kernel: not built yet in this version. Not supported in this build Format Status glTF, OBJ, IGES, Parasolid, ACIS (in or out) Not built STL or 3MF import Not built DWG Not built. Export DXF; every CAD program opens R12 DXF Assembly STEP and the drawing of an assembly Not built yet (needs an exact kernel). STL, 3MF and the bill of materials export from an assembly Other programs' native part and assembly files Not built See What is not built yet."},{"slug":"trackpad-and-touch","scope":"partcraft","title":"Trackpad, touch and small screens","summary":"How to turn, pan and zoom with a Mac or laptop trackpad, what works on a touch screen and what still needs a keyboard, and how the screen rearranges on a phone or narrow window.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["trackpad","touchpad","magic mouse","mac","touch","tablet","ipad","phone","pinch","two finger","right click","secondary click","gestures","small screen","mobile"],"headings":[{"id":"trackpad","text":"Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"touch-screens","text":"Touch screens","level":2},{"id":"small-screens-and-phones","text":"Small screens and phones","level":2},{"id":"browsers","text":"Browsers","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft is made for a mouse with a wheel and a middle button, but a trackpad works well once you know the gestures, and a touch screen works for looking at parts and simple sketching.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trackpad\"\u003ETrackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the part (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick and drag with one finger (or drag with three fingers, if you turned on three-finger drag)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or ⌘) and click-drag, or click-drag with two fingers pressed (a secondary-click drag)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the part, swiping\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwipe with two fingers; with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, the swipe pans. Needs \u003Cstrong\u003EScrolling\u003C/strong\u003E set to &quot;Work it out&quot; (the start) or &quot;Trackpad&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom (3D and sketch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch. Zoom goes toward the pointer. With \u003Cstrong\u003EScrolling\u003C/strong\u003E set to &quot;Mouse wheel&quot;, a two-finger scroll zooms too\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick with two fingers, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan in a sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwipe with two fingers (&quot;Work it out&quot; or &quot;Trackpad&quot;), or click-drag with two fingers pressed. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+drag does not pan in a sketch (it draws a selection window)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd to a selection in a sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click or ⌘+click\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn on secondary click in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings, Trackpad, Point &amp; Click\u003C/strong\u003E if two-finger click does nothing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the part (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress the touchpad (or tap and hold) and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag, or drag with the right button area pressed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the part, swiping\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwipe with two fingers; with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, the swipe pans. Needs \u003Cstrong\u003EScrolling\u003C/strong\u003E set to &quot;Work it out&quot; (the start) or &quot;Trackpad&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom (3D and sketch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch where the touchpad supports it. Zoom goes toward the pointer. With \u003Cstrong\u003EScrolling\u003C/strong\u003E set to &quot;Mouse wheel&quot;, a two-finger scroll zooms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger tap, or the lower-right corner of the touchpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan in a sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwipe with two fingers (&quot;Work it out&quot; or &quot;Trackpad&quot;), or drag with the right button area pressed. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+drag does not pan in a sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd to a selection in a sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScrolling\u003C/strong\u003E is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"partcraft/settings\"\u003EPartCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E: &quot;Work it out&quot; (the start) treats sideways or fine-grained scrolling as a trackpad, &quot;Trackpad&quot; always does, &quot;Mouse wheel&quot; never does. A pinch zooms in all three.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe arrow keys turn the 3D view and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the arrows pans, once you have clicked in the view: handy on a trackpad. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E zoom.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"touch-screens\"\u003ETouch screens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft reads touch input, so tablets, touch laptops and phones work, with limits.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETouch keys\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnce you touch the screen, a small bar appears at the lower right of the view with the keys a finger lacks. It goes away when you use a mouse.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E: the Esc key. It cancels what you started or steps back, exactly as the key does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E (in a sketch, with Select): while it is on, each tap adds an item to the selection or takes it out, like \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click. Tap it again to go back to one item at a time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E (while you draw a line chain, a spline or a polygon): finishes it, like a right-click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHolding a finger still for about half a second does the same for one tap: with a drawing tool it finishes what you draw, and with Select it adds the item under your finger to the selection or takes it out. Points and lines are easier to hit with a finger: PartCraft looks about twice as far round a tap as round a click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn the 3D view\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne finger: turn the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo fingers: pinch to zoom, move together to pan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETap: pick a face, an edge or a plane. There is no hover highlight before you tap.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn a sketch\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETap with a drawing tool to place each point: tap the start, then tap the end. Snapping happens where you lift your finger.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo fingers pinch and pan. Putting a second finger down cancels a drag the first finger began.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo end a line chain, tap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E, hold your finger still, or tap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E again (or another tool) in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith Select, tap to select; drag a point or line to move it; drag on empty space for a window. A second tap replaces the selection unless \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E is on (or you hold the finger still on the second item).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo change a dimension, use \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E beside it in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drawing sheet\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap to place and select symbols, and drag a symbol to move it. Pinch to zoom, move two fingers to pan, or drag one finger across empty paper. A second finger stops a symbol you were dragging, and it goes back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhat still needs a keyboard\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETyping numbers: the screen keyboard comes up in number boxes and dimension fields.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeyboard shortcuts for tools: use the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"small-screens-and-phones\"\u003ESmall screens and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt 1280 pixels or wider, the toolbar fits on one row.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBetween about 760 and 1100 pixels, the side panels get narrower.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBelow about 760 pixels (a phone, or a narrow window), the screen stacks: toolbar (swipe it sideways for more buttons), the 3D view, the status bar, then the properties panel and the history. Scroll the page to reach the panels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMenus (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E) open across the width of the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn iPhone there is no full screen mode in the browser; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the screen-mode button switch only between normal and the bars hidden.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA phone is fine for opening a part, turning it and checking its facts. Real modeling needs a bigger screen and a mouse or trackpad.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browsers\"\u003EBrowsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECurrent Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari run PartCraft on Windows, macOS and Linux, and on tablets and phones, as long as WebGL and WebAssembly are available. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"PartCraft is made for a mouse with a wheel and a middle button, but a trackpad works well once you know the gestures, and a touch screen works for looking at parts and simple sketching. Trackpad To Do this Turn the part (3D) Click and drag with one finger (or drag with three fingers, if you turned on three-finger drag) Pan (3D) Hold Shift (or ⌘) and click-drag, or click-drag with two fingers pressed (a secondary-click drag) Turn the part, swiping Swipe with two fingers; with Shift, the swipe pans. Needs Scrolling set to \"Work it out\" (the start) or \"Trackpad\" Zoom (3D and sketch) Pinch. Zoom goes toward the pointer. With Scrolling set to \"Mouse wheel\", a two-finger scroll zooms too Right-click Click with two fingers, or Ctrl+click Pan in a sketch Swipe with two fingers (\"Work it out\" or \"Trackpad\"), or click-drag with two fingers pressed. Shift+drag does not pan in a sketch (it draws a selection window) Add to a selection in a sketch Shift+click or ⌘+click Turn on secondary click in System Settings, Trackpad, Point & Click if two-finger click does nothing. To Do this Turn the part (3D) Press the touchpad (or tap and hold) and drag Pan (3D) Hold Shift or Ctrl and drag, or drag with the right button area pressed Turn the part, swiping Swipe with two fingers; with Shift, the swipe pans. Needs Scrolling set to \"Work it out\" (the start) or \"Trackpad\" Zoom (3D and sketch) Pinch where the touchpad supports it. Zoom goes toward the pointer. With Scrolling set to \"Mouse wheel\", a two-finger scroll zooms Right-click Two-finger tap, or the lower-right corner of the touchpad Pan in a sketch Swipe with two fingers (\"Work it out\" or \"Trackpad\"), or drag with the right button area pressed. Shift+drag does not pan in a sketch Add to a selection in a sketch Shift+click or Ctrl+click Scrolling is in PartCraft settings: \"Work it out\" (the start) treats sideways or fine-grained scrolling as a trackpad, \"Trackpad\" always does, \"Mouse wheel\" never does. A pinch zooms in all three. The arrow keys turn the 3D view and Shift with the arrows pans, once you have clicked in the view: handy on a trackpad. + and − zoom. Touch screens PartCraft reads touch input, so tablets, touch laptops and phones work, with limits. Touch keys Once you touch the screen, a small bar appears at the lower right of the view with the keys a finger lacks. It goes away when you use a mouse. Esc: the Esc key. It cancels what you started or steps back, exactly as the key does. Add (in a sketch, with Select): while it is on, each tap adds an item to the selection or takes it out, like Shift+click. Tap it again to go back to one item at a time. Done (while you draw a line chain, a spline or a polygon): finishes it, like a right-click. Holding a finger still for about half a second does the same for one tap: with a drawing tool it finishes what you draw, and with Select it adds the item under your finger to the selection or takes it out. Points and lines are easier to hit with a finger: PartCraft looks about twice as far round a tap as round a click. In the 3D view One finger: turn the part. Two fingers: pinch to zoom, move together to pan. Tap: pick a face, an edge or a plane. There is no hover highlight before you tap. In a sketch Tap with a drawing tool to place each point: tap the start, then tap the end. Snapping happens where you lift your finger. Two fingers pinch and pan. Putting a second finger down cancels a drag the first finger began. To end a line chain, tap Done, hold your finger still, or tap Line again (or another tool) in the toolbar. With Select, tap to select; drag a point or line to move it; drag on empty space for a window. A second tap replaces the selection unless Add is on (or you hold the finger still on the second item). To change a dimension, use Edit beside it in the right panel. The drawing sheet Tap to place and select symbols, and drag a symbol to move it. Pinch to zoom, move two fingers to pan, or drag one finger across empty paper. A second finger stops a symbol you were dragging, and it goes back. What still needs a keyboard Typing numbers: the screen keyboard comes up in number boxes and dimension fields. Keyboard shortcuts for tools: use the toolbar. Small screens and phones At 1280 pixels or wider, the toolbar fits on one row. Between about 760 and 1100 pixels, the side panels get narrower. Below about 760 pixels (a phone, or a narrow window), the screen stacks: toolbar (swipe it sideways for more buttons), the 3D view, the status bar, then the properties panel and the history. Scroll the page to reach the panels. Menus (Export, Views) open across the width of the screen. On iPhone there is no full screen mode in the browser; Ctrl+Shift+F and the screen-mode button switch only between normal and the bars hidden. A phone is fine for opening a part, turning it and checking its facts. Real modeling needs a bigger screen and a mouse or trackpad. Browsers Current Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari run PartCraft on Windows, macOS and Linux, and on tablets and phones, as long as WebGL and WebAssembly are available. See Browsers and computers."},{"slug":"scripts-and-cli","scope":"partcraft","title":"Scripts and the command line","summary":"The PartCraft script format (.pcscript) that writes a whole part as readable text, and the partcraft command line tool that builds, changes, rolls back and checks parts without the screen.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["script","pcscript","text","command line","cli","terminal","batch","automate","check","lint","build","export","version control","diff","parametric script"],"headings":[{"id":"in-the-app","text":"In the app","level":2},{"id":"the-script-format","text":"The script format","level":2},{"id":"the-command-line-tool","text":"The command line tool","level":2},{"id":"the-check","text":"The check","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECareful modeling is more about discipline than about buttons. A part written as text can be read, reviewed, compared line by line and rebuilt by a script. PartCraft goes both ways: any part can be written as a script, and a script builds the part.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-app\"\u003EIn the app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E tab, under \u003Cstrong\u003EScript\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as script\u003C/span\u003E downloads the open part as \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.pcscript\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen script\u003C/span\u003E builds a part from a \u003Ccode\u003E.pcscript\u003C/code\u003E file. A mistake is reported with its line number and nothing is replaced.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-script-format\"\u003EThe script format\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epcscript 1\npart &quot;Bracket&quot;\n  resolution 64\n\nparam width = 120\n  note Plate width\nparam thick = 8\nparam bolt = 8.5\nparam margin = bolt * 1.5\n\nsketch Plate\n  on top\n  point a 0, 0\n  point b 120, 0\n  point c 120, 60\n  point d 0, 60\n  line bottom a b\n  line right b c\n  line top c d\n  line left d a\n  coincident a O\n  horizontal bottom\n  horizontal top\n  vertical left\n  vertical right\n  distance bottom = width\n  distance left 60\n\nextrude Base\n  sketch Plate\n  depth = thick\n\nsketch HoleCentres\n  on face Base end\n  point h1 12, 12\n  hdistance O h1 = margin\n  vdistance O h1 = margin\n\nhole Bolts\n  sketch HoleCentres\n  d = bolt\n  through\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rules:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA line at the left edge starts a statement: \u003Ccode\u003Epcscript\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Epart\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eparam\u003C/code\u003E, a feature type (\u003Ccode\u003Esketch\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eextrude\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Erevolve\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ehole\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elpattern\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecpattern\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emirror\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Efillet\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Echamfer\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eshell\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eimport\u003C/code\u003E and the newer types) followed by its name, or \u003Ccode\u003Erollback &lt;feature&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIndented lines are the settings of the statement above. A setting on its own (\u003Ccode\u003Ethrough\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ereverse\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esuppressed\u003C/code\u003E) means yes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA number setting is a number, or \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E and an expression, which makes an equation: \u003Ccode\u003Edepth = thick\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a sketch: \u003Ccode\u003Eon\u003C/code\u003E names the plane (\u003Ccode\u003Efront\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etop\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eright\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eface Base end\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eface Revolve1 side l3\u003C/code\u003E); geometry is \u003Ccode\u003Epoint &lt;name&gt; x, y\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eline &lt;name&gt; &lt;point&gt; &lt;point&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecircle &lt;name&gt; &lt;centre&gt; &lt;radius&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Earc &lt;name&gt; &lt;centre&gt; &lt;start&gt; &lt;end&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (counter-clockwise) or \u003Ccode\u003Espline &lt;name&gt; fit|control &lt;points&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; relations are their type and the names they join (\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E is the origin); a dimension ends with its value or \u003Ccode\u003E= expression\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOptional settings of a geometry or relation line go in square brackets at the end, separated by \u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E[construction]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E[side -1; label 5, 3]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E starts a comment (at the start of a line or after a space). Names with spaces go in &quot;double quotes&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFeature ids count from f1 in the order written. Edge and face references use them, for example \u003Ccode\u003Eedge f2.side:l3 f2.end [at 50, 0, 10]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESettings the format has no words for are written \u003Ccode\u003Ejson\u003C/code\u003E and a value; they are read back as they are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaved from the app, the script includes every setting, so it opens as the same part. An imported STEP solid is written inside the script from the app, and as a separate \u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E file named by \u003Ccode\u003Estep-file\u003C/code\u003E from the command line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-command-line-tool\"\u003EThe command line tool\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tool runs on a computer with Node.js 24, from a copy of the CadCraft source: \u003Ccode\u003Enode tools/partcraft-cli/partcraft.mjs &lt;command&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. It is a developer tool and uses the same modules as the app. Parts are \u003Ccode\u003E.partcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E files or \u003Ccode\u003E.pcscript\u003C/code\u003E scripts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECommand\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Einfo &lt;part&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeatures, parameters, rollback bar, the built solid&#039;s volume and size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eparams &lt;part&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery parameter and equation with its value\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eset &lt;part&gt; width=150 Base.depth==thick\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EForward: change parameters, link numbers (\u003Ccode\u003E==\u003C/code\u003E or an expression), set plain numbers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrive &lt;part&gt; Base.depth=12\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackward: solve the parameter behind a driven number (\u003Ccode\u003E--prefer width\u003C/code\u003E picks one)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ebuild &lt;part&gt; --out x.step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrites \u003Ccode\u003E.step\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.stl\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.3mf\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pcscript\u003C/code\u003E or a macro (\u003Ccode\u003E.swb\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ebuild &lt;part&gt; --table sizes.csv --out-dir sizes\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne file per design table row\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Escript &lt;in.pcscript&gt; -o part.json\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScript to part\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eto-script &lt;part&gt; -o part.pcscript\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPart to script\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Erollback &lt;part&gt; [&lt;feature&gt;]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eforward &lt;part&gt; [--end]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the rollback bar in the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Echeck &lt;part&gt; [--strict]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe discipline check (below)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ebuild\u003C/code\u003E also takes \u003Ccode\u003E--set name=value\u003C/code\u003E (for this build only) and \u003Ccode\u003E--upto &lt;feature&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. Every command takes \u003Ccode\u003E--json\u003C/code\u003E. Exit codes: 0 fine, 1 a problem was found, 2 the command was typed wrong. A script is never rewritten in place: give \u003Ccode\u003E-o\u003C/code\u003E to save a changed copy.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-check\"\u003EThe check\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Echeck\u003C/code\u003E reports what makes a part hard to change later:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFinding\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECounts as\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA sketch that is over defined (conflicting or repeated relations)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eerror\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA feature that does not build\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eerror\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA parameter or equation with an error, or parameters that use each other\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eerror\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA sketch that is under defined, with its degrees of freedom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumbers typed straight into features and dimensions that no parameter drives\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA parameter nothing uses\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA suppressed feature; the rollback bar not at the end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E--strict\u003C/code\u003E makes every warning an error, so a build script can refuse a part that is not fully driven.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Ccode\u003E.swb\u003C/code\u003E macro output is for the part modeler most shops use: run inside that program, it builds the part there. It has not been run in that program here (it is not available on our servers), so treat it as untested.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Careful modeling is more about discipline than about buttons. A part written as text can be read, reviewed, compared line by line and rebuilt by a script. PartCraft goes both ways: any part can be written as a script, and a script builds the part. In the app On the Parameters tab, under Script: Save as script downloads the open part as \u003Cname\u003E.pcscript. Open script builds a part from a .pcscript file. A mistake is reported with its line number and nothing is replaced. The script format pcscript 1 part \"Bracket\" resolution 64 param width = 120 note Plate width param thick = 8 param bolt = 8.5 param margin = bolt * 1.5 sketch Plate on top point a 0, 0 point b 120, 0 point c 120, 60 point d 0, 60 line bottom a b line right b c line top c d line left d a coincident a O horizontal bottom horizontal top vertical left vertical right distance bottom = width distance left 60 extrude Base sketch Plate depth = thick sketch HoleCentres on face Base end point h1 12, 12 hdistance O h1 = margin vdistance O h1 = margin hole Bolts sketch HoleCentres d = bolt through The rules: A line at the left edge starts a statement: pcscript, part, param, a feature type (sketch, extrude, revolve, hole, lpattern, cpattern, mirror, fillet, chamfer, shell, import and the newer types) followed by its name, or rollback \u003Cfeature\u003E. Indented lines are the settings of the statement above. A setting on its own (through, reverse, suppressed) means yes. A number setting is a number, or = and an expression, which makes an equation: depth = thick. In a sketch: on names the plane (front, top, right, face Base end, face Revolve1 side l3); geometry is point \u003Cname\u003E x, y, line \u003Cname\u003E \u003Cpoint\u003E \u003Cpoint\u003E, circle \u003Cname\u003E \u003Ccentre\u003E \u003Cradius\u003E, arc \u003Cname\u003E \u003Ccentre\u003E \u003Cstart\u003E \u003Cend\u003E (counter-clockwise) or spline \u003Cname\u003E fit|control \u003Cpoints\u003E; relations are their type and the names they join (O is the origin); a dimension ends with its value or = expression. Optional settings of a geometry or relation line go in square brackets at the end, separated by ;: [construction], [side -1; label 5, 3]. # starts a comment (at the start of a line or after a space). Names with spaces go in \"double quotes\". Feature ids count from f1 in the order written. Edge and face references use them, for example edge f2.side:l3 f2.end [at 50, 0, 10]. Settings the format has no words for are written json and a value; they are read back as they are. Saved from the app, the script includes every setting, so it opens as the same part. An imported STEP solid is written inside the script from the app, and as a separate .step file named by step-file from the command line. The command line tool The tool runs on a computer with Node.js 24, from a copy of the CadCraft source: node tools/partcraft-cli/partcraft.mjs \u003Ccommand\u003E. It is a developer tool and uses the same modules as the app. Parts are .partcraft.json files or .pcscript scripts. Command What it does info \u003Cpart\u003E Features, parameters, rollback bar, the built solid's volume and size params \u003Cpart\u003E Every parameter and equation with its value set \u003Cpart\u003E width=150 Base.depth==thick Forward: change parameters, link numbers (== or an expression), set plain numbers drive \u003Cpart\u003E Base.depth=12 Backward: solve the parameter behind a driven number (--prefer width picks one) build \u003Cpart\u003E --out x.step Writes .step, .stl, .3mf, .json, .pcscript or a macro (.swb) build \u003Cpart\u003E --table sizes.csv --out-dir sizes One file per design table row script \u003Cin.pcscript\u003E -o part.json Script to part to-script \u003Cpart\u003E -o part.pcscript Part to script rollback \u003Cpart\u003E [\u003Cfeature\u003E], forward \u003Cpart\u003E [--end] Move the rollback bar in the file check \u003Cpart\u003E [--strict] The discipline check (below) build also takes --set name=value (for this build only) and --upto \u003Cfeature\u003E. Every command takes --json. Exit codes: 0 fine, 1 a problem was found, 2 the command was typed wrong. A script is never rewritten in place: give -o to save a changed copy. The check check reports what makes a part hard to change later: Finding Counts as A sketch that is over defined (conflicting or repeated relations) error A feature that does not build error A parameter or equation with an error, or parameters that use each other error A sketch that is under defined, with its degrees of freedom warning Numbers typed straight into features and dimensions that no parameter drives warning A parameter nothing uses warning A suppressed feature; the rollback bar not at the end warning --strict makes every warning an error, so a build script can refuse a part that is not fully driven. The .swb macro output is for the part modeler most shops use: run inside that program, it builds the part there. It has not been run in that program here (it is not available on our servers), so treat it as untested."},{"slug":"craftscript-language","scope":"partcraft","title":"The CraftScript language","summary":"CraftScript in one page: matrices, ranges and indexing, operators, control flow, functions, structs and cells, comments, echo, errors and the step budget, and how it differs from MATLAB.","section":"reference","order":61,"keywords":["CraftScript","language","syntax","matrix","array","vector","tensor","index","end","colon","range","operator","element-wise","transpose","if","for","while","switch","try","catch","function","anonymous function","struct","cell","comment","echo","semicolon","MATLAB","Octave","port"],"headings":[{"id":"values","text":"Values","level":2},{"id":"arrays-and-indexing","text":"Arrays and indexing","level":2},{"id":"operators","text":"Operators","level":2},{"id":"control-flow-and-functions","text":"Control flow and functions","level":2},{"id":"echo-comments-output","text":"Echo, comments, output","level":2},{"id":"errors-and-limits","text":"Errors and limits","level":2},{"id":"not-like-matlab","text":"Not like MATLAB","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECraftScript is a matrix language written to read like MATLAB, so most classic numerical codes (the 88-line topology optimization code, for example) run with only small changes. It runs in the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript\"\u003EScript workspace\u003C/a\u003E and from the command line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"values\"\u003EValues\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExample\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber, array\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ea = 3\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ev = [1 2 3]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA = [1 2; 3 4]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery number is an array (a 1×1 one). Arrays are stored column by column, up to 8 dimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELogical\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA &gt; 2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etrue\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArrays of true/false; usable as indices\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&#039;beam&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;beam&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESparse matrix\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esparse(i, j, v, n, n)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFor stiffness matrices\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMesh, field, model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EhexGrid(…)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Epart()\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eload(&#039;part.stl&#039;)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat \u003Ccode\u003Eshow\u003C/code\u003E draws\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStruct\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Es.E = 210e3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENamed fields\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECell\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ec = {1, &#039;two&#039;, [3 4]}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA list of any values\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFunction handle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ef = @(x) x.^2\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arrays-and-indexing\"\u003EArrays and indexing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERanges: \u003Ccode\u003E1:5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0:0.1:1\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003Elinspace(a, b, n)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ezeros(n, m)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eones\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eeye\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Erand\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIndexing starts at 1: \u003Ccode\u003EA(2, 3)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ev(end)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA(:, 1)\u003C/code\u003E (a whole column), \u003Ccode\u003EA(:)\u003C/code\u003E (everything as one column), \u003Ccode\u003Ev(v &gt; 0)\u003C/code\u003E (logical indexing).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAssigning past the end grows the array, as in MATLAB: \u003Ccode\u003Ev(10) = 1\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"operators\"\u003EOperators\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E+ - * / \\ ^\u003C/code\u003E are matrix operations (\u003Ccode\u003EA \\ b\u003C/code\u003E solves); \u003Ccode\u003E.* ./ .^\u003C/code\u003E work element by element. \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E transposes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EComparisons \u003Ccode\u003E== ~= &lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;=\u003C/code\u003E; logic \u003Ccode\u003E&amp; | ~\u003C/code\u003E (element by element) and \u003Ccode\u003E&amp;&amp; ||\u003C/code\u003E (short-circuit).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"control-flow-and-functions\"\u003EControl flow and functions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efor k = 1:10\n  if mod(k, 2) == 0\n    continue\n  elseif k &gt; 7\n    break\n  end\nend\nwhile err &gt; 1e-6, … end\nswitch kind\n  case &#039;steel&#039;\n    E = 210e3;\n  otherwise\n    E = 70e3;\nend\ntry\n  x = A \\ b;\ncatch e\n  disp(e.message)\nend\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFunctions go in a script after its commands, or in their own script tab named after the function:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efunction [k, m] = beam(E, I, L)\n  k = 3 * E * I / L^3;\n  m = 0;\nend\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA name is looked up in this order: a variable, a script in this part with that name, a library function, a built-in function.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"echo-comments-output\"\u003EEcho, comments, output\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA statement without \u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E prints its value; \u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E keeps it quiet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E%\u003C/code\u003E starts a comment; \u003Ccode\u003E%{\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E%}\u003C/code\u003E on lines of their own comment out the lines between them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edisp(x)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Efprintf(&#039;%g\\n&#039;, x)\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Edisplay(x)\u003C/code\u003E print to the console.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"errors-and-limits\"\u003EErrors and limits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EErrors say what went wrong in plain words and give the line (and the chain of function calls).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach run has a step budget (Run options in the panel); an endless loop stops with a message, or press Stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArrays hold up to 134 million values each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-like-matlab\"\u003ENot like MATLAB\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlot commands put their options in the call: \u003Ccode\u003Eplot(x, y, &#039;title&#039;, &#039;Error&#039;, &#039;xlabel&#039;, &#039;Step&#039;)\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003Efigure\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ehold\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eaxis\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etitle(…)\u003C/code\u003E and similar are accepted and do nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Einput\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Ekeyboard\u003C/code\u003E are not supported: a script cannot stop to ask a question.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThere are no toolboxes; the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-library\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-library\"\u003Eanalysis library\u003C/a\u003E and the built-in functions are what there is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"CraftScript is a matrix language written to read like MATLAB, so most classic numerical codes (the 88-line topology optimization code, for example) run with only small changes. It runs in the Script workspace and from the command line. Values Type Example Notes Number, array a = 3, v = [1 2 3], A = [1 2; 3 4] Every number is an array (a 1×1 one). Arrays are stored column by column, up to 8 dimensions Logical A \u003E 2, true Arrays of true/false; usable as indices Text 'beam', \"beam\" Sparse matrix sparse(i, j, v, n, n) For stiffness matrices Mesh, field, model hexGrid(…), part(), load('part.stl') What show draws Struct s.E = 210e3 Named fields Cell c = {1, 'two', [3 4]} A list of any values Function handle f = @(x) x.^2 Arrays and indexing Ranges: 1:5, 0:0.1:1. linspace(a, b, n), zeros(n, m), ones, eye, rand. Indexing starts at 1: A(2, 3), v(end), A(:, 1) (a whole column), A(:) (everything as one column), v(v \u003E 0) (logical indexing). Assigning past the end grows the array, as in MATLAB: v(10) = 1. Operators + - * / \\ ^ are matrix operations (A \\ b solves); .* ./ .^ work element by element. ' transposes. Comparisons == ~= \u003C \u003C= \u003E \u003E=; logic & | ~ (element by element) and && || (short-circuit). Control flow and functions for k = 1:10 if mod(k, 2) == 0 continue elseif k \u003E 7 break end end while err \u003E 1e-6, … end switch kind case 'steel' E = 210e3; otherwise E = 70e3; end try x = A \\ b; catch e disp(e.message) end Functions go in a script after its commands, or in their own script tab named after the function: function [k, m] = beam(E, I, L) k = 3 * E * I / L^3; m = 0; end A name is looked up in this order: a variable, a script in this part with that name, a library function, a built-in function. Echo, comments, output A statement without ; prints its value; ; keeps it quiet. % starts a comment; %{ and %} on lines of their own comment out the lines between them. disp(x), fprintf('%g\\n', x) and display(x) print to the console. Errors and limits Errors say what went wrong in plain words and give the line (and the chain of function calls). Each run has a step budget (Run options in the panel); an endless loop stops with a message, or press Stop. Arrays hold up to 134 million values each. Not like MATLAB Plot commands put their options in the call: plot(x, y, 'title', 'Error', 'xlabel', 'Step'). figure, hold, axis, title(…) and similar are accepted and do nothing. input and keyboard are not supported: a script cannot stop to ask a question. There are no toolboxes; the analysis library and the built-in functions are what there is."},{"slug":"craftscript-lua","scope":"partcraft","title":"Scripts in Lua","summary":"Write Script workspace scripts in Lua 5.4: the cs table of built-in functions, arrays as values with indexing and arithmetic, the shared ws workspace, printing and showing results, and what is switched off.","section":"reference","order":62,"keywords":["Lua","Lua 5.4","script","language","cs table","ws","workspace","userdata","tensor","array","matrix","print","display","sandbox"],"headings":[{"id":"the-essentials","text":"The essentials","level":2},{"id":"call-the-analysis-library","text":"Call the analysis library","level":2},{"id":"switched-off","text":"Switched off","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELua is the second language of the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript\"\u003EScript workspace\u003C/a\u003E. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003ELua\u003C/strong\u003E in the language box above the editor (a \u003Ccode\u003E.lua\u003C/code\u003E file opens in Lua by itself). Lua and CraftScript share the same variables and the same built-in functions, so a Lua script can use what a CraftScript script made, and the other way round.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-essentials\"\u003EThe essentials\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E-- every built-in function is in the cs table\nlocal K = cs.zeros(3, 3)\nK:set(1, 1, 4)                 -- 1-based, like CraftScript\nprint(K(1, 1), #K)             -- an element; the number of elements\n\n-- the workspace: the same variables every script sees\nws.total = K(1, 1) * 2\nprint(ws.total)\n\n-- several results from one function\nlocal V, D = cs.call(&#039;eig&#039;, 2, cs.tensor({{2, 1}, {1, 2}}))\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ecs.&lt;name&gt;(…)\u003C/code\u003E calls any built-in function and returns its first result; \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.call(name, n, …)\u003C/code\u003E returns \u003Ccode\u003En\u003C/code\u003E results.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ecs.tensor({1, 2, 3})\u003C/code\u003E makes a row, \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.tensor({{1, 2}, {3, 4}})\u003C/code\u003E a matrix.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArrays, sparse matrices, meshes and fields are values with methods: \u003Ccode\u003Et(i, j)\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Et:get(i, j)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Et:set(i, j, v)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Et:shape()\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E#t\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Et:T()\u003C/code\u003E (transpose), \u003Ccode\u003Et:totable()\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003E+ - * / ^\u003C/code\u003E mean what they mean in CraftScript (\u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E is the matrix product); \u003Ccode\u003Et:times(u)\u003C/code\u003E multiplies element by element.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA number that is a 1×1 array arrives as a plain Lua number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ews.name\u003C/code\u003E reads and writes the shared workspace. Global variables your script leaves behind also land there when it ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eprint(…)\u003C/code\u003E writes to the console; \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.show(f)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.plot(y)\u003C/code\u003E and the other display functions work as in CraftScript.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"call-the-analysis-library\"\u003ECall the analysis library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe analysis library&#039;s functions (the Library tab) and the CraftScript functions your part&#039;s own scripts define are reached through \u003Ccode\u003Ecs\u003C/code\u003E too:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Elocal t = cs.box_mesh(100, 10, 10, 5, &#039;tet10&#039;)\nlocal bc = cs.struct({ fixedFaces = 5, loads = { cs.struct({ face = 6, force = cs.tensor({ 0, -100, 0 }) }) } })\nlocal r = cs.static_tet(t, 200000, 0.3, bc)\nprint(r.maxDisp)\nlocal nodes, tris = cs.call(&#039;face_nodes&#039;, 2, t, 6)   -- two outputs\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey are found before the script starts, so write the name in the script: \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.static_tet(\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.call(&#039;static_tet&#039;, …)\u003C/code\u003E. A name put together while the script runs only reaches the built-in functions. A struct built with \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.struct({ … })\u003C/code\u003E is a CraftScript struct, and a Lua list of them (\u003Ccode\u003E{ cs.struct(…), … }\u003C/code\u003E) is a cell, which is what the library&#039;s settings expect. If something goes wrong inside a library function, the message names the function and its line, and points at your line that called it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"switched-off\"\u003ESwitched off\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EScripts cannot reach files, the network or the page: \u003Ccode\u003Eio\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eos\u003C/code\u003E (except the clock), \u003Ccode\u003Erequire\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edofile\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eloadfile\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Edebug\u003C/code\u003E are removed. Use the Script workspace&#039;s Open and the variables to bring data in. The step budget and Stop work as for CraftScript.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Lua is the second language of the Script workspace. Choose Lua in the language box above the editor (a .lua file opens in Lua by itself). Lua and CraftScript share the same variables and the same built-in functions, so a Lua script can use what a CraftScript script made, and the other way round. The essentials -- every built-in function is in the cs table local K = cs.zeros(3, 3) K:set(1, 1, 4) -- 1-based, like CraftScript print(K(1, 1), #K) -- an element; the number of elements -- the workspace: the same variables every script sees ws.total = K(1, 1) * 2 print(ws.total) -- several results from one function local V, D = cs.call('eig', 2, cs.tensor({{2, 1}, {1, 2}})) cs.\u003Cname\u003E(…) calls any built-in function and returns its first result; cs.call(name, n, …) returns n results. cs.tensor({1, 2, 3}) makes a row, cs.tensor({{1, 2}, {3, 4}}) a matrix. Arrays, sparse matrices, meshes and fields are values with methods: t(i, j) or t:get(i, j), t:set(i, j, v), t:shape(), #t, t:T() (transpose), t:totable(). + - * / ^ mean what they mean in CraftScript (* is the matrix product); t:times(u) multiplies element by element. A number that is a 1×1 array arrives as a plain Lua number. ws.name reads and writes the shared workspace. Global variables your script leaves behind also land there when it ends. print(…) writes to the console; cs.show(f), cs.plot(y) and the other display functions work as in CraftScript. Call the analysis library The analysis library's functions (the Library tab) and the CraftScript functions your part's own scripts define are reached through cs too: local t = cs.box_mesh(100, 10, 10, 5, 'tet10') local bc = cs.struct({ fixedFaces = 5, loads = { cs.struct({ face = 6, force = cs.tensor({ 0, -100, 0 }) }) } }) local r = cs.static_tet(t, 200000, 0.3, bc) print(r.maxDisp) local nodes, tris = cs.call('face_nodes', 2, t, 6) -- two outputs They are found before the script starts, so write the name in the script: cs.static_tet( or cs.call('static_tet', …). A name put together while the script runs only reaches the built-in functions. A struct built with cs.struct({ … }) is a CraftScript struct, and a Lua list of them ({ cs.struct(…), … }) is a cell, which is what the library's settings expect. If something goes wrong inside a library function, the message names the function and its line, and points at your line that called it. Switched off Scripts cannot reach files, the network or the page: io, os (except the clock), require, dofile, loadfile and debug are removed. Use the Script workspace's Open and the variables to bring data in. The step budget and Stop work as for CraftScript."},{"slug":"craftscript-python","scope":"partcraft","title":"Scripts in Python","summary":"Write Script workspace scripts in Python 3: the cs object of built-in functions, arrays as numpy-like values with 0-based indexing, the shared workspace, printing, what is not there yet, and how Python is sealed off like CraftScript and Lua.","section":"reference","order":63,"keywords":["Python","Python 3","CPython","WebAssembly","sealed","safe","script","language","cs","ws","workspace","numpy","array","matrix","indexing","print","display","trust"],"headings":[{"id":"python-is-sealed-off","text":"Python is sealed off","level":2},{"id":"the-essentials","text":"The essentials","level":2},{"id":"call-the-analysis-library","text":"Call the analysis library","level":2},{"id":"not-there-yet","text":"Not there yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPython is the third language of the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript\"\u003EScript workspace\u003C/a\u003E. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003EPython\u003C/strong\u003E in the language box above the editor (a \u003Ccode\u003E.py\u003C/code\u003E file opens in Python by itself). Python, Lua and CraftScript share the same variables and the same built-in functions, so a Python script can use what a CraftScript script made, and the other way round.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first Python run in a visit loads Python itself (CPython 3.14, about 7 MB) from this site, which takes a few seconds. Python is not part of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E yet: run one Python script while online first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"python-is-sealed-off\"\u003EPython is sealed off\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPython scripts are sealed off like CraftScript and Lua scripts: they reach the part, the variables and the built-in functions, and nothing else. Each run is a fresh Python in its own sandbox with a small file tree in memory (its standard library and a \u003Ccode\u003E/tmp\u003C/code\u003E folder that is gone after the run). It cannot reach the network, your files or the rest of the page, in the browser and on the command line alike. A script from someone else can still use a lot of time or memory: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-essentials\"\u003EThe essentials\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA = cs.magic(3)              # every built-in function is cs.&lt;name&gt;\nB = A @ A.T                  # @ is the matrix product, .T the transpose\nC = A * 2 + 1                # + - * / ** are elementwise, as in numpy\nprint(A[0, 1], A[-1, -1])    # 0-based; negative counts from the end\nrow = A[1]                   # one index on a matrix: a row\nbig = A[A &gt; 4]               # a logical mask picks elements\nA[0, 0] = -5                 # assigning into an array\nV, D = cs.call(&#039;eig&#039;, 2, A)  # several results: cs.call(name, how many, …)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA 1 × 1 array arrives as a plain number; other arrays, sparse matrices, meshes, fields and models are \u003Cstrong\u003EValues\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA Value has \u003Ccode\u003Eshape\u003C/code\u003E (a tuple), \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etype\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Enumel()\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecopy()\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etolist()\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elen()\u003C/code\u003E (its rows), and \u003Ccode\u003Efloat()\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eint()\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Ebool()\u003C/code\u003E for a single element.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EComparisons (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E==\u003C/code\u003E, …) are elementwise, as in numpy: they give a logical Value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELists of numbers passed to a built-in function become arrays: \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.sum([1, 2, 3])\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.tensor([[1, 2], [3, 4]])\u003C/code\u003E makes a matrix.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA dict becomes a struct and a list of mixed things a cell, and back again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery global your script makes (a number, a Value, a dict, a list…) lands in the \u003Cstrong\u003EVariables\u003C/strong\u003E tab when the run ends, even after an error. Names starting with \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E, functions, classes and modules stay in Python. \u003Ccode\u003Ews.name\u003C/code\u003E reads or sets a variable directly, and \u003Ccode\u003Ews_names()\u003C/code\u003E lists them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eprint(…)\u003C/code\u003E writes to the console. \u003Ccode\u003Edisplay(…)\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.display\u003C/code\u003E) shows a mesh, a field or a plot, as in CraftScript.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"call-the-analysis-library\"\u003ECall the analysis library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELibrary functions are called like built-in ones: \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.box_mesh(20, 10, 10, 5, &#039;hex&#039;)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecs.call(&#039;modal_tet&#039;, 2, …)\u003C/code\u003E. Write the name in the script as it is: PartCraft finds the library functions a script uses before it runs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-there-yet\"\u003ENot there yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Enumpy, scipy and other packages: only Python&#039;s own standard library (\u003Ccode\u003Emath\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ejson\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eitertools\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edecimal\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Estatistics\u003C/code\u003E…) is here, without its windows, terminal and database parts (\u003Ccode\u003Etkinter\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecurses\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esqlite3\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESockets and anything else that would reach outside: Python has no network here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etime.sleep\u003C/code\u003E returns at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFolding in the editor, and calling a Python function from a CraftScript script.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA run checks the instruction budget and Stop every 1000 lines; a long calculation inside one built-in function finishes first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Python is the third language of the Script workspace. Choose Python in the language box above the editor (a .py file opens in Python by itself). Python, Lua and CraftScript share the same variables and the same built-in functions, so a Python script can use what a CraftScript script made, and the other way round. The first Python run in a visit loads Python itself (CPython 3.14, about 7 MB) from this site, which takes a few seconds. Python is not part of Save for offline yet: run one Python script while online first. Python is sealed off Python scripts are sealed off like CraftScript and Lua scripts: they reach the part, the variables and the built-in functions, and nothing else. Each run is a fresh Python in its own sandbox with a small file tree in memory (its standard library and a /tmp folder that is gone after the run). It cannot reach the network, your files or the rest of the page, in the browser and on the command line alike. A script from someone else can still use a lot of time or memory: Stop ends it. The essentials A = cs.magic(3) # every built-in function is cs.\u003Cname\u003E B = A @ A.T # @ is the matrix product, .T the transpose C = A * 2 + 1 # + - * / ** are elementwise, as in numpy print(A[0, 1], A[-1, -1]) # 0-based; negative counts from the end row = A[1] # one index on a matrix: a row big = A[A \u003E 4] # a logical mask picks elements A[0, 0] = -5 # assigning into an array V, D = cs.call('eig', 2, A) # several results: cs.call(name, how many, …) A 1 × 1 array arrives as a plain number; other arrays, sparse matrices, meshes, fields and models are Values. A Value has shape (a tuple), T, type, numel(), copy(), tolist(), len() (its rows), and float(), int() and bool() for a single element. Comparisons (\u003C, ==, …) are elementwise, as in numpy: they give a logical Value. Lists of numbers passed to a built-in function become arrays: cs.sum([1, 2, 3]). cs.tensor([[1, 2], [3, 4]]) makes a matrix. A dict becomes a struct and a list of mixed things a cell, and back again. Every global your script makes (a number, a Value, a dict, a list…) lands in the Variables tab when the run ends, even after an error. Names starting with _, functions, classes and modules stay in Python. ws.name reads or sets a variable directly, and ws_names() lists them. print(…) writes to the console. display(…) (or cs.display) shows a mesh, a field or a plot, as in CraftScript. Call the analysis library Library functions are called like built-in ones: cs.box_mesh(20, 10, 10, 5, 'hex'), cs.call('modal_tet', 2, …). Write the name in the script as it is: PartCraft finds the library functions a script uses before it runs. Not there yet numpy, scipy and other packages: only Python's own standard library (math, json, itertools, decimal, statistics…) is here, without its windows, terminal and database parts (tkinter, curses, sqlite3). Sockets and anything else that would reach outside: Python has no network here. time.sleep returns at once. Folding in the editor, and calling a Python function from a CraftScript script. A run checks the instruction budget and Stop every 1000 lines; a long calculation inside one built-in function finishes first."},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"partcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Problems you may meet in PartCraft, from the app not starting to features failing, sketches that will not solve, files that will not open and joints that misbehave, with the message you see and the fix.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problem","error","help","not working","fails","failing","crash","blank","webgl","webassembly","kernel","not built","over defined","open profile","cannot open","nothing happens","wrong direction","slow"],"headings":[{"id":"the-app-does-not-start","text":"The app does not start","level":2},{"id":"this-browser-cannot-show-3d-webgl-is-off-or-missing-so-partcraft-cannot-run-here","text":"\"This browser cannot show 3D (WebGL is off or missing), so PartCraft cannot run here.\"","level":3},{"id":"the-solid-kernel-did-not-load","text":"\"The solid kernel did not load: …\"","level":3},{"id":"the-offline-button-says-could-not-save-or-offline-not-available","text":"The offline button says \"Could not save\" or \"Offline not available\"","level":3},{"id":"partcraft-is-offline-but-out-of-date","text":"PartCraft is offline but out of date","level":3},{"id":"sweep-not-built-yet-it-needs-an-exact-kernel","text":"\"Sweep: not built yet. It needs an exact kernel, …\"","level":3},{"id":"loading-the-solid-kernel-never-goes-away","text":"\"Loading the solid kernel…\" never goes away","level":3},{"id":"nothing-happens-when-i-press-a-key","text":"Nothing happens when I press a key","level":2},{"id":"a-toolbar-button-is-grayed-out","text":"A toolbar button is grayed out","level":2},{"id":"sketch-problems","text":"Sketch problems","level":2},{"id":"the-sketch-will-not-let-me-add-a-relation","text":"The sketch will not let me add a relation","level":3},{"id":"over-defined-cannot-all-be-met-undo-or-delete-one-of-them","text":"\"Over defined: … cannot all be met. Undo, or delete one of them.\"","level":3},{"id":"over-defined-is-already-implied-delete-it","text":"\"Over defined: … is already implied. Delete it.\"","level":3},{"id":"those-items-cannot-be-dimensioned-together","text":"\"Those items cannot be dimensioned together.\"","level":3},{"id":"a-fillet-needs-a-corner-where-exactly-two-lines-meet-that-radius-is-too-big-for-these-lines","text":"\"A fillet needs a corner where exactly two lines meet.\" / \"That radius is too big for these lines.\"","level":3},{"id":"the-geometry-jumps-back-when-i-drag-it","text":"The geometry jumps back when I drag it","level":3},{"id":"this-sketch-cannot-open-needs-extrude2-which-is-failing","text":"\"This sketch cannot open: Needs Extrude2, which is failing.\"","level":3},{"id":"feature-problems","text":"Feature problems","level":2},{"id":"picking-problems","text":"Picking problems","level":2},{"id":"file-problems","text":"File problems","level":2},{"id":"drawing-problems","text":"Drawing problems","level":2},{"id":"assembly-problems","text":"Assembly problems","level":2},{"id":"it-is-slow","text":"It is slow","level":2},{"id":"still-stuck","text":"Still stuck","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhen something does not work, read the \u003Cstrong\u003Estatus bar\u003C/strong\u003E at the bottom first: PartCraft says there why it refused or what it expects next. A failing feature also shows its message at the top of its right panel and when you hover its row in the history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-app-does-not-start\"\u003EThe app does not start\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"this-browser-cannot-show-3d-webgl-is-off-or-missing-so-partcraft-cannot-run-here\"\u003E&quot;This browser cannot show 3D (WebGL is off or missing), so PartCraft cannot run here.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E the browser has 3D graphics (WebGL) turned off, blocked, or not supported by the graphics driver.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Ccode\u003Echrome://settings/system\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003Eedge://settings/system\u003C/code\u003E in Edge) and turn on &quot;Use graphics acceleration when available&quot;, then restart the browser. If it is on, check \u003Ccode\u003Echrome://gpu\u003C/code\u003E for WebGL status, and update your graphics driver.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Ccode\u003Eabout:support\u003C/code\u003E and look at Graphics, WebGL. If it is blocked, update the graphics driver. In \u003Ccode\u003Eabout:config\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ewebgl.disabled\u003C/code\u003E must be \u003Ccode\u003Efalse\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWebGL is always on in current Safari. Update macOS (Safari updates with it). If it still fails, try Chrome or Firefox on the same Mac to rule out the graphics hardware.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERemote desktops and some virtual machines have no WebGL. Try on the computer itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"the-solid-kernel-did-not-load\"\u003E&quot;The solid kernel did not load: …&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E the geometry kernel (a WebAssembly program) could not be fetched or started. Usually the connection dropped while the app loaded, WebAssembly is turned off by a company policy or a browser hardening setting, or the browser is very old.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E reload the page while online. If it keeps failing, try another current browser. In Firefox, \u003Ccode\u003Ejavascript.options.wasm\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Ccode\u003Eabout:config\u003C/code\u003E must be \u003Ccode\u003Etrue\u003C/code\u003E. Offline, the app and its kernel load only if the app bar said \u003Cstrong\u003EOffline ready\u003C/strong\u003E before you lost the connection (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E while online; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"the-offline-button-says-could-not-save-or-offline-not-available\"\u003EThe offline button says &quot;Could not save&quot; or &quot;Offline not available&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECould not save\u003C/strong\u003E: some files did not download. Click it to try again while online; a slow or metered connection may need a second try. \u003Cstrong\u003EOffline not available\u003C/strong\u003E: this browser window cannot keep an offline copy (private windows and some managed computers block it). Use a normal window, or keep the tab open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"partcraft-is-offline-but-out-of-date\"\u003EPartCraft is offline but out of date\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003EOffline ready\u003C/strong\u003E while online and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"sweep-not-built-yet-it-needs-an-exact-kernel\"\u003E&quot;Sweep: not built yet. It needs an exact kernel, …&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe full message is &quot;&lt;Name&gt;: not built yet. It needs an exact kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have. Help › What is not built yet says what to do instead.&quot; You see it when you choose Sweep, Loft, Rib, a sheet metal flange, a surface feature, Combine, Move body, Scale body or Path pattern, and for the other things only an exact kernel does. Nothing is wrong with your browser or your part: this version of PartCraft builds every part with the mesh kernel. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E for what to do instead, and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"loading-the-solid-kernel-never-goes-away\"\u003E&quot;Loading the solid kernel…&quot; never goes away\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWait a little on a slow connection: the kernel and the 3D library are downloaded the first time. Then reload. If the page itself shows an error, see above.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nothing-happens-when-i-press-a-key\"\u003ENothing happens when I press a key\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeys do not work while the cursor is in a box in a panel. Click in the 3D view or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after typing (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E out).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArrow keys and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E/\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E turn and zoom the view only after you click in the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA dialog is open: answer it first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a sketch, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is the Fillet tool, not Fit. Use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-toolbar-button-is-grayed-out\"\u003EA toolbar button is grayed out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFeature buttons are grayed while a sketch is open. Close the sketch first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESketch tools work only inside a sketch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E are grayed when there is nothing to undo or redo.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E needs a part with a solid. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoint\u003C/span\u003E needs two parts in the assembly. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit part\u003C/span\u003E needs a part selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP242 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP AP214 (exact solid)\u003C/span\u003E are always grayed: exact STEP is not built yet in this version. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESweep\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoft\u003C/span\u003E and the other features that need an exact kernel are not grayed: they stay clickable, their tooltips end with &quot;not built yet (needs an exact kernel)&quot;, and clicking one says so in the status bar. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E needs a part with a solid, and is grayed while a sketch is open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E items are grayed in an assembly: assemblies cannot be exported yet. Close it, or edit a part, to export.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sketch-problems\"\u003ESketch problems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"the-sketch-will-not-let-me-add-a-relation\"\u003EThe sketch will not let me add a relation\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;No relation fits this selection. Select one or two items (Shift+click adds).&quot; The relation needs a different selection. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sketch-relations\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sketch-relations\"\u003ESketch relations and dimensions\u003C/a\u003E for what each needs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"over-defined-cannot-all-be-met-undo-or-delete-one-of-them\"\u003E&quot;Over defined: … cannot all be met. Undo, or delete one of them.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E the new relation or dimension contradicts the ones already there. The sketch did not move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or delete one of the red items in the right panel&#039;s list (choose \u003Cstrong\u003EDelete\u003C/strong\u003E beside it).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"over-defined-is-already-implied-delete-it\"\u003E&quot;Over defined: … is already implied. Delete it.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E the relation adds nothing: others already force it (for example Parallel on two sides of a rectangle, or a dimension that is already fixed). There are no driven dimensions in this build.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E delete it. The feature builds anyway, with a warning.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"those-items-cannot-be-dimensioned-together\"\u003E&quot;Those items cannot be dimensioned together.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou clicked, for example, a point and a circle. Dimension to the circle&#039;s center point instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"a-fillet-needs-a-corner-where-exactly-two-lines-meet-that-radius-is-too-big-for-these-lines\"\u003E&quot;A fillet needs a corner where exactly two lines meet.&quot; / &quot;That radius is too big for these lines.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick exactly on the corner point of two solid lines. Use a smaller radius than the shorter line allows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"the-geometry-jumps-back-when-i-drag-it\"\u003EThe geometry jumps back when I drag it\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drag would break a relation or a dimension, so PartCraft puts it back. Only blue (under defined) geometry can move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"this-sketch-cannot-open-needs-extrude2-which-is-failing\"\u003E&quot;This sketch cannot open: Needs Extrude2, which is failing.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sketch sits on a face of a feature that is not built. Fix that feature first, or unsuppress it, or roll forward.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"feature-problems\"\u003EFeature problems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Make a sketch first: pick a plane, Sketch, draw a closed profile, Exit.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo unused sketch with curves, and none selected\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw a sketch, or select the sketch to use in the history\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The sketch has no closed profile to extrude.&quot; (or &quot;to revolve&quot;)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo closed loop of solid lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose the gaps; switch construction lines back with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The profile is open: an end at (x, y) is not joined to anything.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA gap at that point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the sketch, drag the end onto its neighbor so it snaps\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The profile branches at (x, y): more than two ends meet there.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThree or more lines meet at one point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the extra line, or switch it to construction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A closed loop needs at least three sides or a curve.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo lines back and forth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedraw the loop\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Through all is for cuts. Use a depth for a boss.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA boss set to Through all\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet End to Blind or Mid-plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;There is nothing to cut on that side of the sketch. Try the other direction.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe cut points away from the material\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETick Reverse direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;There is nothing to cut yet.&quot; / &quot;There is nothing to drill yet.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo solid before this feature\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake a boss first, or move the feature down\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Draw a centerline (a construction line) in the sketch to revolve about.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERevolve without an axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd a centerline, or choose a line in Axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The profile crosses the axis. Keep it on one side of the centerline.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfile on both sides of the axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedraw half the profile\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Put sketch points where the holes go (Point tool), then make the hole.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo loose points in the sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd points with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The counterbore must be wider than the hole.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECounterbore ⌀ not bigger than Diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake the counterbore wider\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The counterbore must be shallower than the hole.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECounterbore deeper than a blind hole\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake it shallower, or the hole deeper\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Choose the features to copy.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA pattern or mirror with nothing ticked\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETick a feature under Features to copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Extrude2 has no solid to copy.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe copied feature failed or is off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFix or unsuppress it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Needs Sketch3, which is failing.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA feature it depends on is not built\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFix the feature it names\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The face it sits on is gone (Extrude1 is not built).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sketch&#039;s face no longer exists\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFix that feature, or delete the sketch and sketch again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Nothing is left of the part after this.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA cut removed all material\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck the cut&#039;s size and direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The solid kernel could not build this (…).&quot; or &quot;The solid kernel could not combine this with the body.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe kernel refused the geometry\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo, or change the sizes slightly; faces that exactly touch can cause it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Sweep: not built yet. It needs an exact kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have.&quot; (also Loft, Rib and the others)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA feature made in an earlier build that only an exact kernel can build. It stays in the history and is saved back unchanged\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESuppress or delete it; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E for what to do instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A 5 mm round does not fit here (…).&quot; / &quot;A 2 mm chamfer …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe size is too big for a face next to the edge, or the corner cannot be blended\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESmaller size, or fewer edges per feature\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Fillet: in this version only straight edges between two flat faces (the mesh kernel builds them). Round or bevel curved edges in the sketch.&quot; (also Chamfer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picked edge is round, such as a hole rim, or lies on a curved face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick edges\u003C/span\u003E and remove it; round or bevel that corner in the sketch instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A fillet whose radius changes along the edge: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel). Give it one radius.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVariable radius\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUntick it and give one radius\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Fillet by the mesh kernel: where rounded edges meet they are mitred, not blended.&quot; (⚠, a warning)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPicked edges meet at a corner; the rounds meet there in a sharp crease\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing to fix: the part is built. Fillet fewer edges if the crease matters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;An edge it rounds is gone: the edge between Extrude1 and Cut2.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn earlier change removed the edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick edges\u003C/span\u003E, remove the red edge, pick the new one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Pick the edges in the view, then add them.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe fillet or chamfer has no edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick edges\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Pick the faces to remove (open), then shell.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe shell has no open faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick faces\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The shell could not be made with this thickness (…).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe wall is thicker than a thin part of the model or a fillet radius\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA thinner wall, or larger fillets\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Shell: in this version it opens flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn open face is curved\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick faces\u003C/span\u003E and remove it; open only flat faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Draft: in this version flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA drafted face is a cylinder, cone or freeform face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove it; draft only flat faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A face it opens is gone (…).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn earlier change removed the face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick faces\u003C/span\u003E again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Fillet1 changes the body&#039;s edges or walls; it cannot be copied. …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA pattern or mirror copies a fillet, chamfer or shell\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUntick it; pattern the cuts first, then fillet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;It sits on a face only an exact kernel can find: not built yet in this version.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA sketch on a face the mesh kernel cannot find, such as one made in an earlier build\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch on a base plane or a flat face of an extrude, and position it with dimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;It uses a face or edge of the solid, which only an exact kernel can find: not built yet in this version.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA reference plane, axis or point on a face or edge of the solid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake it from base planes, sketch points or coordinates instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Extruding up to a face, the next face or a vertex: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel).&quot; / &quot;A taper: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn end condition or taper only an exact kernel builds\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a Blind or Mid-plane depth you measure; leave Taper at 0\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"picking-problems\"\u003EPicking problems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;That face is curved. Pick a flat face or a plane to sketch on.&quot; Sketches need a flat face. Sketch on a base plane and position the sketch with dimensions instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA face says &quot;Fixed where picked&quot;: it was not made by a sketch edge (for example a face of a pattern copy), so a sketch on it stays put when the part changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEdges are hard to hit: zoom in. An edge wins over the face next to it when the pointer is right on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"file-problems\"\u003EFile problems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&#039;PartCraft opens its own .partcraft.json files, exports from the CadCraft macro for SolidWorks (.cadcraft-sw.json) and STEP files. &quot;bracket.igs&quot; is none of these.&#039;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly those files are recognized, and opening STEP is not built yet in this version\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&#039;&quot;part.stp&quot; is not a STEP file (it does not start with ISO-10303-21).&#039;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is not STEP text\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;That STEP file is too big (over 60 MB) to import here.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESimplify it at the source\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Opening STEP files: not built yet. It needs an exact kernel, …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTEP import is not built yet in this version. Model the part from its drawing or dimensions; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"partcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;That file is too big to be a PartCraft part.&quot; on your own saved part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA large STEP solid imported in an earlier build made the part file over 50 MB. Use an earlier copy without it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;That file is too big to be a PartCraft part.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is over 50 MB; it is not a PartCraft file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is not a PartCraft file (it is not JSON).&quot; / &quot;This is not a PartCraft file.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is damaged or another program&#039;s\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This file was saved by a newer PartCraft (file version …); this one reads version …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it in the newer PartCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Extrude2: the depth is bad.&quot; (or another value)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file was edited by hand or damaged. Use an earlier copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInsert in an assembly says &quot;Part1.partcraft.json has no solid to insert.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe part file has no solid. Open it, build a solid, save, and insert again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMy saved file is not where I expected.\u003C/strong\u003E Saving is a download: look in your browser&#039;s downloads folder, or open the downloads list from the browser&#039;s toolbar. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI opened a part but the assembly is still on screen.\u003C/strong\u003E The part loaded behind the assembly. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E to close the assembly and see the part. Close assemblies before opening part files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-problems\"\u003EDrawing problems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Build a solid first, then make its drawing.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuild the part first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;No room on the sheet for: detail. Make the detail smaller or drop a view.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESmaller \u003Cstrong\u003EDetail radius\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EDetail scale\u003C/strong\u003E, or remove the section\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A detail is taken from the front, top, right or section view.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick on one of those views\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Click on one of the views.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe click was on empty paper\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Position needs at least one datum reference.&quot; and other frame messages\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet the datums the panel asks for, or choose another characteristic\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The drawing views could not be made: …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo the last change. If it keeps happening, save, reload and reopen the part\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assembly-problems\"\u003EAssembly problems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Build a solid first, then put it into an assembly.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe part has no solid yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Insert a second part first (Add copy, Insert, or Edit › Duplicate).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA joint needs two parts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Pick a face on a different part.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoth faces of a joint must be on different parts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;That face is curved. Pick a flat face …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoints start from flat faces. Then use \u003Cstrong\u003ECentre on\u003C/strong\u003E for a hole or revolve axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Bracket2 is not joined to the grounded part, so it stays where it was put.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd a joint from it to a part that is placed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Revolute3 closes a loop and is off by 2.000 mm. Joints in a loop are not solved yet; remove one.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove one joint of the loop\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The face this joint uses is gone from Bracket.&quot; / &quot;The axis this joint uses is gone from Bracket.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe part was edited. Remove the joint and make it again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The axis runs along the face, so it cannot set the joint&#039;s centre.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose an axis that crosses the face, or &quot;The face&#039;s own origin&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;An assembly keeps at least one part. Close it instead.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe parts are joined but in the wrong spot.\u003C/strong\u003E Set \u003Cstrong\u003ECentre on\u003C/strong\u003E for both sides of the joint to the hole or revolve axes that should line up, then adjust \u003Cstrong\u003EAngle (°)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EOffset (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"it-is-slow\"\u003EIt is slow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E to Normal or Coarse while modeling; switch to Fine before exporting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELarge pattern counts (hundreds of copies) take time to rebuild on every change. Suppress the pattern while you work on other features.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClash and the motion check compare every pair of parts; big assemblies take longer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose other heavy tabs. PartCraft runs on your own computer&#039;s processor and graphics.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-stuck\"\u003EStill stuck\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESave your file first (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), then reload the page and open it again. If a feature still fails, select it, read its message, and try \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E back to the last good state.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"When something does not work, read the status bar at the bottom first: PartCraft says there why it refused or what it expects next. A failing feature also shows its message at the top of its right panel and when you hover its row in the history. The app does not start \"This browser cannot show 3D (WebGL is off or missing), so PartCraft cannot run here.\" Cause: the browser has 3D graphics (WebGL) turned off, blocked, or not supported by the graphics driver. Fix: Open chrome://settings/system (or edge://settings/system in Edge) and turn on \"Use graphics acceleration when available\", then restart the browser. If it is on, check chrome://gpu for WebGL status, and update your graphics driver. Open about:support and look at Graphics, WebGL. If it is blocked, update the graphics driver. In about:config, webgl.disabled must be false. WebGL is always on in current Safari. Update macOS (Safari updates with it). If it still fails, try Chrome or Firefox on the same Mac to rule out the graphics hardware. Remote desktops and some virtual machines have no WebGL. Try on the computer itself. \"The solid kernel did not load: …\" Cause: the geometry kernel (a WebAssembly program) could not be fetched or started. Usually the connection dropped while the app loaded, WebAssembly is turned off by a company policy or a browser hardening setting, or the browser is very old. Fix: reload the page while online. If it keeps failing, try another current browser. In Firefox, javascript.options.wasm in about:config must be true. Offline, the app and its kernel load only if the app bar said Offline ready before you lost the connection (Save for offline while online; see Working offline). The offline button says \"Could not save\" or \"Offline not available\" Could not save: some files did not download. Click it to try again while online; a slow or metered connection may need a second try. Offline not available: this browser window cannot keep an offline copy (private windows and some managed computers block it). Use a normal window, or keep the tab open. PartCraft is offline but out of date Click Offline ready while online and choose Save again (get the newest version). \"Sweep: not built yet. It needs an exact kernel, …\" The full message is \"\u003CName\u003E: not built yet. It needs an exact kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have. Help › What is not built yet says what to do instead.\" You see it when you choose Sweep, Loft, Rib, a sheet metal flange, a surface feature, Combine, Move body, Scale body or Path pattern, and for the other things only an exact kernel does. Nothing is wrong with your browser or your part: this version of PartCraft builds every part with the mesh kernel. See What is not built yet for what to do instead, and The mesh kernel. \"Loading the solid kernel…\" never goes away Wait a little on a slow connection: the kernel and the 3D library are downloaded the first time. Then reload. If the page itself shows an error, see above. Nothing happens when I press a key Keys do not work while the cursor is in a box in a panel. Click in the 3D view or press Esc after typing (or Tab out). Arrow keys and +/− turn and zoom the view only after you click in the view. A dialog is open: answer it first. In a sketch, F is the Fillet tool, not Fit. Use the Fit button. A toolbar button is grayed out Feature buttons are grayed while a sketch is open. Close the sketch first. Sketch tools work only inside a sketch. Undo and Redo are grayed when there is nothing to undo or redo. Assembly needs a part with a solid. Joint needs two parts in the assembly. Edit part needs a part selected. STEP AP242 (exact solid) and STEP AP214 (exact solid) are always grayed: exact STEP is not built yet in this version. Use STEP (faceted, mesh kernel). Sweep, Loft and the other features that need an exact kernel are not grayed: they stay clickable, their tooltips end with \"not built yet (needs an exact kernel)\", and clicking one says so in the status bar. See What is not built yet. Drawing needs a part with a solid, and is grayed while a sketch is open. Export items are grayed in an assembly: assemblies cannot be exported yet. Close it, or edit a part, to export. Sketch problems The sketch will not let me add a relation \"No relation fits this selection. Select one or two items (Shift+click adds).\" The relation needs a different selection. See Sketch relations and dimensions for what each needs. \"Over defined: … cannot all be met. Undo, or delete one of them.\" Cause: the new relation or dimension contradicts the ones already there. The sketch did not move. Fix: Ctrl+Z, or delete one of the red items in the right panel's list (choose Delete beside it). \"Over defined: … is already implied. Delete it.\" Cause: the relation adds nothing: others already force it (for example Parallel on two sides of a rectangle, or a dimension that is already fixed). There are no driven dimensions in this build. Fix: delete it. The feature builds anyway, with a warning. \"Those items cannot be dimensioned together.\" You clicked, for example, a point and a circle. Dimension to the circle's center point instead. \"A fillet needs a corner where exactly two lines meet.\" / \"That radius is too big for these lines.\" Click exactly on the corner point of two solid lines. Use a smaller radius than the shorter line allows. The geometry jumps back when I drag it The drag would break a relation or a dimension, so PartCraft puts it back. Only blue (under defined) geometry can move. \"This sketch cannot open: Needs Extrude2, which is failing.\" The sketch sits on a face of a feature that is not built. Fix that feature first, or unsuppress it, or roll forward. Feature problems Message Cause Fix \"Make a sketch first: pick a plane, Sketch, draw a closed profile, Exit.\" No unused sketch with curves, and none selected Draw a sketch, or select the sketch to use in the history \"The sketch has no closed profile to extrude.\" (or \"to revolve\") No closed loop of solid lines Close the gaps; switch construction lines back with Q \"The profile is open: an end at (x, y) is not joined to anything.\" A gap at that point Open the sketch, drag the end onto its neighbor so it snaps \"The profile branches at (x, y): more than two ends meet there.\" Three or more lines meet at one point Delete the extra line, or switch it to construction \"A closed loop needs at least three sides or a curve.\" Two lines back and forth Redraw the loop \"Through all is for cuts. Use a depth for a boss.\" A boss set to Through all Set End to Blind or Mid-plane \"There is nothing to cut on that side of the sketch. Try the other direction.\" The cut points away from the material Tick Reverse direction \"There is nothing to cut yet.\" / \"There is nothing to drill yet.\" No solid before this feature Make a boss first, or move the feature down \"Draw a centerline (a construction line) in the sketch to revolve about.\" Revolve without an axis Add a centerline, or choose a line in Axis \"The profile crosses the axis. Keep it on one side of the centerline.\" Profile on both sides of the axis Redraw half the profile \"Put sketch points where the holes go (Point tool), then make the hole.\" No loose points in the sketch Add points with P \"The counterbore must be wider than the hole.\" Counterbore ⌀ not bigger than Diameter Make the counterbore wider \"The counterbore must be shallower than the hole.\" Counterbore deeper than a blind hole Make it shallower, or the hole deeper \"Choose the features to copy.\" A pattern or mirror with nothing ticked Tick a feature under Features to copy \"Extrude2 has no solid to copy.\" The copied feature failed or is off Fix or unsuppress it \"Needs Sketch3, which is failing.\" A feature it depends on is not built Fix the feature it names \"The face it sits on is gone (Extrude1 is not built).\" The sketch's face no longer exists Fix that feature, or delete the sketch and sketch again \"Nothing is left of the part after this.\" A cut removed all material Check the cut's size and direction \"The solid kernel could not build this (…).\" or \"The solid kernel could not combine this with the body.\" The kernel refused the geometry Undo, or change the sizes slightly; faces that exactly touch can cause it \"Sweep: not built yet. It needs an exact kernel, which this version of PartCraft does not have.\" (also Loft, Rib and the others) A feature made in an earlier build that only an exact kernel can build. It stays in the history and is saved back unchanged Suppress or delete it; see What is not built yet for what to do instead \"A 5 mm round does not fit here (…).\" / \"A 2 mm chamfer …\" The size is too big for a face next to the edge, or the corner cannot be blended Smaller size, or fewer edges per feature \"Fillet: in this version only straight edges between two flat faces (the mesh kernel builds them). Round or bevel curved edges in the sketch.\" (also Chamfer) A picked edge is round, such as a hole rim, or lies on a curved face Pick edges and remove it; round or bevel that corner in the sketch instead \"A fillet whose radius changes along the edge: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel). Give it one radius.\" Variable radius is ticked Untick it and give one radius \"Fillet by the mesh kernel: where rounded edges meet they are mitred, not blended.\" (⚠, a warning) Picked edges meet at a corner; the rounds meet there in a sharp crease Nothing to fix: the part is built. Fillet fewer edges if the crease matters \"An edge it rounds is gone: the edge between Extrude1 and Cut2.\" An earlier change removed the edge Pick edges, remove the red edge, pick the new one \"Pick the edges in the view, then add them.\" The fillet or chamfer has no edges Pick edges \"Pick the faces to remove (open), then shell.\" The shell has no open faces Pick faces \"The shell could not be made with this thickness (…).\" The wall is thicker than a thin part of the model or a fillet radius A thinner wall, or larger fillets \"Shell: in this version it opens flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).\" An open face is curved Pick faces and remove it; open only flat faces \"Draft: in this version flat faces only (the mesh kernel builds it).\" A drafted face is a cylinder, cone or freeform face Remove it; draft only flat faces \"A face it opens is gone (…).\" An earlier change removed the face Pick faces again \"Fillet1 changes the body's edges or walls; it cannot be copied. …\" A pattern or mirror copies a fillet, chamfer or shell Untick it; pattern the cuts first, then fillet \"It sits on a face only an exact kernel can find: not built yet in this version.\" A sketch on a face the mesh kernel cannot find, such as one made in an earlier build Sketch on a base plane or a flat face of an extrude, and position it with dimensions \"It uses a face or edge of the solid, which only an exact kernel can find: not built yet in this version.\" A reference plane, axis or point on a face or edge of the solid Make it from base planes, sketch points or coordinates instead \"Extruding up to a face, the next face or a vertex: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel).\" / \"A taper: not built yet (it needs an exact kernel).\" An end condition or taper only an exact kernel builds Use a Blind or Mid-plane depth you measure; leave Taper at 0 Picking problems \"That face is curved. Pick a flat face or a plane to sketch on.\" Sketches need a flat face. Sketch on a base plane and position the sketch with dimensions instead. A face says \"Fixed where picked\": it was not made by a sketch edge (for example a face of a pattern copy), so a sketch on it stays put when the part changes. Edges are hard to hit: zoom in. An edge wins over the face next to it when the pointer is right on it. File problems Message Fix 'PartCraft opens its own .partcraft.json files, exports from the CadCraft macro for SolidWorks (.cadcraft-sw.json) and STEP files. \"bracket.igs\" is none of these.' Only those files are recognized, and opening STEP is not built yet in this version '\"part.stp\" is not a STEP file (it does not start with ISO-10303-21).' The file is not STEP text \"That STEP file is too big (over 60 MB) to import here.\" Simplify it at the source \"Opening STEP files: not built yet. It needs an exact kernel, …\" STEP import is not built yet in this version. Model the part from its drawing or dimensions; see What is not built yet \"That file is too big to be a PartCraft part.\" on your own saved part A large STEP solid imported in an earlier build made the part file over 50 MB. Use an earlier copy without it \"That file is too big to be a PartCraft part.\" The file is over 50 MB; it is not a PartCraft file \"This is not a PartCraft file (it is not JSON).\" / \"This is not a PartCraft file.\" The file is damaged or another program's \"This file was saved by a newer PartCraft (file version …); this one reads version …\" Open it in the newer PartCraft \"Extrude2: the depth is bad.\" (or another value) The file was edited by hand or damaged. Use an earlier copy Insert in an assembly says \"Part1.partcraft.json has no solid to insert.\" The part file has no solid. Open it, build a solid, save, and insert again My saved file is not where I expected. Saving is a download: look in your browser's downloads folder, or open the downloads list from the browser's toolbar. See Where your files go. I opened a part but the assembly is still on screen. The part loaded behind the assembly. Choose Close to close the assembly and see the part. Close assemblies before opening part files. Drawing problems Message Fix \"Build a solid first, then make its drawing.\" Build the part first \"No room on the sheet for: detail. Make the detail smaller or drop a view.\" Smaller Detail radius or Detail scale, or remove the section \"A detail is taken from the front, top, right or section view.\" Click on one of those views \"Click on one of the views.\" The click was on empty paper \"Position needs at least one datum reference.\" and other frame messages Set the datums the panel asks for, or choose another characteristic \"The drawing views could not be made: …\" Undo the last change. If it keeps happening, save, reload and reopen the part Assembly problems Message Fix \"Build a solid first, then put it into an assembly.\" The part has no solid yet \"Insert a second part first (Add copy, Insert, or Edit › Duplicate).\" A joint needs two parts \"Pick a face on a different part.\" Both faces of a joint must be on different parts \"That face is curved. Pick a flat face …\" Joints start from flat faces. Then use Centre on for a hole or revolve axis \"Bracket2 is not joined to the grounded part, so it stays where it was put.\" Add a joint from it to a part that is placed \"Revolute3 closes a loop and is off by 2.000 mm. Joints in a loop are not solved yet; remove one.\" Remove one joint of the loop \"The face this joint uses is gone from Bracket.\" / \"The axis this joint uses is gone from Bracket.\" The part was edited. Remove the joint and make it again \"The axis runs along the face, so it cannot set the joint's centre.\" Choose an axis that crosses the face, or \"The face's own origin\" \"An assembly keeps at least one part. Close it instead.\" Use Close The parts are joined but in the wrong spot. Set Centre on for both sides of the joint to the hole or revolve axes that should line up, then adjust Angle (°) and Offset (mm). It is slow Set Curves to Normal or Coarse while modeling; switch to Fine before exporting. Large pattern counts (hundreds of copies) take time to rebuild on every change. Suppress the pattern while you work on other features. Clash and the motion check compare every pair of parts; big assemblies take longer. Close other heavy tabs. PartCraft runs on your own computer's processor and graphics. Still stuck Save your file first (Ctrl+S), then reload the page and open it again. If a feature still fails, select it, read its message, and try Ctrl+Z back to the last good state."},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"partcraft","title":"What is not built yet","summary":"Everything this PartCraft build does not do yet, from twisting sweeps to inch display, drawing dimensions and sub-assemblies, with what to do instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["missing","not yet","limits","limitations","roadmap","exact kernel","fillet","chamfer","shell","sweep","loft","step import","simulation","manufacture","twist sweep","loft end tangency","face fillet","equations","inches","drawing dimensions","sub-assemblies","cloud","tablet","phone"],"headings":[{"id":"needs-an-exact-kernel","text":"Needs an exact kernel","level":2},{"id":"features","text":"Features","level":2},{"id":"sketches","text":"Sketches","level":2},{"id":"parameters-and-units","text":"Parameters and units","level":2},{"id":"files","text":"Files","level":2},{"id":"drawings","text":"Drawings","level":2},{"id":"assemblies","text":"Assemblies","level":2},{"id":"manufacture","text":"Manufacture","level":2},{"id":"simulation-and-generative-design","text":"Simulation and generative design","level":2},{"id":"scripts","text":"Scripts","level":2},{"id":"the-app","text":"The app","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft is an early build. This page lists what it does not do yet, so you do not search for it, and what to do instead today. Nothing here has a date.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"needs-an-exact-kernel\"\u003ENeeds an exact kernel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis version builds every part with the mesh kernel (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/solid-kernels\" data-topic=\"partcraft/solid-kernels\"\u003EThe mesh kernel\u003C/a\u003E). Everything below needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version does not have yet. Parts saved with these features still open: the features stay in the history, marked as not built, and are kept when you save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/span\u003E on round edges; fillets whose radius changes along the edge; rounds blended smoothly where they meet (the mesh kernel mitres them)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet and Chamfer work on straight edges between flat faces; round other corners in the sketch before you extrude (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E in a sketch, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShell\u003C/span\u003E opening curved faces; walls of different thicknesses\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen flat faces; add material with extrudes where a wall must be thicker\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESweep\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E on curved faces; extruding up to a face, the next face or a vertex; tapered extrudes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude or revolve, in steps where needed; extrude to a depth you measure\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERib\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBase flange\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdge flange\u003C/span\u003E and flat patterns\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude the plate or the rib&#039;s profile\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurfaces: finishing a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForm\u003C/span\u003E into the part, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPatch\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoft surface\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGordon surface\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStitch\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThicken\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset surface\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtend surface\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim surface\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeveral bodies: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECombine\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove body\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale body\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPath pattern\u003C/span\u003E, features that make a new body\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne solid; patterns and mirror copy features\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketches, planes, axes and points on faces that only an exact kernel can find (a face made by a fillet, a hole wall, a face of an imported solid)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch on base planes, reference planes and the flat faces of extrudes and revolves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA generative design outcome opened as a part or added to it (it becomes an imported solid)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownload the outcome as STL or STEP\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening STEP files, exact STEP export (AP242, AP214), STEP for other part modelers, opening a part modeler&#039;s export\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTEP (faceted, mesh kernel)\u003C/span\u003E, STL or 3MF; the macro for another part modeler still writes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrue arcs in drawing views\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EViews and section views are drawn from the facets\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExact volumes, areas and distances\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E gives the faceted solid&#039;s numbers, a little low on round parts; a finer \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E setting brings them closer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome rows in the tables below suggest one of these features as the way round something else. Those suggestions apply once an exact kernel is back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"features\"\u003EFeatures\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFace fillets, full-round fillets, fillets with radii set at points along an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstant or linear variable \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E; several fillet features\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShells with different wall thicknesses\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShell\u003C/span\u003E thickness; add material with extrudes where a wall must be thicker\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraft on freeform (spline) faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E works on flat, cylindrical and conical faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwisting sweeps, thin-wall and surface sweeps; end tangency on the Loft feature; a loft with one guide rail; spline paths\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESweep\u003C/span\u003E with a fixed orientation or an end scale; a loft with two or more guide rails: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sweep-loft-draft\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sweep-loft-draft\"\u003ESweep, loft and draft\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERibs from arcs or splines; ribs grown square to the sketch (from a top view); draft on ribs; several ribs from one sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERib\u003C/span\u003E from one chain of lines in the rib&#039;s own plane: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/rib\" data-topic=\"partcraft/rib\"\u003ERibs and gussets\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Up to&quot; a curved surface, &quot;up to next&quot; stopping at a curved face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUp to a flat face or a reference plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECounterdrilled holes, inch and pipe threads, tapered threads, thread run-outs and chamfered thread starts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECounterbore with a small depth; ISO metric and trapezoidal threads, cosmetic or modelled: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/threads\" data-topic=\"partcraft/threads\"\u003EThreads on shafts and bores, drawn or modelled\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThin-feature extrudes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw both walls of the profile\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPattern copies skipped one by one, fill patterns\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse separate features; patterns along a curve: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPath pattern\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopying patterns, mirrors, fillets, chamfers, shells and imported solids\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the sketch or feature they are built on, or pattern it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExact (G1) tangency at a form&#039;s extraordinary points; merging a form&#039;s patches into larger faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERaise the form&#039;s Level: the approximated patches get smaller; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/form-surfaces\" data-topic=\"partcraft/form-surfaces\"\u003EFreeform shapes with Form\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA form made of several separate closed pieces in one feature; bridges between forms\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne closed piece per Form feature; join them with Join\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPicking surface bodies in the view (except trim and patch points); surface fillets; untrim; G2 patches; Gordon surfaces tangent to curved neighbours (tangent to planes and flat faces is built)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose them by name in the feature&#039;s panel; thicken first, then fillet the solid\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA body list in the history; leaving hidden bodies out of exports\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERename and hide bodies in the part panel&#039;s body list; delete or suppress the feature that made a body to leave it out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA non-uniform scale that keeps cylinders and cones analytic; non-uniform scaling of offset surfaces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale body\u003C/span\u003E makes curved faces close B-splines (within 0.0000004 mm); a uniform scale stays exact\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECombining surface bodies; Minkowski sums\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStitch\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThicken\u003C/span\u003E first, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECombine\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETolerance analysis: stack-ups that turn corners (2D and 3D vector loops), sensitivities worked out from the geometry, Monte Carlo with other distributions, size tolerances on sketch dimensions, bonus tolerance from material condition\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStack-up\u003C/span\u003E in an assembly: one direction, worst case and RSS, tolerances typed, from the title block&#039;s general tolerance or from GD&amp;T frames: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/tolerance-stackup\" data-topic=\"partcraft/tolerance-stackup\"\u003ETolerance stack-ups\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENesting: cutting order and lead-ins, cutting shared edges once, keeping and reusing leftover sheet, parts that must be kept together\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENest flat parts on sheets…\u003C/span\u003E with spacing, margins, turns and grain: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/nest-flat-parts\" data-topic=\"partcraft/nest-flat-parts\"\u003ENest flat parts on sheets\u003C/a\u003E; plan the cut in your cutter&#039;s own software\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheet metal: hems, jogs, miter and contour flanges; flanges on curved edges or on a bent base flange&#039;s faces; sketched bends; corner closures; forming tools; converting a solid to sheet metal; the flat of a bent base flange with cuts in it; bend tables\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBase flange\u003C/span\u003E (a plate or a bent profile), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdge flange\u003C/span\u003E on a plate&#039;s straight edges with bend reliefs, and the flat pattern with a K-factor: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/sheet-metal\" data-topic=\"partcraft/sheet-metal\"\u003ESheet metal\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraft analysis on assemblies; finding the parting line; wall-thickness analysis\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft analysis\u003C/span\u003E on the part in Design: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/draft-analysis\" data-topic=\"partcraft/draft-analysis\"\u003ECheck draft for moulding and casting\u003C/a\u003E; open each part of an assembly to check it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sketches\"\u003ESketches\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEllipses, tangent arcs, conics, splines with a set degree or weights (NURBS)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESplines (cubic), arcs and circles with Tangent relations\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA point on a spline (a relation), curvature (G2) relations\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut a spline&#039;s own point where it must pass\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset copies of splines that follow later changes; offsets of chains with sharp spline corners\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset again after the change; round the corner first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPattern copies that stay linked to the original; patterns along a curve\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPattern again after a change\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProjecting free-form or tilted circular edges, projected geometry that follows the part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject straight and parallel circular edges, then dimension\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDriven (reference) dimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead sizes from the panel or the part facts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECollinear relations\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParallel with a 0 distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPasting into another program (the system clipboard)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport DXF, STEP, STL or 3MF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping arcs, relative or polar coordinates\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType lines and circles; draw arcs with the Arc tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelecting geometry without a pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENeeds a click or a tap\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"parameters-and-units\"\u003EParameters and units\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilding a part&#039;s saved configurations from the command line by name (with their features turned off); configurations that change materials\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport them as a CSV design table and pass it with \u003Ccode\u003E--table\u003C/code\u003E (values only); configurations set parameters and turn features on and off: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/design-tables\" data-topic=\"partcraft/design-tables\"\u003EConfigurations and design tables\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquations on the numbers of the newest features&#039; own panels (plane, sweep, draft and similar)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrite them in a script or with \u003Ccode\u003Epartcraft set\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/scripts-and-cli\" data-topic=\"partcraft/scripts-and-cli\"\u003EScripts and the command line\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA part shown in inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESizes show in millimeters; type \u003Ccode\u003E2in\u003C/code\u003E in a number box or an expression and it becomes 50.8\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing an imported STEP solid&#039;s own features (it arrives without history)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd or cut material with your own features\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReading colors, names, assembly structure and PMI from STEP\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EglTF, OBJ, IGES, Parasolid, DWG export\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTL or 3MF for meshes; STEP for solids; DXF for 2D\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening STL, IGES, DXF or other programs&#039; own files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk for STEP\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly structure (a tree with part names) in exported STEP\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly STEP is one compound of the placed solids\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving to a folder of your choice without the browser&#039;s download\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn on &quot;ask where to save&quot; in the browser&#039;s download settings\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensions beyond the three overall sizes (hole sizes, positions, tolerances on sizes)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMark up the PDF, or export DXF and dimension it in a 2D CAD program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMore than one section or detail; aligned, broken-out and auxiliary views\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne section and one detail per drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFree text, notes and tables on the sheet (other than the title and revision blocks)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe finish note, or text added on the PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirst-angle projection, moving views, portrait sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThird angle, automatic layout, landscape sheets A4–A0 and ANSI A–E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELive section view: several planes or a section box at once, an outline round the cut, saving the section in the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne plane at a time from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection view\u003C/span\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/section-view\" data-topic=\"partcraft/section-view\"\u003ELook inside with a section view\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly drawings with more views, dimensions or balloons you can move\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne isometric (or exploded) view with automatic balloons and the parts list\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy and paste of GD&amp;T and finish symbols\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace each with Datum, Frame or Finish\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assemblies\"\u003EAssemblies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoints in a loop (more than one path to the grounded part)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep the joints a tree; remove one joint of the loop\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGear, rack, cam and screw joints; a ball joint limited to a cone; joints on curved faces or between points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERigid, revolute, slider, cylindrical, planar, ball and pin-in-slot joints on flat faces, each value with its own limits: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/build-assembly\" data-topic=\"partcraft/build-assembly\"\u003EBuild an assembly\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExploded steps that turn parts, and dragging parts that joints place\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStraight explode steps; remove a joint to move a part by hand\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESub-assemblies\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne level of parts only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGears, cams, contact\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"manufacture\"\u003EManufacture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D roughing and finishing of curved faces, 3D adaptive clearing, rest machining; adaptive passes written as arcs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5-axis face, pocket, 2D adaptive, contour, drill and chamfer: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/manufacture\" data-topic=\"partcraft/manufacture\"\u003EMake toolpaths and G-code\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4- and 5-axis toolpaths, turning, probing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet up one side at a time with the tool axis on the Setup tab\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHolder and shank collision checks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWatch the flute-length warnings and check in your control&#039;s simulator\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing posts or adding other controls\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFanuc-style, GRBL or LinuxCNC; adapt the downloaded file by hand\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInch G-code (G20)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMillimetres (G21) only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"simulation-and-generative-design\"\u003ESimulation and generative design\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENonlinear studies: yielding, large deflections, contact between parts, fatigue\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear static and natural frequencies: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/simulation\" data-topic=\"partcraft/simulation\"\u003ECheck stress and deflection\u003C/a\u003E; keep the safety factor well above 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENonlinear buckling (imperfections, post-buckling, snap-through)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear buckling load factors with a generous margin: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/thermal-and-buckling\" data-topic=\"partcraft/thermal-and-buckling\"\u003EHeat, thermal stress and buckling\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadiation between faces (view factors), contact resistance, temperature-dependent materials, inertia in thermal stress (thermal shock waves)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadiation to the surroundings, steady or quasi-static thermal stress over time, constant properties: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/thermal-and-buckling\" data-topic=\"partcraft/thermal-and-buckling\"\u003EHeat, thermal stress and buckling\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAutomatic refinement for transient heat and studies with a thermal load; making the mesh coarser where the error is small; a target on a local quantity (the peak stress at one spot)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERefine automatically\u003C/strong\u003E for static, buckling, natural-frequency and steady thermal studies, on the error over the whole part: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/simulation\" data-topic=\"partcraft/simulation\"\u003ECheck stress and deflection\u003C/a\u003E; otherwise lower the element size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBearing and bolt loads, remote forces, moments, springs, prescribed displacements\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EForces and pressures on faces, gravity; fixed and frictionless faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESimulation of assemblies; materials that differ by direction (printed layers, composites)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne part, one isotropic material\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeveral solver threads for tetrahedral studies, heat transfer and generative design; any threads on a page that is not cross-origin isolated\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVoxel studies, buckling, stress over time and laminar flow use the threads where the page allows it: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"partcraft/settings\"\u003EPartCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurbulent flow with design accuracy (the Spalart–Allmaras model is in as experimental: pipe friction 30 to 34 % high, backward-step reattachment 21 to 39 % short; planned: finer cells near the walls), heat in flow, unsteady (time-accurate) flow, free surfaces, non-Newtonian fluids, rotating parts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELaminar, steady, isothermal flow of a Newtonian fluid, or the experimental turbulent model for qualitative patterns: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/flow-simulation\" data-topic=\"partcraft/flow-simulation\"\u003ESimulate fluid flow\u003C/a\u003E; the panel warns when the Reynolds number is past laminar\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStress limits and manufacturing rules (overhangs, milling directions) in generative design; several materials in one shape\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStiffest shape for a volume; check the outcome in Simulation: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/generative-design\" data-topic=\"partcraft/generative-design\"\u003EGrow a shape from loads\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn outcome as an editable (smooth, parametric) solid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTL, faceted STEP or a part with the outcome as an imported faceted solid\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElectric machine design: a rotor turning in a stator, torque against angle, saturating steel, magnets that lose strength past their knee, eddy currents and iron and copper losses; magnetic fields in 3-D or on curved meshes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinear 2-D magnetostatics on a grid in the analysis library: coils, iron, permanent magnets on their recoil line, gaps, inductance and pull: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/magnetic-fields-2d\" data-topic=\"partcraft/magnetic-fields-2d\"\u003EMagnetic fields in 2-D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scripts\"\u003EScripts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHover help, breakpoints and stepping in the script editor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFind, fold and complete are in (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript\"\u003EWrite and run scripts\u003C/a\u003E); for the rest, your own editor: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E in the Script group move scripts in and out; built-in functions are listed in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-language\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-language\"\u003EThe CraftScript language\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreakpoints and stepping through a script\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edisp\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Efprintf\u003C/code\u003E along the way; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E runs a few selected lines at a time\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFields and plots a script shows drawn in the Design workspace; scripts changing the part&#039;s parameters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey show in the Script workspace; scripts read parameters with \u003Ccode\u003Eparam\u003C/code\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript\"\u003EWrite and run scripts\u003C/a\u003E), change them on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E tab\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPictures and tables side by side with plots; editing a plot (zoom, axis limits) before saving it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESide by side shows the plots; save a plot as PNG, SVG or CSV and finish it elsewhere\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enumpy and other Python packages; Python offline; folding Python in the editor; Pascal Script\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPython&#039;s standard library, CraftScript and Lua: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/partcraft/craftscript-python\" data-topic=\"partcraft/craftscript-python\"\u003EScripts in Python\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPassing Lua functions to built-in functions; calling a library function from Lua by a name built while the script runs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrite the library function&#039;s name in the script (\u003Ccode\u003Ecs.static_tet(…)\u003C/code\u003E), or call it from a CraftScript script\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-app\"\u003EThe app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving, sharing and version history\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available yet. Save files to your computer and keep versions by name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPartCraft as an installed app on iPad and iPhone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse PartCraft in the device&#039;s browser; on Android and on the desktop it also runs in CadCraft Studio (getcadcraft.com/download)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContext (right-click) menus\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EToolbar and right panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorking fully offline from a fresh start\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly when the app has been saved for offline use: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReport what you miss most through the site&#039;s support page: it shapes what comes next.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"PartCraft is an early build. This page lists what it does not do yet, so you do not search for it, and what to do instead today. Nothing here has a date. Needs an exact kernel This version builds every part with the mesh kernel (see The mesh kernel). Everything below needs an exact geometry kernel, which this version does not have yet. Parts saved with these features still open: the features stay in the history, marked as not built, and are kept when you save. Not built yet Instead, today Fillet and Chamfer on round edges; fillets whose radius changes along the edge; rounds blended smoothly where they meet (the mesh kernel mitres them) Fillet and Chamfer work on straight edges between flat faces; round other corners in the sketch before you extrude (Fillet in a sketch, F) Shell opening curved faces; walls of different thicknesses Open flat faces; add material with extrudes where a wall must be thicker Sweep, Loft, Draft on curved faces; extruding up to a face, the next face or a vertex; tapered extrudes Extrude or revolve, in steps where needed; extrude to a depth you measure Rib, Base flange, Edge flange and flat patterns Extrude the plate or the rib's profile Surfaces: finishing a Form into the part, Patch, Loft surface, Gordon surface, Stitch, Thicken, Offset surface, Extend surface, Trim surface Not available Several bodies: Combine, Move body, Scale body, Path pattern, features that make a new body One solid; patterns and mirror copy features Sketches, planes, axes and points on faces that only an exact kernel can find (a face made by a fillet, a hole wall, a face of an imported solid) Sketch on base planes, reference planes and the flat faces of extrudes and revolves A generative design outcome opened as a part or added to it (it becomes an imported solid) Download the outcome as STL or STEP Opening STEP files, exact STEP export (AP242, AP214), STEP for other part modelers, opening a part modeler's export Export › STEP (faceted, mesh kernel), STL or 3MF; the macro for another part modeler still writes True arcs in drawing views Views and section views are drawn from the facets Exact volumes, areas and distances Measure gives the faceted solid's numbers, a little low on round parts; a finer Curves setting brings them closer Some rows in the tables below suggest one of these features as the way round something else. Those suggestions apply once an exact kernel is back. Features Not built yet Instead, today Face fillets, full-round fillets, fillets with radii set at points along an edge Constant or linear variable Fillet; several fillet features Shells with different wall thicknesses One Shell thickness; add material with extrudes where a wall must be thicker Draft on freeform (spline) faces Draft works on flat, cylindrical and conical faces Twisting sweeps, thin-wall and surface sweeps; end tangency on the Loft feature; a loft with one guide rail; spline paths Sweep with a fixed orientation or an end scale; a loft with two or more guide rails: see Sweep, loft and draft Ribs from arcs or splines; ribs grown square to the sketch (from a top view); draft on ribs; several ribs from one sketch Rib from one chain of lines in the rib's own plane: see Ribs and gussets \"Up to\" a curved surface, \"up to next\" stopping at a curved face Up to a flat face or a reference plane Counterdrilled holes, inch and pipe threads, tapered threads, thread run-outs and chamfered thread starts Counterbore with a small depth; ISO metric and trapezoidal threads, cosmetic or modelled: see Threads on shafts and bores, drawn or modelled Thin-feature extrudes Draw both walls of the profile Pattern copies skipped one by one, fill patterns Use separate features; patterns along a curve: Path pattern Copying patterns, mirrors, fillets, chamfers, shells and imported solids Copy the sketch or feature they are built on, or pattern it Exact (G1) tangency at a form's extraordinary points; merging a form's patches into larger faces Raise the form's Level: the approximated patches get smaller; see Freeform shapes with Form A form made of several separate closed pieces in one feature; bridges between forms One closed piece per Form feature; join them with Join Picking surface bodies in the view (except trim and patch points); surface fillets; untrim; G2 patches; Gordon surfaces tangent to curved neighbours (tangent to planes and flat faces is built) Choose them by name in the feature's panel; thicken first, then fillet the solid A body list in the history; leaving hidden bodies out of exports Rename and hide bodies in the part panel's body list; delete or suppress the feature that made a body to leave it out A non-uniform scale that keeps cylinders and cones analytic; non-uniform scaling of offset surfaces Scale body makes curved faces close B-splines (within 0.0000004 mm); a uniform scale stays exact Combining surface bodies; Minkowski sums Stitch or Thicken first, then Combine Tolerance analysis: stack-ups that turn corners (2D and 3D vector loops), sensitivities worked out from the geometry, Monte Carlo with other distributions, size tolerances on sketch dimensions, bonus tolerance from material condition Stack-up in an assembly: one direction, worst case and RSS, tolerances typed, from the title block's general tolerance or from GD&T frames: see Tolerance stack-ups Nesting: cutting order and lead-ins, cutting shared edges once, keeping and reusing leftover sheet, parts that must be kept together Nest flat parts on sheets… with spacing, margins, turns and grain: see Nest flat parts on sheets; plan the cut in your cutter's own software Sheet metal: hems, jogs, miter and contour flanges; flanges on curved edges or on a bent base flange's faces; sketched bends; corner closures; forming tools; converting a solid to sheet metal; the flat of a bent base flange with cuts in it; bend tables Base flange (a plate or a bent profile), Edge flange on a plate's straight edges with bend reliefs, and the flat pattern with a K-factor: see Sheet metal Draft analysis on assemblies; finding the parting line; wall-thickness analysis Draft analysis on the part in Design: see Check draft for moulding and casting; open each part of an assembly to check it Sketches Not built yet Instead, today Ellipses, tangent arcs, conics, splines with a set degree or weights (NURBS) Splines (cubic), arcs and circles with Tangent relations A point on a spline (a relation), curvature (G2) relations Put a spline's own point where it must pass Offset copies of splines that follow later changes; offsets of chains with sharp spline corners Offset again after the change; round the corner first Pattern copies that stay linked to the original; patterns along a curve Pattern again after a change Projecting free-form or tilted circular edges, projected geometry that follows the part Project straight and parallel circular edges, then dimension Driven (reference) dimensions Read sizes from the panel or the part facts Collinear relations Parallel with a 0 distance Pasting into another program (the system clipboard) Export DXF, STEP, STL or 3MF Typing arcs, relative or polar coordinates Type lines and circles; draw arcs with the Arc tool Selecting geometry without a pointer Needs a click or a tap Parameters and units Not built yet Instead, today Building a part's saved configurations from the command line by name (with their features turned off); configurations that change materials Export them as a CSV design table and pass it with --table (values only); configurations set parameters and turn features on and off: see Configurations and design tables Equations on the numbers of the newest features' own panels (plane, sweep, draft and similar) Write them in a script or with partcraft set (Scripts and the command line) A part shown in inches Sizes show in millimeters; type 2in in a number box or an expression and it becomes 50.8 Files Not built yet Instead, today Editing an imported STEP solid's own features (it arrives without history) Add or cut material with your own features Reading colors, names, assembly structure and PMI from STEP Not available glTF, OBJ, IGES, Parasolid, DWG export STL or 3MF for meshes; STEP for solids; DXF for 2D Opening STL, IGES, DXF or other programs' own files Ask for STEP Assembly structure (a tree with part names) in exported STEP Assembly STEP is one compound of the placed solids Saving to a folder of your choice without the browser's download Turn on \"ask where to save\" in the browser's download settings Drawings Not built yet Instead, today Dimensions beyond the three overall sizes (hole sizes, positions, tolerances on sizes) Mark up the PDF, or export DXF and dimension it in a 2D CAD program More than one section or detail; aligned, broken-out and auxiliary views One section and one detail per drawing Free text, notes and tables on the sheet (other than the title and revision blocks) The finish note, or text added on the PDF First-angle projection, moving views, portrait sheets Third angle, automatic layout, landscape sheets A4–A0 and ANSI A–E Live section view: several planes or a section box at once, an outline round the cut, saving the section in the file One plane at a time from Views › Section view; see Look inside with a section view Assembly drawings with more views, dimensions or balloons you can move One isometric (or exploded) view with automatic balloons and the parts list Copy and paste of GD&T and finish symbols Place each with Datum, Frame or Finish Assemblies Not built yet Instead, today Joints in a loop (more than one path to the grounded part) Keep the joints a tree; remove one joint of the loop Gear, rack, cam and screw joints; a ball joint limited to a cone; joints on curved faces or between points Rigid, revolute, slider, cylindrical, planar, ball and pin-in-slot joints on flat faces, each value with its own limits: see Build an assembly Exploded steps that turn parts, and dragging parts that joints place Straight explode steps; remove a joint to move a part by hand Sub-assemblies One level of parts only Gears, cams, contact Not available Manufacture Not built yet Instead, today 3D roughing and finishing of curved faces, 3D adaptive clearing, rest machining; adaptive passes written as arcs 2.5-axis face, pocket, 2D adaptive, contour, drill and chamfer: see Make toolpaths and G-code 4- and 5-axis toolpaths, turning, probing Set up one side at a time with the tool axis on the Setup tab Holder and shank collision checks Watch the flute-length warnings and check in your control's simulator Editing posts or adding other controls Fanuc-style, GRBL or LinuxCNC; adapt the downloaded file by hand Inch G-code (G20) Millimetres (G21) only Simulation and generative design Not built yet Instead, today Nonlinear studies: yielding, large deflections, contact between parts, fatigue Linear static and natural frequencies: see Check stress and deflection; keep the safety factor well above 1 Nonlinear buckling (imperfections, post-buckling, snap-through) Linear buckling load factors with a generous margin: see Heat, thermal stress and buckling Radiation between faces (view factors), contact resistance, temperature-dependent materials, inertia in thermal stress (thermal shock waves) Radiation to the surroundings, steady or quasi-static thermal stress over time, constant properties: see Heat, thermal stress and buckling Automatic refinement for transient heat and studies with a thermal load; making the mesh coarser where the error is small; a target on a local quantity (the peak stress at one spot) Refine automatically for static, buckling, natural-frequency and steady thermal studies, on the error over the whole part: see Check stress and deflection; otherwise lower the element size Bearing and bolt loads, remote forces, moments, springs, prescribed displacements Forces and pressures on faces, gravity; fixed and frictionless faces Simulation of assemblies; materials that differ by direction (printed layers, composites) One part, one isotropic material Several solver threads for tetrahedral studies, heat transfer and generative design; any threads on a page that is not cross-origin isolated Voxel studies, buckling, stress over time and laminar flow use the threads where the page allows it: see PartCraft settings Turbulent flow with design accuracy (the Spalart–Allmaras model is in as experimental: pipe friction 30 to 34 % high, backward-step reattachment 21 to 39 % short; planned: finer cells near the walls), heat in flow, unsteady (time-accurate) flow, free surfaces, non-Newtonian fluids, rotating parts Laminar, steady, isothermal flow of a Newtonian fluid, or the experimental turbulent model for qualitative patterns: see Simulate fluid flow; the panel warns when the Reynolds number is past laminar Stress limits and manufacturing rules (overhangs, milling directions) in generative design; several materials in one shape Stiffest shape for a volume; check the outcome in Simulation: see Grow a shape from loads An outcome as an editable (smooth, parametric) solid STL, faceted STEP or a part with the outcome as an imported faceted solid Electric machine design: a rotor turning in a stator, torque against angle, saturating steel, magnets that lose strength past their knee, eddy currents and iron and copper losses; magnetic fields in 3-D or on curved meshes Linear 2-D magnetostatics on a grid in the analysis library: coils, iron, permanent magnets on their recoil line, gaps, inductance and pull: see Magnetic fields in 2-D Scripts Not built yet Instead, today Hover help, breakpoints and stepping in the script editor Find, fold and complete are in (see Write and run scripts); for the rest, your own editor: Save and Open in the Script group move scripts in and out; built-in functions are listed in The CraftScript language Breakpoints and stepping through a script disp and fprintf along the way; Ctrl+Enter runs a few selected lines at a time Fields and plots a script shows drawn in the Design workspace; scripts changing the part's parameters They show in the Script workspace; scripts read parameters with param (see Write and run scripts), change them on the Parameters tab Pictures and tables side by side with plots; editing a plot (zoom, axis limits) before saving it Side by side shows the plots; save a plot as PNG, SVG or CSV and finish it elsewhere numpy and other Python packages; Python offline; folding Python in the editor; Pascal Script Python's standard library, CraftScript and Lua: see Scripts in Python Passing Lua functions to built-in functions; calling a library function from Lua by a name built while the script runs Write the library function's name in the script (cs.static_tet(…)), or call it from a CraftScript script The app Not built yet Instead, today Cloud saving, sharing and version history Not available yet. Save files to your computer and keep versions by name PartCraft as an installed app on iPad and iPhone Use PartCraft in the device's browser; on Android and on the desktop it also runs in CadCraft Studio (getcadcraft.com/download) Context (right-click) menus Toolbar and right panel Working fully offline from a fresh start Only when the app has been saved for offline use: see Working offline Report what you miss most through the site's support page: it shapes what comes next."},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"Using this help","summary":"Open the help, search it, and see the steps for your computer.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["help","f1","search","tutorials","manual"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-help","text":"Open the help","level":2},{"id":"find-a-topic","text":"Find a topic","level":2},{"id":"steps-for-your-computer","text":"Steps for your computer","level":2},{"id":"the-same-help-on-the-website","text":"The same help on the website","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-help\"\u003EOpen the help\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top of the app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E together with the \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E key (or the globe key), or use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EKeys on a Mac\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-a-topic\"\u003EFind a topic\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs&#039; command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETopics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E returns to where you were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-for-your-computer\"\u003ESteps for your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example &quot;Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChange\u003C/span\u003E on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E to see every version of every step, each labeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-same-help-on-the-website\"\u003EThe same help on the website\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform&#039;s steps. In the app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running. Open the help Press F1, or choose Help in the bar at the top of the app. Press Esc or choose Close to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was. On most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press F1 together with the fn key (or the globe key), or use the Help button. See Keys on a Mac. Find a topic Type in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs' command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page. Topics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting. Links inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. Back returns to where you were. Steps for your computer Some steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example \"Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online\". Choose Change on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague. Choose Show all to see every version of every step, each labeled. Keyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: Ctrl+S reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux. The same help on the website The website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform's steps. In the app, Open on the website at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online."},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"Browsers and computers","summary":"Which browsers the apps run in, what differs between them, and what your computer needs.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","browser","mac","windows","linux","ipad","tablet","webgl","requirements","memory"],"headings":[{"id":"which-browser","text":"Which browser","level":2},{"id":"what-differs-between-browsers","text":"What differs between browsers","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-and-linux","text":"Mac, Windows and Linux","level":2},{"id":"tablets-and-touch-screens","text":"Tablets and touch screens","level":2},{"id":"what-your-computer-needs","text":"What your computer needs","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"which-browser\"\u003EWhich browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an up-to-date version of one of these:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrowser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEngine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EWhat differs between browsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a folder.\u003C/strong\u003E Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving.\u003C/strong\u003E The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Chrome or Edge, turn on &quot;Ask where to save each file before downloading&quot; (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose &quot;Always ask you where to save files&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Safari, open Settings, then General, and set &quot;File download location&quot; to &quot;Ask for each download&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-and-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows and Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tablets-and-touch-screens\"\u003ETablets and touch screens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app&#039;s help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-your-computer-needs\"\u003EWhat your computer needs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVery large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see Where your files go). Which browser Use an up-to-date version of one of these: Browser Engine Notes Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium The most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling. Firefox Gecko Works. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows. Safari WebKit Works on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks. All three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser. What differs between browsers Opening a folder. Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three. Saving. The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first. In Chrome or Edge, turn on \"Ask where to save each file before downloading\" (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time. In Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose \"Always ask you where to save files\" to pick a folder every time. In Safari, open Settings, then General, and set \"File download location\" to \"Ask for each download\" to pick a folder every time. Mac, Windows and Linux The apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See Keys, mice and trackpads. Tablets and touch screens Some apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app's help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands. What your computer needs These are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements: 8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models). A graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser's settings. A screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them. Very large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down."},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"Keys, mice and trackpads","summary":"How shortcuts are written, function keys on a Mac, right-clicking and panning without a mouse.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcut","ctrl","command","cmd","option","fn","function keys","trackpad","right click","middle button","magic mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"how-shortcuts-are-written","text":"How shortcuts are written","level":2},{"id":"function-keys-on-a-mac","text":"Function keys on a Mac","level":2},{"id":"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps","text":"Shortcuts the browser keeps","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad","text":"Mouse buttons on a trackpad","level":2},{"id":"typing-in-a-text-box","text":"Typing in a text box","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"how-shortcuts-are-written\"\u003EHow shortcuts are written\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWritten\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn a Mac\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn Windows and Linux\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECommand (⌘) and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOption (⌥) and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe Delete key\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn and Delete (forward delete)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plus sign means hold the keys together. &quot;Then&quot; means press one after the other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works both ways. A few, such as full screen (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EFunction keys on a Mac\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ehold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (or the globe key) while you press the key, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eopen \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard Shortcuts\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EFunction Keys\u003C/strong\u003E, and turn on &quot;Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EShortcuts the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a Mac and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;); choose to stay, then save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser&#039;s own full screen (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F, or the green button on the window\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, which can hide the app&#039;s bars too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad\"\u003EMouse buttons on a trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers. Turn on &quot;Secondary click&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click opens the browser&#039;s menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-in-a-text-box\"\u003ETyping in a text box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app&#039;s one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing &quot;L&quot; types an L. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"How shortcuts are written The help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it. Written On a Mac On Windows and Linux Ctrl+S Command (⌘) and S Ctrl and S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F Ctrl, Shift and F Alt+← Option (⌥) and ← Alt and ← Backspace the Delete key Backspace Delete fn and Delete (forward delete) Delete Enter Return Enter A plus sign means hold the keys together. \"Then\" means press one after the other. Most app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so Ctrl+Z works both ways. A few, such as full screen (Ctrl+Shift+F), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies. Function keys on a Mac On most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12: hold fn (or the globe key) while you press the key, or open System Settings, then Keyboard, then Keyboard Shortcuts, then Function Keys, and turn on \"Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys\". Some F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead. Shortcuts the browser keeps A web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T and Ctrl+W always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead. Ctrl+Q on a Mac and Alt+F4 on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"); choose to stay, then save. The browser's own full screen (Control-Command-F, or the green button on the window / F11) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on Ctrl+Shift+F, which can hide the app's bars too. Mouse buttons on a trackpad The apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse: Right-click: click with two fingers. Turn on \"Secondary click\" in System Settings, then Trackpad, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding Ctrl while you click opens the browser's menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it. Right-click: click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps. Typing in a text box While you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app's one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing \"L\" types an L. Press Esc or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app."},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"Find a tool or setting","summary":"Press Ctrl+K and type a few letters to run any tool, change a setting or open a help topic.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["find","search","command palette","quick open","run command","ctrl k","cmd k","tool","setting","shortcut"],"headings":[{"id":"find-and-run","text":"Find and run","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don&#039;t need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-and-run\"\u003EFind and run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a few letters of what you want: &quot;fillet&quot;, &quot;panel text&quot;, &quot;units&quot;. Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETools that can&#039;t run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd-ons&#039; tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Every app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don't need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar. Find and run Press Ctrl+K, or choose Find in the bar at the top. Type a few letters of what you want: \"fillet\", \"panel text\", \"units\". Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list. Press Enter to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. Esc closes the box. A tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens Settings at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel. Things to know What you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away. Tools that can't run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out. Add-ons' tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on."},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"Arrange the panels and use a second monitor","summary":"Dock the side panels left, right or at the bottom, float them over the drawing, or move them to their own window on another screen.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["panels","dock","float","move panel","drag","layout","second monitor","two screens","dual monitor","multiple monitors","window","pop-up","reset layout"],"headings":[{"id":"move-a-panel-in-the-window","text":"Move a panel in the window","level":2},{"id":"use-the-keyboard","text":"Use the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor","text":"Put the panels on a second monitor","level":2},{"id":"more-than-one-panels-window","text":"More than one panels window","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-back","text":"Put the panels back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel&#039;s name and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-panel-in-the-window\"\u003EMove a panel in the window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the panel by its name at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with one finger on the panel&#039;s name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-the-keyboard\"\u003EUse the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button opens a menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the left\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock at the bottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloat over the drawing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it back\u003C/span\u003E. Tab to the button, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, move with the arrow keys and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor\"\u003EPut the panels on a second monitor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E on a panel, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove all panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E. You can also choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-than-one-panels-window\"\u003EMore than one panels window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E opens another window for that panel, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to panels window 2\u003C/span\u003E (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called &quot;panels 2&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA panels window that has no panels left closes by itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the next one\u003C/span\u003E once for each window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-back\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClosing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/span\u003E to bring the window back, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep them here\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E puts every panel back where it started.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one. This works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel's name and a ⋯ button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next. Move a panel in the window Drag the panel by its name at the top. While you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens. Drop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float. Both panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back. A floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was. Drag with one finger on the panel's name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the ⋯ menu. Use the keyboard Every panel's ⋯ button opens a menu: Dock on the left, Dock on the right, Dock at the bottom, Float over the drawing, Move to a new window and Put it back. Tab to the button, press Enter, move with the arrow keys and press Enter again. Esc closes the menu. Put the panels on a second monitor Choose ⋯ on a panel, then Move all panels to a new window. You can also choose Move panels to a new window in Settings. A small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like. Keep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window. Shortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window. Chrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose Put it on my other screen in the panels window (or Put the panels on my other screen in a panel's ⋯ menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time. Drag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time. More than one panels window Each panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor. In a panel's ⋯ menu, Move to a new window opens another window for that panel, and Move to panels window 2 (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called \"panels 2\". Drag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was. A panels window that has no panels left closes by itself. After a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose Open the next one once for each window. Put the panels back Choose Put the panels back in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were. Closing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose Open it to bring the window back, or Keep them here. Settings › General › Reset layout puts every panel back where it started. If nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again. The layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings."},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"Working offline","summary":"Save an app for offline use, what works with no connection, and what needs one.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["offline","no internet","airplane","site visit","cache","service worker","install","download","connection"],"headings":[{"id":"save-an-app-for-offline-use","text":"Save an app for offline use","level":2},{"id":"what-works-offline","text":"What works offline","level":2},{"id":"what-needs-a-connection","text":"What needs a connection","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app&#039;s files saved first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-an-app-for-offline-use\"\u003ESave an app for offline use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the app while you are online.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for &quot;Offline ready&quot;. The app&#039;s files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDo this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don&#039;t reload it until you are online again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-works-offline\"\u003EWhat works offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis help, including search.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-needs-a-connection\"\u003EWhat needs a connection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGetting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E refreshes the saved copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks to the website, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E and links to other pages.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser&#039;s site data removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install, is on getcadcraft.com/download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app's files saved first. Save an app for offline use Open the app while you are online. Choose Save for offline in the bar at the top. Wait for \"Offline ready\". The app's files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time. Do this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it. You are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don't reload it until you are online again. What works offline Opening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking. Saving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer. This help, including search. What needs a connection Opening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use. Getting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and Save for offline refreshes the saved copy. Links to the website, such as Open on the website and links to other pages. Cloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet. Things to know A saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser's site data removes it. Private windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save. Safari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip. CadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install, is on getcadcraft.com/download."},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"Where your files go","summary":"Files stay on your computer, how saving works, and what the browser remembers.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["privacy","upload","cloud","save","download","storage","local","security","confidential"],"headings":[{"id":"your-files-stay-on-your-computer","text":"Your files stay on your computer","level":2},{"id":"how-saving-works","text":"How saving works","level":2},{"id":"what-the-browser-remembers","text":"What the browser remembers","level":2},{"id":"cloud-saving","text":"Cloud saving","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-stay-on-your-computer\"\u003EYour files stay on your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. \u003Cstrong\u003EThe file is not uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E We never see it, and it works the same with no connection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-saving-works\"\u003EHow saving works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by \u003Cstrong\u003Edownloading\u003C/strong\u003E a new file:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as &quot;(edited)&quot; or &quot;(marked up)&quot;, so your original stays untouched.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-browser-remembers\"\u003EWhat the browser remembers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app&#039;s help says what it keeps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cloud-saving\"\u003ECloud saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Your files stay on your computer When you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. The file is not uploaded. We never see it, and it works the same with no connection. How saving works A browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by downloading a new file: The download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see Browsers and computers). Apps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as \"(edited)\" or \"(marked up)\", so your original stays untouched. If you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"). What the browser remembers Some apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app's help says what it keeps. Cloud saving Cloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way."},{"slug":"work-together-live","scope":"basics","title":"Work together live","summary":"Share the file open in an app so teammates edit it with you on your own network with a short code, and join someone else's session. Invite links over the internet are not open yet.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["live","studio live","work together","collaborate","share live","join","code","qr","invite link","link","host","approve","guest","network","relay","license","many people","limit","allow anyway","slow","recent edits","edit log","who changed","history"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"share-the-file-you-have-open","text":"Share the file you have open","level":2},{"id":"let-people-in","text":"Let people in","level":2},{"id":"when-many-people-join","text":"When many people join","level":2},{"id":"codes-on-your-own-network","text":"Codes on your own network","level":2},{"id":"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet","text":"Invite links over the internet: not open yet","level":2},{"id":"join-someone-else-s-session","text":"Join someone else's session","level":2},{"id":"who-changed-what-recent-edits","text":"Who changed what: Recent edits","level":2},{"id":"assistants-in-a-session","text":"Assistants in a session","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app&#039;s own page about live sessions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-the-file-you-have-open\"\u003EShare the file you have open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E in the app&#039;s bar, type the name others should see, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShare live\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStudio shows a code like \u003Ccode\u003EK7-Q4M2X\u003C/code\u003E with a picture to scan and the computer&#039;s address. Give the code to the people you want to invite.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-people-in\"\u003ELet people in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E lets them change the file with you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E turns them away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E shows everyone. Change a person&#039;s role there, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can&#039;t come back without a new invitation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWhen many people join\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA session&#039;s usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo allow more before anyone knocks, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow more people…\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to 20\u003C/span\u003E puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"codes-on-your-own-network\"\u003ECodes on your own network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA code works for 10 minutes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew code\u003C/span\u003E makes another; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop inviting\u003C/span\u003E closes it. People already in stay.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne-time code\u003C/span\u003E lets one person in per code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESessions on your own network are free, for everyone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet\"\u003EInvite links over the internet: not open yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInvite links aren&#039;t open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E part of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen invite links open, this is how they will work:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvite by link\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy the link and send it like a meeting link.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe link&#039;s secret part (after \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can&#039;t read, and you still approve each person. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew link\u003C/span\u003E makes the old one stop working at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop the link\u003C/span\u003E ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaking an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-someone-else-s-session\"\u003EJoin someone else&#039;s session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft Studio&#039;s home screen, find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin a live session\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host&#039;s link here too.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your name and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host&#039;s file once the host lets you in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout Studio, scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E, then under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin with the drawing open here\u003C/span\u003E type the code or the address and code, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin here\u003C/span\u003E. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host&#039;s drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can&#039;t find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"who-changed-what-recent-edits\"\u003EWho changed what: Recent edits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Live panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003ERecent edits\u003C/strong\u003E: who made each change and what it was (the step&#039;s name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow more\u003C/span\u003E lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly what is selected\u003C/span\u003E keeps just the edits that touched it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list is made in your own window from the session&#039;s edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants-in-a-session\"\u003EAssistants in a session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf someone turned on assistants in their app&#039;s settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host&#039;s own assistant joins at once; anyone else&#039;s waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host chooses \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd the session\u003C/span\u003E, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave\u003C/span\u003E does the same for you alone.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app's own page about live sessions. Share the file you have open Open the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer. Choose Live in the app's bar, type the name others should see, then choose Share live. Studio shows a code like K7-Q4M2X with a picture to scan and the computer's address. Give the code to the people you want to invite. Your file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves. Let people in Nobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet: Edit lets them change the file with you. View only lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back. Deny turns them away. The list under In the session shows everyone. Change a person's role there, or Remove them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can't come back without a new invitation. When many people join A session's usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you. When someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose: Allow anyway lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your Edit, View only or Deny. Keep it at 20 turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small. To allow more before anyone knocks, choose Allow more people… under In the session. Back to 20 puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again. If things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps. Codes on your own network A code works for 10 minutes. New code makes another; Stop inviting closes it. People already in stay. One-time code lets one person in per code. After 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you. Sessions on your own network are free, for everyone. Invite links over the internet: not open yet Invite links aren't open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the Over the internet part of the Live panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link. When invite links open, this is how they will work: Under Over the internet, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then Invite by link. Copy the link and send it like a meeting link. The link's secret part (after #) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can't read, and you still approve each person. New link makes the old one stop working at once; Stop the link ends it. Making an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license. Join someone else's session In CadCraft Studio's home screen, find Join a live session. Type the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host's link here too.) Type your name and choose Join. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host's file once the host lets you in. Without Studio, scan the picture under the host's code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser. In CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose Live, then under Join with the drawing open here type the code or the address and code, and choose Join here. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host's drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is. Guest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can't find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet. Who changed what: Recent edits The Live panel lists Recent edits: who made each change and what it was (the step's name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. Show more lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and Only what is selected keeps just the edits that touched it. The list is made in your own window from the session's edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so. Assistants in a session If someone turned on assistants in their app's settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host's own assistant joins at once; anyone else's waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: Allow or Deny. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves. When the session ends The host chooses End the session, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. Leave does the same for you alone."},{"slug":"pen-and-touch","scope":"basics","title":"Draw with a pen on a tablet","summary":"What the app does when you draw with a pen on a touch screen: it ignores your palm, can leave the drawing to the pen while your fingers move the view, and lets the pen's buttons and a two-finger tap undo.","section":"tasks","order":94,"keywords":["pen","stylus","tablet","touch","palm","palm rejection","finger","fingers","two fingers","tap","undo","redo","side button","barrel button","eraser","pressure","hover","phone"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time","text":"A pen is noticed the first time","level":2},{"id":"your-palm-is-ignored","text":"Your palm is ignored","level":2},{"id":"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view","text":"Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view","level":2},{"id":"undo-with-a-tap","text":"Undo with a tap","level":2},{"id":"the-pen-s-buttons","text":"The pen's buttons","level":2},{"id":"these-are-the-device-s-settings","text":"These are the device's settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time\"\u003EA pen is noticed the first time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-palm-is-ignored\"\u003EYour palm is ignored\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIgnore my palm while I use a pen\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view\"\u003ELet the pen draw and your fingers move the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw and select\u003C/span\u003E (the start): a finger does what the pen does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly move the view\u003C/span\u003E: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-with-a-tap\"\u003EUndo with a tap\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETap with two fingers to undo, three to redo\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-pen-s-buttons\"\u003EThe pen&#039;s buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen side button\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does (a right click)\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen eraser end\u003C/span\u003E (on pens that have one): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"these-are-the-device-s-settings\"\u003EThese are the device&#039;s settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E and resetting an app&#039;s settings leave them as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPen pressure and tilt don&#039;t change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen&#039;s pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in Settings › General › Pen and touch, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen. A pen is noticed the first time Nothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual. Your palm is ignored With Ignore my palm while I use a pen on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw. If a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work. Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view Under Fingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here: Draw and select (the start): a finger does what the pen does. Only move the view: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual. The toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way. Undo with a tap With Tap with two fingers to undo, three to redo on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap. The pen's buttons Pen side button: What the app does (a right click) at the start, or Undo or Redo. Pen eraser end (on pens that have one): What the app does at the start, or Undo or Redo. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches. These are the device's settings The pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. Reset the layout and resetting an app's settings leave them as they are. Not built yet Pen pressure and tilt don't change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen's pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app."},{"slug":"lost-toolbar","scope":"basics","title":"Where did my toolbar go?","summary":"The bars, menus or panels went missing. Here is what hides them on purpose, the keys that bring them back, and one button that puts the whole layout back.","section":"trouble","order":5,"keywords":["toolbar gone","toolbar missing","lost toolbar","lost menu","menu disappeared","ribbon disappeared","bars hidden","show bars","full screen","panels gone","reset layout","layout","ribbon","collapsed","locked toolbar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout","text":"The quickest fix: reset the layout","level":2},{"id":"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen","text":"The bars are hidden (full screen)","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-looks-different","text":"The toolbar looks different","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-s-own-menu","text":"The toolbar's own menu","level":2},{"id":"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small","text":"Everything is too big or too small","level":2},{"id":"the-panels-are-gone","text":"The panels are gone","level":2},{"id":"on-a-phone","text":"On a phone","level":2},{"id":"still-missing","text":"Still missing?","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ENothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout\"\u003EThe quickest fix: reset the layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear button in the app bar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app&#039;s own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E in Studio&#039;s own window also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. Each app&#039;s windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen\"\u003EThe bars are hidden (full screen)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe screen-mode button in the app bar, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the bars are hidden, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave this mode, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScreen\u003C/span\u003E: the app does what is chosen there when it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab, or tap the top edge of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-looks-different\"\u003EThe toolbar looks different\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools in another order, or tools you can&#039;t find:\u003C/strong\u003E someone arranged the toolbar. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOriginal order\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly icons, or only words:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar buttons\u003C/span\u003E in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools won&#039;t move:\u003C/strong\u003E the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E, or clear \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA ribbon that shows only its tab names:\u003C/strong\u003E it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMenus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar:\u003C/strong\u003E a menu style is on. Pick the app&#039;s own style under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › the app&#039;s tab › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E. Every command is in every style; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E finds any of them by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-s-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone arranging\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, when it looks right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: a locked toolbar can&#039;t be arranged or reset by accident.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: tools back in their first order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon menu style: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollapse the ribbon\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin the ribbon open\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a keyboard, the menu key (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift F10\"\u003E⇧ F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens it for the tool that has the focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanel text size\u003C/span\u003E beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio&#039;s own window has one in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-panels-are-gone\"\u003EThe panels are gone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for a second window called after the app with &quot;panels&quot;, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanels\u003C/span\u003E, which brings every panel home.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-phone\"\u003EOn a phone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-missing\"\u003EStill missing?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind any tool by name with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app&#039;s name and what you were doing.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Nothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back. The quickest fix: reset the layout Open Settings with the gear button in the app bar, or press Ctrl+,. Choose the General tab. Under Layout, choose Reset the layout. The toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app's own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under Menus, keys and mouse). In CadCraft Studio, Settings in Studio's own window also has Reset the layout in every app. Each app's windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout. The bars are hidden (full screen) The screen-mode button in the app bar, and Ctrl+Shift+F, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back. While the bars are hidden, a small Show bars tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press Ctrl+Shift+F to leave this mode, or Esc to leave full screen. If the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at Settings › General › Screen: the app does what is chosen there when it opens. Tap the Show bars tab, or tap the top edge of the screen. The toolbar looks different Tools in another order, or tools you can't find: someone arranged the toolbar. Choose Original order under Settings › General › Toolbar. Only icons, or only words: Toolbar buttons in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name. Tools won't move: the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose Unlock the toolbar, or clear Lock the toolbar under Settings › General › Toolbar. A ribbon that shows only its tab names: it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open. Menus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar: a menu style is on. Pick the app's own style under Settings › the app's tab › Menus, keys and mouse. Every command is in every style; Ctrl+K finds any of them by name. The toolbar's own menu Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu: Arrange the toolbar: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose Done arranging in the same menu, or press Esc, when it looks right. Lock the toolbar / Unlock the toolbar: a locked toolbar can't be arranged or reset by accident. Reset the toolbar: tools back in their first order. With a ribbon menu style: Collapse the ribbon or Pin the ribbon open. On a keyboard, the menu key (or Shift+F10) opens it for the tool that has the focus. Everything is too big or too small Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. Panel text size beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio's own window has one in its Settings too. The panels are gone Panels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window). Look for a second window called after the app with \"panels\", and choose Put the panels back in it. Or choose Reset layout under Settings › General › Panels, which brings every panel home. On a phone On a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The Tools button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and Move puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen. Still missing? Find any tool by name with Ctrl+K, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app's name and what you were doing."},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does."}]};
